const questions = [{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0001","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes scarcity?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Enterprise involves organising other factors of production and taking business risks.","Scarcity occurs when limited resources cannot satisfy all human wants.","Interest is the reward earned for supplying capital or lending funds.","A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used."],"answer":1,"why":"Scarcity: Scarcity occurs when limited resources cannot satisfy all human wants."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0002","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A town has funds for either a water plant or a new stadium, but not both. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Scarcity","Point beyond a PPC","Labour","Firm opportunity cost"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates scarcity: Scarcity occurs when limited resources cannot satisfy all human wants."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0003","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of scarcity?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A town has funds for either a water plant or a new stadium, but not both.","A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom.","A farmer pays an annual fee to use a neighbouring field.","An entrepreneur combines a premises, staff and equipment to open a food-processing firm."],"answer":0,"why":"A town has funds for either a water plant or a new stadium, but not both. This illustrates scarcity occurs when limited resources cannot satisfy all human wants."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0004","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to scarcity?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Interest rewards the postponement of spending and can encourage saving.","The holiday is forgone when scarce savings are committed to repairs.","Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other.","Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone."],"answer":3,"why":"Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0005","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to prioritise essential water infrastructure. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The student loses leisure time and enjoyment.","Training costs reduce funds available for current services.","It could improve public health by providing safe water to more households.","The family must give up the planned holiday."],"answer":2,"why":"It could improve public health by providing safe water to more households."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0006","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to prioritise essential water infrastructure. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["It could postpone leisure facilities valued by local residents.","Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs.","Future production possibilities may increase.","Households may build larger financial reserves."],"answer":0,"why":"It could postpone leisure facilities valued by local residents."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0007","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 54 tonnes of rice and 103 rolls of cloth to 69 tonnes of rice and 97 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["15 tonnes of rice","6 rolls of cloth","97 rolls of cloth","21 rolls of cloth"],"answer":1,"why":"Increasing rice output by 15 tonnes sacrifices 6 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0008","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to prioritise essential water infrastructure less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Extra capacity may create additional local jobs.","Higher wheat output may reduce dependence on food imports.","The business may reduce labour cost per item.","Its benefits may be delayed if construction is costly or poorly managed."],"answer":3,"why":"Its benefits may be delayed if construction is costly or poorly managed."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0009","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to prioritise essential water infrastructure. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["New firms may introduce innovation and more choice.","It could improve public health by providing safe water to more households.","Future production possibilities may increase.","Resources are not left unnecessarily idle."],"answer":1,"why":"It could improve public health by providing safe water to more households."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0010","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A town has funds for either a water plant or a new stadium, but not both. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone.","Ten extra tonnes of wheat require sacrificing eight rolls of cloth in this example.","The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone.","The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds."],"answer":0,"why":"Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0011","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes unlimited wants?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The opportunity cost of an investment is the value of the next best alternative investment not selected.","Public spending on one project prevents use of the same funds for another.","Human wants are numerous and expand as people seek more goods and services.","Moving along a production possibility curve involves reallocating resources between two goods."],"answer":2,"why":"Unlimited wants: Human wants are numerous and expand as people seek more goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0012","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Capital","Opportunity cost","Point beyond a PPC","Unlimited wants"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates unlimited wants: Human wants are numerous and expand as people seek more goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0013","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of unlimited wants?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks.","A saver receives interest after depositing money in a bank.","As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips.","A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours."],"answer":2,"why":"As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips. This illustrates human wants are numerous and expand as people seek more goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0014","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to unlimited wants?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices.","Ten extra tonnes of wheat require sacrificing eight rolls of cloth in this example.","Workers use their skills and time to deliver services.","Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other."],"answer":0,"why":"Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0015","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to allocate a larger budget to basic needs rather than luxury projects. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher wage bills may lead to higher fares.","It can restrict housing supply near growing cities.","Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households.","Reconstruction uses scarce funds that could serve other needs."],"answer":2,"why":"Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0016","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to allocate a larger budget to basic needs rather than luxury projects. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Some households may prefer other goods and services.","It could preserve the capacity to grow food.","Training may increase the designer's future productivity.","New skills may improve later employment opportunities."],"answer":0,"why":"Some households may prefer other goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0017","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to allocate a larger budget to basic needs rather than luxury projects?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The spending may reduce hardship where essential services are scarce.","Farm productivity may remain low without investment.","Operating at full capacity can leave little spare capacity for emergencies.","Projects may take years before capacity rises."],"answer":0,"why":"The spending may reduce hardship where essential services are scarce."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0018","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to allocate a larger budget to basic needs rather than luxury projects less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["It may protect ecosystems that support future production.","Limited tax revenue may prevent all basic needs from being met.","The economy could move closer to efficient production.","The designer could offer higher-value services later."],"answer":1,"why":"Limited tax revenue may prevent all basic needs from being met."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0019","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 58 tonnes of rice and 106 rolls of cloth to 78 tonnes of rice and 98 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["98 rolls of cloth","8 rolls of cloth","28 rolls of cloth","20 tonnes of rice"],"answer":1,"why":"Increasing rice output by 20 tonnes sacrifices 8 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0020","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Lost productive resources reduce the economy's possible output.","Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone.","Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices.","Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative."],"answer":2,"why":"Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0021","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes what to produce?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["This allocation question concerns which goods and services an economy should make.","A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used.","An economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost of provision.","Rent is the income earned by owners for supplying land or natural resources."],"answer":0,"why":"What to produce: This allocation question concerns which goods and services an economy should make."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0022","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A region considers producing more solar panels or more furniture with its existing factories. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["What to produce","Firm opportunity cost","Allocation choices","Scarcity"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates what to produce: This allocation question concerns which goods and services an economy should make."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0023","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of what to produce?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom.","A saver receives interest after depositing money in a bank.","A region considers producing more solar panels or more furniture with its existing factories.","A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday."],"answer":2,"why":"A region considers producing more solar panels or more furniture with its existing factories. This illustrates this allocation question concerns which goods and services an economy should make."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0024","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to what to produce?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output.","Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other.","The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment.","The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone."],"answer":0,"why":"Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0025","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase production of renewable-energy equipment. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Training costs reduce funds available for current services.","Machinery purchases require significant finance.","Reconstruction uses scarce funds that could serve other needs.","It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment."],"answer":3,"why":"It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0026","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase production of renewable-energy equipment. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["New firms may introduce innovation and more choice.","Fewer resources may remain for furniture manufacturing.","Higher skills could improve the quality of treatment.","Packaging could become faster and less wasteful."],"answer":1,"why":"Fewer resources may remain for furniture manufacturing."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0027","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to increase production of renewable-energy equipment?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Unexpected repair costs may exhaust emergency savings.","Competition may limit the café's future profit.","It could create employment in a growing industry.","Unused capacity may lack the skills demanded by buyers."],"answer":2,"why":"It could create employment in a growing industry."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0028","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to increase production of renewable-energy equipment less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher wheat output may reduce dependence on food imports.","Maximum feasible output can be obtained with current technology.","Successful start-ups can create jobs and raise output.","Demand for solar equipment may be too weak to justify the switch."],"answer":3,"why":"Demand for solar equipment may be too weak to justify the switch."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0029","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to increase production of renewable-energy equipment. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["New firms may introduce innovation and more choice.","It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment.","The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","Output and employment could rise together."],"answer":1,"why":"It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0030","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A region considers producing more solar panels or more furniture with its existing factories. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Better equipment can increase the productive capacity of workers.","Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output.","The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone.","Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change."],"answer":1,"why":"Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0031","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes how to produce?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["An economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost of provision.","This allocation question concerns which methods and combinations of inputs should be used.","A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used.","This allocation question concerns which goods and services an economy should make."],"answer":1,"why":"How to produce: This allocation question concerns which methods and combinations of inputs should be used."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0032","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 62 tonnes of rice and 109 rolls of cloth to 87 tonnes of rice and 99 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["25 tonnes of rice","35 rolls of cloth","10 rolls of cloth","99 rolls of cloth"],"answer":2,"why":"Increasing rice output by 25 tonnes sacrifices 10 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0033","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of how to produce?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours.","A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment.","A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre.","A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers."],"answer":3,"why":"A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers. This illustrates this allocation question concerns which methods and combinations of inputs should be used."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0034","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to how to produce?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Relative factor costs, productivity and technology determine production techniques.","Natural resources provide inputs whose availability constrains output.","Positive profit rewards risk-taking and can finance expansion.","At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other."],"answer":0,"why":"Relative factor costs, productivity and technology determine production techniques."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0035","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce automated production at a bakery. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.","Training and investment consume resources today.","Restarting factories may require extra investment.","Some new enterprises will fail and waste invested resources."],"answer":0,"why":"Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0036","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce automated production at a bakery. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["New skills may improve later employment opportunities.","High machinery costs may strain cash flow.","Long-run productive capacity may rise.","Resources are not left unnecessarily idle."],"answer":1,"why":"High machinery costs may strain cash flow."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0037","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to introduce automated production at a bakery?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Projects may take years before capacity rises.","Protection may be inadequate if flood conditions worsen.","Consistent production may reduce waste and improve reliability.","Operating at full capacity can leave little spare capacity for emergencies."],"answer":2,"why":"Consistent production may reduce waste and improve reliability."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0038","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to introduce automated production at a bakery less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Improved reliability can lower the rate of faulty products.","The work may build experience useful in later jobs.","The economy could move closer to efficient production.","Automation may reduce demand for some workers."],"answer":3,"why":"Automation may reduce demand for some workers."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0039","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to introduce automated production at a bakery. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["New skills may improve later employment opportunities.","The student earns income for future purchases.","Training may increase the designer's future productivity.","Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs."],"answer":3,"why":"Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0040","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Relative factor costs, productivity and technology determine production techniques.","Interest rewards the postponement of spending and can encourage saving.","Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change.","Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone."],"answer":0,"why":"Relative factor costs, productivity and technology determine production techniques."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0041","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes for whom to produce?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["This allocation question concerns which methods and combinations of inputs should be used.","This allocation question concerns who receives goods and services that are produced.","Human wants are numerous and expand as people seek more goods and services.","Public spending on one project prevents use of the same funds for another."],"answer":1,"why":"For whom to produce: This allocation question concerns who receives goods and services that are produced."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0042","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A housing developer must decide whether to build high-price apartments or low-cost homes. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["For whom to produce","Wages as a factor reward","Household opportunity cost","Scarcity"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates for whom to produce: This allocation question concerns who receives goods and services that are produced."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0043","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of for whom to produce?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist.","A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment.","A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert.","A housing developer must decide whether to build high-price apartments or low-cost homes."],"answer":3,"why":"A housing developer must decide whether to build high-price apartments or low-cost homes. This illustrates this allocation question concerns who receives goods and services that are produced."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0044","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 66 tonnes of rice and 112 rolls of cloth to 96 tonnes of rice and 100 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["100 rolls of cloth","12 rolls of cloth","42 rolls of cloth","30 tonnes of rice"],"answer":1,"why":"Increasing rice output by 30 tonnes sacrifices 12 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0045","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reserve some new homes for lower-income households. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The immediate fee from the client project is forgone.","Delaying vehicle purchases may cause missed deliveries.","It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes.","Consumers may face fewer refrigerators."],"answer":2,"why":"It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0046","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reserve some new homes for lower-income households. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["New skills may improve later employment opportunities.","It could preserve the capacity to grow food.","The student earns income for future purchases.","The developer may receive less revenue per home."],"answer":3,"why":"The developer may receive less revenue per home."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0047","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to reserve some new homes for lower-income households?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Advice alone may not solve a shortage of finance.","Unused capacity may lack the skills demanded by buyers.","Fatigue may reduce performance in schoolwork.","It could reduce overcrowding and associated social costs."],"answer":3,"why":"It could reduce overcrowding and associated social costs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0048","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to reserve some new homes for lower-income households less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["It may protect ecosystems that support future production.","The worker may access a growing sector.","Subsidies may be needed to make the policy financially viable.","Farmers could expand agricultural output."],"answer":2,"why":"Subsidies may be needed to make the policy financially viable."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0049","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to reserve some new homes for lower-income households. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["New skills may improve later employment opportunities.","It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes.","It could preserve the capacity to grow food.","Long-run productive capacity may rise."],"answer":1,"why":"It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0050","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A housing developer must decide whether to build high-price apartments or low-cost homes. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other.","The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills.","Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output.","The holiday is forgone when scarce savings are committed to repairs."],"answer":2,"why":"Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0051","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes economic good?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["An economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost of provision.","This allocation question concerns which methods and combinations of inputs should be used.","Time is a scarce resource, and its allocation involves opportunity cost.","Enterprise involves organising other factors of production and taking business risks."],"answer":0,"why":"Economic good: An economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost of provision."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0052","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Interest as a factor reward","Outward shift of a PPC","Free good","Economic good"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates economic good: An economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost of provision."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0053","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of economic good?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water.","A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment.","A qualified mechanic chooses a lower-paid apprenticeship in renewable energy.","A self-employed designer can accept either a new client project or an advanced training course this week."],"answer":0,"why":"A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water. This illustrates an economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost of provision."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0054","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to economic good?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment.","Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods.","Better equipment can increase the productive capacity of workers.","The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds."],"answer":1,"why":"Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0055","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest public funds in a reliable water-supply network. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Current income will be lower during training.","Machinery purchases require significant finance.","Banks may face a higher cost of funding.","Households may save time previously spent collecting water."],"answer":3,"why":"Households may save time previously spent collecting water."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0056","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest public funds in a reliable water-supply network. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","Infrastructure requires scarce funds and maintenance.","Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong.","The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit."],"answer":1,"why":"Infrastructure requires scarce funds and maintenance."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0057","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 70 tonnes of rice and 115 rolls of cloth to 105 tonnes of rice and 101 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["49 rolls of cloth","35 tonnes of rice","101 rolls of cloth","14 rolls of cloth"],"answer":3,"why":"Increasing rice output by 35 tonnes sacrifices 14 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0058","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to invest public funds in a reliable water-supply network less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Improved reliability can lower the rate of faulty products.","Water pricing may exclude low-income households without support.","The business may reduce labour cost per item.","Higher productive capacity could support economic growth."],"answer":1,"why":"Water pricing may exclude low-income households without support."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0059","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to invest public funds in a reliable water-supply network. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Packaging could become faster and less wasteful.","It could preserve the capacity to grow food.","Households may save time previously spent collecting water.","Households may build larger financial reserves."],"answer":2,"why":"Households may save time previously spent collecting water."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0060","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods.","Pay encourages people to supply time and skills to employers.","Payment provides an incentive to make land available for production.","More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods."],"answer":0,"why":"Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0061","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes free good?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Human wants are numerous and expand as people seek more goods and services.","Resource allocation is the decision about how scarce inputs are distributed among alternative uses.","A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision.","Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made."],"answer":2,"why":"Free good: A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0062","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Free good","Point on a PPC","Land","Labour"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates free good: A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0063","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of free good?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A self-employed designer can accept either a new client project or an advanced training course this week.","On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it.","A region has idle workshops and unemployed workers despite adequate raw materials.","A country uses more of its factories for buses and fewer for refrigerators."],"answer":1,"why":"On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it. This illustrates a free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0064","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to free good?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting.","Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other.","Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone.","The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills."],"answer":0,"why":"Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0065","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to protect public access to naturally available coastal wind and open space. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Training and investment consume resources today.","Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price.","Current income will be lower during training.","Residents lose the benefits of the delayed sports centre."],"answer":1,"why":"Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0066","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to protect public access to naturally available coastal wind and open space. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","Nearby construction may reduce access to the open space.","Resources are not left unnecessarily idle.","It could preserve the capacity to grow food."],"answer":1,"why":"Nearby construction may reduce access to the open space."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0067","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to protect public access to naturally available coastal wind and open space?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Further floods could damage repaired infrastructure.","A shortage of suitable farmland may raise the sacrifice required.","Public access may support well-being and recreation.","Competition may limit the café's future profit."],"answer":2,"why":"Public access may support well-being and recreation."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0068","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to protect public access to naturally available coastal wind and open space less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Successful start-ups can create jobs and raise output.","The business may reduce labour cost per item.","Poor environmental management could reduce amenity quality.","The designer could offer higher-value services later."],"answer":2,"why":"Poor environmental management could reduce amenity quality."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0069","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 74 tonnes of rice and 118 rolls of cloth to 114 tonnes of rice and 102 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["16 rolls of cloth","40 tonnes of rice","56 rolls of cloth","102 rolls of cloth"],"answer":0,"why":"Increasing rice output by 40 tonnes sacrifices 16 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0070","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Ten extra tonnes of wheat require sacrificing eight rolls of cloth in this example.","The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills.","Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting.","Payment provides an incentive to make land available for production."],"answer":2,"why":"Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0071","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes allocation choices?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used.","Scarcity occurs when limited resources cannot satisfy all human wants.","A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision.","Resource allocation is the decision about how scarce inputs are distributed among alternative uses."],"answer":3,"why":"Allocation choices: Resource allocation is the decision about how scarce inputs are distributed among alternative uses."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0072","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Allocation choices","Wages as a factor reward","Outward shift of a PPC","Scarcity in time allocation"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates allocation choices: Resource allocation is the decision about how scarce inputs are distributed among alternative uses."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0073","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of allocation choices?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs.","A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday.","A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert.","A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons."],"answer":3,"why":"A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons. This illustrates resource allocation is the decision about how scarce inputs are distributed among alternative uses."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0074","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to allocation choices?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Interest rewards the postponement of spending and can encourage saving.","Time spent on one activity cannot be used for the next best option.","Using the room for one activity prevents its simultaneous use for the other.","Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative."],"answer":2,"why":"Using the room for one activity prevents its simultaneous use for the other."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0075","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to give priority to science classes in the spare room. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Some new enterprises will fail and waste invested resources.","Banks may face a higher cost of funding.","Higher wage bills may lead to higher fares.","More pupils could access specialist science teaching."],"answer":3,"why":"More pupils could access specialist science teaching."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0076","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to give priority to science classes in the spare room. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Language classes may become overcrowded.","Higher wages may attract qualified drivers.","The owner could earn rent from previously idle land.","Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Language classes may become overcrowded."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0077","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to give priority to science classes in the spare room?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Poor soil quality may limit the value of leasing the land.","Advice alone may not solve a shortage of finance.","The choice may improve science-related skills.","Future wages are uncertain and may not justify the sacrifice."],"answer":2,"why":"The choice may improve science-related skills."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0078","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to give priority to science classes in the spare room less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Successful start-ups can create jobs and raise output.","Reduced disruption may help economic activity.","Expected gains depend on the demand for each subject.","Extra capacity may create additional local jobs."],"answer":2,"why":"Expected gains depend on the demand for each subject."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0079","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to give priority to science classes in the spare room. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Restored infrastructure could recover lost production.","Higher skills could improve the quality of treatment.","The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","More pupils could access specialist science teaching."],"answer":3,"why":"More pupils could access specialist science teaching."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0080","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Lost productive resources reduce the economy's possible output.","The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds.","Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other.","Using the room for one activity prevents its simultaneous use for the other."],"answer":3,"why":"Using the room for one activity prevents its simultaneous use for the other."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0081","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes land?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Time is a scarce resource, and its allocation involves opportunity cost.","A point on a production possibility curve represents an attainable combination using resources efficiently at a given time.","Moving along a production possibility curve involves reallocating resources between two goods.","Land is the factor of production comprising natural resources used to produce goods and services."],"answer":3,"why":"Land: Land is the factor of production comprising natural resources used to produce goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0082","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 78 tonnes of rice and 121 rolls of cloth to 88 tonnes of rice and 103 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["103 rolls of cloth","28 rolls of cloth","18 rolls of cloth","10 tonnes of rice"],"answer":2,"why":"Increasing rice output by 10 tonnes sacrifices 18 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0083","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of land?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks.","A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert.","A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water.","A farm uses fertile soil and a river to grow vegetables."],"answer":3,"why":"A farm uses fertile soil and a river to grow vegetables. This illustrates land is the factor of production comprising natural resources used to produce goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0084","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to land?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Natural resources provide inputs whose availability constrains output.","Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other.","Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output.","The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone."],"answer":0,"why":"Natural resources provide inputs whose availability constrains output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0085","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Investment diverts funds from current consumption.","It could preserve the capacity to grow food.","Current income will be lower during training.","The country gives up cloth output that could have been sold."],"answer":1,"why":"It could preserve the capacity to grow food."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0086","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["New skills may improve later employment opportunities.","It can restrict housing supply near growing cities.","Long-run productive capacity may rise.","Output and employment could rise together."],"answer":1,"why":"It can restrict housing supply near growing cities."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0087","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["It may protect ecosystems that support future production.","Subsidies may be needed to make the policy financially viable.","Automation may reduce demand for some workers.","Future wages are uncertain and may not justify the sacrifice."],"answer":0,"why":"It may protect ecosystems that support future production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0088","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Firms could produce more with available inputs.","Consistent production may reduce waste and improve reliability.","The choice may improve science-related skills.","Farm productivity may remain low without investment."],"answer":3,"why":"Farm productivity may remain low without investment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0089","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes.","It could preserve the capacity to grow food.","Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price.","Restored infrastructure could recover lost production."],"answer":1,"why":"It could preserve the capacity to grow food."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0090","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A farm uses fertile soil and a river to grow vegetables. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Using the room for one activity prevents its simultaneous use for the other.","Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change.","More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods.","Natural resources provide inputs whose availability constrains output."],"answer":3,"why":"Natural resources provide inputs whose availability constrains output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0091","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Resource allocation is the decision about how scarce inputs are distributed among alternative uses.","Rent is the income earned by owners for supplying land or natural resources.","Land is the factor of production comprising natural resources used to produce goods and services.","Labour is the human physical and mental effort used in production."],"answer":3,"why":"Labour: Labour is the human physical and mental effort used in production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0092","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Economic good","Labour","Wages as a factor reward","Outward shift of a PPC"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates labour: Labour is the human physical and mental effort used in production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0093","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A town has funds for either a water plant or a new stadium, but not both.","An economy increases wheat production from 50 to 60 tonnes by reducing cloth output from 90 to 82 rolls.","A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff.","A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre."],"answer":2,"why":"A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff. This illustrates labour is the human physical and mental effort used in production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0094","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 82 tonnes of rice and 124 rolls of cloth to 97 tonnes of rice and 104 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["104 rolls of cloth","20 rolls of cloth","35 rolls of cloth","15 tonnes of rice"],"answer":1,"why":"Increasing rice output by 15 tonnes sacrifices 20 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0095","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to fund training for healthcare workers. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Nearby construction may reduce access to the open space.","Reconstruction uses scarce funds that could serve other needs.","Consumers may face fewer refrigerators.","Higher skills could improve the quality of treatment."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher skills could improve the quality of treatment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0096","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to fund training for healthcare workers. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Public transport supply could increase.","Training costs reduce funds available for current services.","Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households.","Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs."],"answer":1,"why":"Training costs reduce funds available for current services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0097","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to fund training for healthcare workers?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Subsidies may be needed to make the policy financially viable.","Forecast benefits of each alternative may turn out to be inaccurate.","Operating at full capacity can leave little spare capacity for emergencies.","A larger skilled workforce could expand healthcare capacity."],"answer":3,"why":"A larger skilled workforce could expand healthcare capacity."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0098","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to fund training for healthcare workers less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Repair work may reopen trade routes and jobs.","Higher productive capacity could support economic growth.","Trained workers may move to higher-paying regions.","It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness."],"answer":2,"why":"Trained workers may move to higher-paying regions."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0099","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to fund training for healthcare workers. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher skills could improve the quality of treatment.","It could improve public health by providing safe water to more households.","Future production possibilities may increase.","Output and employment could rise together."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher skills could improve the quality of treatment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0100","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Workers use their skills and time to deliver services.","Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other.","Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change.","Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone."],"answer":0,"why":"Workers use their skills and time to deliver services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0101","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes capital?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The opportunity cost of an investment is the value of the next best alternative investment not selected.","An inward shift of a production possibility curve shows a fall in the maximum attainable output.","Capital consists of man-made resources used to produce other goods and services.","For a firm, using funds in one project means forgoing the best alternative investment."],"answer":2,"why":"Capital: Capital consists of man-made resources used to produce other goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0102","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["For whom to produce","Capital","Profit as a factor reward","Firm opportunity cost"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates capital: Capital consists of man-made resources used to produce other goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0103","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of capital?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips.","A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons.","A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist.","A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment."],"answer":3,"why":"A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment. This illustrates capital consists of man-made resources used to produce other goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0104","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to capital?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods.","Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone.","The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone.","Better equipment can increase the productive capacity of workers."],"answer":3,"why":"Better equipment can increase the productive capacity of workers."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0105","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in more efficient factory machinery. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The country gives up cloth output that could have been sold.","Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs.","Training and investment consume resources today.","It could postpone leisure facilities valued by local residents."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0106","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in more efficient factory machinery. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Machinery purchases require significant finance.","The student earns income for future purchases.","Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses.","Output and employment could rise together."],"answer":0,"why":"Machinery purchases require significant finance."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0107","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 86 tonnes of rice and 127 rolls of cloth to 106 tonnes of rice and 123 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["20 tonnes of rice","123 rolls of cloth","4 rolls of cloth","24 rolls of cloth"],"answer":2,"why":"Increasing rice output by 20 tonnes sacrifices 4 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0108","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to invest in more efficient factory machinery less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Equipment may become obsolete before costs are recovered.","The choice may improve science-related skills.","It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness.","Higher wheat output may reduce dependence on food imports."],"answer":0,"why":"Equipment may become obsolete before costs are recovered."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0109","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to invest in more efficient factory machinery. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs.","The student earns income for future purchases.","Future production possibilities may increase.","Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0110","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone.","Better equipment can increase the productive capacity of workers.","More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods.","Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other."],"answer":1,"why":"Better equipment can increase the productive capacity of workers."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0111","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes enterprise?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Capital consists of man-made resources used to produce other goods and services.","Enterprise involves organising other factors of production and taking business risks.","Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made.","For a worker, choosing one occupation involves giving up the next best job or use of time."],"answer":1,"why":"Enterprise: Enterprise involves organising other factors of production and taking business risks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0112","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: An entrepreneur combines a premises, staff and equipment to open a food-processing firm. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Land","Movement along a PPC","Labour","Enterprise"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates enterprise: Enterprise involves organising other factors of production and taking business risks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0113","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of enterprise?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment.","A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday.","An entrepreneur combines a premises, staff and equipment to open a food-processing firm.","A region has idle workshops and unemployed workers despite adequate raw materials."],"answer":2,"why":"An entrepreneur combines a premises, staff and equipment to open a food-processing firm. This illustrates enterprise involves organising other factors of production and taking business risks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0114","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to enterprise?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods.","The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds.","Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other.","Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative."],"answer":1,"why":"The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0115","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to support new entrepreneurs with business advice. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The showroom refurbishment must be postponed.","The country gives up cloth output that could have been sold.","Current income will be lower during training.","New firms may introduce innovation and more choice."],"answer":3,"why":"New firms may introduce innovation and more choice."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0116","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to support new entrepreneurs with business advice. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Some new enterprises will fail and waste invested resources.","Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.","Repairs may prevent more expensive damage later.","Public transport supply could increase."],"answer":0,"why":"Some new enterprises will fail and waste invested resources."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0117","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to support new entrepreneurs with business advice?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Automation may reduce demand for some workers.","Factories may not be easily adapted to bus production.","Its benefits may be delayed if construction is costly or poorly managed.","Successful start-ups can create jobs and raise output."],"answer":3,"why":"Successful start-ups can create jobs and raise output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0118","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to support new entrepreneurs with business advice less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Advice alone may not solve a shortage of finance.","Higher wheat output may reduce dependence on food imports.","The designer could offer higher-value services later.","It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness."],"answer":0,"why":"Advice alone may not solve a shortage of finance."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0119","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 90 tonnes of rice and 130 rolls of cloth to 115 tonnes of rice and 124 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["25 tonnes of rice","6 rolls of cloth","124 rolls of cloth","31 rolls of cloth"],"answer":1,"why":"Increasing rice output by 25 tonnes sacrifices 6 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0120","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: An entrepreneur combines a premises, staff and equipment to open a food-processing firm. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change.","The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds.","Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output.","The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone."],"answer":1,"why":"The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0121","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes rent as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["For a worker, choosing one occupation involves giving up the next best job or use of time.","Rent is the income earned by owners for supplying land or natural resources.","Wages are payments received for supplying labour in production.","A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used."],"answer":1,"why":"Rent as a factor reward: Rent is the income earned by owners for supplying land or natural resources."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0122","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A farmer pays an annual fee to use a neighbouring field. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Interest as a factor reward","Rent as a factor reward","Firm opportunity cost","Allocation choices"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates rent as a factor reward: Rent is the income earned by owners for supplying land or natural resources."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0123","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of rent as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A qualified mechanic chooses a lower-paid apprenticeship in renewable energy.","A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist.","A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water.","A farmer pays an annual fee to use a neighbouring field."],"answer":3,"why":"A farmer pays an annual fee to use a neighbouring field. This illustrates rent is the income earned by owners for supplying land or natural resources."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0124","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to rent as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods.","Payment provides an incentive to make land available for production.","The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills.","Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change."],"answer":1,"why":"Payment provides an incentive to make land available for production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0125","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Language classes may become overcrowded.","Residents lose the benefits of the delayed sports centre.","The owner could earn rent from previously idle land.","The student loses leisure time and enjoyment."],"answer":2,"why":"The owner could earn rent from previously idle land."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0126","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Restored infrastructure could recover lost production.","Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong.","Rent payments increase farmers' production costs.","Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs."],"answer":2,"why":"Rent payments increase farmers' production costs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0127","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Future wages are uncertain and may not justify the sacrifice.","Fatigue may reduce performance in schoolwork.","Automation may reduce demand for some workers.","Farmers could expand agricultural output."],"answer":3,"why":"Farmers could expand agricultural output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0128","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Poor soil quality may limit the value of leasing the land.","Reduced disruption may help economic activity.","Maximum feasible output can be obtained with current technology.","The choice may improve science-related skills."],"answer":0,"why":"Poor soil quality may limit the value of leasing the land."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0129","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The student earns income for future purchases.","The owner could earn rent from previously idle land.","The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit.","Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses."],"answer":1,"why":"The owner could earn rent from previously idle land."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0130","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A farmer pays an annual fee to use a neighbouring field. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Payment provides an incentive to make land available for production.","The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment.","More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods.","Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices."],"answer":0,"why":"Payment provides an incentive to make land available for production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0131","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes wages as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used.","Wages are payments received for supplying labour in production.","Enterprise involves organising other factors of production and taking business risks.","This allocation question concerns who receives goods and services that are produced."],"answer":1,"why":"Wages as a factor reward: Wages are payments received for supplying labour in production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0132","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 50 tonnes of rice and 133 rolls of cloth to 80 tonnes of rice and 125 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["125 rolls of cloth","30 tonnes of rice","38 rolls of cloth","8 rolls of cloth"],"answer":3,"why":"Increasing rice output by 30 tonnes sacrifices 8 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0133","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of wages as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks.","A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday.","A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist.","A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours."],"answer":3,"why":"A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours. This illustrates wages are payments received for supplying labour in production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0134","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to wages as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other.","The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills.","Pay encourages people to supply time and skills to employers.","Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone."],"answer":2,"why":"Pay encourages people to supply time and skills to employers."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0135","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Language classes may become overcrowded.","Higher wages may attract qualified drivers.","Nearby construction may reduce access to the open space.","The student loses leisure time and enjoyment."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher wages may attract qualified drivers."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0136","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher wage bills may lead to higher fares.","New skills may improve later employment opportunities.","It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment.","It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher wage bills may lead to higher fares."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0137","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A shortage of suitable farmland may raise the sacrifice required.","Operating at full capacity can leave little spare capacity for emergencies.","Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs.","Automation may reduce demand for some workers."],"answer":2,"why":"Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0138","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The choice may improve science-related skills.","Consistent production may reduce waste and improve reliability.","Wages alone may not solve inconvenient working hours.","Repair work may reopen trade routes and jobs."],"answer":2,"why":"Wages alone may not solve inconvenient working hours."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0139","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher wages may attract qualified drivers.","It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment.","The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit.","Packaging could become faster and less wasteful."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher wages may attract qualified drivers."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0140","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Pay encourages people to supply time and skills to employers.","The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment.","The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills.","Using the room for one activity prevents its simultaneous use for the other."],"answer":0,"why":"Pay encourages people to supply time and skills to employers."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0141","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes interest as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made.","Capital consists of man-made resources used to produce other goods and services.","An economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost of provision.","Interest is the reward earned for supplying capital or lending funds."],"answer":3,"why":"Interest as a factor reward: Interest is the reward earned for supplying capital or lending funds."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0142","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A saver receives interest after depositing money in a bank. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Interest as a factor reward","Land","Outward shift of a PPC","Opportunity cost of investment"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates interest as a factor reward: Interest is the reward earned for supplying capital or lending funds."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0143","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of interest as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday.","A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons.","A saver receives interest after depositing money in a bank.","An economy increases wheat production from 50 to 60 tonnes by reducing cloth output from 90 to 82 rolls."],"answer":2,"why":"A saver receives interest after depositing money in a bank. This illustrates interest is the reward earned for supplying capital or lending funds."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0144","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 54 tonnes of rice and 136 rolls of cloth to 89 tonnes of rice and 126 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["45 rolls of cloth","35 tonnes of rice","126 rolls of cloth","10 rolls of cloth"],"answer":3,"why":"Increasing rice output by 35 tonnes sacrifices 10 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0145","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The student loses leisure time and enjoyment.","Training and investment consume resources today.","High machinery costs may strain cash flow.","Households may build larger financial reserves."],"answer":3,"why":"Households may build larger financial reserves."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0146","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The student earns income for future purchases.","Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong.","Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.","Banks may face a higher cost of funding."],"answer":3,"why":"Banks may face a higher cost of funding."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0147","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Projects may take years before capacity rises.","Banks may attract funds they can use to finance lending.","Limited tax revenue may prevent all basic needs from being met.","Its benefits may be delayed if construction is costly or poorly managed."],"answer":1,"why":"Banks may attract funds they can use to finance lending."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0148","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher saving may reduce household consumption in the short run.","The economy could move closer to efficient production.","A safer home can improve living conditions.","Considering alternatives can reduce waste of scarce investment funds."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher saving may reduce household consumption in the short run."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0149","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Restored infrastructure could recover lost production.","The student earns income for future purchases.","Households may save time previously spent collecting water.","Households may build larger financial reserves."],"answer":3,"why":"Households may build larger financial reserves."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0150","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A saver receives interest after depositing money in a bank. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment.","Interest rewards the postponement of spending and can encourage saving.","Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods.","Ten extra tonnes of wheat require sacrificing eight rolls of cloth in this example."],"answer":1,"why":"Interest rewards the postponement of spending and can encourage saving."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0151","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes profit as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["For households, spending on one good prevents spending the same money on an alternative.","Profit is the residual reward to enterprise after business costs are deducted from revenue.","Land is the factor of production comprising natural resources used to produce goods and services.","An outward shift of a production possibility curve shows growth in maximum potential output."],"answer":1,"why":"Profit as a factor reward: Profit is the residual reward to enterprise after business costs are deducted from revenue."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0152","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Profit as a factor reward","Firm opportunity cost","For whom to produce","Outward shift of a PPC"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates profit as a factor reward: Profit is the residual reward to enterprise after business costs are deducted from revenue."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0153","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of profit as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs.","A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment.","A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers.","A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert."],"answer":0,"why":"A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs. This illustrates profit is the residual reward to enterprise after business costs are deducted from revenue."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0154","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to profit as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Positive profit rewards risk-taking and can finance expansion.","More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods.","Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other.","Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting."],"answer":0,"why":"Positive profit rewards risk-taking and can finance expansion."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0155","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reinvest a café's profit in expanding seating capacity. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Consumers may face fewer refrigerators.","Investment diverts funds from current consumption.","The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","The country gives up cloth output that could have been sold."],"answer":2,"why":"The café may serve more customers and increase revenue."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0156","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reinvest a café's profit in expanding seating capacity. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit.","It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment.","Training may increase the designer's future productivity.","Expansion can raise fixed costs before demand grows."],"answer":3,"why":"Expansion can raise fixed costs before demand grows."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0157","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 58 tonnes of rice and 100 rolls of cloth to 98 tonnes of rice and 88 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["88 rolls of cloth","52 rolls of cloth","12 rolls of cloth","40 tonnes of rice"],"answer":2,"why":"Increasing rice output by 40 tonnes sacrifices 12 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0158","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to reinvest a café's profit in expanding seating capacity less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Firms could produce more with available inputs.","Competition may limit the café's future profit.","Repair work may reopen trade routes and jobs.","Considering alternatives can reduce waste of scarce investment funds."],"answer":1,"why":"Competition may limit the café's future profit."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0159","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to reinvest a café's profit in expanding seating capacity. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","Repairs may prevent more expensive damage later.","The student earns income for future purchases.","It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment."],"answer":0,"why":"The café may serve more customers and increase revenue."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0160","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone.","Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone.","Positive profit rewards risk-taking and can finance expansion.","Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output."],"answer":2,"why":"Positive profit rewards risk-taking and can finance expansion."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0161","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision.","For a firm, using funds in one project means forgoing the best alternative investment.","Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made.","A point outside the current production possibility curve is unattainable with existing resources and technology."],"answer":2,"why":"Opportunity cost: Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0162","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Economic good","Point inside a PPC","Opportunity cost","Movement along a PPC"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates opportunity cost: Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0163","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks.","A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours.","On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it.","A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert."],"answer":3,"why":"A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert. This illustrates opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0164","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods.","Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output.","Workers use their skills and time to deliver services.","Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative."],"answer":3,"why":"Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0165","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to choose a paid shift instead of a leisure activity. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Training costs reduce funds available for current services.","Language classes may become overcrowded.","Some new enterprises will fail and waste invested resources.","The student earns income for future purchases."],"answer":3,"why":"The student earns income for future purchases."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0166","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to choose a paid shift instead of a leisure activity. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.","Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households.","The student loses leisure time and enjoyment.","New firms may introduce innovation and more choice."],"answer":2,"why":"The student loses leisure time and enjoyment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0167","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to choose a paid shift instead of a leisure activity?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The work may build experience useful in later jobs.","Its benefits may be delayed if construction is costly or poorly managed.","Higher saving may reduce household consumption in the short run.","A shortage of suitable farmland may raise the sacrifice required."],"answer":0,"why":"The work may build experience useful in later jobs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0168","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to choose a paid shift instead of a leisure activity less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["It could create employment in a growing industry.","It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness.","Fatigue may reduce performance in schoolwork.","A larger skilled workforce could expand healthcare capacity."],"answer":2,"why":"Fatigue may reduce performance in schoolwork."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0169","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 62 tonnes of rice and 103 rolls of cloth to 72 tonnes of rice and 89 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["89 rolls of cloth","24 rolls of cloth","14 rolls of cloth","10 tonnes of rice"],"answer":2,"why":"Increasing rice output by 10 tonnes sacrifices 14 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0170","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative.","Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other.","Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices.","More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods."],"answer":0,"why":"Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0171","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes household opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Wages are payments received for supplying labour in production.","This allocation question concerns who receives goods and services that are produced.","For households, spending on one good prevents spending the same money on an alternative.","An economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost of provision."],"answer":2,"why":"Household opportunity cost: For households, spending on one good prevents spending the same money on an alternative."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0172","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Scarcity in time allocation","Enterprise","Household opportunity cost","How to produce"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates household opportunity cost: For households, spending on one good prevents spending the same money on an alternative."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0173","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of household opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons.","A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff.","A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers.","A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday."],"answer":3,"why":"A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday. This illustrates for households, spending on one good prevents spending the same money on an alternative."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0174","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to household opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Better equipment can increase the productive capacity of workers.","The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds.","Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting.","The holiday is forgone when scarce savings are committed to repairs."],"answer":3,"why":"The holiday is forgone when scarce savings are committed to repairs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0175","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use household savings to repair a leaking roof. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Repairs may prevent more expensive damage later.","Fewer resources may remain for furniture manufacturing.","Consumers may face fewer refrigerators.","Infrastructure requires scarce funds and maintenance."],"answer":0,"why":"Repairs may prevent more expensive damage later."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0176","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use household savings to repair a leaking roof. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Restored infrastructure could recover lost production.","The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","Long-run productive capacity may rise.","The family must give up the planned holiday."],"answer":3,"why":"The family must give up the planned holiday."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0177","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to use household savings to repair a leaking roof?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A safer home can improve living conditions.","Projects may take years before capacity rises.","Higher saving may reduce household consumption in the short run.","Farm productivity may remain low without investment."],"answer":0,"why":"A safer home can improve living conditions."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0178","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to use household savings to repair a leaking roof less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Banks may attract funds they can use to finance lending.","Public access may support well-being and recreation.","It may protect ecosystems that support future production.","Unexpected repair costs may exhaust emergency savings."],"answer":3,"why":"Unexpected repair costs may exhaust emergency savings."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0179","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to use household savings to repair a leaking roof. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Repairs may prevent more expensive damage later.","Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households.","Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.","Public transport supply could increase."],"answer":0,"why":"Repairs may prevent more expensive damage later."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0180","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Ten extra tonnes of wheat require sacrificing eight rolls of cloth in this example.","At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other.","Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output.","The holiday is forgone when scarce savings are committed to repairs."],"answer":3,"why":"The holiday is forgone when scarce savings are committed to repairs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0181","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes worker opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["This allocation question concerns who receives goods and services that are produced.","For a worker, choosing one occupation involves giving up the next best job or use of time.","Land is the factor of production comprising natural resources used to produce goods and services.","The slope of a production possibility curve reflects the opportunity cost of producing more of one product."],"answer":1,"why":"Worker opportunity cost: For a worker, choosing one occupation involves giving up the next best job or use of time."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0182","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 66 tonnes of rice and 106 rolls of cloth to 81 tonnes of rice and 90 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["90 rolls of cloth","16 rolls of cloth","31 rolls of cloth","15 tonnes of rice"],"answer":1,"why":"Increasing rice output by 15 tonnes sacrifices 16 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0183","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of worker opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A farmer pays an annual fee to use a neighbouring field.","A qualified mechanic chooses a lower-paid apprenticeship in renewable energy.","A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water.","A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs."],"answer":1,"why":"A qualified mechanic chooses a lower-paid apprenticeship in renewable energy. This illustrates for a worker, choosing one occupation involves giving up the next best job or use of time."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0184","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to worker opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other.","Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods.","The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills.","Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices."],"answer":2,"why":"The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0185","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["New skills may improve later employment opportunities.","Consumers may face fewer refrigerators.","Rent payments increase farmers' production costs.","Restarting factories may require extra investment."],"answer":0,"why":"New skills may improve later employment opportunities."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0186","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["It could preserve the capacity to grow food.","Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong.","More pupils could access specialist science teaching.","Current income will be lower during training."],"answer":3,"why":"Current income will be lower during training."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0187","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Projects may take years before capacity rises.","Water pricing may exclude low-income households without support.","The worker may access a growing sector.","Subsidies may be needed to make the policy financially viable."],"answer":2,"why":"The worker may access a growing sector."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0188","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Farmers could expand agricultural output.","It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness.","The spending may reduce hardship where essential services are scarce.","Future wages are uncertain and may not justify the sacrifice."],"answer":3,"why":"Future wages are uncertain and may not justify the sacrifice."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0189","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["New firms may introduce innovation and more choice.","It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment.","New skills may improve later employment opportunities.","Restored infrastructure could recover lost production."],"answer":2,"why":"New skills may improve later employment opportunities."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0190","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A qualified mechanic chooses a lower-paid apprenticeship in renewable energy. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Natural resources provide inputs whose availability constrains output.","Workers use their skills and time to deliver services.","The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills.","Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output."],"answer":2,"why":"The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0191","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes firm opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["For a firm, using funds in one project means forgoing the best alternative investment.","This allocation question concerns which methods and combinations of inputs should be used.","Rent is the income earned by owners for supplying land or natural resources.","Labour is the human physical and mental effort used in production."],"answer":0,"why":"Firm opportunity cost: For a firm, using funds in one project means forgoing the best alternative investment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0192","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Capital","Firm opportunity cost","Interest as a factor reward","Land"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates firm opportunity cost: For a firm, using funds in one project means forgoing the best alternative investment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0193","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of firm opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff.","A farm uses fertile soil and a river to grow vegetables.","A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist.","A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom."],"answer":3,"why":"A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom. This illustrates for a firm, using funds in one project means forgoing the best alternative investment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0194","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 70 tonnes of rice and 109 rolls of cloth to 90 tonnes of rice and 91 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["38 rolls of cloth","91 rolls of cloth","18 rolls of cloth","20 tonnes of rice"],"answer":2,"why":"Increasing rice output by 20 tonnes sacrifices 18 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0195","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in a new packaging line. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Restarting factories may require extra investment.","Higher wage bills may lead to higher fares.","Packaging could become faster and less wasteful.","It could postpone leisure facilities valued by local residents."],"answer":2,"why":"Packaging could become faster and less wasteful."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0196","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in a new packaging line. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs.","The showroom refurbishment must be postponed.","Resources are not left unnecessarily idle.","It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes."],"answer":1,"why":"The showroom refurbishment must be postponed."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0197","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to invest in a new packaging line?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The business may reduce labour cost per item.","Factories may not be easily adapted to bus production.","Poor environmental management could reduce amenity quality.","Poor soil quality may limit the value of leasing the land."],"answer":0,"why":"The business may reduce labour cost per item."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0198","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to invest in a new packaging line less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The economy could move closer to efficient production.","Machine performance might be worse than expected.","It may protect ecosystems that support future production.","It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness."],"answer":1,"why":"Machine performance might be worse than expected."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0199","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to invest in a new packaging line. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs.","Packaging could become faster and less wasteful.","Households may save time previously spent collecting water.","It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes."],"answer":1,"why":"Packaging could become faster and less wasteful."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0200","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone.","The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment.","Lost productive resources reduce the economy's possible output.","Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output."],"answer":1,"why":"The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0201","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes government opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["An inward shift of a production possibility curve shows a fall in the maximum attainable output.","A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision.","The opportunity cost of an investment is the value of the next best alternative investment not selected.","Public spending on one project prevents use of the same funds for another."],"answer":3,"why":"Government opportunity cost: Public spending on one project prevents use of the same funds for another."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0202","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Government opportunity cost","Opportunity cost","PPC opportunity cost","Household opportunity cost"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates government opportunity cost: Public spending on one project prevents use of the same funds for another."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0203","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of government opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist.","A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment.","A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre.","A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons."],"answer":2,"why":"A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre. This illustrates public spending on one project prevents use of the same funds for another."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0204","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to government opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone.","Pay encourages people to supply time and skills to employers.","Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other.","Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices."],"answer":0,"why":"The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0205","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to fund flood protection ahead of leisure facilities. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses.","Some new enterprises will fail and waste invested resources.","Training and investment consume resources today.","Restarting factories may require extra investment."],"answer":0,"why":"Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0206","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to fund flood protection ahead of leisure facilities. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher wages may attract qualified drivers.","Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households.","Resources are not left unnecessarily idle.","Residents lose the benefits of the delayed sports centre."],"answer":3,"why":"Residents lose the benefits of the delayed sports centre."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0207","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 74 tonnes of rice and 112 rolls of cloth to 99 tonnes of rice and 92 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["92 rolls of cloth","25 tonnes of rice","20 rolls of cloth","45 rolls of cloth"],"answer":2,"why":"Increasing rice output by 25 tonnes sacrifices 20 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0208","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to fund flood protection ahead of leisure facilities less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs.","Protection may be inadequate if flood conditions worsen.","The spending may reduce hardship where essential services are scarce.","Extra capacity may create additional local jobs."],"answer":1,"why":"Protection may be inadequate if flood conditions worsen."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0209","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to fund flood protection ahead of leisure facilities. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong.","The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.","Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses."],"answer":3,"why":"Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0210","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices.","Interest rewards the postponement of spending and can encourage saving.","Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting.","The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone."],"answer":3,"why":"The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0211","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes opportunity cost of investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Human wants are numerous and expand as people seek more goods and services.","A point outside the current production possibility curve is unattainable with existing resources and technology.","The opportunity cost of an investment is the value of the next best alternative investment not selected.","Capital consists of man-made resources used to produce other goods and services."],"answer":2,"why":"Opportunity cost of investment: The opportunity cost of an investment is the value of the next best alternative investment not selected."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0212","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A delivery firm can afford new vehicles or new warehouse software, but not both. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Opportunity cost of investment","Inward shift of a PPC","Scarcity in time allocation","Profit as a factor reward"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates opportunity cost of investment: The opportunity cost of an investment is the value of the next best alternative investment not selected."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0213","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of opportunity cost of investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A delivery firm can afford new vehicles or new warehouse software, but not both.","A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers.","A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff.","As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips."],"answer":0,"why":"A delivery firm can afford new vehicles or new warehouse software, but not both. This illustrates the opportunity cost of an investment is the value of the next best alternative investment not selected."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0214","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to opportunity cost of investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone.","Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other.","Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices.","Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone."],"answer":3,"why":"Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0215","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to compare alternative investments carefully before buying vehicles. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Consumers may face fewer refrigerators.","The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit.","Some households may prefer other goods and services.","The developer may receive less revenue per home."],"answer":1,"why":"The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0216","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to compare alternative investments carefully before buying vehicles. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["It could preserve the capacity to grow food.","Households may save time previously spent collecting water.","Future production possibilities may increase.","Delaying vehicle purchases may cause missed deliveries."],"answer":3,"why":"Delaying vehicle purchases may cause missed deliveries."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0217","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to compare alternative investments carefully before buying vehicles?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Limited tax revenue may prevent all basic needs from being met.","Considering alternatives can reduce waste of scarce investment funds.","Demand for solar equipment may be too weak to justify the switch.","Its benefits may be delayed if construction is costly or poorly managed."],"answer":1,"why":"Considering alternatives can reduce waste of scarce investment funds."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0218","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to compare alternative investments carefully before buying vehicles less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["It may protect ecosystems that support future production.","Extra capacity may create additional local jobs.","Repair work may reopen trade routes and jobs.","Forecast benefits of each alternative may turn out to be inaccurate."],"answer":3,"why":"Forecast benefits of each alternative may turn out to be inaccurate."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0219","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 78 tonnes of rice and 115 rolls of cloth to 108 tonnes of rice and 111 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["4 rolls of cloth","111 rolls of cloth","34 rolls of cloth","30 tonnes of rice"],"answer":0,"why":"Increasing rice output by 30 tonnes sacrifices 4 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0220","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A delivery firm can afford new vehicles or new warehouse software, but not both. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices.","Workers use their skills and time to deliver services.","Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone.","Relative factor costs, productivity and technology determine production techniques."],"answer":2,"why":"Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0221","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes scarcity in time allocation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Human wants are numerous and expand as people seek more goods and services.","Public spending on one project prevents use of the same funds for another.","This allocation question concerns which methods and combinations of inputs should be used.","Time is a scarce resource, and its allocation involves opportunity cost."],"answer":3,"why":"Scarcity in time allocation: Time is a scarce resource, and its allocation involves opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0222","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A self-employed designer can accept either a new client project or an advanced training course this week. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["How to produce","Scarcity in time allocation","Scarcity","Outward shift of a PPC"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates scarcity in time allocation: Time is a scarce resource, and its allocation involves opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0223","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of scarcity in time allocation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["An economy increases wheat production from 50 to 60 tonnes by reducing cloth output from 90 to 82 rolls.","A self-employed designer can accept either a new client project or an advanced training course this week.","A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons.","An economy operates on its boundary while producing medicines and clothing."],"answer":1,"why":"A self-employed designer can accept either a new client project or an advanced training course this week. This illustrates time is a scarce resource, and its allocation involves opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0224","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to scarcity in time allocation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Relative factor costs, productivity and technology determine production techniques.","Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods.","Time spent on one activity cannot be used for the next best option.","Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone."],"answer":2,"why":"Time spent on one activity cannot be used for the next best option."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0225","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to take the training course rather than the new client project. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Nearby construction may reduce access to the open space.","Training may increase the designer's future productivity.","Some households may prefer other goods and services.","Machinery purchases require significant finance."],"answer":1,"why":"Training may increase the designer's future productivity."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0226","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to take the training course rather than the new client project. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The immediate fee from the client project is forgone.","It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes.","The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households."],"answer":0,"why":"The immediate fee from the client project is forgone."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0227","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to take the training course rather than the new client project?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Poor environmental management could reduce amenity quality.","Automation may reduce demand for some workers.","Wages alone may not solve inconvenient working hours.","The designer could offer higher-value services later."],"answer":3,"why":"The designer could offer higher-value services later."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0228","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to take the training course rather than the new client project less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Banks may attract funds they can use to finance lending.","Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs.","Higher productive capacity could support economic growth.","The course may not improve marketable skills."],"answer":3,"why":"The course may not improve marketable skills."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0229","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to take the training course rather than the new client project. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Households may build larger financial reserves.","Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households.","More pupils could access specialist science teaching.","Training may increase the designer's future productivity."],"answer":3,"why":"Training may increase the designer's future productivity."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0230","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A self-employed designer can accept either a new client project or an advanced training course this week. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other.","Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output.","Time spent on one activity cannot be used for the next best option.","More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods."],"answer":2,"why":"Time spent on one activity cannot be used for the next best option."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0231","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes point on a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Rent is the income earned by owners for supplying land or natural resources.","An economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost of provision.","A point on a production possibility curve represents an attainable combination using resources efficiently at a given time.","For a worker, choosing one occupation involves giving up the next best job or use of time."],"answer":2,"why":"Point on a PPC: A point on a production possibility curve represents an attainable combination using resources efficiently at a given time."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0232","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 82 tonnes of rice and 118 rolls of cloth to 117 tonnes of rice and 112 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["6 rolls of cloth","112 rolls of cloth","35 tonnes of rice","41 rolls of cloth"],"answer":0,"why":"Increasing rice output by 35 tonnes sacrifices 6 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0233","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of point on a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment.","A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers.","An economy operates on its boundary while producing medicines and clothing.","A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom."],"answer":2,"why":"An economy operates on its boundary while producing medicines and clothing. This illustrates a point on a production possibility curve represents an attainable combination using resources efficiently at a given time."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0234","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to point on a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The holiday is forgone when scarce savings are committed to repairs.","The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone.","At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other.","Payment provides an incentive to make land available for production."],"answer":2,"why":"At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0235","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to keep existing resources fully employed in current production. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Current income will be lower during training.","Resources are not left unnecessarily idle.","Language classes may become overcrowded.","Banks may face a higher cost of funding."],"answer":1,"why":"Resources are not left unnecessarily idle."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0236","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to keep existing resources fully employed in current production. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["New firms may introduce innovation and more choice.","The output mix may not match society's priorities.","The owner could earn rent from previously idle land.","Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses."],"answer":1,"why":"The output mix may not match society's priorities."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0237","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to keep existing resources fully employed in current production?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Limited tax revenue may prevent all basic needs from being met.","Advice alone may not solve a shortage of finance.","Subsidies may be needed to make the policy financially viable.","Maximum feasible output can be obtained with current technology."],"answer":3,"why":"Maximum feasible output can be obtained with current technology."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0238","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to keep existing resources fully employed in current production less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Operating at full capacity can leave little spare capacity for emergencies.","Public access may support well-being and recreation.","It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness.","Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Operating at full capacity can leave little spare capacity for emergencies."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0239","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to keep existing resources fully employed in current production. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs.","Households may build larger financial reserves.","New firms may introduce innovation and more choice.","Resources are not left unnecessarily idle."],"answer":3,"why":"Resources are not left unnecessarily idle."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0240","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: An economy operates on its boundary while producing medicines and clothing. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Better equipment can increase the productive capacity of workers.","At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other.","The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds.","Natural resources provide inputs whose availability constrains output."],"answer":1,"why":"At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0241","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes point inside a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["This allocation question concerns who receives goods and services that are produced.","A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used.","Resource allocation is the decision about how scarce inputs are distributed among alternative uses.","An outward shift of a production possibility curve shows growth in maximum potential output."],"answer":1,"why":"Point inside a PPC: A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0242","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A region has idle workshops and unemployed workers despite adequate raw materials. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Enterprise","Economic good","Point inside a PPC","Scarcity in time allocation"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates point inside a ppc: A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0243","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of point inside a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre.","A town has funds for either a water plant or a new stadium, but not both.","A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours.","A region has idle workshops and unemployed workers despite adequate raw materials."],"answer":3,"why":"A region has idle workshops and unemployed workers despite adequate raw materials. This illustrates a point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0244","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 86 tonnes of rice and 121 rolls of cloth to 126 tonnes of rice and 113 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["48 rolls of cloth","40 tonnes of rice","113 rolls of cloth","8 rolls of cloth"],"answer":3,"why":"Increasing rice output by 40 tonnes sacrifices 8 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0245","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reactivate unused productive capacity. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["It could postpone leisure facilities valued by local residents.","Output and employment could rise together.","Some households may prefer other goods and services.","The student loses leisure time and enjoyment."],"answer":1,"why":"Output and employment could rise together."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0246","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reactivate unused productive capacity. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.","Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price.","Restarting factories may require extra investment.","The owner could earn rent from previously idle land."],"answer":2,"why":"Restarting factories may require extra investment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0247","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to reactivate unused productive capacity?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The economy could move closer to efficient production.","Advice alone may not solve a shortage of finance.","Poor soil quality may limit the value of leasing the land.","Limited tax revenue may prevent all basic needs from being met."],"answer":0,"why":"The economy could move closer to efficient production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0248","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to reactivate unused productive capacity less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Unused capacity may lack the skills demanded by buyers.","It may protect ecosystems that support future production.","The business may reduce labour cost per item.","Improved reliability can lower the rate of faulty products."],"answer":0,"why":"Unused capacity may lack the skills demanded by buyers."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0249","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to reactivate unused productive capacity. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Training may increase the designer's future productivity.","Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.","Output and employment could rise together.","It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment."],"answer":2,"why":"Output and employment could rise together."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0250","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A region has idle workshops and unemployed workers despite adequate raw materials. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting.","Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other.","Relative factor costs, productivity and technology determine production techniques.","Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative."],"answer":1,"why":"Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0251","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes point beyond a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The opportunity cost of an investment is the value of the next best alternative investment not selected.","A point on a production possibility curve represents an attainable combination using resources efficiently at a given time.","A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision.","A point outside the current production possibility curve is unattainable with existing resources and technology."],"answer":3,"why":"Point beyond a PPC: A point outside the current production possibility curve is unattainable with existing resources and technology."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0252","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Point beyond a PPC","Point inside a PPC","Opportunity cost of investment","Scarcity"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates point beyond a ppc: A point outside the current production possibility curve is unattainable with existing resources and technology."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0253","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of point beyond a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist.","A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert.","A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom.","A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water."],"answer":0,"why":"A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist. This illustrates a point outside the current production possibility curve is unattainable with existing resources and technology."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0254","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to point beyond a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change.","Positive profit rewards risk-taking and can finance expansion.","Time spent on one activity cannot be used for the next best option.","Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output."],"answer":0,"why":"Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0255","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand productive capacity through training and investment. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Residents lose the benefits of the delayed sports centre.","Rent payments increase farmers' production costs.","Future production possibilities may increase.","The student loses leisure time and enjoyment."],"answer":2,"why":"Future production possibilities may increase."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0256","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand productive capacity through training and investment. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Packaging could become faster and less wasteful.","Training and investment consume resources today.","The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment."],"answer":1,"why":"Training and investment consume resources today."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0257","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 90 tonnes of rice and 124 rolls of cloth to 100 tonnes of rice and 114 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["10 rolls of cloth","114 rolls of cloth","20 rolls of cloth","10 tonnes of rice"],"answer":0,"why":"Increasing rice output by 10 tonnes sacrifices 10 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0258","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand productive capacity through training and investment less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A larger skilled workforce could expand healthcare capacity.","Projects may take years before capacity rises.","It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness.","Considering alternatives can reduce waste of scarce investment funds."],"answer":1,"why":"Projects may take years before capacity rises."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0259","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand productive capacity through training and investment. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Future production possibilities may increase.","It could improve public health by providing safe water to more households.","Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price.","Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses."],"answer":0,"why":"Future production possibilities may increase."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0260","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone.","Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change.","The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds.","Interest rewards the postponement of spending and can encourage saving."],"answer":1,"why":"Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0261","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes movement along a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Enterprise involves organising other factors of production and taking business risks.","Wages are payments received for supplying labour in production.","The slope of a production possibility curve reflects the opportunity cost of producing more of one product.","Moving along a production possibility curve involves reallocating resources between two goods."],"answer":3,"why":"Movement along a PPC: Moving along a production possibility curve involves reallocating resources between two goods."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0262","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country uses more of its factories for buses and fewer for refrigerators. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["What to produce","Movement along a PPC","PPC opportunity cost","Interest as a factor reward"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates movement along a ppc: Moving along a production possibility curve involves reallocating resources between two goods."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0263","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of movement along a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre.","A region considers producing more solar panels or more furniture with its existing factories.","A country uses more of its factories for buses and fewer for refrigerators.","On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it."],"answer":2,"why":"A country uses more of its factories for buses and fewer for refrigerators. This illustrates moving along a production possibility curve involves reallocating resources between two goods."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0264","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to movement along a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices.","Positive profit rewards risk-taking and can finance expansion.","Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other.","The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment."],"answer":2,"why":"Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0265","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to shift some factories from refrigerators to buses. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Public transport supply could increase.","Residents lose the benefits of the delayed sports centre.","Some new enterprises will fail and waste invested resources.","The showroom refurbishment must be postponed."],"answer":0,"why":"Public transport supply could increase."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0266","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to shift some factories from refrigerators to buses. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price.","It could improve public health by providing safe water to more households.","The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit.","Consumers may face fewer refrigerators."],"answer":3,"why":"Consumers may face fewer refrigerators."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0267","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to shift some factories from refrigerators to buses?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Better transport could support workers' mobility.","Subsidies may be needed to make the policy financially viable.","Future wages are uncertain and may not justify the sacrifice.","Competition may limit the café's future profit."],"answer":0,"why":"Better transport could support workers' mobility."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0268","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to shift some factories from refrigerators to buses less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Factories may not be easily adapted to bus production.","Reduced disruption may help economic activity.","Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs.","Public access may support well-being and recreation."],"answer":0,"why":"Factories may not be easily adapted to bus production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0269","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 50 tonnes of rice and 127 rolls of cloth to 65 tonnes of rice and 115 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["115 rolls of cloth","15 tonnes of rice","27 rolls of cloth","12 rolls of cloth"],"answer":3,"why":"Increasing rice output by 15 tonnes sacrifices 12 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0270","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country uses more of its factories for buses and fewer for refrigerators. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other.","The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment.","Pay encourages people to supply time and skills to employers.","Natural resources provide inputs whose availability constrains output."],"answer":0,"why":"Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0271","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes outward shift of a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision.","An outward shift of a production possibility curve shows growth in maximum potential output.","A point on a production possibility curve represents an attainable combination using resources efficiently at a given time.","A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used."],"answer":1,"why":"Outward shift of a PPC: An outward shift of a production possibility curve shows growth in maximum potential output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0272","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Scarcity in time allocation","Firm opportunity cost","Point on a PPC","Outward shift of a PPC"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates outward shift of a ppc: An outward shift of a production possibility curve shows growth in maximum potential output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0273","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of outward shift of a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A housing developer must decide whether to build high-price apartments or low-cost homes.","A qualified mechanic chooses a lower-paid apprenticeship in renewable energy.","A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment.","On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it."],"answer":2,"why":"A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment. This illustrates an outward shift of a production possibility curve shows growth in maximum potential output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0274","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to outward shift of a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative.","More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods.","Workers use their skills and time to deliver services.","Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting."],"answer":1,"why":"More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0275","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to finance training and productivity-improving equipment. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Long-run productive capacity may rise.","Some new enterprises will fail and waste invested resources.","It can restrict housing supply near growing cities.","The student loses leisure time and enjoyment."],"answer":0,"why":"Long-run productive capacity may rise."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0276","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to finance training and productivity-improving equipment. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Households may save time previously spent collecting water.","It could preserve the capacity to grow food.","The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","Investment diverts funds from current consumption."],"answer":3,"why":"Investment diverts funds from current consumption."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0277","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to finance training and productivity-improving equipment?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Unexpected repair costs may exhaust emergency savings.","Poor environmental management could reduce amenity quality.","Advice alone may not solve a shortage of finance.","Firms could produce more with available inputs."],"answer":3,"why":"Firms could produce more with available inputs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0278","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to finance training and productivity-improving equipment less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Benefits depend on the quality of training and equipment.","Farmers could expand agricultural output.","It may protect ecosystems that support future production.","Banks may attract funds they can use to finance lending."],"answer":0,"why":"Benefits depend on the quality of training and equipment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0279","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to finance training and productivity-improving equipment. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Training may increase the designer's future productivity.","Long-run productive capacity may rise.","New firms may introduce innovation and more choice.","Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price."],"answer":1,"why":"Long-run productive capacity may rise."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0280","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone.","More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods.","The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds.","Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting."],"answer":1,"why":"More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0281","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes inward shift of a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["For a worker, choosing one occupation involves giving up the next best job or use of time.","Time is a scarce resource, and its allocation involves opportunity cost.","An inward shift of a production possibility curve shows a fall in the maximum attainable output.","A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision."],"answer":2,"why":"Inward shift of a PPC: An inward shift of a production possibility curve shows a fall in the maximum attainable output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0282","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 54 tonnes of rice and 130 rolls of cloth to 74 tonnes of rice and 116 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["34 rolls of cloth","14 rolls of cloth","20 tonnes of rice","116 rolls of cloth"],"answer":1,"why":"Increasing rice output by 20 tonnes sacrifices 14 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0283","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of inward shift of a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs.","As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips.","Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks.","A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment."],"answer":2,"why":"Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks. This illustrates an inward shift of a production possibility curve shows a fall in the maximum attainable output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0284","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to inward shift of a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output.","The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone.","Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output.","Lost productive resources reduce the economy's possible output."],"answer":3,"why":"Lost productive resources reduce the economy's possible output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0285","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Rent payments increase farmers' production costs.","Nearby construction may reduce access to the open space.","It could postpone leisure facilities valued by local residents.","Restored infrastructure could recover lost production."],"answer":3,"why":"Restored infrastructure could recover lost production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0286","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher wages may attract qualified drivers.","Reconstruction uses scarce funds that could serve other needs.","Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses.","The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit."],"answer":1,"why":"Reconstruction uses scarce funds that could serve other needs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0287","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Trained workers may move to higher-paying regions.","Poor soil quality may limit the value of leasing the land.","Competition may limit the café's future profit.","Repair work may reopen trade routes and jobs."],"answer":3,"why":"Repair work may reopen trade routes and jobs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0288","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs.","Further floods could damage repaired infrastructure.","It may protect ecosystems that support future production.","Consistent production may reduce waste and improve reliability."],"answer":1,"why":"Further floods could damage repaired infrastructure."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0289","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Restored infrastructure could recover lost production.","Households may save time previously spent collecting water.","Households may build larger financial reserves.","It could preserve the capacity to grow food."],"answer":0,"why":"Restored infrastructure could recover lost production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0290","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output.","Lost productive resources reduce the economy's possible output.","Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output.","Relative factor costs, productivity and technology determine production techniques."],"answer":1,"why":"Lost productive resources reduce the economy's possible output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0291","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes ppc opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Enterprise involves organising other factors of production and taking business risks.","The slope of a production possibility curve reflects the opportunity cost of producing more of one product.","Scarcity occurs when limited resources cannot satisfy all human wants.","A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision."],"answer":1,"why":"PPC opportunity cost: The slope of a production possibility curve reflects the opportunity cost of producing more of one product."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0292","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: An economy increases wheat production from 50 to 60 tonnes by reducing cloth output from 90 to 82 rolls. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Rent as a factor reward","Profit as a factor reward","PPC opportunity cost","Scarcity"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates ppc opportunity cost: The slope of a production possibility curve reflects the opportunity cost of producing more of one product."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0293","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of ppc opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips.","A farm uses fertile soil and a river to grow vegetables.","An economy increases wheat production from 50 to 60 tonnes by reducing cloth output from 90 to 82 rolls.","A region considers producing more solar panels or more furniture with its existing factories."],"answer":2,"why":"An economy increases wheat production from 50 to 60 tonnes by reducing cloth output from 90 to 82 rolls. This illustrates the slope of a production possibility curve reflects the opportunity cost of producing more of one product."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0294","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 58 tonnes of rice and 133 rolls of cloth to 83 tonnes of rice and 117 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["25 tonnes of rice","16 rolls of cloth","117 rolls of cloth","41 rolls of cloth"],"answer":1,"why":"Increasing rice output by 25 tonnes sacrifices 16 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0295","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reallocate productive resources towards wheat. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["High machinery costs may strain cash flow.","The family must give up the planned holiday.","Fewer resources may remain for furniture manufacturing.","Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong."],"answer":3,"why":"Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0296","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reallocate productive resources towards wheat. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit.","Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price.","The country gives up cloth output that could have been sold.","It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes."],"answer":2,"why":"The country gives up cloth output that could have been sold."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0297","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to reallocate productive resources towards wheat?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher wheat output may reduce dependence on food imports.","Expected gains depend on the demand for each subject.","Equipment may become obsolete before costs are recovered.","Subsidies may be needed to make the policy financially viable."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher wheat output may reduce dependence on food imports."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0298","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to reallocate productive resources towards wheat less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A shortage of suitable farmland may raise the sacrifice required.","The business may reduce labour cost per item.","It could create employment in a growing industry.","Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs."],"answer":0,"why":"A shortage of suitable farmland may raise the sacrifice required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0299","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to reallocate productive resources towards wheat. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes.","Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong.","Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.","Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price."],"answer":1,"why":"Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0300","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: An economy increases wheat production from 50 to 60 tonnes by reducing cloth output from 90 to 82 rolls. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills.","Ten extra tonnes of wheat require sacrificing eight rolls of cloth in this example.","Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone.","Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output."],"answer":1,"why":"Ten extra tonnes of wheat require sacrificing eight rolls of cloth in this example."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0001","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A town has funds for either a water plant or a new stadium, but not both. Decision-makers are considering whether to prioritise essential water infrastructure. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by scarcity. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Scarcity occurs when limited resources cannot satisfy all human wants.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A town has funds for either a water plant or a new stadium, but not both.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0002","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips. Decision-makers are considering whether to allocate a larger budget to basic needs rather than luxury projects. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by unlimited wants. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Human wants are numerous and expand as people seek more goods and services.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0003","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A region considers producing more solar panels or more furniture with its existing factories. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase production of renewable-energy equipment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by what to produce. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — This allocation question concerns which goods and services an economy should make.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A region considers producing more solar panels or more furniture with its existing factories.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0004","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce automated production at a bakery. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by how to produce. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — This allocation question concerns which methods and combinations of inputs should be used.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0005","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A housing developer must decide whether to build high-price apartments or low-cost homes. Decision-makers are considering whether to reserve some new homes for lower-income households. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by for whom to produce. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — This allocation question concerns who receives goods and services that are produced.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A housing developer must decide whether to build high-price apartments or low-cost homes.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0006","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it. Decision-makers are considering whether to protect public access to naturally available coastal wind and open space. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by free good. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0007","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons. Decision-makers are considering whether to give priority to science classes in the spare room. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by allocation choices. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Resource allocation is the decision about how scarce inputs are distributed among alternative uses.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0008","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A farm uses fertile soil and a river to grow vegetables. Decision-makers are considering whether to protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by land. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Land is the factor of production comprising natural resources used to produce goods and services.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A farm uses fertile soil and a river to grow vegetables.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0009","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff. Decision-makers are considering whether to fund training for healthcare workers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by labour. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Labour is the human physical and mental effort used in production.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0010","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in more efficient factory machinery. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by capital. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Capital consists of man-made resources used to produce other goods and services.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0011","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A farmer pays an annual fee to use a neighbouring field. Decision-makers are considering whether to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by rent as a factor reward. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Rent is the income earned by owners for supplying land or natural resources.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A farmer pays an annual fee to use a neighbouring field.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0012","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by wages as a factor reward. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Wages are payments received for supplying labour in production.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0013","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A saver receives interest after depositing money in a bank. Decision-makers are considering whether to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by interest as a factor reward. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Interest is the reward earned for supplying capital or lending funds.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A saver receives interest after depositing money in a bank.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0014","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs. Decision-makers are considering whether to reinvest a café's profit in expanding seating capacity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by profit as a factor reward. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Profit is the residual reward to enterprise after business costs are deducted from revenue.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0015","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert. Decision-makers are considering whether to choose a paid shift instead of a leisure activity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by opportunity cost. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0016","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A qualified mechanic chooses a lower-paid apprenticeship in renewable energy. Decision-makers are considering whether to take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by worker opportunity cost. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — For a worker, choosing one occupation involves giving up the next best job or use of time.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A qualified mechanic chooses a lower-paid apprenticeship in renewable energy.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0017","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in a new packaging line. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by firm opportunity cost. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — For a firm, using funds in one project means forgoing the best alternative investment.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0018","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre. Decision-makers are considering whether to fund flood protection ahead of leisure facilities. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by government opportunity cost. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Public spending on one project prevents use of the same funds for another.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0019","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A delivery firm can afford new vehicles or new warehouse software, but not both. Decision-makers are considering whether to compare alternative investments carefully before buying vehicles. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by opportunity cost of investment. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — The opportunity cost of an investment is the value of the next best alternative investment not selected.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A delivery firm can afford new vehicles or new warehouse software, but not both.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0020","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A self-employed designer can accept either a new client project or an advanced training course this week. Decision-makers are considering whether to take the training course rather than the new client project. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by scarcity in time allocation. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Time is a scarce resource, and its allocation involves opportunity cost.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A self-employed designer can accept either a new client project or an advanced training course this week.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0021","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A region has idle workshops and unemployed workers despite adequate raw materials. Decision-makers are considering whether to reactivate unused productive capacity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by point inside a ppc. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A region has idle workshops and unemployed workers despite adequate raw materials.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0022","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand productive capacity through training and investment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by point beyond a ppc. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A point outside the current production possibility curve is unattainable with existing resources and technology.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0023","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A country uses more of its factories for buses and fewer for refrigerators. Decision-makers are considering whether to shift some factories from refrigerators to buses. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by movement along a ppc. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Moving along a production possibility curve involves reallocating resources between two goods.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A country uses more of its factories for buses and fewer for refrigerators.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0024","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment. Decision-makers are considering whether to finance training and productivity-improving equipment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by outward shift of a ppc. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — An outward shift of a production possibility curve shows growth in maximum potential output.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0025","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks. Decision-makers are considering whether to rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by inward shift of a ppc. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — An inward shift of a production possibility curve shows a fall in the maximum attainable output.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0001","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A town has funds for either a water plant or a new stadium, but not both. Decision-makers are considering whether to prioritise essential water infrastructure. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to prioritise essential water infrastructure. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: It could improve public health by providing safe water to more households. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0002","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips. Decision-makers are considering whether to allocate a larger budget to basic needs rather than luxury projects. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to allocate a larger budget to basic needs rather than luxury projects might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: The spending may reduce hardship where essential services are scarce. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0003","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A region considers producing more solar panels or more furniture with its existing factories. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase production of renewable-energy equipment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to increase production of renewable-energy equipment. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: It could create employment in a growing industry. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0004","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce automated production at a bakery. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers introduce automated production at a bakery. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Consistent production may reduce waste and improve reliability. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0005","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A housing developer must decide whether to build high-price apartments or low-cost homes. Decision-makers are considering whether to reserve some new homes for lower-income households. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to reserve some new homes for lower-income households could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: It could reduce overcrowding and associated social costs. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0006","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest public funds in a reliable water-supply network. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to invest public funds in a reliable water-supply network. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Households may save time previously spent collecting water. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Businesses may gain a dependable input for production. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0007","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it. Decision-makers are considering whether to protect public access to naturally available coastal wind and open space. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to protect public access to naturally available coastal wind and open space might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Public access may support well-being and recreation. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0008","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons. Decision-makers are considering whether to give priority to science classes in the spare room. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to give priority to science classes in the spare room. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: More pupils could access specialist science teaching. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: The choice may improve science-related skills. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0009","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A farm uses fertile soil and a river to grow vegetables. Decision-makers are considering whether to protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: It could preserve the capacity to grow food. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: It may protect ecosystems that support future production. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0010","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff. Decision-makers are considering whether to fund training for healthcare workers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to fund training for healthcare workers could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Higher skills could improve the quality of treatment. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: A larger skilled workforce could expand healthcare capacity. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0011","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in more efficient factory machinery. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to invest in more efficient factory machinery. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Improved reliability can lower the rate of faulty products. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0012","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"An entrepreneur combines a premises, staff and equipment to open a food-processing firm. Decision-makers are considering whether to support new entrepreneurs with business advice. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to support new entrepreneurs with business advice might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: New firms may introduce innovation and more choice. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Successful start-ups can create jobs and raise output. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0013","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A farmer pays an annual fee to use a neighbouring field. Decision-makers are considering whether to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: The owner could earn rent from previously idle land. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Farmers could expand agricultural output. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0014","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Higher wages may attract qualified drivers. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0015","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A saver receives interest after depositing money in a bank. Decision-makers are considering whether to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Households may build larger financial reserves. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Banks may attract funds they can use to finance lending. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0016","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs. Decision-makers are considering whether to reinvest a café's profit in expanding seating capacity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to reinvest a café's profit in expanding seating capacity. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: The café may serve more customers and increase revenue. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Extra capacity may create additional local jobs. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0017","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert. Decision-makers are considering whether to choose a paid shift instead of a leisure activity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to choose a paid shift instead of a leisure activity might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: The student earns income for future purchases. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: The work may build experience useful in later jobs. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0018","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday. Decision-makers are considering whether to use household savings to repair a leaking roof. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to use household savings to repair a leaking roof. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Repairs may prevent more expensive damage later. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: A safer home can improve living conditions. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0019","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A qualified mechanic chooses a lower-paid apprenticeship in renewable energy. Decision-makers are considering whether to take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: New skills may improve later employment opportunities. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: The worker may access a growing sector. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0020","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in a new packaging line. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to invest in a new packaging line could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Packaging could become faster and less wasteful. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: The business may reduce labour cost per item. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0021","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre. Decision-makers are considering whether to fund flood protection ahead of leisure facilities. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to fund flood protection ahead of leisure facilities. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Reduced disruption may help economic activity. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0022","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A delivery firm can afford new vehicles or new warehouse software, but not both. Decision-makers are considering whether to compare alternative investments carefully before buying vehicles. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to compare alternative investments carefully before buying vehicles might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Considering alternatives can reduce waste of scarce investment funds. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0023","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A self-employed designer can accept either a new client project or an advanced training course this week. Decision-makers are considering whether to take the training course rather than the new client project. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to take the training course rather than the new client project. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Training may increase the designer's future productivity. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: The designer could offer higher-value services later. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0024","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"An economy operates on its boundary while producing medicines and clothing. Decision-makers are considering whether to keep existing resources fully employed in current production. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers keep existing resources fully employed in current production. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Resources are not left unnecessarily idle. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Maximum feasible output can be obtained with current technology. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0025","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A region has idle workshops and unemployed workers despite adequate raw materials. Decision-makers are considering whether to reactivate unused productive capacity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to reactivate unused productive capacity could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Output and employment could rise together. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: The economy could move closer to efficient production. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0026","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand productive capacity through training and investment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to expand productive capacity through training and investment. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Future production possibilities may increase. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Higher productive capacity could support economic growth. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0027","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country uses more of its factories for buses and fewer for refrigerators. Decision-makers are considering whether to shift some factories from refrigerators to buses. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to shift some factories from refrigerators to buses might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Public transport supply could increase. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Better transport could support workers' mobility. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0028","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment. Decision-makers are considering whether to finance training and productivity-improving equipment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to finance training and productivity-improving equipment. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Long-run productive capacity may rise. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Firms could produce more with available inputs. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0029","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks. Decision-makers are considering whether to rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Restored infrastructure could recover lost production. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Repair work may reopen trade routes and jobs. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0030","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"An economy increases wheat production from 50 to 60 tonnes by reducing cloth output from 90 to 82 rolls. Decision-makers are considering whether to reallocate productive resources towards wheat. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to reallocate productive resources towards wheat could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Higher wheat output may reduce dependence on food imports. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0001","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A town has funds for either a water plant or a new stadium, but not both. Decision-makers are considering whether to prioritise essential water infrastructure. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to prioritise essential water infrastructure. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: It could improve public health by providing safe water to more households. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0002","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips. Decision-makers are considering whether to allocate a larger budget to basic needs rather than luxury projects. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to allocate a larger budget to basic needs rather than luxury projects may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: The spending may reduce hardship where essential services are scarce. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0003","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A region considers producing more solar panels or more furniture with its existing factories. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase production of renewable-energy equipment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to increase production of renewable-energy equipment could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: It could create employment in a growing industry. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0004","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce automated production at a bakery. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to introduce automated production at a bakery in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Consistent production may reduce waste and improve reliability. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Relative factor costs, productivity and technology determine production techniques. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0005","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A housing developer must decide whether to build high-price apartments or low-cost homes. Decision-makers are considering whether to reserve some new homes for lower-income households. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to reserve some new homes for lower-income households could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: It could reduce overcrowding and associated social costs. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0006","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest public funds in a reliable water-supply network. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to invest public funds in a reliable water-supply network. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Households may save time previously spent collecting water. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Businesses may gain a dependable input for production. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0007","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it. Decision-makers are considering whether to protect public access to naturally available coastal wind and open space. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to protect public access to naturally available coastal wind and open space may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Public access may support well-being and recreation. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0008","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons. Decision-makers are considering whether to give priority to science classes in the spare room. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to give priority to science classes in the spare room could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: More pupils could access specialist science teaching. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: The choice may improve science-related skills. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Using the room for one activity prevents its simultaneous use for the other. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0009","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A farm uses fertile soil and a river to grow vegetables. Decision-makers are considering whether to protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: It could preserve the capacity to grow food. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: It may protect ecosystems that support future production. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Natural resources provide inputs whose availability constrains output. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0010","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff. Decision-makers are considering whether to fund training for healthcare workers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to fund training for healthcare workers could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Higher skills could improve the quality of treatment. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: A larger skilled workforce could expand healthcare capacity. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Workers use their skills and time to deliver services. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0011","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in more efficient factory machinery. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to invest in more efficient factory machinery. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Improved reliability can lower the rate of faulty products. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Better equipment can increase the productive capacity of workers. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0012","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"An entrepreneur combines a premises, staff and equipment to open a food-processing firm. Decision-makers are considering whether to support new entrepreneurs with business advice. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to support new entrepreneurs with business advice may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: New firms may introduce innovation and more choice. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Successful start-ups can create jobs and raise output. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0013","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A farmer pays an annual fee to use a neighbouring field. Decision-makers are considering whether to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: The owner could earn rent from previously idle land. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Farmers could expand agricultural output. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Payment provides an incentive to make land available for production. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0014","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Higher wages may attract qualified drivers. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Pay encourages people to supply time and skills to employers. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0015","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A saver receives interest after depositing money in a bank. Decision-makers are considering whether to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Households may build larger financial reserves. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Banks may attract funds they can use to finance lending. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Interest rewards the postponement of spending and can encourage saving. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0016","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs. Decision-makers are considering whether to reinvest a café's profit in expanding seating capacity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to reinvest a café's profit in expanding seating capacity. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: The café may serve more customers and increase revenue. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Extra capacity may create additional local jobs. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Positive profit rewards risk-taking and can finance expansion. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0017","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert. Decision-makers are considering whether to choose a paid shift instead of a leisure activity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to choose a paid shift instead of a leisure activity may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: The student earns income for future purchases. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: The work may build experience useful in later jobs. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0018","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday. Decision-makers are considering whether to use household savings to repair a leaking roof. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to use household savings to repair a leaking roof could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Repairs may prevent more expensive damage later. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: A safer home can improve living conditions. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The holiday is forgone when scarce savings are committed to repairs. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0019","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A qualified mechanic chooses a lower-paid apprenticeship in renewable energy. Decision-makers are considering whether to take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: New skills may improve later employment opportunities. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: The worker may access a growing sector. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0020","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in a new packaging line. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to invest in a new packaging line could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Packaging could become faster and less wasteful. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: The business may reduce labour cost per item. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0021","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre. Decision-makers are considering whether to fund flood protection ahead of leisure facilities. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to fund flood protection ahead of leisure facilities. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Reduced disruption may help economic activity. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0022","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A delivery firm can afford new vehicles or new warehouse software, but not both. Decision-makers are considering whether to compare alternative investments carefully before buying vehicles. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to compare alternative investments carefully before buying vehicles may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Considering alternatives can reduce waste of scarce investment funds. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0023","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A self-employed designer can accept either a new client project or an advanced training course this week. Decision-makers are considering whether to take the training course rather than the new client project. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to take the training course rather than the new client project could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Training may increase the designer's future productivity. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: The designer could offer higher-value services later. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Time spent on one activity cannot be used for the next best option. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0024","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"An economy operates on its boundary while producing medicines and clothing. Decision-makers are considering whether to keep existing resources fully employed in current production. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to keep existing resources fully employed in current production in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Resources are not left unnecessarily idle. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Maximum feasible output can be obtained with current technology. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0025","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A region has idle workshops and unemployed workers despite adequate raw materials. Decision-makers are considering whether to reactivate unused productive capacity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to reactivate unused productive capacity could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Output and employment could rise together. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: The economy could move closer to efficient production. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0026","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand productive capacity through training and investment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to expand productive capacity through training and investment. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Future production possibilities may increase. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Higher productive capacity could support economic growth. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0027","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country uses more of its factories for buses and fewer for refrigerators. Decision-makers are considering whether to shift some factories from refrigerators to buses. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to shift some factories from refrigerators to buses may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Public transport supply could increase. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Better transport could support workers' mobility. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0028","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment. Decision-makers are considering whether to finance training and productivity-improving equipment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to finance training and productivity-improving equipment could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Long-run productive capacity may rise. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Firms could produce more with available inputs. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0029","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks. Decision-makers are considering whether to rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Restored infrastructure could recover lost production. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Repair work may reopen trade routes and jobs. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Lost productive resources reduce the economy's possible output. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0030","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"An economy increases wheat production from 50 to 60 tonnes by reducing cloth output from 90 to 82 rolls. Decision-makers are considering whether to reallocate productive resources towards wheat. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to reallocate productive resources towards wheat could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Higher wheat output may reduce dependence on food imports. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Ten extra tonnes of wheat require sacrificing eight rolls of cloth in this example. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0001","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A town has funds for either a water plant or a new stadium, but not both. Decision-makers are considering whether to prioritise essential water infrastructure. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to prioritise essential water infrastructure would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): It could improve public health by providing safe water to more households.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): It could postpone leisure facilities valued by local residents.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Its benefits may be delayed if construction is costly or poorly managed.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0002","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips. Decision-makers are considering whether to allocate a larger budget to basic needs rather than luxury projects. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to allocate a larger budget to basic needs rather than luxury projects in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): The spending may reduce hardship where essential services are scarce.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Some households may prefer other goods and services.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Limited tax revenue may prevent all basic needs from being met.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0003","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A region considers producing more solar panels or more furniture with its existing factories. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase production of renewable-energy equipment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to increase production of renewable-energy equipment. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): It could create employment in a growing industry.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Fewer resources may remain for furniture manufacturing.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Demand for solar equipment may be too weak to justify the switch.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0004","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce automated production at a bakery. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to introduce automated production at a bakery is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Consistent production may reduce waste and improve reliability.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): High machinery costs may strain cash flow.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Automation may reduce demand for some workers.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0005","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A housing developer must decide whether to build high-price apartments or low-cost homes. Decision-makers are considering whether to reserve some new homes for lower-income households. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to reserve some new homes for lower-income households would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): It could reduce overcrowding and associated social costs.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): The developer may receive less revenue per home.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Subsidies may be needed to make the policy financially viable.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0006","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest public funds in a reliable water-supply network. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to invest public funds in a reliable water-supply network would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Households may save time previously spent collecting water.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Businesses may gain a dependable input for production.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Infrastructure requires scarce funds and maintenance.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Water pricing may exclude low-income households without support.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0007","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it. Decision-makers are considering whether to protect public access to naturally available coastal wind and open space. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to protect public access to naturally available coastal wind and open space would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Public access may support well-being and recreation.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Nearby construction may reduce access to the open space.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Poor environmental management could reduce amenity quality.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0008","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons. Decision-makers are considering whether to give priority to science classes in the spare room. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to give priority to science classes in the spare room in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): More pupils could access specialist science teaching.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): The choice may improve science-related skills.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Language classes may become overcrowded.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Expected gains depend on the demand for each subject.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0009","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A farm uses fertile soil and a river to grow vegetables. Decision-makers are considering whether to protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): It could preserve the capacity to grow food.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): It may protect ecosystems that support future production.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): It can restrict housing supply near growing cities.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Farm productivity may remain low without investment.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0010","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff. Decision-makers are considering whether to fund training for healthcare workers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to fund training for healthcare workers is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Higher skills could improve the quality of treatment.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): A larger skilled workforce could expand healthcare capacity.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Training costs reduce funds available for current services.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Trained workers may move to higher-paying regions.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0011","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in more efficient factory machinery. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to invest in more efficient factory machinery would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Improved reliability can lower the rate of faulty products.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Machinery purchases require significant finance.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Equipment may become obsolete before costs are recovered.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0012","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"An entrepreneur combines a premises, staff and equipment to open a food-processing firm. Decision-makers are considering whether to support new entrepreneurs with business advice. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to support new entrepreneurs with business advice would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): New firms may introduce innovation and more choice.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Successful start-ups can create jobs and raise output.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Some new enterprises will fail and waste invested resources.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Advice alone may not solve a shortage of finance.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0013","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A farmer pays an annual fee to use a neighbouring field. Decision-makers are considering whether to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): The owner could earn rent from previously idle land.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Farmers could expand agricultural output.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Rent payments increase farmers' production costs.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Poor soil quality may limit the value of leasing the land.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0014","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Higher wages may attract qualified drivers.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Higher wage bills may lead to higher fares.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Wages alone may not solve inconvenient working hours.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0015","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A saver receives interest after depositing money in a bank. Decision-makers are considering whether to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Households may build larger financial reserves.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Banks may attract funds they can use to finance lending.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Banks may face a higher cost of funding.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Higher saving may reduce household consumption in the short run.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0016","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs. Decision-makers are considering whether to reinvest a café's profit in expanding seating capacity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to reinvest a café's profit in expanding seating capacity is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Extra capacity may create additional local jobs.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Expansion can raise fixed costs before demand grows.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Competition may limit the café's future profit.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0017","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert. Decision-makers are considering whether to choose a paid shift instead of a leisure activity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to choose a paid shift instead of a leisure activity would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): The student earns income for future purchases.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): The work may build experience useful in later jobs.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): The student loses leisure time and enjoyment.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Fatigue may reduce performance in schoolwork.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0018","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday. Decision-makers are considering whether to use household savings to repair a leaking roof. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to use household savings to repair a leaking roof would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Repairs may prevent more expensive damage later.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): A safer home can improve living conditions.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): The family must give up the planned holiday.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Unexpected repair costs may exhaust emergency savings.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0019","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A qualified mechanic chooses a lower-paid apprenticeship in renewable energy. Decision-makers are considering whether to take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): New skills may improve later employment opportunities.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): The worker may access a growing sector.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Current income will be lower during training.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Future wages are uncertain and may not justify the sacrifice.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0020","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in a new packaging line. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to invest in a new packaging line in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Packaging could become faster and less wasteful.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): The business may reduce labour cost per item.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): The showroom refurbishment must be postponed.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Machine performance might be worse than expected.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0021","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre. Decision-makers are considering whether to fund flood protection ahead of leisure facilities. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to fund flood protection ahead of leisure facilities. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Reduced disruption may help economic activity.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Residents lose the benefits of the delayed sports centre.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Protection may be inadequate if flood conditions worsen.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0022","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A delivery firm can afford new vehicles or new warehouse software, but not both. Decision-makers are considering whether to compare alternative investments carefully before buying vehicles. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to compare alternative investments carefully before buying vehicles is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Considering alternatives can reduce waste of scarce investment funds.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Delaying vehicle purchases may cause missed deliveries.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Forecast benefits of each alternative may turn out to be inaccurate.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0023","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A self-employed designer can accept either a new client project or an advanced training course this week. Decision-makers are considering whether to take the training course rather than the new client project. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to take the training course rather than the new client project would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Training may increase the designer's future productivity.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): The designer could offer higher-value services later.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): The immediate fee from the client project is forgone.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): The course may not improve marketable skills.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0024","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"An economy operates on its boundary while producing medicines and clothing. Decision-makers are considering whether to keep existing resources fully employed in current production. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to keep existing resources fully employed in current production would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Resources are not left unnecessarily idle.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Maximum feasible output can be obtained with current technology.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): The output mix may not match society's priorities.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Operating at full capacity can leave little spare capacity for emergencies.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0025","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A region has idle workshops and unemployed workers despite adequate raw materials. Decision-makers are considering whether to reactivate unused productive capacity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to reactivate unused productive capacity would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Output and employment could rise together.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): The economy could move closer to efficient production.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Restarting factories may require extra investment.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Unused capacity may lack the skills demanded by buyers.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0026","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand productive capacity through training and investment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to expand productive capacity through training and investment in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Future production possibilities may increase.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Higher productive capacity could support economic growth.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Training and investment consume resources today.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Projects may take years before capacity rises.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0027","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country uses more of its factories for buses and fewer for refrigerators. Decision-makers are considering whether to shift some factories from refrigerators to buses. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to shift some factories from refrigerators to buses. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Public transport supply could increase.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Better transport could support workers' mobility.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Consumers may face fewer refrigerators.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Factories may not be easily adapted to bus production.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0028","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment. Decision-makers are considering whether to finance training and productivity-improving equipment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to finance training and productivity-improving equipment is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Long-run productive capacity may rise.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Firms could produce more with available inputs.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Investment diverts funds from current consumption.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Benefits depend on the quality of training and equipment.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0029","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks. Decision-makers are considering whether to rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Restored infrastructure could recover lost production.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Repair work may reopen trade routes and jobs.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Reconstruction uses scarce funds that could serve other needs.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Further floods could damage repaired infrastructure.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0030","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"An economy increases wheat production from 50 to 60 tonnes by reducing cloth output from 90 to 82 rolls. Decision-makers are considering whether to reallocate productive resources towards wheat. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to reallocate productive resources towards wheat would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Higher wheat output may reduce dependence on food imports.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): The country gives up cloth output that could have been sold.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): A shortage of suitable farmland may raise the sacrifice required.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0001","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes market?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Price elasticity of supply measures how strongly quantity supplied responds to a percentage change in price.","Markets may allocate goods according to income rather than need and fail to account for external costs.","A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services.","An increase in demand tends to raise equilibrium price and quantity if supply is unchanged."],"answer":2,"why":"Market: A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0002","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Independent fruit growers meet customers at a weekly neighbourhood market. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Sellers in a market","Inelastic demand","Market","Market equilibrium"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates market: A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0003","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of market?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises.","A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price.","At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280.","Independent fruit growers meet customers at a weekly neighbourhood market."],"answer":3,"why":"Independent fruit growers meet customers at a weekly neighbourhood market. This illustrates a market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0004","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to market?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The magnitude of PED is 2, indicating elastic demand.","Profit and consumer choices influence the goods firms produce.","The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information.","Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain."],"answer":2,"why":"The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0005","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A producer initially supplies 210 units and observes a 10% price rise followed by a 30% increase in quantity supplied. What is price elasticity of supply (PES)?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["0.333","20","3","4"],"answer":2,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (30%) / percentage change in price (10%) = 3."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0006","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to create a digital trading platform for local growers. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More people may purchase bicycles.","The firm may expand output when prices rise.","River pollution may fall.","Some growers may not have reliable internet access."],"answer":3,"why":"Some growers may not have reliable internet access."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0007","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to create a digital trading platform for local growers?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Buyers could compare products and prices more easily.","Operating and maintenance costs may be high.","Production may not expand quickly enough to remove the shortage.","Public and private responsibilities may be poorly coordinated."],"answer":0,"why":"Buyers could compare products and prices more easily."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0008","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to create a digital trading platform for local growers less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The service can be provided even when individual users cannot be charged.","Platform fees might increase sellers' costs.","Tax revenue could fund waste management.","Poor housing conditions may become less widespread."],"answer":1,"why":"Platform fees might increase sellers' costs."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0009","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to create a digital trading platform for local growers. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The firm may expand output when prices rise.","Higher prices may discourage plastic use.","Growers could reach more potential customers.","Managers may estimate how sales volume will change."],"answer":2,"why":"Growers could reach more potential customers."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0010","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Independent fruit growers meet customers at a weekly neighbourhood market. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods.","Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain.","Profit and consumer choices influence the goods firms produce.","The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information."],"answer":3,"why":"The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0011","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes buyers in a market?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A change in the product's own price causes movement along its supply curve.","A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services.","Buyers signal demand through their willingness and ability to purchase products.","A non-price determinant changes the whole demand schedule for a good."],"answer":2,"why":"Buyers in a market: Buyers signal demand through their willingness and ability to purchase products."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0012","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A bakery notices customers buying more wholegrain bread than white bread. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Shortage","Negative external costs","Price elasticity of supply","Buyers in a market"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates buyers in a market: Buyers signal demand through their willingness and ability to purchase products."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0013","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A firm sells 110 units at $5 each. What is its total sales revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$110","$115","$555","$550"],"answer":3,"why":"Total revenue = price × quantity = $5 × 110 = $550."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0014","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to buyers in a market?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price.","Excess supply can put downward pressure on price and encourage stock clearance.","Changes in purchases signal which goods consumers value at prevailing prices.","A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve."],"answer":2,"why":"Changes in purchases signal which goods consumers value at prevailing prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0015","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use customer demand information to plan bread output. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Past responsiveness may not hold after competitors change prices.","Cycle routes use public funds and road space.","Tax finance has an opportunity cost for other services.","The bakery may reduce unsold stock."],"answer":3,"why":"The bakery may reduce unsold stock."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0016","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use customer demand information to plan bread output. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Demand could change before new production is ready.","More customers may buy tickets.","Fares may fall and ridership may rise.","River pollution may fall."],"answer":0,"why":"Demand could change before new production is ready."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0017","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to use customer demand information to plan bread output?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Output may better match what customers want.","It does not immediately increase the harvested quantity.","Prices might fall before the extra crop reaches buyers.","PED estimates can be unreliable with limited sales data."],"answer":0,"why":"Output may better match what customers want."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0018","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to use customer demand information to plan bread output less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Unsold inventory and storage costs may fall.","Tax revenue could fund waste management.","Past purchasing patterns may not predict future preferences.","Price decisions may better reflect consumer sensitivity."],"answer":2,"why":"Past purchasing patterns may not predict future preferences."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0019","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to use customer demand information to plan bread output. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices.","Farm revenue may increase if extra output sells.","Lower costs may support lower bread prices.","The bakery may reduce unsold stock."],"answer":3,"why":"The bakery may reduce unsold stock."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0020","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A bakery notices customers buying more wholegrain bread than white bread. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus.","Changes in purchases signal which goods consumers value at prevailing prices.","The combination aims to retain market incentives while addressing some market failures.","The tax raises producers' costs and tends to raise consumer prices while reducing sales."],"answer":1,"why":"Changes in purchases signal which goods consumers value at prevailing prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0021","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A market schedule gives these data (price, quantity demanded, quantity supplied): ($6, 99, 59); ($8, 89, 69); ($10, 79, 79); ($12, 69, 89). What is the equilibrium price?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$8","$10","$6","$12"],"answer":1,"why":"At $10, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 79 units."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0022","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Several farmers offer tomatoes at a wholesale market each morning. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Demand","Mixed economic system","Price elasticity of demand","Sellers in a market"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates sellers in a market: Sellers supply products and respond to prices, costs and expected profit."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0023","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of sellers in a market?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A government pays bus operators for each low-emission journey provided.","A popular sports event increases demand for nearby hotel rooms.","Several farmers offer tomatoes at a wholesale market each morning.","The price of fresh strawberries rises rapidly but the harvest cannot increase for several months."],"answer":2,"why":"Several farmers offer tomatoes at a wholesale market each morning. This illustrates sellers supply products and respond to prices, costs and expected profit."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0024","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to sellers in a market?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged.","Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision.","When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus.","Sellers' responses to prices help allocate resources across products."],"answer":3,"why":"Sellers' responses to prices help allocate resources across products."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0025","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher fares impose a greater burden on regular passengers.","Investment could be wasted if demand growth slows.","Competition may increase the quantity offered.","Revenue per pair of shoes will decline."],"answer":2,"why":"Competition may increase the quantity offered."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0026","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Lower costs may support lower bread prices.","Managers may estimate how sales volume will change.","Farmers may face lower prices and profits.","Higher prices may discourage plastic use."],"answer":2,"why":"Farmers may face lower prices and profits."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0027","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Poor access or misinformation may still reduce take-up.","Buyers may benefit from greater choice.","Some buyers lack information about alternative products.","Demand may become more elastic over a longer time period."],"answer":1,"why":"Buyers may benefit from greater choice."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0028","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Entry may remain difficult if transport costs are high.","Reduced car use can lower congestion and pollution.","The service can be provided even when individual users cannot be charged.","Less unsold food may be wasted."],"answer":0,"why":"Entry may remain difficult if transport costs are high."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0029","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Competition may improve retail efficiency.","Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers.","Competition may increase the quantity offered.","More people may purchase bicycles."],"answer":2,"why":"Competition may increase the quantity offered."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0030","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A retailer selling initially 160 units raises its price by 5% and quantity demanded decreases by 30%. What is the magnitude of price elasticity of demand (PED)?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["7","6","25","0.167"],"answer":1,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (30%) / percentage change in price (5%) = 6."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0031","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A shortage occurs when quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at a particular price.","A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services.","An indirect tax is a tax on spending on goods and services.","Demand is the quantity buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices during a period."],"answer":3,"why":"Demand: Demand is the quantity buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices during a period."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0032","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A shop records how many bicycles customers would buy at each possible selling price. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Demand","Mixed economic system","Increase in demand and price","Price elasticity of supply"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates demand: Demand is the quantity buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices during a period."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0033","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market.","A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity.","A shop records how many bicycles customers would buy at each possible selling price.","A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price."],"answer":2,"why":"A shop records how many bicycles customers would buy at each possible selling price. This illustrates demand is the quantity buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices during a period."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0034","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market.","Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged.","Lower costs can shift supply right and make the service cheaper or more available.","Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power."],"answer":3,"why":"Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0035","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to survey local demand before ordering bicycles. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Ordering decisions may match expected sales.","Low-income consumers exert less influence because purchasing power differs.","Treatment raises operating costs for the factory.","Tax finance has an opportunity cost for other services."],"answer":0,"why":"Ordering decisions may match expected sales."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0036","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to survey local demand before ordering bicycles. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Survey answers may differ from actual purchases.","Lower costs may support lower bread prices.","Lower prices could attract more buyers.","More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation."],"answer":0,"why":"Survey answers may differ from actual purchases."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0037","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to survey local demand before ordering bicycles?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The retailer may avoid spending on unwanted stock.","Platform fees might increase sellers' costs.","Weak enforcement may make regulation ineffective.","Some buyers lack information about alternative products."],"answer":0,"why":"The retailer may avoid spending on unwanted stock."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0038","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A producer initially supplies 250 units and observes a 10% price rise followed by a 20% increase in quantity supplied. What is price elasticity of supply (PES)?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["3","10","0.5","2"],"answer":3,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (20%) / percentage change in price (10%) = 2."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0039","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to survey local demand before ordering bicycles. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Orders may be met without a long delay.","Ordering decisions may match expected sales.","The firm may expand output when prices rise.","More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation."],"answer":1,"why":"Ordering decisions may match expected sales."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0040","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A shop records how many bicycles customers would buy at each possible selling price. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption.","Changes in purchases signal which goods consumers value at prevailing prices.","Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision.","Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power."],"answer":3,"why":"Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0041","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes movement along demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services.","Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed.","A market economic system relies mainly on private ownership and price signals to allocate resources.","A change in a good's own price causes movement along its demand curve, other factors unchanged."],"answer":3,"why":"Movement along demand: A change in a good's own price causes movement along its demand curve, other factors unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0042","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Cinema ticket sales rise when the cinema lowers its ticket price. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Movement along demand","Negative external costs","Determinants of PES","Public goods"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates movement along demand: A change in a good's own price causes movement along its demand curve, other factors unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0043","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of movement along demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["At $20 a pair, a shop offers 180 shoes but customers buy only 110.","Cinema ticket sales rise when the cinema lowers its ticket price.","A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market.","A paint factory discharges waste into a river used by downstream fishers."],"answer":1,"why":"Cinema ticket sales rise when the cinema lowers its ticket price. This illustrates a change in a good's own price causes movement along its demand curve, other factors unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0044","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to movement along demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Profit and consumer choices influence the goods firms produce.","A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve.","The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction.","Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market."],"answer":1,"why":"A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0045","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reduce cinema ticket prices on quiet weekdays. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Extra harvesting can raise marginal production costs.","More customers may buy tickets.","The subsidy has a cost to taxpayers.","Cold storage has high energy and capital costs."],"answer":1,"why":"More customers may buy tickets."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0046","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A firm sells 170 units at $9 each. What is its total sales revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$179","$1,539","$170","$1,530"],"answer":3,"why":"Total revenue = price × quantity = $9 × 170 = $1,530."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0047","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to reduce cinema ticket prices on quiet weekdays?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Weak enforcement may make regulation ineffective.","Higher attendance could increase sales of refreshments.","PED estimates can be unreliable with limited sales data.","Savings depend on electricity use and machine reliability."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher attendance could increase sales of refreshments."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0048","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to reduce cinema ticket prices on quiet weekdays less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Poor housing conditions may become less widespread.","The increase in ticket sales may be small if demand is inelastic.","Higher productive efficiency may increase output.","Customers may be less likely to encounter shortages."],"answer":1,"why":"The increase in ticket sales may be small if demand is inelastic."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0049","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to reduce cinema ticket prices on quiet weekdays. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More customers may buy tickets.","Competition may increase the quantity offered.","Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers.","Growers could reach more potential customers."],"answer":0,"why":"More customers may buy tickets."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0050","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Cinema ticket sales rise when the cinema lowers its ticket price. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve.","Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged.","The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction.","Sellers' responses to prices help allocate resources across products."],"answer":0,"why":"A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0051","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes shift in demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A non-price determinant changes the whole demand schedule for a good.","Demand is the quantity buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices during a period.","Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed.","Consumer sovereignty means buying decisions influence what firms offer for sale."],"answer":0,"why":"Shift in demand: A non-price determinant changes the whole demand schedule for a good."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0052","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Demand for electric bicycles rises after more cycling lanes are built. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Surplus","Negative external costs","Shift in demand","Inelastic supply"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates shift in demand: A non-price determinant changes the whole demand schedule for a good."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0053","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of shift in demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Demand for electric bicycles rises after more cycling lanes are built.","A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price.","A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises.","A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%."],"answer":0,"why":"Demand for electric bicycles rises after more cycling lanes are built. This illustrates a non-price determinant changes the whole demand schedule for a good."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0054","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to shift in demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption.","Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market.","Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price.","Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged."],"answer":2,"why":"Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0055","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A market schedule gives these data (price, quantity demanded, quantity supplied): ($10, 111, 71); ($12, 101, 81); ($14, 91, 91); ($16, 81, 101). What is the equilibrium price?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$10","$14","$12","$16"],"answer":1,"why":"At $14, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 91 units."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0056","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to build safer cycle routes to encourage bicycle use. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Cycle routes use public funds and road space.","Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year.","River pollution may fall.","More people may receive protection from disease."],"answer":0,"why":"Cycle routes use public funds and road space."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0057","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to build safer cycle routes to encourage bicycle use?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The equilibrium estimate may use inaccurate data.","Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods.","It does not immediately increase the harvested quantity.","Reduced car travel may lower local pollution."],"answer":3,"why":"Reduced car travel may lower local pollution."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0058","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to build safer cycle routes to encourage bicycle use less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Customers may be less likely to encounter shortages.","Greater output may make fruit more accessible.","Buyers may benefit from greater choice.","Weather or safety concerns may still limit cycling."],"answer":3,"why":"Weather or safety concerns may still limit cycling."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0059","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to build safer cycle routes to encourage bicycle use. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More people may purchase bicycles.","River pollution may fall.","More people may receive protection from disease.","Lower costs may support lower bread prices."],"answer":0,"why":"More people may purchase bicycles."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0060","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Demand for electric bicycles rises after more cycling lanes are built. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain.","The combination aims to retain market incentives while addressing some market failures.","Consumers can switch more easily when close substitutes are available.","Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price."],"answer":3,"why":"Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0061","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Demand is the quantity buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices during a period.","Non-price changes in production conditions shift the supply curve.","A mixed economic system combines market allocation with government intervention and public provision.","Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to sell at different prices over a period."],"answer":3,"why":"Supply: Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to sell at different prices over a period."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0062","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A tomato producer lists quantities offered for sale at a series of prices. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Supply","Increase in demand and price","Demand","Inelastic supply"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates supply: Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to sell at different prices over a period."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0063","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A retailer selling initially 200 units raises its price by 5% and quantity demanded decreases by 25%. What is the magnitude of price elasticity of demand (PED)?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["6","20","0.2","5"],"answer":3,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (25%) / percentage change in price (5%) = 5."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0064","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The magnitude of PED is 2, indicating elastic demand.","Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price.","Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal.","Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0065","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to plan output using a detailed supply schedule. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The farm may identify profitable production levels.","Some growers may not have reliable internet access.","The subsidy has a cost to taxpayers.","Survey answers may differ from actual purchases."],"answer":0,"why":"The farm may identify profitable production levels."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0066","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to plan output using a detailed supply schedule. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher prices may encourage additional supply.","Orders may be met without a long delay.","Weather may make planned supply impossible.","More people may receive protection from disease."],"answer":2,"why":"Weather may make planned supply impossible."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0067","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to plan output using a detailed supply schedule?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The discount might still fail to clear unpopular designs.","Unexpected income changes could alter demand.","Production can be aligned more closely with expected market prices.","Poor access or misinformation may still reduce take-up."],"answer":2,"why":"Production can be aligned more closely with expected market prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0068","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to plan output using a detailed supply schedule less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Fishing livelihoods and health may improve.","Extra competition may limit price increases.","Input prices may change after plans are made.","Less unsold food may be wasted."],"answer":2,"why":"Input prices may change after plans are made."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0069","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to plan output using a detailed supply schedule. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More people may purchase bicycles.","Orders may be met without a long delay.","The farm may identify profitable production levels.","Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year."],"answer":2,"why":"The farm may identify profitable production levels."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0070","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A tomato producer lists quantities offered for sale at a series of prices. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve.","When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus.","Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price.","Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0071","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A producer initially supplies 290 units and observes a 10% price rise followed by a 60% increase in quantity supplied. What is price elasticity of supply (PES)?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["6","7","50","0.167"],"answer":0,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (60%) / percentage change in price (10%) = 6."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0072","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Shift in supply","Market economy limitations","Movement along supply","Subsidy"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates movement along supply: A change in the product's own price causes movement along its supply curve."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0073","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of movement along supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises.","A bakery notices customers buying more wholegrain bread than white bread.","At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280.","Demand for electric bicycles rises after more cycling lanes are built."],"answer":0,"why":"A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises. This illustrates a change in the product's own price causes movement along its supply curve."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0074","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to movement along supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Profit and consumer choices influence the goods firms produce.","A higher selling price can justify output from more expensive production methods.","The PES value is 2, indicating supply is price elastic.","Consumers can switch more easily when close substitutes are available."],"answer":1,"why":"A higher selling price can justify output from more expensive production methods."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0075","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Farm revenue may increase if extra output sells.","Low-income consumers exert less influence because purchasing power differs.","State services require sufficient tax revenue.","Spare capacity is costly to maintain."],"answer":0,"why":"Farm revenue may increase if extra output sells."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0076","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The firm may expand output when prices rise.","Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase.","Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered.","Extra harvesting can raise marginal production costs."],"answer":3,"why":"Extra harvesting can raise marginal production costs."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0077","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More seasonal jobs may be offered.","It does not immediately increase the harvested quantity.","Past purchasing patterns may not predict future preferences.","Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods."],"answer":0,"why":"More seasonal jobs may be offered."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0078","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Prices might fall before the extra crop reaches buyers.","Some consumers may reduce demand, easing the shortage.","Price decisions may better reflect consumer sensitivity.","Buyers could compare products and prices more easily."],"answer":0,"why":"Prices might fall before the extra crop reaches buyers."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0079","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Farm revenue may increase if extra output sells.","More people may purchase bicycles.","Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered.","All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation."],"answer":0,"why":"Farm revenue may increase if extra output sells."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0080","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A firm sells 125 units at $4 each. What is its total sales revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$125","$129","$504","$500"],"answer":3,"why":"Total revenue = price × quantity = $4 × 125 = $500."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0081","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes shift in supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed.","Non-price changes in production conditions shift the supply curve.","Consumer sovereignty means buying decisions influence what firms offer for sale.","An increase in supply tends to reduce equilibrium price and raise quantity sold if demand is unchanged."],"answer":1,"why":"Shift in supply: Non-price changes in production conditions shift the supply curve."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0082","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Increase in demand and price","Market economic system","Mixed economic system","Shift in supply"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates shift in supply: Non-price changes in production conditions shift the supply curve."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0083","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of shift in supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase.","A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price.","A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others.","A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%."],"answer":1,"why":"A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price. This illustrates non-price changes in production conditions shift the supply curve."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0084","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to shift in supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information.","Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic.","The PES value is 2, indicating supply is price elastic.","Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged."],"answer":3,"why":"Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0085","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce energy-saving ovens in bakeries. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Low-income consumers exert less influence because purchasing power differs.","Visitors may face higher accommodation costs.","Tax finance has an opportunity cost for other services.","Lower costs may support lower bread prices."],"answer":3,"why":"Lower costs may support lower bread prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0086","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce energy-saving ovens in bakeries. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation.","New ovens require investment.","Consumers may pay less for apples.","Competition may improve retail efficiency."],"answer":1,"why":"New ovens require investment."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0087","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to introduce energy-saving ovens in bakeries?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher productive efficiency may increase output.","Production may not expand quickly enough to remove the shortage.","Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods.","Platform fees might increase sellers' costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher productive efficiency may increase output."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0088","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A market schedule gives these data (price, quantity demanded, quantity supplied): ($14, 123, 83); ($16, 113, 93); ($18, 103, 103); ($20, 93, 113). What is the equilibrium price?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$18","$14","$20","$16"],"answer":0,"why":"At $18, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 103 units."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0089","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to introduce energy-saving ovens in bakeries. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices.","Lower costs may support lower bread prices.","Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year.","Managers may estimate how sales volume will change."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower costs may support lower bread prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0090","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Sellers' responses to prices help allocate resources across products.","Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic.","Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged.","The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information."],"answer":2,"why":"Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0091","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes market equilibrium?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Public goods are non-excludable and non-rival in consumption.","Sellers supply products and respond to prices, costs and expected profit.","Negative external costs are harmful effects of production or consumption imposed on third parties.","Equilibrium is the price and quantity at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied."],"answer":3,"why":"Market equilibrium: Equilibrium is the price and quantity at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0092","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Inelastic demand","Increase in supply and price","Market equilibrium","Price elasticity of supply"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates market equilibrium: Equilibrium is the price and quantity at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0093","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of market equilibrium?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200.","A popular sports event increases demand for nearby hotel rooms.","A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others.","Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding."],"answer":0,"why":"At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200. This illustrates equilibrium is the price and quantity at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0094","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to market equilibrium?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information.","When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus.","Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision.","The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction."],"answer":1,"why":"When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0095","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to adjust umbrella orders towards the equilibrium quantity. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Unsold stock could be reduced.","Some consumers may be unable to afford essential services.","Idle machines still require maintenance and space.","Demand could change before new production is ready."],"answer":0,"why":"Unsold stock could be reduced."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0096","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A retailer selling initially 240 units raises its price by 5% and quantity demanded decreases by 20%. What is the magnitude of price elasticity of demand (PED)?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["15","4","0.25","5"],"answer":1,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (20%) / percentage change in price (5%) = 4."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0097","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to adjust umbrella orders towards the equilibrium quantity?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Specialist inputs may still limit rapid expansion.","Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods.","Customers may be less likely to encounter shortages.","Public and private responsibilities may be poorly coordinated."],"answer":2,"why":"Customers may be less likely to encounter shortages."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0098","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to adjust umbrella orders towards the equilibrium quantity less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The equilibrium estimate may use inaccurate data.","Reduced car use can lower congestion and pollution.","The firm may gain sales during sudden demand increases.","The retailer may avoid spending on unwanted stock."],"answer":0,"why":"The equilibrium estimate may use inaccurate data."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0099","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to adjust umbrella orders towards the equilibrium quantity. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers.","Competition may improve retail efficiency.","Unsold stock could be reduced.","Managers may estimate how sales volume will change."],"answer":2,"why":"Unsold stock could be reduced."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0100","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal.","Lower costs can shift supply right and make the service cheaper or more available.","When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus.","Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods."],"answer":2,"why":"When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0101","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes shortage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A subsidy is government financial assistance that lowers production costs or consumer prices.","Negative external costs are harmful effects of production or consumption imposed on third parties.","An increase in demand tends to raise equilibrium price and quantity if supply is unchanged.","A shortage occurs when quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at a particular price."],"answer":3,"why":"Shortage: A shortage occurs when quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at a particular price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0102","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Mixed economic system","Sellers in a market","Inelastic demand","Shortage"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates shortage: A shortage occurs when quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at a particular price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0103","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of shortage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A tomato producer lists quantities offered for sale at a series of prices.","Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase.","At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280.","A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises."],"answer":2,"why":"At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280. This illustrates a shortage occurs when quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at a particular price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0104","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to shortage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods.","Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market.","The combination aims to retain market incentives while addressing some market failures.","Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power."],"answer":1,"why":"Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0105","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A producer initially supplies 330 units and observes a 10% price rise followed by a 50% increase in quantity supplied. What is price elasticity of supply (PES)?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["40","0.2","6","5"],"answer":3,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (50%) / percentage change in price (10%) = 5."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0106","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to permit prices to respond to a temporary bread shortage. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Low-income households may find bread less affordable.","Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered.","Growers could reach more potential customers.","Orders may be met without a long delay."],"answer":0,"why":"Low-income households may find bread less affordable."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0107","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to permit prices to respond to a temporary bread shortage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Some buyers lack information about alternative products.","Some consumers may reduce demand, easing the shortage.","Weak enforcement may make regulation ineffective.","Poor oversight may reward inefficient operators."],"answer":1,"why":"Some consumers may reduce demand, easing the shortage."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0108","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to permit prices to respond to a temporary bread shortage less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Better forecasts may guide pricing and revenue decisions.","The service can be provided even when individual users cannot be charged.","More seasonal jobs may be offered.","Production may not expand quickly enough to remove the shortage."],"answer":3,"why":"Production may not expand quickly enough to remove the shortage."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0109","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to permit prices to respond to a temporary bread shortage. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Growers could reach more potential customers.","Higher prices may encourage additional supply.","Competition may increase the quantity offered.","Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher prices may encourage additional supply."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0110","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain.","Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price.","The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction.","Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market."],"answer":3,"why":"Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0111","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes surplus?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed.","A mixed economic system combines market allocation with government intervention and public provision.","A surplus occurs when quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded at a particular price.","Buyers signal demand through their willingness and ability to purchase products."],"answer":2,"why":"Surplus: A surplus occurs when quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded at a particular price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0112","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: At $20 a pair, a shop offers 180 shoes but customers buy only 110. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Inelastic supply","Surplus","Price elasticity of demand","Sellers in a market"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates surplus: A surplus occurs when quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded at a particular price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0113","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A firm sells 80 units at $8 each. What is its total sales revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$648","$640","$80","$88"],"answer":1,"why":"Total revenue = price × quantity = $8 × 80 = $640."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0114","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to surplus?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price.","Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged.","Excess supply can put downward pressure on price and encourage stock clearance.","Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic."],"answer":2,"why":"Excess supply can put downward pressure on price and encourage stock clearance."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0115","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to discount shoes when inventories are excessive. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Spare capacity is costly to maintain.","Some consumers may be unable to afford essential services.","Lower prices could attract more buyers.","Treatment raises operating costs for the factory."],"answer":2,"why":"Lower prices could attract more buyers."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0116","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to discount shoes when inventories are excessive. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The bakery may reduce unsold stock.","More people may receive protection from disease.","Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller.","Revenue per pair of shoes will decline."],"answer":3,"why":"Revenue per pair of shoes will decline."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0117","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to discount shoes when inventories are excessive?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Unsold inventory and storage costs may fall.","Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods.","Weak targeting may benefit households that are not most in need.","The increase in ticket sales may be small if demand is inelastic."],"answer":0,"why":"Unsold inventory and storage costs may fall."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0118","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to discount shoes when inventories are excessive less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The discount might still fail to clear unpopular designs.","Fishing livelihoods and health may improve.","More seasonal jobs may be offered.","Extra revenue could fund service improvements."],"answer":0,"why":"The discount might still fail to clear unpopular designs."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0119","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to discount shoes when inventories are excessive. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Lower prices could attract more buyers.","The bakery may reduce unsold stock.","More people may purchase bicycles.","Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase."],"answer":0,"why":"Lower prices could attract more buyers."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0120","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: At $20 a pair, a shop offers 180 shoes but customers buy only 110. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods.","Lower costs can shift supply right and make the service cheaper or more available.","Excess supply can put downward pressure on price and encourage stock clearance.","Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain."],"answer":2,"why":"Excess supply can put downward pressure on price and encourage stock clearance."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0121","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A market schedule gives these data (price, quantity demanded, quantity supplied): ($18, 135, 95); ($20, 125, 105); ($22, 115, 115); ($24, 105, 125). What is the equilibrium price?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$22","$18","$20","$24"],"answer":0,"why":"At $22, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 115 units."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0122","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A popular sports event increases demand for nearby hotel rooms. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Negative external costs","Increase in demand and price","Market","Shift in supply"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates increase in demand and price: An increase in demand tends to raise equilibrium price and quantity if supply is unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0123","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of increase in demand and price?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A popular sports event increases demand for nearby hotel rooms.","A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%.","A medicine with few substitutes has less price-sensitive buyers than an optional entertainment service.","Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding."],"answer":0,"why":"A popular sports event increases demand for nearby hotel rooms. This illustrates an increase in demand tends to raise equilibrium price and quantity if supply is unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0124","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to increase in demand and price?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Consumers can switch more easily when close substitutes are available.","Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal.","External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption.","The demand curve shifts right, creating upward price pressure at the original price."],"answer":3,"why":"The demand curve shifts right, creating upward price pressure at the original price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0125","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Revenue per pair of shoes will decline.","Demand could change before new production is ready.","Treatment raises operating costs for the factory.","Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0126","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More customers may buy tickets.","More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation.","Visitors may face higher accommodation costs.","Lower prices could attract more buyers."],"answer":2,"why":"Visitors may face higher accommodation costs."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0127","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Some hotels can fund future capacity investment.","Weak enforcement may make regulation ineffective.","Some buyers lack information about alternative products.","Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods."],"answer":0,"why":"Some hotels can fund future capacity investment."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0128","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Supply may be fixed in the very short run.","Reduced car travel may lower local pollution.","Poor housing conditions may become less widespread.","Output may better match what customers want."],"answer":0,"why":"Supply may be fixed in the very short run."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0129","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered.","The firm may expand output when prices rise.","The farm may identify profitable production levels.","Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0130","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A retailer selling initially 280 units raises its price by 5% and quantity demanded decreases by 15%. What is the magnitude of price elasticity of demand (PED)?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["10","0.333","4","3"],"answer":3,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (15%) / percentage change in price (5%) = 3."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0131","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes increase in supply and price?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A market economic system relies mainly on private ownership and price signals to allocate resources.","Price elasticity of demand measures responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price.","An increase in supply tends to reduce equilibrium price and raise quantity sold if demand is unchanged.","An increase in demand tends to raise equilibrium price and quantity if supply is unchanged."],"answer":2,"why":"Increase in supply and price: An increase in supply tends to reduce equilibrium price and raise quantity sold if demand is unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0132","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Merit goods","Increase in supply and price","Inelastic demand","Mixed economic system"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates increase in supply and price: An increase in supply tends to reduce equilibrium price and raise quantity sold if demand is unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0133","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of increase in supply and price?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding.","A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity.","A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market.","Private shops sell food while the government operates local public hospitals."],"answer":2,"why":"A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market. This illustrates an increase in supply tends to reduce equilibrium price and raise quantity sold if demand is unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0134","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to increase in supply and price?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The supply curve shifts right and produces excess supply at the old price.","A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve.","Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal.","Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic."],"answer":0,"why":"The supply curve shifts right and produces excess supply at the old price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0135","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to support more efficient apple cultivation. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Tax finance has an opportunity cost for other services.","Consumers may pay less for apples.","Survey answers may differ from actual purchases.","Idle machines still require maintenance and space."],"answer":1,"why":"Consumers may pay less for apples."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0136","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to support more efficient apple cultivation. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices.","The farm may identify profitable production levels.","Farmers may receive a lower price per kilogram.","Orders may be met without a long delay."],"answer":2,"why":"Farmers may receive a lower price per kilogram."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0137","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to support more efficient apple cultivation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Demand may become more elastic over a longer time period.","Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods.","Greater output may make fruit more accessible.","PED estimates can be unreliable with limited sales data."],"answer":2,"why":"Greater output may make fruit more accessible."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0138","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A producer initially supplies 370 units and observes a 10% price rise followed by a 40% increase in quantity supplied. What is price elasticity of supply (PES)?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["30","4","5","0.25"],"answer":1,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (40%) / percentage change in price (10%) = 4."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0139","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to support more efficient apple cultivation. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Consumers may pay less for apples.","Growers could reach more potential customers.","More customers may buy tickets.","The farm may identify profitable production levels."],"answer":0,"why":"Consumers may pay less for apples."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0140","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A higher selling price can justify output from more expensive production methods.","The supply curve shifts right and produces excess supply at the old price.","The combination aims to retain market incentives while addressing some market failures.","Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods."],"answer":1,"why":"The supply curve shifts right and produces excess supply at the old price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0141","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes simultaneous shifts?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Public goods are non-excludable and non-rival in consumption.","When demand and supply both change, the effect on price depends on the relative sizes of the shifts.","A mixed economic system combines market allocation with government intervention and public provision.","Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to sell at different prices over a period."],"answer":1,"why":"Simultaneous shifts: When demand and supply both change, the effect on price depends on the relative sizes of the shifts."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0142","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Movement along supply","Sellers in a market","Merit goods","Simultaneous shifts"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates simultaneous shifts: When demand and supply both change, the effect on price depends on the relative sizes of the shifts."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0143","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of simultaneous shifts?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase.","A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%.","A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others.","At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200."],"answer":0,"why":"Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase. This illustrates when demand and supply both change, the effect on price depends on the relative sizes of the shifts."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0144","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to simultaneous shifts?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain.","Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged.","External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption.","Sellers' responses to prices help allocate resources across products."],"answer":0,"why":"Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0145","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand delivery-kitchen capacity as demand grows. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Demand could change before new production is ready.","New ovens require investment.","Cold storage has high energy and capital costs.","Higher sales volumes may be served."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher sales volumes may be served."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0146","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A firm sells 140 units at $3 each. What is its total sales revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$420","$143","$423","$140"],"answer":0,"why":"Total revenue = price × quantity = $3 × 140 = $420."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0147","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand delivery-kitchen capacity as demand grows?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["It does not immediately increase the harvested quantity.","Poor oversight may reward inefficient operators.","Extra competition may limit price increases.","Pollution and other external costs may be ignored."],"answer":2,"why":"Extra competition may limit price increases."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0148","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand delivery-kitchen capacity as demand grows less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Reduced car use can lower congestion and pollution.","Extra revenue could fund service improvements.","Public hospitals may improve access for low-income households.","The direction of price change is uncertain without shift magnitudes."],"answer":3,"why":"The direction of price change is uncertain without shift magnitudes."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0149","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand delivery-kitchen capacity as demand grows. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher sales volumes may be served.","All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation.","The firm may expand output when prices rise.","Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher sales volumes may be served."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0150","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged.","Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain.","Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods.","A higher selling price can justify output from more expensive production methods."],"answer":1,"why":"Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0151","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes price elasticity of demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services.","Price elasticity of demand measures responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price.","Equilibrium is the price and quantity at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.","Sellers supply products and respond to prices, costs and expected profit."],"answer":1,"why":"Price elasticity of demand: Price elasticity of demand measures responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0152","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Price elasticity of demand","Increase in supply and price","Determinants of PES","Inelastic demand"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates price elasticity of demand: Price elasticity of demand measures responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0153","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of price elasticity of demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A bakery notices customers buying more wholegrain bread than white bread.","Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding.","A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises.","A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%."],"answer":3,"why":"A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%. This illustrates price elasticity of demand measures responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0154","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to price elasticity of demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction.","The demand curve shifts right, creating upward price pressure at the original price.","The magnitude of PED is 2, indicating elastic demand.","Changes in purchases signal which goods consumers value at prevailing prices."],"answer":2,"why":"The magnitude of PED is 2, indicating elastic demand."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0155","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A market schedule gives these data (price, quantity demanded, quantity supplied): ($22, 147, 107); ($24, 137, 117); ($26, 127, 127); ($28, 117, 137). What is the equilibrium price?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$22","$26","$24","$28"],"answer":1,"why":"At $26, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 127 units."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0156","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to calculate PED before changing gym membership fees. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered.","The farm may identify profitable production levels.","Past responsiveness may not hold after competitors change prices.","Fares may fall and ridership may rise."],"answer":2,"why":"Past responsiveness may not hold after competitors change prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0157","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to calculate PED before changing gym membership fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Platform fees might increase sellers' costs.","Public and private responsibilities may be poorly coordinated.","Better forecasts may guide pricing and revenue decisions.","Poor oversight may reward inefficient operators."],"answer":2,"why":"Better forecasts may guide pricing and revenue decisions."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0158","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to calculate PED before changing gym membership fees less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Unsold inventory and storage costs may fall.","Buyers may benefit from greater choice.","PED estimates can be unreliable with limited sales data.","Public hospitals may improve access for low-income households."],"answer":2,"why":"PED estimates can be unreliable with limited sales data."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0159","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to calculate PED before changing gym membership fees. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Growers could reach more potential customers.","Unsold stock could be reduced.","More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation.","Managers may estimate how sales volume will change."],"answer":3,"why":"Managers may estimate how sales volume will change."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0160","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price.","A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve.","The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information.","The magnitude of PED is 2, indicating elastic demand."],"answer":3,"why":"The magnitude of PED is 2, indicating elastic demand."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0161","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes inelastic demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed.","Demand is price inelastic when the percentage change in quantity demanded is smaller than the percentage price change.","An increase in supply tends to reduce equilibrium price and raise quantity sold if demand is unchanged.","A change in a good's own price causes movement along its demand curve, other factors unchanged."],"answer":1,"why":"Inelastic demand: Demand is price inelastic when the percentage change in quantity demanded is smaller than the percentage price change."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0162","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A bus operator raises fares by 10% while passenger journeys fall by 3%. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Market","Inelastic demand","Merit goods","Simultaneous shifts"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates inelastic demand: Demand is price inelastic when the percentage change in quantity demanded is smaller than the percentage price change."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0163","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A retailer selling initially 320 units raises its price by 5% and quantity demanded decreases by 10%. What is the magnitude of price elasticity of demand (PED)?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["2","5","3","0.5"],"answer":0,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (10%) / percentage change in price (5%) = 2."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0164","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to inelastic demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information.","Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic.","Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain.","The PED magnitude is 0.3; quantity responds proportionately less than price."],"answer":3,"why":"The PED magnitude is 0.3; quantity responds proportionately less than price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0165","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller.","Revenue from each ticket will be lower.","Demand could change before new production is ready.","Extra harvesting can raise marginal production costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0166","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year.","Higher fares impose a greater burden on regular passengers.","The farm may identify profitable production levels.","More people may receive protection from disease."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher fares impose a greater burden on regular passengers."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0167","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Weak enforcement may make regulation ineffective.","Businesses may promote harmful products despite consumer demand.","Extra revenue could fund service improvements.","Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods."],"answer":2,"why":"Extra revenue could fund service improvements."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0168","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Buyers may benefit from greater choice.","The service can be provided even when individual users cannot be charged.","Output may better match what customers want.","Demand may become more elastic over a longer time period."],"answer":3,"why":"Demand may become more elastic over a longer time period."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0169","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller.","Higher sales volumes may be served.","More customers may buy tickets.","The farm may identify profitable production levels."],"answer":0,"why":"Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0170","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A bus operator raises fares by 10% while passenger journeys fall by 3%. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain.","The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction.","A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve.","The PED magnitude is 0.3; quantity responds proportionately less than price."],"answer":3,"why":"The PED magnitude is 0.3; quantity responds proportionately less than price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0171","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A producer initially supplies 410 units and observes a 10% price rise followed by a 30% increase in quantity supplied. What is price elasticity of supply (PES)?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["20","3","0.333","4"],"answer":1,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (30%) / percentage change in price (10%) = 3."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0172","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A medicine with few substitutes has less price-sensitive buyers than an optional entertainment service. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Shift in supply","Movement along supply","Mixed economic system","Determinants of PED"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates determinants of ped: Availability of substitutes, necessity and time influence the price elasticity of demand."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0173","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of determinants of ped?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises.","At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200.","A medicine with few substitutes has less price-sensitive buyers than an optional entertainment service.","At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280."],"answer":2,"why":"A medicine with few substitutes has less price-sensitive buyers than an optional entertainment service. This illustrates availability of substitutes, necessity and time influence the price elasticity of demand."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0174","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to determinants of ped?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Consumers can switch more easily when close substitutes are available.","The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction.","Changes in purchases signal which goods consumers value at prevailing prices.","Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price."],"answer":0,"why":"Consumers can switch more easily when close substitutes are available."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0175","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to consider substitute availability when pricing products. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers.","Visitors may face higher accommodation costs.","Demand may change with the weather.","Survey answers may differ from actual purchases."],"answer":0,"why":"Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0176","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to consider substitute availability when pricing products. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher prices may encourage additional supply.","The farm may identify profitable production levels.","Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year.","Competitors can introduce new substitutes unexpectedly."],"answer":3,"why":"Competitors can introduce new substitutes unexpectedly."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0177","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to consider substitute availability when pricing products?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Demand may be too inelastic for usage to fall significantly.","Supply may be fixed in the very short run.","Price decisions may better reflect consumer sensitivity.","Public and private responsibilities may be poorly coordinated."],"answer":2,"why":"Price decisions may better reflect consumer sensitivity."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0178","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to consider substitute availability when pricing products less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Some buyers lack information about alternative products.","Extra competition may limit price increases.","Lower transmission may benefit the wider community.","Higher productive efficiency may increase output."],"answer":0,"why":"Some buyers lack information about alternative products."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0179","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to consider substitute availability when pricing products. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase.","Competition may increase the quantity offered.","Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers.","The firm may expand output when prices rise."],"answer":2,"why":"Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0180","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A firm sells 95 units at $7 each. What is its total sales revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$102","$672","$665","$95"],"answer":2,"why":"Total revenue = price × quantity = $7 × 95 = $665."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0181","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes price elasticity of supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Supply is price inelastic when quantity supplied changes by a smaller percentage than price.","Consumer sovereignty means buying decisions influence what firms offer for sale.","Spare capacity, stock levels, production time and factor mobility influence elasticity of supply.","Price elasticity of supply measures how strongly quantity supplied responds to a percentage change in price."],"answer":3,"why":"Price elasticity of supply: Price elasticity of supply measures how strongly quantity supplied responds to a percentage change in price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0182","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Simultaneous shifts","Subsidy","Price elasticity of supply","Determinants of PED"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates price elasticity of supply: Price elasticity of supply measures how strongly quantity supplied responds to a percentage change in price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0183","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of price elasticity of supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Several cafés add plant-based meals after sustained customer demand.","Independent fruit growers meet customers at a weekly neighbourhood market.","A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%.","A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price."],"answer":2,"why":"A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%. This illustrates price elasticity of supply measures how strongly quantity supplied responds to a percentage change in price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0184","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to price elasticity of supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods.","The PED magnitude is 0.3; quantity responds proportionately less than price.","The PES value is 2, indicating supply is price elastic.","Excess supply can put downward pressure on price and encourage stock clearance."],"answer":2,"why":"The PES value is 2, indicating supply is price elastic."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0185","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in flexible production capacity to respond to prices. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Revenue per pair of shoes will decline.","The firm may expand output when prices rise.","New ovens require investment.","Demand may change with the weather."],"answer":1,"why":"The firm may expand output when prices rise."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0186","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in flexible production capacity to respond to prices. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Spare capacity is costly to maintain.","Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices.","More people may purchase bicycles.","Competition may improve retail efficiency."],"answer":0,"why":"Spare capacity is costly to maintain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0187","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to invest in flexible production capacity to respond to prices?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Demand may become more elastic over a longer time period.","Platform fees might increase sellers' costs.","Demand may be too inelastic for usage to fall significantly.","Flexibility can help meet sudden increases in demand."],"answer":3,"why":"Flexibility can help meet sudden increases in demand."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0188","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A market schedule gives these data (price, quantity demanded, quantity supplied): ($26, 159, 119); ($28, 149, 129); ($30, 139, 139); ($32, 129, 149). What is the equilibrium price?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$30","$26","$28","$32"],"answer":0,"why":"At $30, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 139 units."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0189","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to invest in flexible production capacity to respond to prices. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller.","Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices.","Higher sales volumes may be served.","The firm may expand output when prices rise."],"answer":3,"why":"The firm may expand output when prices rise."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0190","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision.","Excess supply can put downward pressure on price and encourage stock clearance.","The PES value is 2, indicating supply is price elastic.","The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information."],"answer":2,"why":"The PES value is 2, indicating supply is price elastic."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0191","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes inelastic supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["When demand and supply both change, the effect on price depends on the relative sizes of the shifts.","Equilibrium is the price and quantity at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.","Supply is price inelastic when quantity supplied changes by a smaller percentage than price.","A change in the product's own price causes movement along its supply curve."],"answer":2,"why":"Inelastic supply: Supply is price inelastic when quantity supplied changes by a smaller percentage than price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0192","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: The price of fresh strawberries rises rapidly but the harvest cannot increase for several months. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Negative external costs","Inelastic supply","Price elasticity of supply","Movement along demand"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates inelastic supply: Supply is price inelastic when quantity supplied changes by a smaller percentage than price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0193","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of inelastic supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Independent fruit growers meet customers at a weekly neighbourhood market.","The price of fresh strawberries rises rapidly but the harvest cannot increase for several months.","At $20 a pair, a shop offers 180 shoes but customers buy only 110.","A paint factory discharges waste into a river used by downstream fishers."],"answer":1,"why":"The price of fresh strawberries rises rapidly but the harvest cannot increase for several months. This illustrates supply is price inelastic when quantity supplied changes by a smaller percentage than price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0194","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to inelastic supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods.","Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision.","Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price.","Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged."],"answer":2,"why":"Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0195","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve cold storage for a crop with inelastic short-run supply. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Weather may make planned supply impossible.","Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year.","New ovens require investment.","Extra harvesting can raise marginal production costs."],"answer":1,"why":"Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0196","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A retailer selling initially 360 units raises its price by 5% and quantity demanded decreases by 30%. What is the magnitude of price elasticity of demand (PED)?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["25","6","7","0.167"],"answer":1,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (30%) / percentage change in price (5%) = 6."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0197","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve cold storage for a crop with inelastic short-run supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The discount might still fail to clear unpopular designs.","Operating and maintenance costs may be high.","Less spoilage may increase the quantity available for purchase.","Weak targeting may benefit households that are not most in need."],"answer":2,"why":"Less spoilage may increase the quantity available for purchase."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0198","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to improve cold storage for a crop with inelastic short-run supply less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["It does not immediately increase the harvested quantity.","Reduced car travel may lower local pollution.","Reduced car use can lower congestion and pollution.","Buyers could compare products and prices more easily."],"answer":0,"why":"It does not immediately increase the harvested quantity."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0199","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve cold storage for a crop with inelastic short-run supply. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Growers could reach more potential customers.","Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller.","Higher sales volumes may be served.","Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year."],"answer":3,"why":"Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0200","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: The price of fresh strawberries rises rapidly but the harvest cannot increase for several months. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption.","A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve.","Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal.","Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price."],"answer":3,"why":"Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0201","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes determinants of pes?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Supply is price inelastic when quantity supplied changes by a smaller percentage than price.","A subsidy is government financial assistance that lowers production costs or consumer prices.","A non-price determinant changes the whole demand schedule for a good.","Spare capacity, stock levels, production time and factor mobility influence elasticity of supply."],"answer":3,"why":"Determinants of PES: Spare capacity, stock levels, production time and factor mobility influence elasticity of supply."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0202","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Market economic system","Shift in supply","Indirect tax","Determinants of PES"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates determinants of pes: Spare capacity, stock levels, production time and factor mobility influence elasticity of supply."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0203","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of determinants of pes?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity.","A government adds a per-unit tax to single-use plastic packaging.","At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200.","A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market."],"answer":0,"why":"A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity. This illustrates spare capacity, stock levels, production time and factor mobility influence elasticity of supply."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0204","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to determinants of pes?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power.","The magnitude of PED is 2, indicating elastic demand.","A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve.","Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic."],"answer":3,"why":"Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0205","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A producer initially supplies 450 units and observes a 10% price rise followed by a 20% increase in quantity supplied. What is price elasticity of supply (PES)?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["10","2","3","0.5"],"answer":1,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (20%) / percentage change in price (10%) = 2."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0206","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to maintain some flexible spare capacity at a factory. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Consumers may pay less for apples.","Idle machines still require maintenance and space.","Growers could reach more potential customers.","Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices."],"answer":1,"why":"Idle machines still require maintenance and space."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0207","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to maintain some flexible spare capacity at a factory?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["PED estimates can be unreliable with limited sales data.","Weak targeting may benefit households that are not most in need.","The firm may gain sales during sudden demand increases.","Harvest output depends on weather and storage capacity."],"answer":2,"why":"The firm may gain sales during sudden demand increases."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0208","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to maintain some flexible spare capacity at a factory less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Consumer preferences can affect business decisions.","The service can be provided even when individual users cannot be charged.","Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods.","Customers may be less likely to encounter shortages."],"answer":2,"why":"Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0209","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to maintain some flexible spare capacity at a factory. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Ordering decisions may match expected sales.","Growers could reach more potential customers.","Orders may be met without a long delay.","All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation."],"answer":2,"why":"Orders may be met without a long delay."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0210","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic.","Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market.","The magnitude of PED is 2, indicating elastic demand.","When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus."],"answer":0,"why":"Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0211","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes market economic system?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A market economic system relies mainly on private ownership and price signals to allocate resources.","Price elasticity of supply measures how strongly quantity supplied responds to a percentage change in price.","An increase in demand tends to raise equilibrium price and quantity if supply is unchanged.","Price elasticity of demand measures responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price."],"answer":0,"why":"Market economic system: A market economic system relies mainly on private ownership and price signals to allocate resources."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0212","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Independent firms compete to supply restaurants while customers choose where to eat. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Market economic system","Consumer sovereignty","Buyers in a market","Market equilibrium"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates market economic system: A market economic system relies mainly on private ownership and price signals to allocate resources."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0213","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A firm sells 155 units at $11 each. What is its total sales revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$166","$155","$1,716","$1,705"],"answer":3,"why":"Total revenue = price × quantity = $11 × 155 = $1,705."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0214","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to market economic system?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption.","A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve.","When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus.","Profit and consumer choices influence the goods firms produce."],"answer":3,"why":"Profit and consumer choices influence the goods firms produce."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0215","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices.","Tax finance has an opportunity cost for other services.","Demand could change before new production is ready.","Cold storage has high energy and capital costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0216","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation.","More customers may buy tickets.","Some consumers may be unable to afford essential services.","The firm may expand output when prices rise."],"answer":2,"why":"Some consumers may be unable to afford essential services."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0217","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods.","Consumer preferences can affect business decisions.","Supply may be fixed in the very short run.","Platform fees might increase sellers' costs."],"answer":1,"why":"Consumer preferences can affect business decisions."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0218","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Pollution and other external costs may be ignored.","Reduced car use can lower congestion and pollution.","Extra competition may limit price increases.","The retailer may avoid spending on unwanted stock."],"answer":0,"why":"Pollution and other external costs may be ignored."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0219","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller.","Lower prices could attract more buyers.","Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices.","Higher prices may discourage plastic use."],"answer":2,"why":"Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0220","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Independent firms compete to supply restaurants while customers choose where to eat. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Profit and consumer choices influence the goods firms produce.","The supply curve shifts right and produces excess supply at the old price.","The combination aims to retain market incentives while addressing some market failures.","Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price."],"answer":0,"why":"Profit and consumer choices influence the goods firms produce."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0221","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A market schedule gives these data (price, quantity demanded, quantity supplied): ($30, 171, 131); ($32, 161, 141); ($34, 151, 151); ($36, 141, 161). What is the equilibrium price?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$36","$32","$30","$34"],"answer":3,"why":"At $34, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 151 units."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0222","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Several cafés add plant-based meals after sustained customer demand. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Merit goods","Consumer sovereignty","Determinants of PES","Supply"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates consumer sovereignty: Consumer sovereignty means buying decisions influence what firms offer for sale."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0223","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of consumer sovereignty?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Several cafés add plant-based meals after sustained customer demand.","A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%.","At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280.","A medicine with few substitutes has less price-sensitive buyers than an optional entertainment service."],"answer":0,"why":"Several cafés add plant-based meals after sustained customer demand. This illustrates consumer sovereignty means buying decisions influence what firms offer for sale."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0224","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to consumer sovereignty?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Consumers can switch more easily when close substitutes are available.","Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods.","Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price.","The PES value is 2, indicating supply is price elastic."],"answer":1,"why":"Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0225","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to let customer demand guide café menu decisions. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Idle machines still require maintenance and space.","Subsidies require public funding.","Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase.","Investment could be wasted if demand growth slows."],"answer":2,"why":"Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0226","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to let customer demand guide café menu decisions. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Competition may improve retail efficiency.","Low-income consumers exert less influence because purchasing power differs.","Lower costs may support lower bread prices.","Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers."],"answer":1,"why":"Low-income consumers exert less influence because purchasing power differs."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0227","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to let customer demand guide café menu decisions?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Less unsold food may be wasted.","Prices might fall before the extra crop reaches buyers.","Public and private responsibilities may be poorly coordinated.","The equilibrium estimate may use inaccurate data."],"answer":0,"why":"Less unsold food may be wasted."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0228","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to let customer demand guide café menu decisions less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Lower transmission may benefit the wider community.","Businesses may promote harmful products despite consumer demand.","Price decisions may better reflect consumer sensitivity.","Output may better match what customers want."],"answer":1,"why":"Businesses may promote harmful products despite consumer demand."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0229","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to let customer demand guide café menu decisions. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase.","Competition may increase the quantity offered.","Fares may fall and ridership may rise.","More people may purchase bicycles."],"answer":0,"why":"Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0230","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A retailer selling initially 400 units raises its price by 5% and quantity demanded decreases by 25%. What is the magnitude of price elasticity of demand (PED)?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["6","20","0.2","5"],"answer":3,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (25%) / percentage change in price (5%) = 5."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0231","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes market economy limitations?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Price elasticity of demand measures responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price.","Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to sell at different prices over a period.","A mixed economic system combines market allocation with government intervention and public provision.","Markets may allocate goods according to income rather than need and fail to account for external costs."],"answer":3,"why":"Market economy limitations: Markets may allocate goods according to income rather than need and fail to account for external costs."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0232","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Determinants of PED","Simultaneous shifts","Market economy limitations","Inelastic demand"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates market economy limitations: Markets may allocate goods according to income rather than need and fail to account for external costs."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0233","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of market economy limitations?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price.","Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding.","A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%.","A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%."],"answer":1,"why":"Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding. This illustrates markets may allocate goods according to income rather than need and fail to account for external costs."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0234","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to market economy limitations?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market.","A higher selling price can justify output from more expensive production methods.","Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power.","Price signals alone may not achieve fair distribution or account for social welfare."],"answer":3,"why":"Price signals alone may not achieve fair distribution or account for social welfare."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0235","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to supplement market housing with targeted public housing. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Spare capacity is costly to maintain.","New ovens require investment.","Cycle routes use public funds and road space.","More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation."],"answer":3,"why":"More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0236","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to supplement market housing with targeted public housing. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Fares may fall and ridership may rise.","River pollution may fall.","Orders may be met without a long delay.","Public housing programmes require tax funding."],"answer":3,"why":"Public housing programmes require tax funding."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0237","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to supplement market housing with targeted public housing?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The equilibrium estimate may use inaccurate data.","Poor oversight may reward inefficient operators.","Poor housing conditions may become less widespread.","Specialist inputs may still limit rapid expansion."],"answer":2,"why":"Poor housing conditions may become less widespread."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0238","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A producer initially supplies 490 units and observes a 10% price rise followed by a 60% increase in quantity supplied. What is price elasticity of supply (PES)?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["7","0.167","6","50"],"answer":2,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (60%) / percentage change in price (10%) = 6."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0239","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to supplement market housing with targeted public housing. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation.","Ordering decisions may match expected sales.","The firm may expand output when prices rise.","Competition may improve retail efficiency."],"answer":0,"why":"More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0240","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Price signals alone may not achieve fair distribution or account for social welfare.","Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision.","The PES value is 2, indicating supply is price elastic.","The demand curve shifts right, creating upward price pressure at the original price."],"answer":0,"why":"Price signals alone may not achieve fair distribution or account for social welfare."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0241","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes public goods?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Non-price changes in production conditions shift the supply curve.","A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services.","Public goods are non-excludable and non-rival in consumption.","Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to sell at different prices over a period."],"answer":2,"why":"Public goods: Public goods are non-excludable and non-rival in consumption."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0242","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A coastal lighthouse guides all nearby ships without preventing others from using its signal. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Supply","Surplus","Public goods","Negative external costs"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates public goods: Public goods are non-excludable and non-rival in consumption."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0243","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of public goods?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Independent firms compete to supply restaurants while customers choose where to eat.","A shop records how many bicycles customers would buy at each possible selling price.","A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises.","A coastal lighthouse guides all nearby ships without preventing others from using its signal."],"answer":3,"why":"A coastal lighthouse guides all nearby ships without preventing others from using its signal. This illustrates public goods are non-excludable and non-rival in consumption."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0244","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to public goods?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision.","Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price.","When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus.","Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods."],"answer":0,"why":"Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0245","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to finance a public lighthouse through taxation. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation.","Higher fares impose a greater burden on regular passengers.","Past responsiveness may not hold after competitors change prices.","Revenue from each ticket will be lower."],"answer":0,"why":"All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0246","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A firm sells 110 units at $6 each. What is its total sales revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$666","$110","$116","$660"],"answer":3,"why":"Total revenue = price × quantity = $6 × 110 = $660."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0247","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to finance a public lighthouse through taxation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The service can be provided even when individual users cannot be charged.","Specialist inputs may still limit rapid expansion.","Businesses may promote harmful products despite consumer demand.","Prices might fall before the extra crop reaches buyers."],"answer":0,"why":"The service can be provided even when individual users cannot be charged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0248","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to finance a public lighthouse through taxation less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Better forecasts may guide pricing and revenue decisions.","Flexibility can help meet sudden increases in demand.","Poor housing conditions may become less widespread.","Operating and maintenance costs may be high."],"answer":3,"why":"Operating and maintenance costs may be high."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0249","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to finance a public lighthouse through taxation. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation.","Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year.","Consumers may pay less for apples.","More customers may buy tickets."],"answer":0,"why":"All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0250","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A coastal lighthouse guides all nearby ships without preventing others from using its signal. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price.","Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic.","Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision.","Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain."],"answer":2,"why":"Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0251","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes merit goods?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Supply is price inelastic when quantity supplied changes by a smaller percentage than price.","Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed.","Buyers signal demand through their willingness and ability to purchase products.","Equilibrium is the price and quantity at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied."],"answer":1,"why":"Merit goods: Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0252","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Simultaneous shifts","Price elasticity of supply","Movement along demand","Merit goods"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates merit goods: Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0253","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of merit goods?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Several farmers offer tomatoes at a wholesale market each morning.","A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others.","A government adds a per-unit tax to single-use plastic packaging.","A popular sports event increases demand for nearby hotel rooms."],"answer":1,"why":"A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others. This illustrates merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0254","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to merit goods?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged.","The tax raises producers' costs and tends to raise consumer prices while reducing sales.","The combination aims to retain market incentives while addressing some market failures.","External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption."],"answer":3,"why":"External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0255","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A market schedule gives these data (price, quantity demanded, quantity supplied): ($34, 183, 143); ($36, 173, 153); ($38, 163, 163); ($40, 153, 173). What is the equilibrium price?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$36","$40","$38","$34"],"answer":2,"why":"At $38, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 163 units."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0256","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to subsidise childhood vaccination services. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Managers may estimate how sales volume will change.","Subsidies require public funding.","Ordering decisions may match expected sales.","Farm revenue may increase if extra output sells."],"answer":1,"why":"Subsidies require public funding."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0257","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to subsidise childhood vaccination services?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Prices might fall before the extra crop reaches buyers.","Lower transmission may benefit the wider community.","Weak targeting may benefit households that are not most in need.","Weather or safety concerns may still limit cycling."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower transmission may benefit the wider community."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0258","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to subsidise childhood vaccination services less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Consumer preferences can affect business decisions.","The firm may gain sales during sudden demand increases.","Unsold inventory and storage costs may fall.","Poor access or misinformation may still reduce take-up."],"answer":3,"why":"Poor access or misinformation may still reduce take-up."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0259","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to subsidise childhood vaccination services. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Farm revenue may increase if extra output sells.","Orders may be met without a long delay.","Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers.","More people may receive protection from disease."],"answer":3,"why":"More people may receive protection from disease."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0260","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption.","The supply curve shifts right and produces excess supply at the old price.","Lower costs can shift supply right and make the service cheaper or more available.","A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve."],"answer":0,"why":"External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0261","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes negative external costs?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A market economic system relies mainly on private ownership and price signals to allocate resources.","Negative external costs are harmful effects of production or consumption imposed on third parties.","Sellers supply products and respond to prices, costs and expected profit.","A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services."],"answer":1,"why":"Negative external costs: Negative external costs are harmful effects of production or consumption imposed on third parties."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0262","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A paint factory discharges waste into a river used by downstream fishers. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Negative external costs","Surplus","Market","Determinants of PES"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates negative external costs: Negative external costs are harmful effects of production or consumption imposed on third parties."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0263","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A retailer selling initially 440 units raises its price by 5% and quantity demanded decreases by 20%. What is the magnitude of price elasticity of demand (PED)?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["0.25","15","5","4"],"answer":3,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (20%) / percentage change in price (5%) = 4."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0264","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to negative external costs?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The tax raises producers' costs and tends to raise consumer prices while reducing sales.","The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction.","Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal.","Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic."],"answer":1,"why":"The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0265","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to require the paint factory to treat wastewater. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["River pollution may fall.","Farmers may face lower prices and profits.","Demand may change with the weather.","Extra harvesting can raise marginal production costs."],"answer":0,"why":"River pollution may fall."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0266","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to require the paint factory to treat wastewater. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher prices may discourage plastic use.","Treatment raises operating costs for the factory.","Consumers may pay less for apples.","Managers may estimate how sales volume will change."],"answer":1,"why":"Treatment raises operating costs for the factory."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0267","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to require the paint factory to treat wastewater?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["It does not immediately increase the harvested quantity.","Production may not expand quickly enough to remove the shortage.","Savings depend on electricity use and machine reliability.","Fishing livelihoods and health may improve."],"answer":3,"why":"Fishing livelihoods and health may improve."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0268","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to require the paint factory to treat wastewater less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Weak enforcement may make regulation ineffective.","The retailer may avoid spending on unwanted stock.","Production can be aligned more closely with expected market prices.","Reduced car travel may lower local pollution."],"answer":0,"why":"Weak enforcement may make regulation ineffective."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0269","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to require the paint factory to treat wastewater. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["River pollution may fall.","Higher sales volumes may be served.","Growers could reach more potential customers.","The firm may expand output when prices rise."],"answer":0,"why":"River pollution may fall."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0270","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A paint factory discharges waste into a river used by downstream fishers. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction.","Consumers can switch more easily when close substitutes are available.","The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information.","Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power."],"answer":0,"why":"The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0271","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A producer initially supplies 530 units and observes a 10% price rise followed by a 50% increase in quantity supplied. What is price elasticity of supply (PES)?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["40","5","0.2","6"],"answer":1,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (50%) / percentage change in price (10%) = 5."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0272","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Private shops sell food while the government operates local public hospitals. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Sellers in a market","Demand","Merit goods","Mixed economic system"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates mixed economic system: A mixed economic system combines market allocation with government intervention and public provision."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0273","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of mixed economic system?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Cinema ticket sales rise when the cinema lowers its ticket price.","Private shops sell food while the government operates local public hospitals.","A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%.","Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase."],"answer":1,"why":"Private shops sell food while the government operates local public hospitals. This illustrates a mixed economic system combines market allocation with government intervention and public provision."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0274","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to mixed economic system?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The combination aims to retain market incentives while addressing some market failures.","Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal.","External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption.","When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus."],"answer":0,"why":"The combination aims to retain market incentives while addressing some market failures."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0275","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to combine private retail markets with public health provision. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Demand may change with the weather.","Competition may improve retail efficiency.","Higher fares impose a greater burden on regular passengers.","Farmers may face lower prices and profits."],"answer":1,"why":"Competition may improve retail efficiency."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0276","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to combine private retail markets with public health provision. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase.","More people may receive protection from disease.","River pollution may fall.","State services require sufficient tax revenue."],"answer":3,"why":"State services require sufficient tax revenue."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0277","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to combine private retail markets with public health provision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Weak targeting may benefit households that are not most in need.","Public hospitals may improve access for low-income households.","Demand may become more elastic over a longer time period.","The increase in ticket sales may be small if demand is inelastic."],"answer":1,"why":"Public hospitals may improve access for low-income households."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0278","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to combine private retail markets with public health provision less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Public and private responsibilities may be poorly coordinated.","Less unsold food may be wasted.","More seasonal jobs may be offered.","Flexibility can help meet sudden increases in demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Public and private responsibilities may be poorly coordinated."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0279","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to combine private retail markets with public health provision. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase.","All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation.","Higher sales volumes may be served.","Competition may improve retail efficiency."],"answer":3,"why":"Competition may improve retail efficiency."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0280","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A firm sells 170 units at $10 each. What is its total sales revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$170","$1,700","$180","$1,710"],"answer":1,"why":"Total revenue = price × quantity = $10 × 170 = $1,700."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0281","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes indirect tax?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A subsidy is government financial assistance that lowers production costs or consumer prices.","Price elasticity of demand measures responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price.","Demand is the quantity buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices during a period.","An indirect tax is a tax on spending on goods and services."],"answer":3,"why":"Indirect tax: An indirect tax is a tax on spending on goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0282","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government adds a per-unit tax to single-use plastic packaging. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Buyers in a market","Movement along supply","Indirect tax","Market economic system"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates indirect tax: An indirect tax is a tax on spending on goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0283","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of indirect tax?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A government adds a per-unit tax to single-use plastic packaging.","A bus operator raises fares by 10% while passenger journeys fall by 3%.","A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity.","Demand for electric bicycles rises after more cycling lanes are built."],"answer":0,"why":"A government adds a per-unit tax to single-use plastic packaging. This illustrates an indirect tax is a tax on spending on goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0284","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to indirect tax?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The tax raises producers' costs and tends to raise consumer prices while reducing sales.","Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power.","Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price.","The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction."],"answer":0,"why":"The tax raises producers' costs and tends to raise consumer prices while reducing sales."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0285","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to tax single-use plastic packaging. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Investment could be wasted if demand growth slows.","Some consumers may be unable to afford essential services.","Higher prices may discourage plastic use.","Tax finance has an opportunity cost for other services."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher prices may discourage plastic use."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0286","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to tax single-use plastic packaging. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More customers may buy tickets.","Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices.","Competition may increase the quantity offered.","Consumers may face higher prices."],"answer":3,"why":"Consumers may face higher prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0287","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to tax single-use plastic packaging?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Tax revenue could fund waste management.","The discount might still fail to clear unpopular designs.","Prices might fall before the extra crop reaches buyers.","Businesses may promote harmful products despite consumer demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Tax revenue could fund waste management."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0288","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A market schedule gives these data (price, quantity demanded, quantity supplied): ($38, 195, 155); ($40, 185, 165); ($42, 175, 175); ($44, 165, 185). What is the equilibrium price?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$42","$40","$38","$44"],"answer":0,"why":"At $42, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 175 units."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0289","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to tax single-use plastic packaging. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher prices may discourage plastic use.","Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller.","More customers may buy tickets.","The bakery may reduce unsold stock."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher prices may discourage plastic use."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0290","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government adds a per-unit tax to single-use plastic packaging. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The tax raises producers' costs and tends to raise consumer prices while reducing sales.","Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain.","The demand curve shifts right, creating upward price pressure at the original price.","The PED magnitude is 0.3; quantity responds proportionately less than price."],"answer":0,"why":"The tax raises producers' costs and tends to raise consumer prices while reducing sales."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0291","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes subsidy?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A surplus occurs when quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded at a particular price.","A subsidy is government financial assistance that lowers production costs or consumer prices.","Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed.","A non-price determinant changes the whole demand schedule for a good."],"answer":1,"why":"Subsidy: A subsidy is government financial assistance that lowers production costs or consumer prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0292","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government pays bus operators for each low-emission journey provided. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Market economic system","Consumer sovereignty","Subsidy","Shift in supply"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates subsidy: A subsidy is government financial assistance that lowers production costs or consumer prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0293","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of subsidy?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A government pays bus operators for each low-emission journey provided.","A bus operator raises fares by 10% while passenger journeys fall by 3%.","Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding.","At $20 a pair, a shop offers 180 shoes but customers buy only 110."],"answer":0,"why":"A government pays bus operators for each low-emission journey provided. This illustrates a subsidy is government financial assistance that lowers production costs or consumer prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0294","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to subsidy?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods.","Lower costs can shift supply right and make the service cheaper or more available.","Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain.","Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower costs can shift supply right and make the service cheaper or more available."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0295","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to subsidise low-emission public transport. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Farmers may face lower prices and profits.","New ovens require investment.","Low-income consumers exert less influence because purchasing power differs.","Fares may fall and ridership may rise."],"answer":3,"why":"Fares may fall and ridership may rise."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0296","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A retailer selling initially 480 units raises its price by 5% and quantity demanded decreases by 15%. What is the magnitude of price elasticity of demand (PED)?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["10","4","0.333","3"],"answer":3,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (15%) / percentage change in price (5%) = 3."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0297","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to subsidise low-emission public transport?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Supply may be fixed in the very short run.","Specialist inputs may still limit rapid expansion.","Reduced car use can lower congestion and pollution.","It does not immediately increase the harvested quantity."],"answer":2,"why":"Reduced car use can lower congestion and pollution."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0298","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to subsidise low-emission public transport less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Poor oversight may reward inefficient operators.","Unsold inventory and storage costs may fall.","Flexibility can help meet sudden increases in demand.","Reduced car travel may lower local pollution."],"answer":0,"why":"Poor oversight may reward inefficient operators."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0299","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to subsidise low-emission public transport. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered.","More people may purchase bicycles.","Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers.","Fares may fall and ridership may rise."],"answer":3,"why":"Fares may fall and ridership may rise."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0300","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government pays bus operators for each low-emission journey provided. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price.","Lower costs can shift supply right and make the service cheaper or more available.","The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction.","Price signals alone may not achieve fair distribution or account for social welfare."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower costs can shift supply right and make the service cheaper or more available."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0001","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Independent fruit growers meet customers at a weekly neighbourhood market. Decision-makers are considering whether to create a digital trading platform for local growers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by market. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Independent fruit growers meet customers at a weekly neighbourhood market.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0002","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A bakery notices customers buying more wholegrain bread than white bread. Decision-makers are considering whether to use customer demand information to plan bread output. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by buyers in a market. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Buyers signal demand through their willingness and ability to purchase products.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A bakery notices customers buying more wholegrain bread than white bread.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0003","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Several farmers offer tomatoes at a wholesale market each morning. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by sellers in a market. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Sellers supply products and respond to prices, costs and expected profit.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Several farmers offer tomatoes at a wholesale market each morning.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0004","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A shop records how many bicycles customers would buy at each possible selling price. Decision-makers are considering whether to survey local demand before ordering bicycles. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by demand. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Demand is the quantity buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices during a period.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A shop records how many bicycles customers would buy at each possible selling price.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0005","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Cinema ticket sales rise when the cinema lowers its ticket price. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce cinema ticket prices on quiet weekdays. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by movement along demand. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A change in a good's own price causes movement along its demand curve, other factors unchanged.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Cinema ticket sales rise when the cinema lowers its ticket price.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0006","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A tomato producer lists quantities offered for sale at a series of prices. Decision-makers are considering whether to plan output using a detailed supply schedule. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by supply. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to sell at different prices over a period.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A tomato producer lists quantities offered for sale at a series of prices.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0007","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by movement along supply. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A change in the product's own price causes movement along its supply curve.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0008","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce energy-saving ovens in bakeries. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by shift in supply. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Non-price changes in production conditions shift the supply curve.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0009","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200. Decision-makers are considering whether to adjust umbrella orders towards the equilibrium quantity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by market equilibrium. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Equilibrium is the price and quantity at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0010","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280. Decision-makers are considering whether to permit prices to respond to a temporary bread shortage. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by shortage. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A shortage occurs when quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at a particular price.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0011","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A popular sports event increases demand for nearby hotel rooms. Decision-makers are considering whether to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by increase in demand and price. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — An increase in demand tends to raise equilibrium price and quantity if supply is unchanged.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A popular sports event increases demand for nearby hotel rooms.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0012","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market. Decision-makers are considering whether to support more efficient apple cultivation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by increase in supply and price. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — An increase in supply tends to reduce equilibrium price and raise quantity sold if demand is unchanged.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0013","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand delivery-kitchen capacity as demand grows. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by simultaneous shifts. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — When demand and supply both change, the effect on price depends on the relative sizes of the shifts.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0014","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%. Decision-makers are considering whether to calculate PED before changing gym membership fees. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by price elasticity of demand. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Price elasticity of demand measures responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0015","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A bus operator raises fares by 10% while passenger journeys fall by 3%. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by inelastic demand. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Demand is price inelastic when the percentage change in quantity demanded is smaller than the percentage price change.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A bus operator raises fares by 10% while passenger journeys fall by 3%.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0016","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in flexible production capacity to respond to prices. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by price elasticity of supply. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Price elasticity of supply measures how strongly quantity supplied responds to a percentage change in price.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0017","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"The price of fresh strawberries rises rapidly but the harvest cannot increase for several months. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve cold storage for a crop with inelastic short-run supply. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by inelastic supply. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Supply is price inelastic when quantity supplied changes by a smaller percentage than price.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — The price of fresh strawberries rises rapidly but the harvest cannot increase for several months.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0018","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity. Decision-makers are considering whether to maintain some flexible spare capacity at a factory. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by determinants of pes. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Spare capacity, stock levels, production time and factor mobility influence elasticity of supply.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0019","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Independent firms compete to supply restaurants while customers choose where to eat. Decision-makers are considering whether to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by market economic system. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A market economic system relies mainly on private ownership and price signals to allocate resources.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Independent firms compete to supply restaurants while customers choose where to eat.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0020","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Several cafés add plant-based meals after sustained customer demand. Decision-makers are considering whether to let customer demand guide café menu decisions. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by consumer sovereignty. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Consumer sovereignty means buying decisions influence what firms offer for sale.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Several cafés add plant-based meals after sustained customer demand.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0021","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A coastal lighthouse guides all nearby ships without preventing others from using its signal. Decision-makers are considering whether to finance a public lighthouse through taxation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by public goods. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Public goods are non-excludable and non-rival in consumption.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A coastal lighthouse guides all nearby ships without preventing others from using its signal.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0022","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others. Decision-makers are considering whether to subsidise childhood vaccination services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by merit goods. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0023","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A paint factory discharges waste into a river used by downstream fishers. Decision-makers are considering whether to require the paint factory to treat wastewater. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by negative external costs. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Negative external costs are harmful effects of production or consumption imposed on third parties.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A paint factory discharges waste into a river used by downstream fishers.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0024","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Private shops sell food while the government operates local public hospitals. Decision-makers are considering whether to combine private retail markets with public health provision. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by mixed economic system. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A mixed economic system combines market allocation with government intervention and public provision.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Private shops sell food while the government operates local public hospitals.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0025","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A government adds a per-unit tax to single-use plastic packaging. Decision-makers are considering whether to tax single-use plastic packaging. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by indirect tax. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — An indirect tax is a tax on spending on goods and services.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A government adds a per-unit tax to single-use plastic packaging.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0001","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Independent fruit growers meet customers at a weekly neighbourhood market. Decision-makers are considering whether to create a digital trading platform for local growers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to create a digital trading platform for local growers. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Growers could reach more potential customers. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Buyers could compare products and prices more easily. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0002","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A bakery notices customers buying more wholegrain bread than white bread. Decision-makers are considering whether to use customer demand information to plan bread output. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to use customer demand information to plan bread output might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: The bakery may reduce unsold stock. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Output may better match what customers want. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0003","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Several farmers offer tomatoes at a wholesale market each morning. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Competition may increase the quantity offered. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Buyers may benefit from greater choice. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0004","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A shop records how many bicycles customers would buy at each possible selling price. Decision-makers are considering whether to survey local demand before ordering bicycles. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers survey local demand before ordering bicycles. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Ordering decisions may match expected sales. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: The retailer may avoid spending on unwanted stock. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0005","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Cinema ticket sales rise when the cinema lowers its ticket price. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce cinema ticket prices on quiet weekdays. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to reduce cinema ticket prices on quiet weekdays could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: More customers may buy tickets. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Higher attendance could increase sales of refreshments. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0006","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Demand for electric bicycles rises after more cycling lanes are built. Decision-makers are considering whether to build safer cycle routes to encourage bicycle use. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to build safer cycle routes to encourage bicycle use. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: More people may purchase bicycles. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Reduced car travel may lower local pollution. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0007","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A tomato producer lists quantities offered for sale at a series of prices. Decision-makers are considering whether to plan output using a detailed supply schedule. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to plan output using a detailed supply schedule might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: The farm may identify profitable production levels. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Production can be aligned more closely with expected market prices. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0008","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Farm revenue may increase if extra output sells. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: More seasonal jobs may be offered. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0009","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce energy-saving ovens in bakeries. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers introduce energy-saving ovens in bakeries. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Lower costs may support lower bread prices. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Higher productive efficiency may increase output. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0010","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200. Decision-makers are considering whether to adjust umbrella orders towards the equilibrium quantity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to adjust umbrella orders towards the equilibrium quantity could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Unsold stock could be reduced. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Customers may be less likely to encounter shortages. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0011","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280. Decision-makers are considering whether to permit prices to respond to a temporary bread shortage. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to permit prices to respond to a temporary bread shortage. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Higher prices may encourage additional supply. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Some consumers may reduce demand, easing the shortage. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0012","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"At $20 a pair, a shop offers 180 shoes but customers buy only 110. Decision-makers are considering whether to discount shoes when inventories are excessive. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to discount shoes when inventories are excessive might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Lower prices could attract more buyers. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Unsold inventory and storage costs may fall. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0013","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A popular sports event increases demand for nearby hotel rooms. Decision-makers are considering whether to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Some hotels can fund future capacity investment. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0014","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market. Decision-makers are considering whether to support more efficient apple cultivation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers support more efficient apple cultivation. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Consumers may pay less for apples. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Greater output may make fruit more accessible. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0015","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand delivery-kitchen capacity as demand grows. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to expand delivery-kitchen capacity as demand grows could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Higher sales volumes may be served. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Extra competition may limit price increases. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0016","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%. Decision-makers are considering whether to calculate PED before changing gym membership fees. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to calculate PED before changing gym membership fees. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Managers may estimate how sales volume will change. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Better forecasts may guide pricing and revenue decisions. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0017","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A bus operator raises fares by 10% while passenger journeys fall by 3%. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Extra revenue could fund service improvements. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0018","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A medicine with few substitutes has less price-sensitive buyers than an optional entertainment service. Decision-makers are considering whether to consider substitute availability when pricing products. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to consider substitute availability when pricing products. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Price decisions may better reflect consumer sensitivity. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0019","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in flexible production capacity to respond to prices. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers invest in flexible production capacity to respond to prices. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: The firm may expand output when prices rise. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Flexibility can help meet sudden increases in demand. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0020","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"The price of fresh strawberries rises rapidly but the harvest cannot increase for several months. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve cold storage for a crop with inelastic short-run supply. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to improve cold storage for a crop with inelastic short-run supply could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Less spoilage may increase the quantity available for purchase. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0021","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity. Decision-makers are considering whether to maintain some flexible spare capacity at a factory. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to maintain some flexible spare capacity at a factory. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Orders may be met without a long delay. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: The firm may gain sales during sudden demand increases. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0022","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Independent firms compete to supply restaurants while customers choose where to eat. Decision-makers are considering whether to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Consumer preferences can affect business decisions. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0023","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Several cafés add plant-based meals after sustained customer demand. Decision-makers are considering whether to let customer demand guide café menu decisions. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to let customer demand guide café menu decisions. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Less unsold food may be wasted. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0024","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding. Decision-makers are considering whether to supplement market housing with targeted public housing. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers supplement market housing with targeted public housing. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Poor housing conditions may become less widespread. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0025","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A coastal lighthouse guides all nearby ships without preventing others from using its signal. Decision-makers are considering whether to finance a public lighthouse through taxation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to finance a public lighthouse through taxation could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: The service can be provided even when individual users cannot be charged. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0026","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others. Decision-makers are considering whether to subsidise childhood vaccination services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to subsidise childhood vaccination services. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: More people may receive protection from disease. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Lower transmission may benefit the wider community. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0027","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A paint factory discharges waste into a river used by downstream fishers. Decision-makers are considering whether to require the paint factory to treat wastewater. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to require the paint factory to treat wastewater might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: River pollution may fall. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Fishing livelihoods and health may improve. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0028","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Private shops sell food while the government operates local public hospitals. Decision-makers are considering whether to combine private retail markets with public health provision. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to combine private retail markets with public health provision. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Competition may improve retail efficiency. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Public hospitals may improve access for low-income households. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0029","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A government adds a per-unit tax to single-use plastic packaging. Decision-makers are considering whether to tax single-use plastic packaging. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers tax single-use plastic packaging. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Higher prices may discourage plastic use. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Tax revenue could fund waste management. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0030","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A government pays bus operators for each low-emission journey provided. Decision-makers are considering whether to subsidise low-emission public transport. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to subsidise low-emission public transport could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Fares may fall and ridership may rise. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Reduced car use can lower congestion and pollution. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0001","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Independent fruit growers meet customers at a weekly neighbourhood market. Decision-makers are considering whether to create a digital trading platform for local growers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to create a digital trading platform for local growers. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Growers could reach more potential customers. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Buyers could compare products and prices more easily. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0002","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A bakery notices customers buying more wholegrain bread than white bread. Decision-makers are considering whether to use customer demand information to plan bread output. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to use customer demand information to plan bread output may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: The bakery may reduce unsold stock. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Output may better match what customers want. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Changes in purchases signal which goods consumers value at prevailing prices. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0003","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Several farmers offer tomatoes at a wholesale market each morning. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Competition may increase the quantity offered. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Buyers may benefit from greater choice. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Sellers' responses to prices help allocate resources across products. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0004","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A shop records how many bicycles customers would buy at each possible selling price. Decision-makers are considering whether to survey local demand before ordering bicycles. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to survey local demand before ordering bicycles in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Ordering decisions may match expected sales. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: The retailer may avoid spending on unwanted stock. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0005","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Cinema ticket sales rise when the cinema lowers its ticket price. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce cinema ticket prices on quiet weekdays. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to reduce cinema ticket prices on quiet weekdays could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: More customers may buy tickets. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Higher attendance could increase sales of refreshments. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0006","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Demand for electric bicycles rises after more cycling lanes are built. Decision-makers are considering whether to build safer cycle routes to encourage bicycle use. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to build safer cycle routes to encourage bicycle use. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: More people may purchase bicycles. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Reduced car travel may lower local pollution. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0007","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A tomato producer lists quantities offered for sale at a series of prices. Decision-makers are considering whether to plan output using a detailed supply schedule. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to plan output using a detailed supply schedule may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: The farm may identify profitable production levels. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Production can be aligned more closely with expected market prices. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0008","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Farm revenue may increase if extra output sells. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: More seasonal jobs may be offered. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: A higher selling price can justify output from more expensive production methods. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0009","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce energy-saving ovens in bakeries. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to introduce energy-saving ovens in bakeries in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Lower costs may support lower bread prices. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Higher productive efficiency may increase output. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0010","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200. Decision-makers are considering whether to adjust umbrella orders towards the equilibrium quantity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to adjust umbrella orders towards the equilibrium quantity could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Unsold stock could be reduced. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Customers may be less likely to encounter shortages. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0011","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280. Decision-makers are considering whether to permit prices to respond to a temporary bread shortage. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to permit prices to respond to a temporary bread shortage. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Higher prices may encourage additional supply. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Some consumers may reduce demand, easing the shortage. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0012","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"At $20 a pair, a shop offers 180 shoes but customers buy only 110. Decision-makers are considering whether to discount shoes when inventories are excessive. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to discount shoes when inventories are excessive may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Lower prices could attract more buyers. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Unsold inventory and storage costs may fall. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Excess supply can put downward pressure on price and encourage stock clearance. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0013","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A popular sports event increases demand for nearby hotel rooms. Decision-makers are considering whether to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Some hotels can fund future capacity investment. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The demand curve shifts right, creating upward price pressure at the original price. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0014","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market. Decision-makers are considering whether to support more efficient apple cultivation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to support more efficient apple cultivation in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Consumers may pay less for apples. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Greater output may make fruit more accessible. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The supply curve shifts right and produces excess supply at the old price. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0015","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand delivery-kitchen capacity as demand grows. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to expand delivery-kitchen capacity as demand grows could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Higher sales volumes may be served. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Extra competition may limit price increases. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0016","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%. Decision-makers are considering whether to calculate PED before changing gym membership fees. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to calculate PED before changing gym membership fees. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Managers may estimate how sales volume will change. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Better forecasts may guide pricing and revenue decisions. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The magnitude of PED is 2, indicating elastic demand. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0017","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A bus operator raises fares by 10% while passenger journeys fall by 3%. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Extra revenue could fund service improvements. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The PED magnitude is 0.3; quantity responds proportionately less than price. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0018","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A medicine with few substitutes has less price-sensitive buyers than an optional entertainment service. Decision-makers are considering whether to consider substitute availability when pricing products. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to consider substitute availability when pricing products could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Price decisions may better reflect consumer sensitivity. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Consumers can switch more easily when close substitutes are available. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0019","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in flexible production capacity to respond to prices. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to invest in flexible production capacity to respond to prices in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: The firm may expand output when prices rise. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Flexibility can help meet sudden increases in demand. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The PES value is 2, indicating supply is price elastic. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0020","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"The price of fresh strawberries rises rapidly but the harvest cannot increase for several months. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve cold storage for a crop with inelastic short-run supply. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to improve cold storage for a crop with inelastic short-run supply could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Less spoilage may increase the quantity available for purchase. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0021","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity. Decision-makers are considering whether to maintain some flexible spare capacity at a factory. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to maintain some flexible spare capacity at a factory. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Orders may be met without a long delay. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: The firm may gain sales during sudden demand increases. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0022","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Independent firms compete to supply restaurants while customers choose where to eat. Decision-makers are considering whether to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Consumer preferences can affect business decisions. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Profit and consumer choices influence the goods firms produce. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0023","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Several cafés add plant-based meals after sustained customer demand. Decision-makers are considering whether to let customer demand guide café menu decisions. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to let customer demand guide café menu decisions could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Less unsold food may be wasted. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0024","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding. Decision-makers are considering whether to supplement market housing with targeted public housing. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to supplement market housing with targeted public housing in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Poor housing conditions may become less widespread. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Price signals alone may not achieve fair distribution or account for social welfare. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0025","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A coastal lighthouse guides all nearby ships without preventing others from using its signal. Decision-makers are considering whether to finance a public lighthouse through taxation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to finance a public lighthouse through taxation could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: The service can be provided even when individual users cannot be charged. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0026","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others. Decision-makers are considering whether to subsidise childhood vaccination services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to subsidise childhood vaccination services. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: More people may receive protection from disease. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Lower transmission may benefit the wider community. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0027","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A paint factory discharges waste into a river used by downstream fishers. Decision-makers are considering whether to require the paint factory to treat wastewater. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to require the paint factory to treat wastewater may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: River pollution may fall. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Fishing livelihoods and health may improve. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0028","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Private shops sell food while the government operates local public hospitals. Decision-makers are considering whether to combine private retail markets with public health provision. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to combine private retail markets with public health provision could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Competition may improve retail efficiency. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Public hospitals may improve access for low-income households. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The combination aims to retain market incentives while addressing some market failures. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0029","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A government adds a per-unit tax to single-use plastic packaging. Decision-makers are considering whether to tax single-use plastic packaging. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to tax single-use plastic packaging in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Higher prices may discourage plastic use. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Tax revenue could fund waste management. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The tax raises producers' costs and tends to raise consumer prices while reducing sales. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0030","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A government pays bus operators for each low-emission journey provided. Decision-makers are considering whether to subsidise low-emission public transport. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to subsidise low-emission public transport could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Fares may fall and ridership may rise. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Reduced car use can lower congestion and pollution. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Lower costs can shift supply right and make the service cheaper or more available. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0001","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Independent fruit growers meet customers at a weekly neighbourhood market. Decision-makers are considering whether to create a digital trading platform for local growers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to create a digital trading platform for local growers would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Growers could reach more potential customers.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Buyers could compare products and prices more easily.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Some growers may not have reliable internet access.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Platform fees might increase sellers' costs.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0002","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A bakery notices customers buying more wholegrain bread than white bread. Decision-makers are considering whether to use customer demand information to plan bread output. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to use customer demand information to plan bread output in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): The bakery may reduce unsold stock.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Output may better match what customers want.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Demand could change before new production is ready.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Past purchasing patterns may not predict future preferences.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0003","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Several farmers offer tomatoes at a wholesale market each morning. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Competition may increase the quantity offered.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Buyers may benefit from greater choice.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Farmers may face lower prices and profits.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Entry may remain difficult if transport costs are high.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0004","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A shop records how many bicycles customers would buy at each possible selling price. Decision-makers are considering whether to survey local demand before ordering bicycles. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to survey local demand before ordering bicycles is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Ordering decisions may match expected sales.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): The retailer may avoid spending on unwanted stock.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Survey answers may differ from actual purchases.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Unexpected income changes could alter demand.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0005","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Cinema ticket sales rise when the cinema lowers its ticket price. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce cinema ticket prices on quiet weekdays. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to reduce cinema ticket prices on quiet weekdays would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): More customers may buy tickets.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Higher attendance could increase sales of refreshments.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Revenue from each ticket will be lower.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): The increase in ticket sales may be small if demand is inelastic.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0006","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Demand for electric bicycles rises after more cycling lanes are built. Decision-makers are considering whether to build safer cycle routes to encourage bicycle use. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to build safer cycle routes to encourage bicycle use would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): More people may purchase bicycles.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Reduced car travel may lower local pollution.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Cycle routes use public funds and road space.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Weather or safety concerns may still limit cycling.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0007","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A tomato producer lists quantities offered for sale at a series of prices. Decision-makers are considering whether to plan output using a detailed supply schedule. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to plan output using a detailed supply schedule would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): The farm may identify profitable production levels.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Production can be aligned more closely with expected market prices.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Weather may make planned supply impossible.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Input prices may change after plans are made.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0008","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Farm revenue may increase if extra output sells.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): More seasonal jobs may be offered.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Extra harvesting can raise marginal production costs.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Prices might fall before the extra crop reaches buyers.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0009","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce energy-saving ovens in bakeries. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to introduce energy-saving ovens in bakeries. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Lower costs may support lower bread prices.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Higher productive efficiency may increase output.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): New ovens require investment.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Savings depend on electricity use and machine reliability.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0010","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200. Decision-makers are considering whether to adjust umbrella orders towards the equilibrium quantity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to adjust umbrella orders towards the equilibrium quantity is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Unsold stock could be reduced.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Customers may be less likely to encounter shortages.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Demand may change with the weather.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): The equilibrium estimate may use inaccurate data.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0011","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280. Decision-makers are considering whether to permit prices to respond to a temporary bread shortage. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to permit prices to respond to a temporary bread shortage would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Higher prices may encourage additional supply.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Some consumers may reduce demand, easing the shortage.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Low-income households may find bread less affordable.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Production may not expand quickly enough to remove the shortage.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0012","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"At $20 a pair, a shop offers 180 shoes but customers buy only 110. Decision-makers are considering whether to discount shoes when inventories are excessive. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to discount shoes when inventories are excessive would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Lower prices could attract more buyers.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Unsold inventory and storage costs may fall.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Revenue per pair of shoes will decline.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): The discount might still fail to clear unpopular designs.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0013","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A popular sports event increases demand for nearby hotel rooms. Decision-makers are considering whether to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Some hotels can fund future capacity investment.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Visitors may face higher accommodation costs.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Supply may be fixed in the very short run.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0014","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market. Decision-makers are considering whether to support more efficient apple cultivation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to support more efficient apple cultivation in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Consumers may pay less for apples.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Greater output may make fruit more accessible.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Farmers may receive a lower price per kilogram.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Harvest output depends on weather and storage capacity.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0015","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand delivery-kitchen capacity as demand grows. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to expand delivery-kitchen capacity as demand grows. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Higher sales volumes may be served.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Extra competition may limit price increases.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Investment could be wasted if demand growth slows.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): The direction of price change is uncertain without shift magnitudes.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0016","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%. Decision-makers are considering whether to calculate PED before changing gym membership fees. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to calculate PED before changing gym membership fees is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Managers may estimate how sales volume will change.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Better forecasts may guide pricing and revenue decisions.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Past responsiveness may not hold after competitors change prices.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): PED estimates can be unreliable with limited sales data.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0017","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A bus operator raises fares by 10% while passenger journeys fall by 3%. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Extra revenue could fund service improvements.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Higher fares impose a greater burden on regular passengers.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Demand may become more elastic over a longer time period.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0018","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A medicine with few substitutes has less price-sensitive buyers than an optional entertainment service. Decision-makers are considering whether to consider substitute availability when pricing products. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to consider substitute availability when pricing products would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Price decisions may better reflect consumer sensitivity.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Competitors can introduce new substitutes unexpectedly.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Some buyers lack information about alternative products.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0019","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in flexible production capacity to respond to prices. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to invest in flexible production capacity to respond to prices would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): The firm may expand output when prices rise.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Flexibility can help meet sudden increases in demand.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Spare capacity is costly to maintain.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Specialist inputs may still limit rapid expansion.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0020","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"The price of fresh strawberries rises rapidly but the harvest cannot increase for several months. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve cold storage for a crop with inelastic short-run supply. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to improve cold storage for a crop with inelastic short-run supply in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Less spoilage may increase the quantity available for purchase.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Cold storage has high energy and capital costs.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): It does not immediately increase the harvested quantity.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0021","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity. Decision-makers are considering whether to maintain some flexible spare capacity at a factory. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to maintain some flexible spare capacity at a factory. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Orders may be met without a long delay.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): The firm may gain sales during sudden demand increases.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Idle machines still require maintenance and space.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0022","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Independent firms compete to supply restaurants while customers choose where to eat. Decision-makers are considering whether to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Consumer preferences can affect business decisions.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Some consumers may be unable to afford essential services.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Pollution and other external costs may be ignored.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0023","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Several cafés add plant-based meals after sustained customer demand. Decision-makers are considering whether to let customer demand guide café menu decisions. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to let customer demand guide café menu decisions would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Less unsold food may be wasted.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Low-income consumers exert less influence because purchasing power differs.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Businesses may promote harmful products despite consumer demand.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0024","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding. Decision-makers are considering whether to supplement market housing with targeted public housing. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to supplement market housing with targeted public housing would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Poor housing conditions may become less widespread.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Public housing programmes require tax funding.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Weak targeting may benefit households that are not most in need.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0025","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A coastal lighthouse guides all nearby ships without preventing others from using its signal. Decision-makers are considering whether to finance a public lighthouse through taxation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to finance a public lighthouse through taxation would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): The service can be provided even when individual users cannot be charged.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Tax finance has an opportunity cost for other services.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Operating and maintenance costs may be high.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0026","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others. Decision-makers are considering whether to subsidise childhood vaccination services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to subsidise childhood vaccination services in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): More people may receive protection from disease.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Lower transmission may benefit the wider community.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Subsidies require public funding.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Poor access or misinformation may still reduce take-up.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0027","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A paint factory discharges waste into a river used by downstream fishers. Decision-makers are considering whether to require the paint factory to treat wastewater. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to require the paint factory to treat wastewater. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): River pollution may fall.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Fishing livelihoods and health may improve.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Treatment raises operating costs for the factory.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Weak enforcement may make regulation ineffective.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0028","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Private shops sell food while the government operates local public hospitals. Decision-makers are considering whether to combine private retail markets with public health provision. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to combine private retail markets with public health provision is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Competition may improve retail efficiency.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Public hospitals may improve access for low-income households.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): State services require sufficient tax revenue.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Public and private responsibilities may be poorly coordinated.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0029","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A government adds a per-unit tax to single-use plastic packaging. Decision-makers are considering whether to tax single-use plastic packaging. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to tax single-use plastic packaging would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Higher prices may discourage plastic use.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Tax revenue could fund waste management.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Consumers may face higher prices.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Demand may be too inelastic for usage to fall significantly.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0030","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A government pays bus operators for each low-emission journey provided. Decision-makers are considering whether to subsidise low-emission public transport. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to subsidise low-emission public transport would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Fares may fall and ridership may rise.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Reduced car use can lower congestion and pollution.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): The subsidy has a cost to taxpayers.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Poor oversight may reward inefficient operators.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0001","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes money as a medium of exchange?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A horizontal merger joins businesses operating at the same stage in the same industry.","Money serves as a commonly accepted means of payment that reduces the need for barter.","The supply of labour is the number of workers willing and able to work at different wage rates.","Average total cost equals total cost divided by units produced."],"answer":1,"why":"Money as a medium of exchange: Money serves as a commonly accepted means of payment that reduces the need for barter."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0002","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Average total cost","Money as a medium of exchange","Secondary-sector firm","Characteristics of money"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates money as a medium of exchange: Money serves as a commonly accepted means of payment that reduces the need for barter."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0003","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of money as a medium of exchange?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A sandwich shop uses more bread and filling when it makes more sandwiches.","A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue.","A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times.","A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table."],"answer":3,"why":"A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table. This illustrates money serves as a commonly accepted means of payment that reduces the need for barter."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0004","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to money as a medium of exchange?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","A common payment medium makes trade easier because a double coincidence of wants is unnecessary.","A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs.","Manufacturing adds value by transforming inputs into finished goods."],"answer":1,"why":"A common payment medium makes trade easier because a double coincidence of wants is unnecessary."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0005","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 50 units for $6 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$56","$306","$300","$50"],"answer":2,"why":"Total revenue = $6 × 50 = $300."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0006","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage reliable digital payment services. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Digital exclusion may affect people without smartphones.","Local managers may respond more quickly to customers.","Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit.","Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges."],"answer":0,"why":"Digital exclusion may affect people without smartphones."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0007","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to encourage reliable digital payment services?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["World timber prices may make income unstable.","Automation may reduce some production jobs.","Small businesses may reach customers who do not carry cash.","Breakdowns can stop a large share of production."],"answer":2,"why":"Small businesses may reach customers who do not carry cash."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0008","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to encourage reliable digital payment services less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Payment systems may fail during electricity or network outages.","Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination.","The shop may improve profitability at unchanged prices.","Service standards may improve through enforceable requirements."],"answer":0,"why":"Payment systems may fail during electricity or network outages."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0009","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to encourage reliable digital payment services. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More transactions may be completed quickly.","The restaurant may serve more meals.","Households may obtain useful equipment sooner.","Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts."],"answer":0,"why":"More transactions may be completed quickly."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0010","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A common payment medium makes trade easier because a double coincidence of wants is unnecessary.","Labour-intensive output may suit small production runs and places with relatively low wages.","A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs.","Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption."],"answer":0,"why":"A common payment medium makes trade easier because a double coincidence of wants is unnecessary."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0011","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes money as a store of value?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Money can retain purchasing power for use in later transactions.","A fixed cost does not vary with output over the relevant short-run period.","Capital-intensive production uses a relatively high proportion of machinery and equipment.","Higher interest rates generally increase rewards for saving and costs of borrowing."],"answer":0,"why":"Money as a store of value: Money can retain purchasing power for use in later transactions."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0012","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A worker saves part of a monthly salary to buy furniture next year. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Consumer confidence","Money as a store of value","Economies of scale","Commercial bank"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates money as a store of value: Money can retain purchasing power for use in later transactions."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0013","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 70 loaves per day with 7 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["70 loaves","7 loaves","10 loaves","17 loaves"],"answer":2,"why":"Labour productivity = 70 / 7 = 10 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0014","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to money as a store of value?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable.","Lack of direct competition may allow higher prices or less choice.","Non-financial benefits can compensate for some differences in wages.","Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it."],"answer":3,"why":"Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0015","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to promote safe and accessible savings facilities. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Households may be better prepared for emergencies.","Small firms may struggle to earn enough profit.","Employers may reduce hiring if labour costs rise sharply.","More transport can increase congestion and emissions."],"answer":0,"why":"Households may be better prepared for emergencies."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0016","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to promote safe and accessible savings facilities. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Households may obtain useful equipment sooner.","Inflation may reduce the real value of cash savings.","Additional sales could increase total revenue.","The business may have lower total costs at every output level."],"answer":1,"why":"Inflation may reduce the real value of cash savings."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0017","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to promote safe and accessible savings facilities?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Low pay may still push qualified teachers elsewhere.","Families on low incomes may be unable to save much.","Borrowers may struggle if their income falls.","Savings can finance larger future purchases."],"answer":3,"why":"Savings can finance larger future purchases."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0018","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to promote safe and accessible savings facilities less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Faster production can allow more sales.","Low-income families may have little income left to save.","Service standards may improve through enforceable requirements.","Happier staff may provide more consistent service."],"answer":1,"why":"Low-income families may have little income left to save."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0019","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to promote safe and accessible savings facilities. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood.","Households may be better prepared for emergencies.","Savers may earn more income from deposits.","Products can be customised for individual buyers."],"answer":1,"why":"Households may be better prepared for emergencies."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0020","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A worker saves part of a monthly salary to buy furniture next year. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it.","Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions.","Poor communication and management can increase cost per unit as size expands."],"answer":0,"why":"Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0021","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 50 items with a total cost of $500. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$10","$500","$15","$60"],"answer":0,"why":"Average total cost = $500 / 50 = $10 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0022","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country's central bank changes the policy interest rate after inflation rises. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Competitive market","Division of labour","Central bank","Average total cost"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates central bank: A central bank oversees monetary conditions and usually issues currency and supports banking-system stability."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0023","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of central bank?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts.","A logistics company transports manufactured goods to stores.","A country's central bank changes the policy interest rate after inflation rises.","A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times."],"answer":2,"why":"A country's central bank changes the policy interest rate after inflation rises. This illustrates a central bank oversees monetary conditions and usually issues currency and supports banking-system stability."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0024","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to central bank?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Fixed costs must be covered even when output falls.","Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability.","Repeated specialist tasks can raise speed but may reduce flexibility.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions."],"answer":1,"why":"Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0025","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Overharvesting may damage forests and biodiversity.","Output beyond efficient capacity may raise costs.","Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand.","Quality may suffer if portions become too small."],"answer":2,"why":"Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0026","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The business may have lower total costs at every output level.","More competition may lower prices.","Higher borrowing costs can discourage investment.","The restaurant may serve more meals."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher borrowing costs can discourage investment."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0027","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Slower price increases can protect purchasing power.","Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment.","Some locations may not support many viable businesses.","Graduates may migrate to higher-paying countries."],"answer":0,"why":"Slower price increases can protect purchasing power."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0028","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The shop may improve profitability at unchanged prices.","Reduced workloads may improve service quality.","Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination.","Policy effects may take time and depend on borrower responses."],"answer":3,"why":"Policy effects may take time and depend on borrower responses."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0029","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices.","Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand.","Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges.","Firms may deliver goods more reliably."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0030","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 80 units for $9 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$89","$720","$729","$80"],"answer":1,"why":"Total revenue = $9 × 80 = $720."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0031","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes commercial bank?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A monopoly market has one supplier of a good or service with no close rival.","Capital-intensive production uses a relatively high proportion of machinery and equipment.","A commercial bank accepts deposits, processes payments and provides loans to households and businesses.","Money can retain purchasing power for use in later transactions."],"answer":2,"why":"Commercial bank: A commercial bank accepts deposits, processes payments and provides loans to households and businesses."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0032","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Supply of labour","Variable cost","Fixed cost","Commercial bank"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates commercial bank: A commercial bank accepts deposits, processes payments and provides loans to households and businesses."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0033","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of commercial bank?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A logistics company transports manufactured goods to stores.","A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles.","A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment.","A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue."],"answer":1,"why":"A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles. This illustrates a commercial bank accepts deposits, processes payments and provides loans to households and businesses."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0034","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to commercial bank?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","Services such as transport and retail help goods reach consumers.","Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production.","Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation."],"answer":2,"why":"Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0035","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand responsible lending to small businesses. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Output beyond efficient capacity may raise costs.","Investment may support more production and jobs.","Wage costs rise with the number of staff.","Consumers may face less choice or higher prices."],"answer":1,"why":"Investment may support more production and jobs."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0036","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand responsible lending to small businesses. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Local managers may respond more quickly to customers.","Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment.","Better conditions may improve staff retention.","Borrowers may default and create bank losses."],"answer":3,"why":"Borrowers may default and create bank losses."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0037","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand responsible lending to small businesses?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Breakdowns can stop a large share of production.","Entrepreneurs may obtain funds unavailable from their own savings.","Excessive confidence could encourage unsustainable debt.","Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns."],"answer":1,"why":"Entrepreneurs may obtain funds unavailable from their own savings."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0038","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 130 loaves per day with 10 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["23 loaves","130 loaves","13 loaves","10 loaves"],"answer":2,"why":"Labour productivity = 130 / 10 = 13 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0039","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand responsible lending to small businesses. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts.","Products can be customised for individual buyers.","Investment may support more production and jobs.","Output per employee may rise."],"answer":2,"why":"Investment may support more production and jobs."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0040","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption.","More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending.","Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production.","More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost."],"answer":2,"why":"Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0041","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes characteristics of money?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Effective money is durable, portable, divisible, recognisable and generally accepted.","Saving is the part of disposable income not spent on current consumption.","The supply of labour is the number of workers willing and able to work at different wage rates.","A competitive market has numerous firms exerting pressure on price, quality and choice."],"answer":0,"why":"Characteristics of money: Effective money is durable, portable, divisible, recognisable and generally accepted."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0042","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Household borrowing","Demand for labour","Characteristics of money","Household saving"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates characteristics of money: Effective money is durable, portable, divisible, recognisable and generally accepted."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0043","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of characteristics of money?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport.","A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts.","A household increases monthly deposits after receiving a pay rise.","A family uses a bank loan to buy a refrigerator rather than waiting two years."],"answer":0,"why":"Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport. This illustrates effective money is durable, portable, divisible, recognisable and generally accepted."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0044","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to characteristics of money?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit.","Poor communication and management can increase cost per unit as size expands.","Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange.","Non-financial benefits can compensate for some differences in wages."],"answer":2,"why":"Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0045","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to replace easily counterfeited notes with secure durable currency. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Expanding requires capital and management capacity.","Traders may become more confident accepting payment.","Initial investment can be very expensive.","Unit costs can be high if wages rise."],"answer":1,"why":"Traders may become more confident accepting payment."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0046","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 80 items with a total cost of $1,040. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$18","$13","$1,040","$93"],"answer":1,"why":"Average total cost = $1,040 / 80 = $13 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0047","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to replace easily counterfeited notes with secure durable currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Fuel-price increases may make services expensive.","Fewer damaged notes could reduce replacement costs.","A missing specialist can interrupt the whole production line.","Graduates may migrate to higher-paying countries."],"answer":1,"why":"Fewer damaged notes could reduce replacement costs."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0048","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to replace easily counterfeited notes with secure durable currency less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Counterfeiters may eventually develop new methods.","Happier staff may provide more consistent service.","Shorter task changes may reduce wasted time.","Faster production can allow more sales."],"answer":0,"why":"Counterfeiters may eventually develop new methods."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0049","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to replace easily counterfeited notes with secure durable currency. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood.","Traders may become more confident accepting payment.","Savers may earn more income from deposits.","More competition may lower prices."],"answer":1,"why":"Traders may become more confident accepting payment."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0050","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs.","Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange.","Lack of direct competition may allow higher prices or less choice.","Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold."],"answer":1,"why":"Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0051","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes household saving?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Money serves as a commonly accepted means of payment that reduces the need for barter.","Money can retain purchasing power for use in later transactions.","Saving is the part of disposable income not spent on current consumption.","People consider conditions, training, security, location and satisfaction as well as pay when choosing occupations."],"answer":2,"why":"Household saving: Saving is the part of disposable income not spent on current consumption."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0052","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A household increases monthly deposits after receiving a pay rise. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Commercial bank","Fixed cost","Capital-intensive production","Household saving"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates household saving: Saving is the part of disposable income not spent on current consumption."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0053","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of household saving?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A country's central bank changes the policy interest rate after inflation rises.","In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles.","A handmade textile producer hires artisans instead of buying automated weaving machines.","A household increases monthly deposits after receiving a pay rise."],"answer":3,"why":"A household increases monthly deposits after receiving a pay rise. This illustrates saving is the part of disposable income not spent on current consumption."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0054","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to household saving?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","Non-financial benefits can compensate for some differences in wages.","Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange.","A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0055","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 110 units for $12 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$122","$1,320","$1,332","$110"],"answer":1,"why":"Total revenue = $12 × 110 = $1,320."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0056","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage households to maintain emergency savings. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The restaurant may serve more meals.","Less spending may reduce current demand for local businesses.","Variable cost per sandwich may fall.","Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood."],"answer":1,"why":"Less spending may reduce current demand for local businesses."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0057","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to encourage households to maintain emergency savings?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment.","World timber prices may make income unstable.","Some locations may not support many viable businesses.","Having reserves may reduce reliance on expensive loans."],"answer":3,"why":"Having reserves may reduce reliance on expensive loans."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0058","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to encourage households to maintain emergency savings less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More consistent products may attract buyers.","Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination.","More customers might make later repeat purchases.","Families on low incomes may be unable to save much."],"answer":3,"why":"Families on low incomes may be unable to save much."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0059","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to encourage households to maintain emergency savings. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More competition may lower prices.","Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices.","Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment.","Local managers may respond more quickly to customers."],"answer":2,"why":"Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0060","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A household increases monthly deposits after receiving a pay rise. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Machines can raise output per worker when production is large and standardised.","Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable.","Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers.","Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0061","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes household borrowing?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher interest rates generally increase rewards for saving and costs of borrowing.","Borrowing enables expenditure now in exchange for future repayments, often including interest.","A variable cost changes as the quantity of output changes.","Capital-intensive production uses a relatively high proportion of machinery and equipment."],"answer":1,"why":"Household borrowing: Borrowing enables expenditure now in exchange for future repayments, often including interest."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0062","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A family uses a bank loan to buy a refrigerator rather than waiting two years. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Monopoly market","Household borrowing","Competitive market","Labour-intensive production"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates household borrowing: Borrowing enables expenditure now in exchange for future repayments, often including interest."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0063","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 208 loaves per day with 13 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["208 loaves","13 loaves","16 loaves","29 loaves"],"answer":2,"why":"Labour productivity = 208 / 13 = 16 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0064","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to household borrowing?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Fixed costs must be covered even when output falls.","Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it.","Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation.","Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability."],"answer":2,"why":"Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0065","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Households may obtain useful equipment sooner.","Wage costs rise with the number of staff.","Borrowers may default and create bank losses.","Small firms may struggle to earn enough profit."],"answer":0,"why":"Households may obtain useful equipment sooner."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0066","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Debt repayments reduce future disposable income.","Traders may become more confident accepting payment.","Firms may deliver goods more reliably.","The firm may meet orders faster."],"answer":0,"why":"Debt repayments reduce future disposable income."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0067","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The firm may face insufficient demand for the larger output.","Purchases can support demand for local suppliers.","Some locations may not support many viable businesses.","Extra lamps may be difficult to sell."],"answer":1,"why":"Purchases can support demand for local suppliers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0068","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Staff shortages and waiting times may decline.","Lower fixed costs may help survival during weak demand.","Fewer damaged notes could reduce replacement costs.","Borrowers may struggle if their income falls."],"answer":3,"why":"Borrowers may struggle if their income falls."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0069","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More competition may lower prices.","Households may obtain useful equipment sooner.","Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand.","Products can be customised for individual buyers."],"answer":1,"why":"Households may obtain useful equipment sooner."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0070","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A family uses a bank loan to buy a refrigerator rather than waiting two years. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it.","Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation.","Total variable costs rise when additional units require more materials.","A common payment medium makes trade easier because a double coincidence of wants is unnecessary."],"answer":1,"why":"Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0071","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 110 items with a total cost of $1,760. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$16","$21","$126","$1,760"],"answer":0,"why":"Average total cost = $1,760 / 110 = $16 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0072","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Following an interest-rate rise, a household postpones replacing its car. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["National minimum wage","Primary-sector firm","Total revenue","Interest rates and households"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates interest rates and households: Higher interest rates generally increase rewards for saving and costs of borrowing."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0073","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of interest rates and households?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Following an interest-rate rise, a household postpones replacing its car.","Many independent cafés compete for customers along a busy street.","A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment.","A growing retailer suffers coordination problems between many regional branches."],"answer":0,"why":"Following an interest-rate rise, a household postpones replacing its car. This illustrates higher interest rates generally increase rewards for saving and costs of borrowing."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0074","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to interest rates and households?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A common payment medium makes trade easier because a double coincidence of wants is unnecessary.","Poor communication and management can increase cost per unit as size expands.","Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production.","More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending."],"answer":3,"why":"More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0075","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Savers may earn more income from deposits.","Initial investment can be very expensive.","Quality may suffer if portions become too small.","Overharvesting may damage forests and biodiversity."],"answer":0,"why":"Savers may earn more income from deposits."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0076","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Traders may become more confident accepting payment.","Output per employee may rise.","Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand.","Borrowers may face higher repayments at the same time."],"answer":3,"why":"Borrowers may face higher repayments at the same time."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0077","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Households may build stronger financial buffers.","Breakdowns can stop a large share of production.","Fuel-price increases may make services expensive.","Counterfeiters may eventually develop new methods."],"answer":0,"why":"Households may build stronger financial buffers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0078","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination.","Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns.","More customers might make later repeat purchases.","Higher pay could reduce in-work poverty."],"answer":1,"why":"Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0079","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Local managers may respond more quickly to customers.","Households may be better prepared for emergencies.","More competition may lower prices.","Savers may earn more income from deposits."],"answer":3,"why":"Savers may earn more income from deposits."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0080","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 50 units for $15 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$765","$750","$65","$50"],"answer":1,"why":"Total revenue = $15 × 50 = $750."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0081","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes consumer confidence?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A commercial bank accepts deposits, processes payments and provides loans to households and businesses.","Economies of scale occur when a larger scale of production lowers average total cost.","A central bank oversees monetary conditions and usually issues currency and supports banking-system stability.","Consumer confidence reflects expectations about future income and economic conditions."],"answer":3,"why":"Consumer confidence: Consumer confidence reflects expectations about future income and economic conditions."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0082","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Consumers reduce discretionary purchases after news of possible redundancies. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Economies of scale","Characteristics of money","Money as a store of value","Consumer confidence"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates consumer confidence: Consumer confidence reflects expectations about future income and economic conditions."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0083","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of consumer confidence?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output.","Consumers reduce discretionary purchases after news of possible redundancies.","Many independent cafés compete for customers along a busy street.","A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment."],"answer":1,"why":"Consumers reduce discretionary purchases after news of possible redundancies. This illustrates consumer confidence reflects expectations about future income and economic conditions."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0084","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to consumer confidence?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable.","Manufacturing adds value by transforming inputs into finished goods.","Larger scale may reduce unit costs but lessen rivalry.","Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption."],"answer":3,"why":"Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0085","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to provide credible information to improve households' economic confidence. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Quality may suffer if portions become too small.","A cheaper location may be less accessible to customers.","Unit costs can be high if wages rise.","Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts."],"answer":3,"why":"Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0086","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to provide credible information to improve households' economic confidence. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Optimism cannot prevent genuine job losses.","Households may be better prepared for emergencies.","Higher productivity may reduce costs per loaf.","Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit."],"answer":0,"why":"Optimism cannot prevent genuine job losses."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0087","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to provide credible information to improve households' economic confidence?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More stable demand can help firms plan production.","Poor motivation may limit gains from new skills.","Automation may reduce some production jobs.","A missing specialist can interrupt the whole production line."],"answer":0,"why":"More stable demand can help firms plan production."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0088","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 304 loaves per day with 16 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["304 loaves","35 loaves","16 loaves","19 loaves"],"answer":3,"why":"Labour productivity = 304 / 16 = 19 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0089","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to provide credible information to improve households' economic confidence. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts.","The future pool of qualified applicants may grow.","More transactions may be completed quickly.","Variable cost per sandwich may fall."],"answer":0,"why":"Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0090","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Consumers reduce discretionary purchases after news of possible redundancies. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Larger scale may reduce unit costs but lessen rivalry.","Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit.","Services such as transport and retail help goods reach consumers.","Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption."],"answer":3,"why":"Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0091","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes non-wage job factors?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Total revenue is the money received from sales, calculated as price times quantity sold.","Labour-intensive methods use a relatively high proportion of labour compared with capital.","People consider conditions, training, security, location and satisfaction as well as pay when choosing occupations.","A secondary-sector firm processes raw materials or manufactures products."],"answer":2,"why":"Non-wage job factors: People consider conditions, training, security, location and satisfaction as well as pay when choosing occupations."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0092","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Economies of scale","Variable cost","Labour-intensive production","Non-wage job factors"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates non-wage job factors: People consider conditions, training, security, location and satisfaction as well as pay when choosing occupations."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0093","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of non-wage job factors?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times.","A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts.","A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output.","A firm spends $12,000 producing 600 lamps, giving an average cost of $20 per lamp."],"answer":0,"why":"A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times. This illustrates people consider conditions, training, security, location and satisfaction as well as pay when choosing occupations."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0094","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to non-wage job factors?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Services such as transport and retail help goods reach consumers.","Larger scale may reduce unit costs but lessen rivalry.","Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","Non-financial benefits can compensate for some differences in wages."],"answer":3,"why":"Non-financial benefits can compensate for some differences in wages."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0095","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve working conditions to attract skilled teachers. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Overharvesting may damage forests and biodiversity.","Better conditions may improve staff retention.","Output beyond efficient capacity may raise costs.","Borrowers may default and create bank losses."],"answer":1,"why":"Better conditions may improve staff retention."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0096","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 140 items with a total cost of $1,120. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$148","$8","$13","$1,120"],"answer":1,"why":"Average total cost = $1,120 / 140 = $8 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0097","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve working conditions to attract skilled teachers?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Poor motivation may limit gains from new skills.","Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment.","The landlord may refuse to change the agreement.","Happier staff may provide more consistent service."],"answer":3,"why":"Happier staff may provide more consistent service."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0098","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to improve working conditions to attract skilled teachers less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Faster production can allow more sales.","Small businesses may reach customers who do not carry cash.","Low pay may still push qualified teachers elsewhere.","Households may build stronger financial buffers."],"answer":2,"why":"Low pay may still push qualified teachers elsewhere."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0099","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve working conditions to attract skilled teachers. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Savers may earn more income from deposits.","Output per employee may rise.","Better conditions may improve staff retention.","Products can be customised for individual buyers."],"answer":2,"why":"Better conditions may improve staff retention."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0100","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending.","Non-financial benefits can compensate for some differences in wages.","Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers.","Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability."],"answer":1,"why":"Non-financial benefits can compensate for some differences in wages."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0101","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes demand for labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Labour productivity measures output per worker or per worker-hour over a period.","The supply of labour is the number of workers willing and able to work at different wage rates.","Diseconomies of scale occur when an excessively large firm experiences rising average total cost.","Demand for labour is derived from demand for the goods and services workers help produce."],"answer":3,"why":"Demand for labour: Demand for labour is derived from demand for the goods and services workers help produce."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0102","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A restaurant recruits additional chefs when bookings rise. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Demand for labour","Commercial bank","Average total cost","Interest rates and households"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates demand for labour: Demand for labour is derived from demand for the goods and services workers help produce."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0103","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of demand for labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A worker saves part of a monthly salary to buy furniture next year.","A restaurant recruits additional chefs when bookings rise.","A sandwich shop uses more bread and filling when it makes more sandwiches.","A workshop pays $2,000 monthly rent whether it produces 100 or 300 tables."],"answer":1,"why":"A restaurant recruits additional chefs when bookings rise. This illustrates demand for labour is derived from demand for the goods and services workers help produce."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0104","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to demand for labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange.","More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending.","Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0105","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 80 units for $6 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$80","$86","$480","$486"],"answer":2,"why":"Total revenue = $6 × 80 = $480."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0106","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to hire additional chefs during sustained demand growth. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Wage costs rise with the number of staff.","The business may have lower total costs at every output level.","Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit.","Additional sales could increase total revenue."],"answer":0,"why":"Wage costs rise with the number of staff."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0107","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to hire additional chefs during sustained demand growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Orders may be temporary, leaving excess staff later.","Reduced workloads may improve service quality.","Ingredient prices may rise despite lower waste.","Integration costs may prevent expected savings."],"answer":1,"why":"Reduced workloads may improve service quality."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0108","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to hire additional chefs during sustained demand growth less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More customers might make later repeat purchases.","Bookings may fall before recruitment costs are recovered.","Entrepreneurs may obtain funds unavailable from their own savings.","Lower unit costs may improve price competitiveness."],"answer":1,"why":"Bookings may fall before recruitment costs are recovered."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0109","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to hire additional chefs during sustained demand growth. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Output per employee may rise.","Fixed cost per unit could fall.","Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand.","The restaurant may serve more meals."],"answer":3,"why":"The restaurant may serve more meals."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0110","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A restaurant recruits additional chefs when bookings rise. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Labour-intensive output may suit small production runs and places with relatively low wages.","Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit.","Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable.","Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0111","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes supply of labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher interest rates generally increase rewards for saving and costs of borrowing.","The supply of labour is the number of workers willing and able to work at different wage rates.","A competitive market has numerous firms exerting pressure on price, quality and choice.","Division of labour is the splitting of production into specialised tasks performed by different workers."],"answer":1,"why":"Supply of labour: The supply of labour is the number of workers willing and able to work at different wage rates."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0112","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A local hospital struggles to recruit nurses because too few people have the required qualifications. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Consumer confidence","Supply of labour","Secondary-sector firm","Primary-sector firm"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates supply of labour: The supply of labour is the number of workers willing and able to work at different wage rates."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0113","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 63 loaves per day with 7 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["16 loaves","63 loaves","9 loaves","7 loaves"],"answer":2,"why":"Labour productivity = 63 / 7 = 9 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0114","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to supply of labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost.","Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled.","Machines can raise output per worker when production is large and standardised.","Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange."],"answer":1,"why":"Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0115","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand training places for nurses. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Optimism cannot prevent genuine job losses.","Initial investment can be very expensive.","Less spending may reduce current demand for local businesses.","The future pool of qualified applicants may grow."],"answer":3,"why":"The future pool of qualified applicants may grow."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0116","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand training places for nurses. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The business may have lower total costs at every output level.","Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges.","Output per employee may rise.","Training is costly and takes several years."],"answer":3,"why":"Training is costly and takes several years."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0117","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand training places for nurses?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment.","Duplicate teams can increase administrative costs.","Staff shortages and waiting times may decline.","Loans may be unaffordable when interest rates are high."],"answer":2,"why":"Staff shortages and waiting times may decline."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0118","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand training places for nurses less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Graduates may migrate to higher-paying countries.","The business may support local craft employment.","Lower fixed costs may help survival during weak demand.","Fewer damaged notes could reduce replacement costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Graduates may migrate to higher-paying countries."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0119","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand training places for nurses. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More competition may lower prices.","The future pool of qualified applicants may grow.","Output per employee may rise.","Investment may support more production and jobs."],"answer":1,"why":"The future pool of qualified applicants may grow."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0120","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A local hospital struggles to recruit nurses because too few people have the required qualifications. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled.","Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption.","Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold.","Total variable costs rise when additional units require more materials."],"answer":0,"why":"Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0121","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 30 items with a total cost of $330. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$330","$11","$41","$16"],"answer":1,"why":"Average total cost = $330 / 30 = $11 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0122","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government increases the legal hourly minimum for retail employees. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Central bank","National minimum wage","Supply of labour","Competitive market"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates national minimum wage: A national minimum wage is a legal lower limit on wages paid to covered workers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0123","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of national minimum wage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A worker saves part of a monthly salary to buy furniture next year.","A government increases the legal hourly minimum for retail employees.","A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue.","A family uses a bank loan to buy a refrigerator rather than waiting two years."],"answer":1,"why":"A government increases the legal hourly minimum for retail employees. This illustrates a national minimum wage is a legal lower limit on wages paid to covered workers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0124","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to national minimum wage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs.","Higher final-product demand raises the need for inputs used in production.","More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending.","Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability."],"answer":0,"why":"A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0125","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Overharvesting may damage forests and biodiversity.","Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income.","Quality may suffer if portions become too small.","Producing secure notes may be expensive."],"answer":1,"why":"Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0126","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Local managers may respond more quickly to customers.","Employers may reduce hiring if labour costs rise sharply.","Fixed cost per unit could fall.","Savers may earn more income from deposits."],"answer":1,"why":"Employers may reduce hiring if labour costs rise sharply."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0127","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Production may be unable to meet a large increase in demand.","Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment.","Higher pay could reduce in-work poverty.","Automation may reduce some production jobs."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher pay could reduce in-work poverty."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0128","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination.","More stable demand can help firms plan production.","Lower fixed costs may help survival during weak demand.","Some workers already earning more than the minimum may not benefit."],"answer":3,"why":"Some workers already earning more than the minimum may not benefit."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0129","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Households may obtain useful equipment sooner.","Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment.","Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts.","Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income."],"answer":3,"why":"Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0130","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 110 units for $9 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$990","$999","$119","$110"],"answer":0,"why":"Total revenue = $9 × 110 = $990."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0131","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes division of labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Money serves as a commonly accepted means of payment that reduces the need for barter.","A monopoly market has one supplier of a good or service with no close rival.","Division of labour is the splitting of production into specialised tasks performed by different workers.","A secondary-sector firm processes raw materials or manufactures products."],"answer":2,"why":"Division of labour: Division of labour is the splitting of production into specialised tasks performed by different workers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0132","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Commercial bank","Division of labour","Household saving","Total revenue"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates division of labour: Division of labour is the splitting of production into specialised tasks performed by different workers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0133","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of division of labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town.","A growing retailer suffers coordination problems between many regional branches.","In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles.","A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue."],"answer":2,"why":"In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles. This illustrates division of labour is the splitting of production into specialised tasks performed by different workers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0134","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to division of labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions.","Repeated specialist tasks can raise speed but may reduce flexibility.","More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending."],"answer":2,"why":"Repeated specialist tasks can raise speed but may reduce flexibility."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0135","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase task specialisation in a shoe factory. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Producing secure notes may be expensive.","Practice may raise labour productivity.","A cheaper location may be less accessible to customers.","Training takes time away from current output."],"answer":1,"why":"Practice may raise labour productivity."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0136","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase task specialisation in a shoe factory. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Households may be better prepared for emergencies.","Repetitive work may reduce staff motivation.","Fixed cost per unit could fall.","Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood."],"answer":1,"why":"Repetitive work may reduce staff motivation."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0137","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to increase task specialisation in a shoe factory?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Shorter task changes may reduce wasted time.","World timber prices may make income unstable.","Orders may be temporary, leaving excess staff later.","Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment."],"answer":0,"why":"Shorter task changes may reduce wasted time."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0138","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 120 loaves per day with 10 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["12 loaves","10 loaves","120 loaves","22 loaves"],"answer":0,"why":"Labour productivity = 120 / 10 = 12 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0139","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to increase task specialisation in a shoe factory. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Traders may become more confident accepting payment.","Practice may raise labour productivity.","Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices.","Households may be better prepared for emergencies."],"answer":1,"why":"Practice may raise labour productivity."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0140","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions.","Repeated specialist tasks can raise speed but may reduce flexibility.","Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability."],"answer":2,"why":"Repeated specialist tasks can raise speed but may reduce flexibility."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0141","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes primary-sector firm?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["People consider conditions, training, security, location and satisfaction as well as pay when choosing occupations.","A primary-sector firm extracts or harvests natural resources.","Diseconomies of scale occur when an excessively large firm experiences rising average total cost.","Total revenue is the money received from sales, calculated as price times quantity sold."],"answer":1,"why":"Primary-sector firm: A primary-sector firm extracts or harvests natural resources."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0142","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A forestry business grows and harvests timber for local sawmills. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Diseconomies of scale","Primary-sector firm","Average total cost","Competitive market"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates primary-sector firm: A primary-sector firm extracts or harvests natural resources."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0143","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of primary-sector firm?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A worker saves part of a monthly salary to buy furniture next year.","A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table.","A local hospital struggles to recruit nurses because too few people have the required qualifications.","A forestry business grows and harvests timber for local sawmills."],"answer":3,"why":"A forestry business grows and harvests timber for local sawmills. This illustrates a primary-sector firm extracts or harvests natural resources."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0144","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to primary-sector firm?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Repeated specialist tasks can raise speed but may reduce flexibility.","More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending.","The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production.","Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers."],"answer":2,"why":"The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0145","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to support sustainable timber harvesting firms. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Producing secure notes may be expensive.","Unit costs can be high if wages rise.","Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood.","Initial investment can be very expensive."],"answer":2,"why":"Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0146","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 60 items with a total cost of $840. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$840","$74","$19","$14"],"answer":3,"why":"Average total cost = $840 / 60 = $14 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0147","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to support sustainable timber harvesting firms?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Families on low incomes may be unable to save much.","Ingredient prices may rise despite lower waste.","Output may be slower than with suitable machinery.","Rural employment may increase."],"answer":3,"why":"Rural employment may increase."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0148","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to support sustainable timber harvesting firms less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Staff shortages and waiting times may decline.","World timber prices may make income unstable.","More stable demand can help firms plan production.","Entrepreneurs may obtain funds unavailable from their own savings."],"answer":1,"why":"World timber prices may make income unstable."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0149","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to support sustainable timber harvesting firms. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The restaurant may serve more meals.","Higher productivity may reduce costs per loaf.","Investment may support more production and jobs.","Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood."],"answer":3,"why":"Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0150","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A forestry business grows and harvests timber for local sawmills. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production.","The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production.","A common payment medium makes trade easier because a double coincidence of wants is unnecessary.","Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption."],"answer":1,"why":"The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0151","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes secondary-sector firm?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["People consider conditions, training, security, location and satisfaction as well as pay when choosing occupations.","A secondary-sector firm processes raw materials or manufactures products.","The supply of labour is the number of workers willing and able to work at different wage rates.","A tertiary-sector firm provides services rather than extracting or manufacturing products."],"answer":1,"why":"Secondary-sector firm: A secondary-sector firm processes raw materials or manufactures products."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0152","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A workshop converts timber into chairs and shelving. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Division of labour","Household saving","Interest rates and households","Secondary-sector firm"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates secondary-sector firm: A secondary-sector firm processes raw materials or manufactures products."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0153","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of secondary-sector firm?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A country's central bank changes the policy interest rate after inflation rises.","A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts.","A workshop converts timber into chairs and shelving.","A government increases the legal hourly minimum for retail employees."],"answer":2,"why":"A workshop converts timber into chairs and shelving. This illustrates a secondary-sector firm processes raw materials or manufactures products."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0154","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to secondary-sector firm?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions.","Manufacturing adds value by transforming inputs into finished goods.","Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange."],"answer":2,"why":"Manufacturing adds value by transforming inputs into finished goods."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0155","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 50 units for $12 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$50","$612","$600","$62"],"answer":2,"why":"Total revenue = $12 × 50 = $600."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0156","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to upgrade machinery at a furniture manufacturer. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Traders may become more confident accepting payment.","Products can be customised for individual buyers.","Additional sales could increase total revenue.","The equipment requires capital expenditure."],"answer":3,"why":"The equipment requires capital expenditure."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0157","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to upgrade machinery at a furniture manufacturer?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Borrowers may struggle if their income falls.","Some workers already earning more than the minimum may not benefit.","More consistent products may attract buyers.","Orders may be temporary, leaving excess staff later."],"answer":2,"why":"More consistent products may attract buyers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0158","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to upgrade machinery at a furniture manufacturer less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Staff shortages and waiting times may decline.","More stable demand can help firms plan production.","Automation may reduce some production jobs.","Small businesses may reach customers who do not carry cash."],"answer":2,"why":"Automation may reduce some production jobs."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0159","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to upgrade machinery at a furniture manufacturer. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Traders may become more confident accepting payment.","Better conditions may improve staff retention.","Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment.","Output per employee may rise."],"answer":3,"why":"Output per employee may rise."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0160","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A workshop converts timber into chairs and shelving. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending.","Manufacturing adds value by transforming inputs into finished goods.","Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold."],"answer":1,"why":"Manufacturing adds value by transforming inputs into finished goods."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0161","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes tertiary-sector firm?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A monopoly market has one supplier of a good or service with no close rival.","A competitive market has numerous firms exerting pressure on price, quality and choice.","A horizontal merger joins businesses operating at the same stage in the same industry.","A tertiary-sector firm provides services rather than extracting or manufacturing products."],"answer":3,"why":"Tertiary-sector firm: A tertiary-sector firm provides services rather than extracting or manufacturing products."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0162","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A logistics company transports manufactured goods to stores. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Tertiary-sector firm","Characteristics of money","Interest rates and households","Household borrowing"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates tertiary-sector firm: A tertiary-sector firm provides services rather than extracting or manufacturing products."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0163","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 195 loaves per day with 13 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["13 loaves","195 loaves","28 loaves","15 loaves"],"answer":3,"why":"Labour productivity = 195 / 13 = 15 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0164","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to tertiary-sector firm?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Fixed costs must be covered even when output falls.","Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it.","Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation.","Services such as transport and retail help goods reach consumers."],"answer":3,"why":"Services such as transport and retail help goods reach consumers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0165","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand local logistics services. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Less spending may reduce current demand for local businesses.","Firms may deliver goods more reliably.","Training takes time away from current output.","Optimism cannot prevent genuine job losses."],"answer":1,"why":"Firms may deliver goods more reliably."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0166","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand local logistics services. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Practice may raise labour productivity.","More transport can increase congestion and emissions.","Traders may become more confident accepting payment.","Variable cost per sandwich may fall."],"answer":1,"why":"More transport can increase congestion and emissions."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0167","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand local logistics services?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Borrowers may struggle if their income falls.","Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns.","Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment.","Improved distribution can support sales."],"answer":3,"why":"Improved distribution can support sales."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0168","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand local logistics services less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Happier staff may provide more consistent service.","Automated systems can improve uniformity.","Faster production can allow more sales.","Fuel-price increases may make services expensive."],"answer":3,"why":"Fuel-price increases may make services expensive."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0169","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand local logistics services. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Firms may deliver goods more reliably.","More competition may lower prices.","Additional sales could increase total revenue.","Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Firms may deliver goods more reliably."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0170","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A logistics company transports manufactured goods to stores. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it.","Services such as transport and retail help goods reach consumers.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions.","Non-financial benefits can compensate for some differences in wages."],"answer":1,"why":"Services such as transport and retail help goods reach consumers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0171","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 90 items with a total cost of $1,530. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$107","$17","$22","$1,530"],"answer":1,"why":"Average total cost = $1,530 / 90 = $17 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0172","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Two competing supermarket chains agree to combine their operations. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Derived demand for factors","Diseconomies of scale","Horizontal merger","Average total cost"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates horizontal merger: A horizontal merger joins businesses operating at the same stage in the same industry."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0173","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of horizontal merger?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A bakery increases loaves per baker-hour after introducing training and better tools.","A household increases monthly deposits after receiving a pay rise.","Two competing supermarket chains agree to combine their operations.","A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts."],"answer":2,"why":"Two competing supermarket chains agree to combine their operations. This illustrates a horizontal merger joins businesses operating at the same stage in the same industry."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0174","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to horizontal merger?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs.","Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions.","Larger scale may reduce unit costs but lessen rivalry."],"answer":3,"why":"Larger scale may reduce unit costs but lessen rivalry."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0175","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Repetitive work may reduce staff motivation.","Training takes time away from current output.","Wage costs rise with the number of staff.","Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices."],"answer":3,"why":"Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0176","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The future pool of qualified applicants may grow.","Consumers may face less choice or higher prices.","Products can be customised for individual buyers.","Variable cost per sandwich may fall."],"answer":1,"why":"Consumers may face less choice or higher prices."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0177","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns.","Breakdowns can stop a large share of production.","Some locations may not support many viable businesses.","Overlapping distribution networks may become more efficient."],"answer":3,"why":"Overlapping distribution networks may become more efficient."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0178","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Fewer damaged notes could reduce replacement costs.","More customers might make later repeat purchases.","Savings can finance larger future purchases.","Integration costs may prevent expected savings."],"answer":3,"why":"Integration costs may prevent expected savings."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0179","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More transactions may be completed quickly.","Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices.","Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income.","Products can be customised for individual buyers."],"answer":1,"why":"Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0180","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 80 units for $15 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$1,200","$95","$1,215","$80"],"answer":0,"why":"Total revenue = $15 × 80 = $1,200."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0181","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes derived demand for factors?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Demand for an input depends partly on demand for the final good it helps produce.","A monopoly market has one supplier of a good or service with no close rival.","The supply of labour is the number of workers willing and able to work at different wage rates.","Higher interest rates generally increase rewards for saving and costs of borrowing."],"answer":0,"why":"Derived demand for factors: Demand for an input depends partly on demand for the final good it helps produce."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0182","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Characteristics of money","Division of labour","Derived demand for factors","Household saving"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates derived demand for factors: Demand for an input depends partly on demand for the final good it helps produce."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0183","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of derived demand for factors?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Following an interest-rate rise, a household postpones replacing its car.","A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table.","A growing retailer suffers coordination problems between many regional branches.","A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts."],"answer":3,"why":"A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts. This illustrates demand for an input depends partly on demand for the final good it helps produce."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0184","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to derived demand for factors?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher final-product demand raises the need for inputs used in production.","Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability.","Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher final-product demand raises the need for inputs used in production."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0185","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to hire more skilled installers when solar-panel orders rise. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Regulation has administrative costs.","Output beyond efficient capacity may raise costs.","A cheaper location may be less accessible to customers.","The firm may meet orders faster."],"answer":3,"why":"The firm may meet orders faster."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0186","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to hire more skilled installers when solar-panel orders rise. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges.","Households may obtain useful equipment sooner.","Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices.","Higher labour costs increase operating expenses."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher labour costs increase operating expenses."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0187","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to hire more skilled installers when solar-panel orders rise?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Low pay may still push qualified teachers elsewhere.","Graduates may migrate to higher-paying countries.","Additional installations may raise revenue.","Some workers already earning more than the minimum may not benefit."],"answer":2,"why":"Additional installations may raise revenue."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0188","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 288 loaves per day with 16 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["16 loaves","34 loaves","288 loaves","18 loaves"],"answer":3,"why":"Labour productivity = 288 / 16 = 18 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0189","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to hire more skilled installers when solar-panel orders rise. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More competition may lower prices.","Output per employee may rise.","Investment may support more production and jobs.","The firm may meet orders faster."],"answer":3,"why":"The firm may meet orders faster."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0190","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Fixed costs must be covered even when output falls.","Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption.","Higher final-product demand raises the need for inputs used in production.","Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher final-product demand raises the need for inputs used in production."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0191","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes labour-intensive production?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["People consider conditions, training, security, location and satisfaction as well as pay when choosing occupations.","Labour-intensive methods use a relatively high proportion of labour compared with capital.","A tertiary-sector firm provides services rather than extracting or manufacturing products.","Demand for labour is derived from demand for the goods and services workers help produce."],"answer":1,"why":"Labour-intensive production: Labour-intensive methods use a relatively high proportion of labour compared with capital."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0192","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A handmade textile producer hires artisans instead of buying automated weaving machines. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Labour-intensive production","Household saving","Primary-sector firm","Non-wage job factors"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates labour-intensive production: Labour-intensive methods use a relatively high proportion of labour compared with capital."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0193","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of labour-intensive production?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A handmade textile producer hires artisans instead of buying automated weaving machines.","Two competing supermarket chains agree to combine their operations.","A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output.","In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles."],"answer":0,"why":"A handmade textile producer hires artisans instead of buying automated weaving machines. This illustrates labour-intensive methods use a relatively high proportion of labour compared with capital."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0194","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to labour-intensive production?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A common payment medium makes trade easier because a double coincidence of wants is unnecessary.","Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production.","Total variable costs rise when additional units require more materials.","Labour-intensive output may suit small production runs and places with relatively low wages."],"answer":3,"why":"Labour-intensive output may suit small production runs and places with relatively low wages."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0195","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to retain skilled handmade production methods. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Borrowers may default and create bank losses.","Repetitive work may reduce staff motivation.","Inflation may reduce the real value of cash savings.","Products can be customised for individual buyers."],"answer":3,"why":"Products can be customised for individual buyers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0196","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 120 items with a total cost of $1,080. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$14","$1,080","$129","$9"],"answer":3,"why":"Average total cost = $1,080 / 120 = $9 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0197","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to retain skilled handmade production methods?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Low pay may still push qualified teachers elsewhere.","Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns.","Excessive confidence could encourage unsustainable debt.","The business may support local craft employment."],"answer":3,"why":"The business may support local craft employment."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0198","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to retain skilled handmade production methods less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More stable demand can help firms plan production.","Output may be slower than with suitable machinery.","Service standards may improve through enforceable requirements.","Reduced workloads may improve service quality."],"answer":1,"why":"Output may be slower than with suitable machinery."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0199","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to retain skilled handmade production methods. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit.","Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts.","Products can be customised for individual buyers.","Traders may become more confident accepting payment."],"answer":2,"why":"Products can be customised for individual buyers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0200","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A handmade textile producer hires artisans instead of buying automated weaving machines. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Larger scale may reduce unit costs but lessen rivalry.","Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","Labour-intensive output may suit small production runs and places with relatively low wages.","Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit."],"answer":2,"why":"Labour-intensive output may suit small production runs and places with relatively low wages."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0201","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes capital-intensive production?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Labour productivity measures output per worker or per worker-hour over a period.","Capital-intensive production uses a relatively high proportion of machinery and equipment.","Money serves as a commonly accepted means of payment that reduces the need for barter.","A tertiary-sector firm provides services rather than extracting or manufacturing products."],"answer":1,"why":"Capital-intensive production: Capital-intensive production uses a relatively high proportion of machinery and equipment."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0202","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Capital-intensive production","Labour productivity","Money as a store of value","National minimum wage"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates capital-intensive production: Capital-intensive production uses a relatively high proportion of machinery and equipment."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0203","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of capital-intensive production?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles.","A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles.","A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment.","One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town."],"answer":2,"why":"A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment. This illustrates capital-intensive production uses a relatively high proportion of machinery and equipment."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0204","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to capital-intensive production?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation.","Machines can raise output per worker when production is large and standardised.","Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it.","Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption."],"answer":1,"why":"Machines can raise output per worker when production is large and standardised."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0205","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 110 units for $6 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$110","$116","$666","$660"],"answer":3,"why":"Total revenue = $6 × 110 = $660."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0206","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to automate a high-volume bottling plant. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Households may obtain useful equipment sooner.","Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand.","Initial investment can be very expensive.","Output per employee may rise."],"answer":2,"why":"Initial investment can be very expensive."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0207","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to automate a high-volume bottling plant?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Automated systems can improve uniformity.","Families on low incomes may be unable to save much.","Policy effects may take time and depend on borrower responses.","Integration costs may prevent expected savings."],"answer":0,"why":"Automated systems can improve uniformity."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0208","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to automate a high-volume bottling plant less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Slower price increases can protect purchasing power.","Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination.","Breakdowns can stop a large share of production.","More customers might make later repeat purchases."],"answer":2,"why":"Breakdowns can stop a large share of production."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0209","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to automate a high-volume bottling plant. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["High output may lower unit costs.","Local managers may respond more quickly to customers.","More competition may lower prices.","The business may have lower total costs at every output level."],"answer":0,"why":"High output may lower unit costs."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0210","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption.","Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled.","Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold.","Machines can raise output per worker when production is large and standardised."],"answer":3,"why":"Machines can raise output per worker when production is large and standardised."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0211","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes labour productivity?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A fixed cost does not vary with output over the relevant short-run period.","A primary-sector firm extracts or harvests natural resources.","Diseconomies of scale occur when an excessively large firm experiences rising average total cost.","Labour productivity measures output per worker or per worker-hour over a period."],"answer":3,"why":"Labour productivity: Labour productivity measures output per worker or per worker-hour over a period."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0212","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A bakery increases loaves per baker-hour after introducing training and better tools. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Labour-intensive production","Labour productivity","Central bank","Commercial bank"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates labour productivity: Labour productivity measures output per worker or per worker-hour over a period."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0213","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 56 loaves per day with 7 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["7 loaves","8 loaves","15 loaves","56 loaves"],"answer":1,"why":"Labour productivity = 56 / 7 = 8 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0214","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to labour productivity?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions.","Manufacturing adds value by transforming inputs into finished goods.","More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost."],"answer":3,"why":"More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0215","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to train bakers to improve output per hour. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Digital exclusion may affect people without smartphones.","The equipment requires capital expenditure.","Higher productivity may reduce costs per loaf.","Employers may reduce hiring if labour costs rise sharply."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher productivity may reduce costs per loaf."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0216","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to train bakers to improve output per hour. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit.","Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment.","Local managers may respond more quickly to customers.","Training takes time away from current output."],"answer":3,"why":"Training takes time away from current output."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0217","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to train bakers to improve output per hour?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns.","Low-income families may have little income left to save.","Faster production can allow more sales.","Some locations may not support many viable businesses."],"answer":2,"why":"Faster production can allow more sales."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0218","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to train bakers to improve output per hour less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Shorter task changes may reduce wasted time.","Slower price increases can protect purchasing power.","More consistent products may attract buyers.","Poor motivation may limit gains from new skills."],"answer":3,"why":"Poor motivation may limit gains from new skills."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0219","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to train bakers to improve output per hour. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher productivity may reduce costs per loaf.","Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges.","Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment.","Additional sales could increase total revenue."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher productivity may reduce costs per loaf."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0220","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A bakery increases loaves per baker-hour after introducing training and better tools. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled.","The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production.","Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability.","More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost."],"answer":3,"why":"More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0221","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 150 items with a total cost of $1,800. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$1,800","$162","$12","$17"],"answer":2,"why":"Average total cost = $1,800 / 150 = $12 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0222","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A workshop pays $2,000 monthly rent whether it produces 100 or 300 tables. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Demand for labour","Secondary-sector firm","Labour-intensive production","Fixed cost"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates fixed cost: A fixed cost does not vary with output over the relevant short-run period."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0223","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of fixed cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A family uses a bank loan to buy a refrigerator rather than waiting two years.","One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town.","A forestry business grows and harvests timber for local sawmills.","A workshop pays $2,000 monthly rent whether it produces 100 or 300 tables."],"answer":3,"why":"A workshop pays $2,000 monthly rent whether it produces 100 or 300 tables. This illustrates a fixed cost does not vary with output over the relevant short-run period."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0224","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to fixed cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Fixed costs must be covered even when output falls.","Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable.","Machines can raise output per worker when production is large and standardised."],"answer":0,"why":"Fixed costs must be covered even when output falls."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0225","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher labour costs increase operating expenses.","The business may have lower total costs at every output level.","Optimism cannot prevent genuine job losses.","Producing secure notes may be expensive."],"answer":1,"why":"The business may have lower total costs at every output level."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0226","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income.","The future pool of qualified applicants may grow.","A cheaper location may be less accessible to customers.","Households may be better prepared for emergencies."],"answer":2,"why":"A cheaper location may be less accessible to customers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0227","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Lower fixed costs may help survival during weak demand.","Automation may reduce some production jobs.","A missing specialist can interrupt the whole production line.","Some locations may not support many viable businesses."],"answer":0,"why":"Lower fixed costs may help survival during weak demand."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0228","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Households may build stronger financial buffers.","Savings can finance larger future purchases.","Purchases can support demand for local suppliers.","The landlord may refuse to change the agreement."],"answer":3,"why":"The landlord may refuse to change the agreement."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0229","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The business may have lower total costs at every output level.","Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand.","Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices.","Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood."],"answer":0,"why":"The business may have lower total costs at every output level."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0230","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 50 units for $9 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$50","$59","$450","$459"],"answer":2,"why":"Total revenue = $9 × 50 = $450."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0231","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes variable cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A variable cost changes as the quantity of output changes.","Money can retain purchasing power for use in later transactions.","Consumer confidence reflects expectations about future income and economic conditions.","A secondary-sector firm processes raw materials or manufactures products."],"answer":0,"why":"Variable cost: A variable cost changes as the quantity of output changes."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0232","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A sandwich shop uses more bread and filling when it makes more sandwiches. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Labour-intensive production","Capital-intensive production","Fixed cost","Variable cost"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates variable cost: A variable cost changes as the quantity of output changes."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0233","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of variable cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles.","A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table.","A government increases the legal hourly minimum for retail employees.","A sandwich shop uses more bread and filling when it makes more sandwiches."],"answer":3,"why":"A sandwich shop uses more bread and filling when it makes more sandwiches. This illustrates a variable cost changes as the quantity of output changes."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0234","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to variable cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers.","Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production.","Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange.","Total variable costs rise when additional units require more materials."],"answer":3,"why":"Total variable costs rise when additional units require more materials."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0235","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reduce ingredient waste per sandwich. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Variable cost per sandwich may fall.","Higher labour costs increase operating expenses.","Expanding requires capital and management capacity.","Borrowers may face higher repayments at the same time."],"answer":0,"why":"Variable cost per sandwich may fall."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0236","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reduce ingredient waste per sandwich. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More transactions may be completed quickly.","Quality may suffer if portions become too small.","Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand.","More competition may lower prices."],"answer":1,"why":"Quality may suffer if portions become too small."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0237","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to reduce ingredient waste per sandwich?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Counterfeiters may eventually develop new methods.","Families on low incomes may be unable to save much.","Orders may be temporary, leaving excess staff later.","The shop may improve profitability at unchanged prices."],"answer":3,"why":"The shop may improve profitability at unchanged prices."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0238","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 110 loaves per day with 10 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["21 loaves","110 loaves","11 loaves","10 loaves"],"answer":2,"why":"Labour productivity = 110 / 10 = 11 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0239","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to reduce ingredient waste per sandwich. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts.","Variable cost per sandwich may fall.","Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit.","Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income."],"answer":1,"why":"Variable cost per sandwich may fall."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0240","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A sandwich shop uses more bread and filling when it makes more sandwiches. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled.","The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production.","Total variable costs rise when additional units require more materials.","Larger scale may reduce unit costs but lessen rivalry."],"answer":2,"why":"Total variable costs rise when additional units require more materials."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0241","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes average total cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A variable cost changes as the quantity of output changes.","Average total cost equals total cost divided by units produced.","Money serves as a commonly accepted means of payment that reduces the need for barter.","Effective money is durable, portable, divisible, recognisable and generally accepted."],"answer":1,"why":"Average total cost: Average total cost equals total cost divided by units produced."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0242","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A firm spends $12,000 producing 600 lamps, giving an average cost of $20 per lamp. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Secondary-sector firm","Tertiary-sector firm","Average total cost","Central bank"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates average total cost: Average total cost equals total cost divided by units produced."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0243","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of average total cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table.","A firm spends $12,000 producing 600 lamps, giving an average cost of $20 per lamp.","A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles.","Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport."],"answer":1,"why":"A firm spends $12,000 producing 600 lamps, giving an average cost of $20 per lamp. This illustrates average total cost equals total cost divided by units produced."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0244","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to average total cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled.","Labour-intensive output may suit small production runs and places with relatively low wages.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions.","Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers."],"answer":2,"why":"Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0245","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase efficient output to spread fixed costs. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More transport can increase congestion and emissions.","Producing secure notes may be expensive.","Fixed cost per unit could fall.","Higher labour costs increase operating expenses."],"answer":2,"why":"Fixed cost per unit could fall."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0246","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 40 items with a total cost of $600. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$15","$20","$55","$600"],"answer":0,"why":"Average total cost = $600 / 40 = $15 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0247","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to increase efficient output to spread fixed costs?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns.","Policy effects may take time and depend on borrower responses.","Lower unit costs may improve price competitiveness.","Borrowers may struggle if their income falls."],"answer":2,"why":"Lower unit costs may improve price competitiveness."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0248","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to increase efficient output to spread fixed costs less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More stable demand can help firms plan production.","Specialised machinery may increase efficiency.","Overlapping distribution networks may become more efficient.","Extra lamps may be difficult to sell."],"answer":3,"why":"Extra lamps may be difficult to sell."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0249","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to increase efficient output to spread fixed costs. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment.","Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices.","High output may lower unit costs.","Fixed cost per unit could fall."],"answer":3,"why":"Fixed cost per unit could fall."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0250","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A firm spends $12,000 producing 600 lamps, giving an average cost of $20 per lamp. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled.","Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation.","More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions."],"answer":3,"why":"Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0251","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes total revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A monopoly market has one supplier of a good or service with no close rival.","Total revenue is the money received from sales, calculated as price times quantity sold.","A competitive market has numerous firms exerting pressure on price, quality and choice.","Borrowing enables expenditure now in exchange for future repayments, often including interest."],"answer":1,"why":"Total revenue: Total revenue is the money received from sales, calculated as price times quantity sold."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0252","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Capital-intensive production","Competitive market","Horizontal merger","Total revenue"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates total revenue: Total revenue is the money received from sales, calculated as price times quantity sold."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0253","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of total revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue.","One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town.","A restaurant recruits additional chefs when bookings rise.","A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles."],"answer":0,"why":"A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue. This illustrates total revenue is the money received from sales, calculated as price times quantity sold."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0254","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to total revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers.","Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold.","Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production.","Higher final-product demand raises the need for inputs used in production."],"answer":1,"why":"Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0255","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 80 units for $12 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$972","$92","$960","$80"],"answer":2,"why":"Total revenue = $12 × 80 = $960."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0256","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to offer a targeted promotion to increase cake sales. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Discounts lower revenue per cake.","High output may lower unit costs.","The restaurant may serve more meals.","Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices."],"answer":0,"why":"Discounts lower revenue per cake."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0257","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to offer a targeted promotion to increase cake sales?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Excessive confidence could encourage unsustainable debt.","World timber prices may make income unstable.","A missing specialist can interrupt the whole production line.","More customers might make later repeat purchases."],"answer":3,"why":"More customers might make later repeat purchases."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0258","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to offer a targeted promotion to increase cake sales less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Shorter task changes may reduce wasted time.","Rural employment may increase.","Production may be unable to meet a large increase in demand.","Automated systems can improve uniformity."],"answer":2,"why":"Production may be unable to meet a large increase in demand."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0259","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to offer a targeted promotion to increase cake sales. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Additional sales could increase total revenue.","Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income.","Products can be customised for individual buyers.","Households may obtain useful equipment sooner."],"answer":0,"why":"Additional sales could increase total revenue."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0260","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation.","Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable.","The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production.","Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold."],"answer":3,"why":"Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0261","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes competitive market?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Total revenue is the money received from sales, calculated as price times quantity sold.","A competitive market has numerous firms exerting pressure on price, quality and choice.","A monopoly market has one supplier of a good or service with no close rival.","Division of labour is the splitting of production into specialised tasks performed by different workers."],"answer":1,"why":"Competitive market: A competitive market has numerous firms exerting pressure on price, quality and choice."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0262","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Many independent cafés compete for customers along a busy street. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Fixed cost","Competitive market","Derived demand for factors","Demand for labour"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates competitive market: A competitive market has numerous firms exerting pressure on price, quality and choice."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0263","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 182 loaves per day with 13 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["14 loaves","27 loaves","13 loaves","182 loaves"],"answer":0,"why":"Labour productivity = 182 / 13 = 14 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0264","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to competitive market?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost.","Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers.","Labour-intensive output may suit small production runs and places with relatively low wages."],"answer":2,"why":"Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0265","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Wage costs rise with the number of staff.","More competition may lower prices.","A cheaper location may be less accessible to customers.","Improving facilities can be expensive."],"answer":1,"why":"More competition may lower prices."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0266","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Small firms may struggle to earn enough profit.","Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood.","Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income.","Households may obtain useful equipment sooner."],"answer":0,"why":"Small firms may struggle to earn enough profit."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0267","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Poor motivation may limit gains from new skills.","Fuel-price increases may make services expensive.","Consumers could gain greater variety.","Production may be unable to meet a large increase in demand."],"answer":2,"why":"Consumers could gain greater variety."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0268","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination.","Improved distribution can support sales.","Some locations may not support many viable businesses.","The shop may improve profitability at unchanged prices."],"answer":2,"why":"Some locations may not support many viable businesses."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0269","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More transactions may be completed quickly.","Practice may raise labour productivity.","The firm may meet orders faster.","More competition may lower prices."],"answer":3,"why":"More competition may lower prices."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0270","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Many independent cafés compete for customers along a busy street. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability.","Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers.","Larger scale may reduce unit costs but lessen rivalry.","The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production."],"answer":1,"why":"Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0271","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 70 items with a total cost of $490. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$490","$7","$12","$77"],"answer":1,"why":"Average total cost = $490 / 70 = $7 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0272","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Horizontal merger","Monopoly market","Consumer confidence","Household borrowing"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates monopoly market: A monopoly market has one supplier of a good or service with no close rival."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0273","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of monopoly market?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times.","Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport.","A restaurant recruits additional chefs when bookings rise.","One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town."],"answer":3,"why":"One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town. This illustrates a monopoly market has one supplier of a good or service with no close rival."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0274","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to monopoly market?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Lack of direct competition may allow higher prices or less choice.","Total variable costs rise when additional units require more materials.","Repeated specialist tasks can raise speed but may reduce flexibility.","Poor communication and management can increase cost per unit as size expands."],"answer":0,"why":"Lack of direct competition may allow higher prices or less choice."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0275","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to regulate the sole piped-water supplier. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More transport can increase congestion and emissions.","Different branches may lose consistency.","Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges.","Consumers may face less choice or higher prices."],"answer":2,"why":"Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0276","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to regulate the sole piped-water supplier. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Regulation has administrative costs.","Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood.","Variable cost per sandwich may fall.","Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts."],"answer":0,"why":"Regulation has administrative costs."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0277","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to regulate the sole piped-water supplier?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Counterfeiters may eventually develop new methods.","Graduates may migrate to higher-paying countries.","Service standards may improve through enforceable requirements.","A missing specialist can interrupt the whole production line."],"answer":2,"why":"Service standards may improve through enforceable requirements."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0278","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to regulate the sole piped-water supplier less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Rural employment may increase.","Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment.","Small businesses may reach customers who do not carry cash.","The business may support local craft employment."],"answer":1,"why":"Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0279","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to regulate the sole piped-water supplier. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges.","Practice may raise labour productivity.","Variable cost per sandwich may fall.","The future pool of qualified applicants may grow."],"answer":0,"why":"Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0280","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 110 units for $15 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$110","$1,665","$1,650","$125"],"answer":2,"why":"Total revenue = $15 × 110 = $1,650."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0281","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes economies of scale?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Labour-intensive methods use a relatively high proportion of labour compared with capital.","A fixed cost does not vary with output over the relevant short-run period.","Labour productivity measures output per worker or per worker-hour over a period.","Economies of scale occur when a larger scale of production lowers average total cost."],"answer":3,"why":"Economies of scale: Economies of scale occur when a larger scale of production lowers average total cost."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0282","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Economies of scale","Tertiary-sector firm","Diseconomies of scale","Money as a medium of exchange"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates economies of scale: Economies of scale occur when a larger scale of production lowers average total cost."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0283","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of economies of scale?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output.","A bakery increases loaves per baker-hour after introducing training and better tools.","Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport.","Following an interest-rate rise, a household postpones replacing its car."],"answer":0,"why":"A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output. This illustrates economies of scale occur when a larger scale of production lowers average total cost."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0284","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to economies of scale?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit.","More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost.","Machines can raise output per worker when production is large and standardised.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions."],"answer":0,"why":"Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0285","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand a food-processing firm to gain scale economies. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Debt repayments reduce future disposable income.","Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit.","Output beyond efficient capacity may raise costs.","Higher labour costs increase operating expenses."],"answer":1,"why":"Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0286","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand a food-processing firm to gain scale economies. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Households may be better prepared for emergencies.","Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income.","Expanding requires capital and management capacity.","More competition may lower prices."],"answer":2,"why":"Expanding requires capital and management capacity."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0287","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand a food-processing firm to gain scale economies?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Families on low incomes may be unable to save much.","Specialised machinery may increase efficiency.","Production may be unable to meet a large increase in demand.","Policy effects may take time and depend on borrower responses."],"answer":1,"why":"Specialised machinery may increase efficiency."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0288","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 272 loaves per day with 16 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["16 loaves","33 loaves","272 loaves","17 loaves"],"answer":3,"why":"Labour productivity = 272 / 16 = 17 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0289","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand a food-processing firm to gain scale economies. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The firm may meet orders faster.","High output may lower unit costs.","Products can be customised for individual buyers.","Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit."],"answer":3,"why":"Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0290","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers.","Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation.","A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs.","Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit."],"answer":3,"why":"Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0291","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes diseconomies of scale?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Effective money is durable, portable, divisible, recognisable and generally accepted.","Capital-intensive production uses a relatively high proportion of machinery and equipment.","Diseconomies of scale occur when an excessively large firm experiences rising average total cost.","A competitive market has numerous firms exerting pressure on price, quality and choice."],"answer":2,"why":"Diseconomies of scale: Diseconomies of scale occur when an excessively large firm experiences rising average total cost."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0292","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A growing retailer suffers coordination problems between many regional branches. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Diseconomies of scale","Consumer confidence","Primary-sector firm","Tertiary-sector firm"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates diseconomies of scale: Diseconomies of scale occur when an excessively large firm experiences rising average total cost."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0293","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of diseconomies of scale?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A handmade textile producer hires artisans instead of buying automated weaving machines.","A government increases the legal hourly minimum for retail employees.","A bakery increases loaves per baker-hour after introducing training and better tools.","A growing retailer suffers coordination problems between many regional branches."],"answer":3,"why":"A growing retailer suffers coordination problems between many regional branches. This illustrates diseconomies of scale occur when an excessively large firm experiences rising average total cost."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0294","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to diseconomies of scale?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Fixed costs must be covered even when output falls.","Repeated specialist tasks can raise speed but may reduce flexibility.","Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable.","Poor communication and management can increase cost per unit as size expands."],"answer":3,"why":"Poor communication and management can increase cost per unit as size expands."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0295","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to decentralise decisions in a very large retailer. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher borrowing costs can discourage investment.","Local managers may respond more quickly to customers.","Improving facilities can be expensive.","Regulation has administrative costs."],"answer":1,"why":"Local managers may respond more quickly to customers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0296","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 100 items with a total cost of $1,000. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$10","$1,000","$15","$110"],"answer":0,"why":"Average total cost = $1,000 / 100 = $10 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0297","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to decentralise decisions in a very large retailer?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination.","Counterfeiters may eventually develop new methods.","Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns.","Low-income families may have little income left to save."],"answer":0,"why":"Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0298","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to decentralise decisions in a very large retailer less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Consumers could gain greater variety.","Service standards may improve through enforceable requirements.","Duplicate teams can increase administrative costs.","The shop may improve profitability at unchanged prices."],"answer":2,"why":"Duplicate teams can increase administrative costs."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0299","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to decentralise decisions in a very large retailer. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More competition may lower prices.","Local managers may respond more quickly to customers.","The future pool of qualified applicants may grow.","Fixed cost per unit could fall."],"answer":1,"why":"Local managers may respond more quickly to customers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0300","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A growing retailer suffers coordination problems between many regional branches. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost.","Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled.","Poor communication and management can increase cost per unit as size expands.","Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers."],"answer":2,"why":"Poor communication and management can increase cost per unit as size expands."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0001","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage reliable digital payment services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by money as a medium of exchange. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Money serves as a commonly accepted means of payment that reduces the need for barter.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0002","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A worker saves part of a monthly salary to buy furniture next year. Decision-makers are considering whether to promote safe and accessible savings facilities. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by money as a store of value. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Money can retain purchasing power for use in later transactions.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A worker saves part of a monthly salary to buy furniture next year.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0003","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A country's central bank changes the policy interest rate after inflation rises. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by central bank. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A central bank oversees monetary conditions and usually issues currency and supports banking-system stability.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A country's central bank changes the policy interest rate after inflation rises.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0004","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand responsible lending to small businesses. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by commercial bank. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A commercial bank accepts deposits, processes payments and provides loans to households and businesses.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0005","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport. Decision-makers are considering whether to replace easily counterfeited notes with secure durable currency. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by characteristics of money. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Effective money is durable, portable, divisible, recognisable and generally accepted.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0006","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A family uses a bank loan to buy a refrigerator rather than waiting two years. Decision-makers are considering whether to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by household borrowing. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Borrowing enables expenditure now in exchange for future repayments, often including interest.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A family uses a bank loan to buy a refrigerator rather than waiting two years.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0007","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Following an interest-rate rise, a household postpones replacing its car. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by interest rates and households. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Higher interest rates generally increase rewards for saving and costs of borrowing.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Following an interest-rate rise, a household postpones replacing its car.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0008","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Consumers reduce discretionary purchases after news of possible redundancies. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide credible information to improve households' economic confidence. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by consumer confidence. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Consumer confidence reflects expectations about future income and economic conditions.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Consumers reduce discretionary purchases after news of possible redundancies.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0009","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve working conditions to attract skilled teachers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by non-wage job factors. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — People consider conditions, training, security, location and satisfaction as well as pay when choosing occupations.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0010","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A restaurant recruits additional chefs when bookings rise. Decision-makers are considering whether to hire additional chefs during sustained demand growth. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by demand for labour. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Demand for labour is derived from demand for the goods and services workers help produce.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A restaurant recruits additional chefs when bookings rise.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0011","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A government increases the legal hourly minimum for retail employees. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by national minimum wage. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A national minimum wage is a legal lower limit on wages paid to covered workers.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A government increases the legal hourly minimum for retail employees.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0012","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase task specialisation in a shoe factory. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by division of labour. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Division of labour is the splitting of production into specialised tasks performed by different workers.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0013","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A forestry business grows and harvests timber for local sawmills. Decision-makers are considering whether to support sustainable timber harvesting firms. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by primary-sector firm. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A primary-sector firm extracts or harvests natural resources.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A forestry business grows and harvests timber for local sawmills.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0014","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A workshop converts timber into chairs and shelving. Decision-makers are considering whether to upgrade machinery at a furniture manufacturer. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by secondary-sector firm. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A secondary-sector firm processes raw materials or manufactures products.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A workshop converts timber into chairs and shelving.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0015","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A logistics company transports manufactured goods to stores. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand local logistics services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by tertiary-sector firm. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A tertiary-sector firm provides services rather than extracting or manufacturing products.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A logistics company transports manufactured goods to stores.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0016","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts. Decision-makers are considering whether to hire more skilled installers when solar-panel orders rise. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by derived demand for factors. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Demand for an input depends partly on demand for the final good it helps produce.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0017","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A handmade textile producer hires artisans instead of buying automated weaving machines. Decision-makers are considering whether to retain skilled handmade production methods. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by labour-intensive production. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Labour-intensive methods use a relatively high proportion of labour compared with capital.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A handmade textile producer hires artisans instead of buying automated weaving machines.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0018","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment. Decision-makers are considering whether to automate a high-volume bottling plant. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by capital-intensive production. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Capital-intensive production uses a relatively high proportion of machinery and equipment.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0019","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A bakery increases loaves per baker-hour after introducing training and better tools. Decision-makers are considering whether to train bakers to improve output per hour. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by labour productivity. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Labour productivity measures output per worker or per worker-hour over a period.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A bakery increases loaves per baker-hour after introducing training and better tools.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0020","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A workshop pays $2,000 monthly rent whether it produces 100 or 300 tables. Decision-makers are considering whether to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by fixed cost. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A fixed cost does not vary with output over the relevant short-run period.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A workshop pays $2,000 monthly rent whether it produces 100 or 300 tables.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0021","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A firm spends $12,000 producing 600 lamps, giving an average cost of $20 per lamp. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase efficient output to spread fixed costs. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by average total cost. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Average total cost equals total cost divided by units produced.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A firm spends $12,000 producing 600 lamps, giving an average cost of $20 per lamp.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0022","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue. Decision-makers are considering whether to offer a targeted promotion to increase cake sales. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by total revenue. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Total revenue is the money received from sales, calculated as price times quantity sold.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0023","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Many independent cafés compete for customers along a busy street. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by competitive market. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A competitive market has numerous firms exerting pressure on price, quality and choice.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Many independent cafés compete for customers along a busy street.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0024","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town. Decision-makers are considering whether to regulate the sole piped-water supplier. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by monopoly market. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A monopoly market has one supplier of a good or service with no close rival.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0025","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand a food-processing firm to gain scale economies. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by economies of scale. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Economies of scale occur when a larger scale of production lowers average total cost.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0001","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage reliable digital payment services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to encourage reliable digital payment services. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: More transactions may be completed quickly. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Small businesses may reach customers who do not carry cash. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0002","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A worker saves part of a monthly salary to buy furniture next year. Decision-makers are considering whether to promote safe and accessible savings facilities. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to promote safe and accessible savings facilities might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Households may be better prepared for emergencies. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Savings can finance larger future purchases. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0003","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country's central bank changes the policy interest rate after inflation rises. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Slower price increases can protect purchasing power. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0004","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand responsible lending to small businesses. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers expand responsible lending to small businesses. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Investment may support more production and jobs. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Entrepreneurs may obtain funds unavailable from their own savings. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0005","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport. Decision-makers are considering whether to replace easily counterfeited notes with secure durable currency. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to replace easily counterfeited notes with secure durable currency could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Traders may become more confident accepting payment. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Fewer damaged notes could reduce replacement costs. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0006","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A household increases monthly deposits after receiving a pay rise. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage households to maintain emergency savings. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to encourage households to maintain emergency savings. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Having reserves may reduce reliance on expensive loans. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0007","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A family uses a bank loan to buy a refrigerator rather than waiting two years. Decision-makers are considering whether to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Households may obtain useful equipment sooner. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Purchases can support demand for local suppliers. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0008","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Following an interest-rate rise, a household postpones replacing its car. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Savers may earn more income from deposits. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Households may build stronger financial buffers. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0009","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Consumers reduce discretionary purchases after news of possible redundancies. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide credible information to improve households' economic confidence. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers provide credible information to improve households' economic confidence. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: More stable demand can help firms plan production. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0010","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve working conditions to attract skilled teachers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to improve working conditions to attract skilled teachers could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Better conditions may improve staff retention. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Happier staff may provide more consistent service. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0011","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A restaurant recruits additional chefs when bookings rise. Decision-makers are considering whether to hire additional chefs during sustained demand growth. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to hire additional chefs during sustained demand growth. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: The restaurant may serve more meals. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Reduced workloads may improve service quality. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0012","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A local hospital struggles to recruit nurses because too few people have the required qualifications. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand training places for nurses. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to expand training places for nurses might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: The future pool of qualified applicants may grow. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Staff shortages and waiting times may decline. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0013","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A government increases the legal hourly minimum for retail employees. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Higher pay could reduce in-work poverty. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0014","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase task specialisation in a shoe factory. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers increase task specialisation in a shoe factory. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Practice may raise labour productivity. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Shorter task changes may reduce wasted time. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0015","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A forestry business grows and harvests timber for local sawmills. Decision-makers are considering whether to support sustainable timber harvesting firms. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to support sustainable timber harvesting firms could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Rural employment may increase. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0016","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A workshop converts timber into chairs and shelving. Decision-makers are considering whether to upgrade machinery at a furniture manufacturer. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to upgrade machinery at a furniture manufacturer. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Output per employee may rise. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: More consistent products may attract buyers. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0017","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A logistics company transports manufactured goods to stores. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand local logistics services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to expand local logistics services might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Firms may deliver goods more reliably. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Improved distribution can support sales. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0018","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Two competing supermarket chains agree to combine their operations. Decision-makers are considering whether to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Overlapping distribution networks may become more efficient. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0019","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts. Decision-makers are considering whether to hire more skilled installers when solar-panel orders rise. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers hire more skilled installers when solar-panel orders rise. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: The firm may meet orders faster. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Additional installations may raise revenue. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0020","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A handmade textile producer hires artisans instead of buying automated weaving machines. Decision-makers are considering whether to retain skilled handmade production methods. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to retain skilled handmade production methods could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Products can be customised for individual buyers. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: The business may support local craft employment. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0021","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment. Decision-makers are considering whether to automate a high-volume bottling plant. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to automate a high-volume bottling plant. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: High output may lower unit costs. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Automated systems can improve uniformity. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0022","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A bakery increases loaves per baker-hour after introducing training and better tools. Decision-makers are considering whether to train bakers to improve output per hour. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to train bakers to improve output per hour might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Higher productivity may reduce costs per loaf. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Faster production can allow more sales. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0023","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A workshop pays $2,000 monthly rent whether it produces 100 or 300 tables. Decision-makers are considering whether to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: The business may have lower total costs at every output level. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Lower fixed costs may help survival during weak demand. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0024","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A sandwich shop uses more bread and filling when it makes more sandwiches. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce ingredient waste per sandwich. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers reduce ingredient waste per sandwich. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Variable cost per sandwich may fall. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: The shop may improve profitability at unchanged prices. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0025","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A firm spends $12,000 producing 600 lamps, giving an average cost of $20 per lamp. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase efficient output to spread fixed costs. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to increase efficient output to spread fixed costs could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Fixed cost per unit could fall. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Lower unit costs may improve price competitiveness. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0026","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue. Decision-makers are considering whether to offer a targeted promotion to increase cake sales. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to offer a targeted promotion to increase cake sales. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Additional sales could increase total revenue. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: More customers might make later repeat purchases. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0027","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Many independent cafés compete for customers along a busy street. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: More competition may lower prices. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Consumers could gain greater variety. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0028","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town. Decision-makers are considering whether to regulate the sole piped-water supplier. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to regulate the sole piped-water supplier. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Service standards may improve through enforceable requirements. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0029","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand a food-processing firm to gain scale economies. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers expand a food-processing firm to gain scale economies. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Specialised machinery may increase efficiency. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0030","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A growing retailer suffers coordination problems between many regional branches. Decision-makers are considering whether to decentralise decisions in a very large retailer. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to decentralise decisions in a very large retailer could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Local managers may respond more quickly to customers. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0001","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage reliable digital payment services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to encourage reliable digital payment services. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: More transactions may be completed quickly. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Small businesses may reach customers who do not carry cash. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: A common payment medium makes trade easier because a double coincidence of wants is unnecessary. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0002","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A worker saves part of a monthly salary to buy furniture next year. Decision-makers are considering whether to promote safe and accessible savings facilities. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to promote safe and accessible savings facilities may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Households may be better prepared for emergencies. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Savings can finance larger future purchases. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0003","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country's central bank changes the policy interest rate after inflation rises. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Slower price increases can protect purchasing power. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0004","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand responsible lending to small businesses. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to expand responsible lending to small businesses in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Investment may support more production and jobs. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Entrepreneurs may obtain funds unavailable from their own savings. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0005","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport. Decision-makers are considering whether to replace easily counterfeited notes with secure durable currency. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to replace easily counterfeited notes with secure durable currency could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Traders may become more confident accepting payment. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Fewer damaged notes could reduce replacement costs. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0006","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A household increases monthly deposits after receiving a pay rise. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage households to maintain emergency savings. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to encourage households to maintain emergency savings. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Having reserves may reduce reliance on expensive loans. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0007","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A family uses a bank loan to buy a refrigerator rather than waiting two years. Decision-makers are considering whether to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Households may obtain useful equipment sooner. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Purchases can support demand for local suppliers. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0008","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Following an interest-rate rise, a household postpones replacing its car. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Savers may earn more income from deposits. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Households may build stronger financial buffers. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0009","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Consumers reduce discretionary purchases after news of possible redundancies. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide credible information to improve households' economic confidence. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to provide credible information to improve households' economic confidence in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: More stable demand can help firms plan production. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0010","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve working conditions to attract skilled teachers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to improve working conditions to attract skilled teachers could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Better conditions may improve staff retention. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Happier staff may provide more consistent service. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Non-financial benefits can compensate for some differences in wages. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0011","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A restaurant recruits additional chefs when bookings rise. Decision-makers are considering whether to hire additional chefs during sustained demand growth. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to hire additional chefs during sustained demand growth. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: The restaurant may serve more meals. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Reduced workloads may improve service quality. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0012","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A local hospital struggles to recruit nurses because too few people have the required qualifications. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand training places for nurses. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to expand training places for nurses may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: The future pool of qualified applicants may grow. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Staff shortages and waiting times may decline. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0013","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A government increases the legal hourly minimum for retail employees. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Higher pay could reduce in-work poverty. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0014","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase task specialisation in a shoe factory. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to increase task specialisation in a shoe factory in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Practice may raise labour productivity. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Shorter task changes may reduce wasted time. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Repeated specialist tasks can raise speed but may reduce flexibility. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0015","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A forestry business grows and harvests timber for local sawmills. Decision-makers are considering whether to support sustainable timber harvesting firms. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to support sustainable timber harvesting firms could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Rural employment may increase. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0016","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A workshop converts timber into chairs and shelving. Decision-makers are considering whether to upgrade machinery at a furniture manufacturer. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to upgrade machinery at a furniture manufacturer. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Output per employee may rise. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: More consistent products may attract buyers. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Manufacturing adds value by transforming inputs into finished goods. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0017","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A logistics company transports manufactured goods to stores. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand local logistics services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to expand local logistics services may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Firms may deliver goods more reliably. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Improved distribution can support sales. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Services such as transport and retail help goods reach consumers. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0018","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Two competing supermarket chains agree to combine their operations. Decision-makers are considering whether to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Overlapping distribution networks may become more efficient. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Larger scale may reduce unit costs but lessen rivalry. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0019","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts. Decision-makers are considering whether to hire more skilled installers when solar-panel orders rise. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to hire more skilled installers when solar-panel orders rise in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: The firm may meet orders faster. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Additional installations may raise revenue. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Higher final-product demand raises the need for inputs used in production. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0020","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A handmade textile producer hires artisans instead of buying automated weaving machines. Decision-makers are considering whether to retain skilled handmade production methods. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to retain skilled handmade production methods could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Products can be customised for individual buyers. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: The business may support local craft employment. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Labour-intensive output may suit small production runs and places with relatively low wages. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0021","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment. Decision-makers are considering whether to automate a high-volume bottling plant. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to automate a high-volume bottling plant. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: High output may lower unit costs. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Automated systems can improve uniformity. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Machines can raise output per worker when production is large and standardised. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0022","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A bakery increases loaves per baker-hour after introducing training and better tools. Decision-makers are considering whether to train bakers to improve output per hour. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to train bakers to improve output per hour may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Higher productivity may reduce costs per loaf. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Faster production can allow more sales. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0023","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A workshop pays $2,000 monthly rent whether it produces 100 or 300 tables. Decision-makers are considering whether to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: The business may have lower total costs at every output level. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Lower fixed costs may help survival during weak demand. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Fixed costs must be covered even when output falls. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0024","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A sandwich shop uses more bread and filling when it makes more sandwiches. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce ingredient waste per sandwich. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to reduce ingredient waste per sandwich in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Variable cost per sandwich may fall. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: The shop may improve profitability at unchanged prices. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Total variable costs rise when additional units require more materials. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0025","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A firm spends $12,000 producing 600 lamps, giving an average cost of $20 per lamp. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase efficient output to spread fixed costs. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to increase efficient output to spread fixed costs could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Fixed cost per unit could fall. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Lower unit costs may improve price competitiveness. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0026","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue. Decision-makers are considering whether to offer a targeted promotion to increase cake sales. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to offer a targeted promotion to increase cake sales. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Additional sales could increase total revenue. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: More customers might make later repeat purchases. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0027","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Many independent cafés compete for customers along a busy street. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: More competition may lower prices. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Consumers could gain greater variety. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0028","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town. Decision-makers are considering whether to regulate the sole piped-water supplier. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to regulate the sole piped-water supplier could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Service standards may improve through enforceable requirements. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Lack of direct competition may allow higher prices or less choice. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0029","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand a food-processing firm to gain scale economies. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to expand a food-processing firm to gain scale economies in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Specialised machinery may increase efficiency. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0030","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A growing retailer suffers coordination problems between many regional branches. Decision-makers are considering whether to decentralise decisions in a very large retailer. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to decentralise decisions in a very large retailer could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Local managers may respond more quickly to customers. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Poor communication and management can increase cost per unit as size expands. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0001","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage reliable digital payment services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to encourage reliable digital payment services would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): More transactions may be completed quickly.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Small businesses may reach customers who do not carry cash.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Digital exclusion may affect people without smartphones.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Payment systems may fail during electricity or network outages.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0002","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A worker saves part of a monthly salary to buy furniture next year. Decision-makers are considering whether to promote safe and accessible savings facilities. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to promote safe and accessible savings facilities in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Households may be better prepared for emergencies.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Savings can finance larger future purchases.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Inflation may reduce the real value of cash savings.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Low-income families may have little income left to save.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0003","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country's central bank changes the policy interest rate after inflation rises. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Slower price increases can protect purchasing power.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Higher borrowing costs can discourage investment.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Policy effects may take time and depend on borrower responses.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0004","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand responsible lending to small businesses. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to expand responsible lending to small businesses is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Investment may support more production and jobs.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Entrepreneurs may obtain funds unavailable from their own savings.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Borrowers may default and create bank losses.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Loans may be unaffordable when interest rates are high.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0005","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport. Decision-makers are considering whether to replace easily counterfeited notes with secure durable currency. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to replace easily counterfeited notes with secure durable currency would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Traders may become more confident accepting payment.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Fewer damaged notes could reduce replacement costs.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Producing secure notes may be expensive.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Counterfeiters may eventually develop new methods.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0006","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A household increases monthly deposits after receiving a pay rise. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage households to maintain emergency savings. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to encourage households to maintain emergency savings would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Having reserves may reduce reliance on expensive loans.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Less spending may reduce current demand for local businesses.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Families on low incomes may be unable to save much.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0007","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A family uses a bank loan to buy a refrigerator rather than waiting two years. Decision-makers are considering whether to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Households may obtain useful equipment sooner.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Purchases can support demand for local suppliers.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Debt repayments reduce future disposable income.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Borrowers may struggle if their income falls.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0008","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Following an interest-rate rise, a household postpones replacing its car. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Savers may earn more income from deposits.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Households may build stronger financial buffers.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Borrowers may face higher repayments at the same time.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0009","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Consumers reduce discretionary purchases after news of possible redundancies. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide credible information to improve households' economic confidence. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to provide credible information to improve households' economic confidence. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): More stable demand can help firms plan production.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Optimism cannot prevent genuine job losses.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Excessive confidence could encourage unsustainable debt.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0010","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve working conditions to attract skilled teachers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to improve working conditions to attract skilled teachers is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Better conditions may improve staff retention.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Happier staff may provide more consistent service.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Improving facilities can be expensive.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Low pay may still push qualified teachers elsewhere.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0011","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A restaurant recruits additional chefs when bookings rise. Decision-makers are considering whether to hire additional chefs during sustained demand growth. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to hire additional chefs during sustained demand growth would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): The restaurant may serve more meals.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Reduced workloads may improve service quality.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Wage costs rise with the number of staff.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Bookings may fall before recruitment costs are recovered.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0012","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A local hospital struggles to recruit nurses because too few people have the required qualifications. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand training places for nurses. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to expand training places for nurses would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): The future pool of qualified applicants may grow.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Staff shortages and waiting times may decline.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Training is costly and takes several years.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Graduates may migrate to higher-paying countries.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0013","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A government increases the legal hourly minimum for retail employees. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Higher pay could reduce in-work poverty.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Employers may reduce hiring if labour costs rise sharply.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Some workers already earning more than the minimum may not benefit.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0014","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase task specialisation in a shoe factory. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to increase task specialisation in a shoe factory in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Practice may raise labour productivity.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Shorter task changes may reduce wasted time.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Repetitive work may reduce staff motivation.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): A missing specialist can interrupt the whole production line.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0015","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A forestry business grows and harvests timber for local sawmills. Decision-makers are considering whether to support sustainable timber harvesting firms. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to support sustainable timber harvesting firms. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Rural employment may increase.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Overharvesting may damage forests and biodiversity.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): World timber prices may make income unstable.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0016","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A workshop converts timber into chairs and shelving. Decision-makers are considering whether to upgrade machinery at a furniture manufacturer. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to upgrade machinery at a furniture manufacturer is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Output per employee may rise.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): More consistent products may attract buyers.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): The equipment requires capital expenditure.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Automation may reduce some production jobs.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0017","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A logistics company transports manufactured goods to stores. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand local logistics services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to expand local logistics services would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Firms may deliver goods more reliably.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Improved distribution can support sales.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): More transport can increase congestion and emissions.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Fuel-price increases may make services expensive.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0018","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Two competing supermarket chains agree to combine their operations. Decision-makers are considering whether to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Overlapping distribution networks may become more efficient.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Consumers may face less choice or higher prices.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Integration costs may prevent expected savings.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0019","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts. Decision-makers are considering whether to hire more skilled installers when solar-panel orders rise. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to hire more skilled installers when solar-panel orders rise would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): The firm may meet orders faster.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Additional installations may raise revenue.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Higher labour costs increase operating expenses.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Orders may be temporary, leaving excess staff later.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0020","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A handmade textile producer hires artisans instead of buying automated weaving machines. Decision-makers are considering whether to retain skilled handmade production methods. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to retain skilled handmade production methods in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Products can be customised for individual buyers.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): The business may support local craft employment.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Unit costs can be high if wages rise.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Output may be slower than with suitable machinery.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0021","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment. Decision-makers are considering whether to automate a high-volume bottling plant. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to automate a high-volume bottling plant. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): High output may lower unit costs.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Automated systems can improve uniformity.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Initial investment can be very expensive.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Breakdowns can stop a large share of production.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0022","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A bakery increases loaves per baker-hour after introducing training and better tools. Decision-makers are considering whether to train bakers to improve output per hour. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to train bakers to improve output per hour is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Higher productivity may reduce costs per loaf.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Faster production can allow more sales.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Training takes time away from current output.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Poor motivation may limit gains from new skills.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0023","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A workshop pays $2,000 monthly rent whether it produces 100 or 300 tables. Decision-makers are considering whether to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): The business may have lower total costs at every output level.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Lower fixed costs may help survival during weak demand.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): A cheaper location may be less accessible to customers.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): The landlord may refuse to change the agreement.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0024","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A sandwich shop uses more bread and filling when it makes more sandwiches. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce ingredient waste per sandwich. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to reduce ingredient waste per sandwich would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Variable cost per sandwich may fall.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): The shop may improve profitability at unchanged prices.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Quality may suffer if portions become too small.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Ingredient prices may rise despite lower waste.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0025","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A firm spends $12,000 producing 600 lamps, giving an average cost of $20 per lamp. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase efficient output to spread fixed costs. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to increase efficient output to spread fixed costs would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Fixed cost per unit could fall.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Lower unit costs may improve price competitiveness.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Output beyond efficient capacity may raise costs.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Extra lamps may be difficult to sell.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0026","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue. Decision-makers are considering whether to offer a targeted promotion to increase cake sales. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to offer a targeted promotion to increase cake sales in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Additional sales could increase total revenue.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): More customers might make later repeat purchases.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Discounts lower revenue per cake.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Production may be unable to meet a large increase in demand.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0027","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Many independent cafés compete for customers along a busy street. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): More competition may lower prices.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Consumers could gain greater variety.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Small firms may struggle to earn enough profit.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Some locations may not support many viable businesses.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0028","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town. Decision-makers are considering whether to regulate the sole piped-water supplier. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to regulate the sole piped-water supplier is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Service standards may improve through enforceable requirements.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Regulation has administrative costs.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0029","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand a food-processing firm to gain scale economies. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to expand a food-processing firm to gain scale economies would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Specialised machinery may increase efficiency.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Expanding requires capital and management capacity.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): The firm may face insufficient demand for the larger output.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0030","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A growing retailer suffers coordination problems between many regional branches. Decision-makers are considering whether to decentralise decisions in a very large retailer. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to decentralise decisions in a very large retailer would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Local managers may respond more quickly to customers.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Different branches may lose consistency.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Duplicate teams can increase administrative costs.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0001","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes macroeconomic aims?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Infrastructure investment improves facilities such as transport, communication and power networks.","A progressive tax takes a larger proportion of income as income rises.","A budget deficit occurs when government expenditure exceeds government revenue in a period.","Macroeconomic aims are broad objectives such as growth, employment, price stability and external balance."],"answer":3,"why":"Macroeconomic aims: Macroeconomic aims are broad objectives such as growth, employment, price stability and external balance."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0002","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A new government sets targets for inflation, joblessness and sustainable growth. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Deregulation","Macroeconomic aims","Cost-push inflation","Money supply"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates macroeconomic aims: Macroeconomic aims are broad objectives such as growth, employment, price stability and external balance."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0003","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of macroeconomic aims?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million.","A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession.","A new government sets targets for inflation, joblessness and sustainable growth.","A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year."],"answer":2,"why":"A new government sets targets for inflation, joblessness and sustainable growth. This illustrates macroeconomic aims are broad objectives such as growth, employment, price stability and external balance."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0004","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to macroeconomic aims?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Lower sales lead firms to cut production and employment.","Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity.","Aims guide policy priorities but actions towards one aim can affect another.","Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand."],"answer":2,"why":"Aims guide policy priorities but actions towards one aim can affect another."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0005","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Government revenue is $53 billion and spending is $58 billion. Calculate the budget balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$111 billion deficit","$5 billion surplus","$5 billion deficit","$58 billion deficit"],"answer":2,"why":"Spending exceeds revenue by $58 − $53 = $5 billion, so this is a deficit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0006","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to publish clear and measurable economic policy targets. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Investment demand and employment may rise.","Governments may focus too narrowly on measured targets.","Higher output may create jobs.","Workers may find suitable vacancies faster."],"answer":1,"why":"Governments may focus too narrowly on measured targets."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0007","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to publish clear and measurable economic policy targets?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Changes in product quality can complicate price comparisons.","Benefits depend on whether enough firms use the bridge.","Spending may be inefficient if projects are poorly selected.","Voters can assess whether economic outcomes match promises."],"answer":3,"why":"Voters can assess whether economic outcomes match promises."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0008","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to publish clear and measurable economic policy targets less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Entrepreneurs may spend less on administration.","Job losses could be smaller during the downturn.","External shocks can make targets difficult to achieve.","Long-run growth and competitiveness may improve."],"answer":2,"why":"External shocks can make targets difficult to achieve."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0009","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to publish clear and measurable economic policy targets. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Repair spending may restore employment and transport links.","Businesses may make plans with more confidence.","Investment demand and employment may rise.","Households may plan spending with more certainty."],"answer":1,"why":"Businesses may make plans with more confidence."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0010","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A new government sets targets for inflation, joblessness and sustainable growth. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Falling total demand can cause output, employment and incomes to contract.","Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity.","A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources.","Aims guide policy priorities but actions towards one aim can affect another."],"answer":3,"why":"Aims guide policy priorities but actions towards one aim can affect another."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0011","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes growth versus environmental sustainability?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Education and training can improve human capital and worker productivity.","Faster output growth can conflict with environmental protection if production increases emissions.","The Consumer Prices Index tracks the price of a representative basket of consumer goods and services.","Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level of goods and services."],"answer":1,"why":"Growth versus environmental sustainability: Faster output growth can conflict with environmental protection if production increases emissions."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0012","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country considers expanding coal-based electricity generation to boost industrial output. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Growth from better resources","Demand-pull inflation","Growth versus environmental sustainability","Education and training policy"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates growth versus environmental sustainability: Faster output growth can conflict with environmental protection if production increases emissions."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0013","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Real GDP rises from $300 billion to $312 billion in one year. What is the real GDP growth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["6%","12%","104%","4%"],"answer":3,"why":"Real GDP growth = ($312 − $300) / $300 × 100 = 4%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0014","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to growth versus environmental sustainability?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand.","A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased.","Higher average tax burdens on the wealthy can help redistribute disposable income.","More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion."],"answer":3,"why":"More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0015","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Training may take years.","Maintaining matching services requires funding.","Output can expand with less pollution per unit.","Growth may increase resource depletion and pollution."],"answer":2,"why":"Output can expand with less pollution per unit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0016","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices.","Workers and firms may negotiate with better price information.","Renewable installations require upfront funding.","Input costs may become more stable."],"answer":2,"why":"Renewable installations require upfront funding."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0017","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Other barriers such as finance may still block entry.","Benefits may be unevenly shared across households.","Cost-push inflation may persist despite weaker demand.","Cleaner technology may create new employment opportunities."],"answer":3,"why":"Cleaner technology may create new employment opportunities."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0018","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Intermittent power generation may require storage investment.","Long-run growth and competitiveness may improve.","A stable financial environment can improve confidence.","Firms can fill open posts more quickly."],"answer":0,"why":"Intermittent power generation may require storage investment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0019","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Workers and firms may negotiate with better price information.","Firms may enter markets more easily.","Demand for essentials may be maintained.","Output can expand with less pollution per unit."],"answer":3,"why":"Output can expand with less pollution per unit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0020","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country considers expanding coal-based electricity generation to boost industrial output. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion.","Lower sales lead firms to cut production and employment.","Higher disposable income and public purchases may support output and employment.","A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased."],"answer":0,"why":"More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0021","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The CPI rises from 100 to 105.0. What is the inflation rate for this period?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["105.0%","5%","8%","105%"],"answer":1,"why":"Inflation rate = (105.0 − 100) / 100 × 100 = 5%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0022","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government proposes a large spending stimulus when factories are near full capacity. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Inflation","Government budget surplus","Expansionary fiscal policy","Employment versus inflation"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates employment versus inflation: Policies that raise total demand may reduce cyclical unemployment but also increase inflationary pressure."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0023","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of employment versus inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods.","A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall.","A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million.","A government proposes a large spending stimulus when factories are near full capacity."],"answer":3,"why":"A government proposes a large spending stimulus when factories are near full capacity. This illustrates policies that raise total demand may reduce cyclical unemployment but also increase inflationary pressure."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0024","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to employment versus inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased.","Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited.","Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices.","Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards."],"answer":1,"why":"Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0025","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Tax revenue may fall.","Tax relief reduces revenue and training costs money.","Surveys may miss discouraged workers outside the labour force.","Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand."],"answer":3,"why":"Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0026","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Workers may find suitable vacancies faster.","Training takes time to improve employment.","Construction jobs may be created.","Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power."],"answer":1,"why":"Training takes time to improve employment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0027","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Productive capacity could expand over time.","A shortage of skilled builders could raise costs.","Stimulus may be ineffective if households save extra income.","Cost-push inflation may persist despite weaker demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Productive capacity could expand over time."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0028","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Some unemployed workers may lack access to courses.","Better roads can support future private investment.","Comparable statistics can guide training and job programmes.","After-tax income inequality may fall."],"answer":0,"why":"Some unemployed workers may lack access to courses."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0029","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand.","Workers and firms may negotiate with better price information.","Firms may access more productive workers.","Input costs may become more stable."],"answer":0,"why":"Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0030","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The labour force of an economy is 80,000 and 4,800 people are unemployed and seeking work. What is the unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["94%","0.06%","6%","8%"],"answer":2,"why":"Unemployment rate = (4,800 / 80,000) × 100 = 6%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0031","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes redistribution of income?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Redistribution policies seek to reduce income inequality through taxes and transfers.","Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level of goods and services.","A budget deficit occurs when government expenditure exceeds government revenue in a period.","The Consumer Prices Index tracks the price of a representative basket of consumer goods and services."],"answer":0,"why":"Redistribution of income: Redistribution policies seek to reduce income inequality through taxes and transfers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0032","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Frictional unemployment","Structural unemployment","Infrastructure investment","Redistribution of income"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates redistribution of income: Redistribution policies seek to reduce income inequality through taxes and transfers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0033","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of redistribution of income?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers.","A flat tax on basic household purchases uses a greater share of a low earner's income.","A central bank cuts its policy interest rate after consumer spending weakens.","A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation."],"answer":3,"why":"A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation. This illustrates redistribution policies seek to reduce income inequality through taxes and transfers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0034","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to redistribution of income?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power.","Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand.","Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable.","Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output."],"answer":0,"why":"Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0035","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand targeted support for the lowest-income households. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More families may afford essential goods.","Inflation may rise if demand grows too quickly.","Lower government spending may weaken current demand.","Very high rates may weaken incentives to work or invest."],"answer":0,"why":"More families may afford essential goods."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0036","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand targeted support for the lowest-income households. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher output may create jobs.","Higher taxes may affect incentives for some earners.","Workers may gain access to new jobs.","Health damage from emissions may fall."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher taxes may affect incentives for some earners."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0037","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand targeted support for the lowest-income households?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Changes in product quality can complicate price comparisons.","Spending may be inefficient if projects are poorly selected.","Greater equality may improve social outcomes.","Other barriers such as finance may still block entry."],"answer":2,"why":"Greater equality may improve social outcomes."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0038","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Government revenue is $65 billion and spending is $78 billion. Calculate the budget balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$143 billion deficit","$13 billion surplus","$78 billion deficit","$13 billion deficit"],"answer":3,"why":"Spending exceeds revenue by $78 − $65 = $13 billion, so this is a deficit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0039","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand targeted support for the lowest-income households. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Workers may find suitable vacancies faster.","More families may afford essential goods.","Firms may produce more at lower unit cost.","Early warnings may help monetary authorities limit inflation risks."],"answer":1,"why":"More families may afford essential goods."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0040","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher average tax burdens on the wealthy can help redistribute disposable income.","Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power.","The 5% real GDP rise signals more goods and services were produced.","More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages."],"answer":1,"why":"Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0041","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes government budget deficit?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Policies that raise total demand may reduce cyclical unemployment but also increase inflationary pressure.","Money supply refers to the stock of money available in an economy at a given time.","A budget deficit occurs when government expenditure exceeds government revenue in a period.","Growth can raise incomes and job opportunities while increasing environmental pressures."],"answer":2,"why":"Government budget deficit: A budget deficit occurs when government expenditure exceeds government revenue in a period."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0042","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Consequences of growth","Government budget deficit","Cyclical unemployment","Deregulation"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates government budget deficit: A budget deficit occurs when government expenditure exceeds government revenue in a period."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0043","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of government budget deficit?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A government proposes a large spending stimulus when factories are near full capacity.","Strong credit growth leads shoppers to increase spending while factories are at full capacity.","A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers.","A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year."],"answer":3,"why":"A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year. This illustrates a budget deficit occurs when government expenditure exceeds government revenue in a period."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0044","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to government budget deficit?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased.","The 5% real GDP rise signals more goods and services were produced.","A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources.","Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited."],"answer":2,"why":"A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0045","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to temporarily run a deficit to repair damaged roads. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Very tight policy may lower employment.","Weak safeguards could reduce product quality or safety.","Repair spending may restore employment and transport links.","Governments may focus too narrowly on measured targets."],"answer":2,"why":"Repair spending may restore employment and transport links."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0046","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Real GDP rises from $700 billion to $756 billion in one year. What is the real GDP growth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["10%","56%","8%","108%"],"answer":2,"why":"Real GDP growth = ($756 − $700) / $700 × 100 = 8%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0047","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to temporarily run a deficit to repair damaged roads?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Benefits depend on whether enough firms use the bridge.","Banks may not pass lower rates to borrowers.","Capital flows also depend on risk and confidence.","Better roads can support future private investment."],"answer":3,"why":"Better roads can support future private investment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0048","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to temporarily run a deficit to repair damaged roads less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher spending may increase production and employment.","Spending may be inefficient if projects are poorly selected.","An appreciated currency can lower input costs.","Workers may qualify for better-paid employment."],"answer":1,"why":"Spending may be inefficient if projects are poorly selected."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0049","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to temporarily run a deficit to repair damaged roads. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand.","Households may plan spending with more certainty.","Firms may access more productive workers.","Repair spending may restore employment and transport links."],"answer":3,"why":"Repair spending may restore employment and transport links."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0050","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Stronger demand for domestic currency may cause appreciation and lower import prices.","Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards.","Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment.","A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources."],"answer":3,"why":"A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0051","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes government budget surplus?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A budget surplus occurs when government revenue exceeds government expenditure in a period.","A progressive tax takes a larger proportion of income as income rises.","Structural unemployment results from a long-term mismatch between workers' skills or locations and available jobs.","Faster output growth can conflict with environmental protection if production increases emissions."],"answer":0,"why":"Government budget surplus: A budget surplus occurs when government revenue exceeds government expenditure in a period."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0052","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government receives $76 billion in revenue but spends $70 billion. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Growth from better resources","Employment versus inflation","Government budget surplus","Recession"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates government budget surplus: A budget surplus occurs when government revenue exceeds government expenditure in a period."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0053","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of government budget surplus?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher domestic interest rates attract some foreign investors seeking yield.","A traditional textile factory closes while new technology firms struggle to recruit technicians.","A government proposes a large spending stimulus when factories are near full capacity.","A government receives $76 billion in revenue but spends $70 billion."],"answer":3,"why":"A government receives $76 billion in revenue but spends $70 billion. This illustrates a budget surplus occurs when government revenue exceeds government expenditure in a period."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0054","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to government budget surplus?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion.","Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand.","Excess demand creates upward pressure on the overall price level.","A $6 billion surplus can reduce borrowing needs or debt."],"answer":3,"why":"A $6 billion surplus can reduce borrowing needs or debt."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0055","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The CPI rises from 110 to 119.9. What is the inflation rate for this period?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["109%","119.9%","12%","9%"],"answer":3,"why":"Inflation rate = (119.9 − 110) / 110 × 100 = 9%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0056","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use a temporary surplus to reduce public debt. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources.","Lower government spending may weaken current demand.","Inflationary pressure may ease.","Cheaper imports may reduce imported inflation."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower government spending may weaken current demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0057","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to use a temporary surplus to reduce public debt?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Fiscal capacity for later emergencies may improve.","Benefits may take years to appear.","Global commodity-price shocks may be difficult to control.","Other barriers such as finance may still block entry."],"answer":0,"why":"Fiscal capacity for later emergencies may improve."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0058","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to use a temporary surplus to reduce public debt less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Firms can fill open posts more quickly.","Debt repayment can crowd out urgent public investment.","A stable financial environment can improve confidence.","Long-run growth and competitiveness may improve."],"answer":1,"why":"Debt repayment can crowd out urgent public investment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0059","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to use a temporary surplus to reduce public debt. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices.","Workers may gain access to new jobs.","Future interest payments may decline.","Businesses may retain more workers."],"answer":2,"why":"Future interest payments may decline."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0060","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government receives $76 billion in revenue but spends $70 billion. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion.","Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power.","A $6 billion surplus can reduce borrowing needs or debt.","Falling total demand can cause output, employment and incomes to contract."],"answer":2,"why":"A $6 billion surplus can reduce borrowing needs or debt."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0061","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes progressive taxation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A budget surplus occurs when government revenue exceeds government expenditure in a period.","Increasing the policy interest rate generally discourages borrowing and encourages saving.","A progressive tax takes a larger proportion of income as income rises.","Cyclical unemployment occurs when weak total demand reduces firms' need for workers."],"answer":2,"why":"Progressive taxation: A progressive tax takes a larger proportion of income as income rises."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0062","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: High-income households pay a higher marginal rate of income tax than low-income households. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Recession","Progressive taxation","Inflation","Cyclical unemployment"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates progressive taxation: A progressive tax takes a larger proportion of income as income rises."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0063","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The labour force of an economy is 120,000 and 12,000 people are unemployed and seeking work. What is the unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["90%","0.1%","10%","12%"],"answer":2,"why":"Unemployment rate = (12,000 / 120,000) × 100 = 10%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0064","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to progressive taxation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power.","Stronger demand for domestic currency may cause appreciation and lower import prices.","Higher average tax burdens on the wealthy can help redistribute disposable income.","The unemployment rate is 5%, showing the share of the labour force without work and seeking it."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher average tax burdens on the wealthy can help redistribute disposable income."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0065","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Exports may become more expensive abroad.","Revenue may finance public health and education.","Training takes time to improve employment.","Projects need substantial funds up front."],"answer":1,"why":"Revenue may finance public health and education."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0066","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Very high rates may weaken incentives to work or invest.","Health damage from emissions may fall.","Output can expand with less pollution per unit.","Inflationary pressure may ease."],"answer":0,"why":"Very high rates may weaken incentives to work or invest."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0067","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Changes in product quality can complicate price comparisons.","After-tax income inequality may fall.","External shocks can make targets difficult to achieve.","Cost-push inflation may persist despite weaker demand."],"answer":1,"why":"After-tax income inequality may fall."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0068","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["An appreciated currency can lower input costs.","Productive capacity could expand over time.","Firms may make long-term contracts with greater confidence.","Tax avoidance can reduce the expected revenue."],"answer":3,"why":"Tax avoidance can reduce the expected revenue."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0069","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Households may plan spending with more certainty.","Inflationary pressure may ease.","Policy-makers can track labour-market changes.","Revenue may finance public health and education."],"answer":3,"why":"Revenue may finance public health and education."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0070","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: High-income households pay a higher marginal rate of income tax than low-income households. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher average tax burdens on the wealthy can help redistribute disposable income.","More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion.","Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices.","Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher average tax burdens on the wealthy can help redistribute disposable income."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0071","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Government revenue is $77 billion and spending is $82 billion. Calculate the budget balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$5 billion deficit","$159 billion deficit","$82 billion deficit","$5 billion surplus"],"answer":0,"why":"Spending exceeds revenue by $82 − $77 = $5 billion, so this is a deficit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0072","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A flat tax on basic household purchases uses a greater share of a low earner's income. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Infrastructure investment","Regressive taxation","Recession","Policies to promote growth"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates regressive taxation: A regressive tax takes a larger percentage of income from lower-income people than higher-income people."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0073","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of regressive taxation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation.","A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods.","A flat tax on basic household purchases uses a greater share of a low earner's income.","A statistics office compares the cost of a weighted basket with its cost in a base year."],"answer":2,"why":"A flat tax on basic household purchases uses a greater share of a low earner's income. This illustrates a regressive tax takes a larger percentage of income from lower-income people than higher-income people."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0074","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to regressive taxation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity.","Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment.","The same payment consumes a larger proportion of smaller incomes.","Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output."],"answer":2,"why":"The same payment consumes a larger proportion of smaller incomes."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0075","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power.","Alternative energy projects require large initial investments.","Government deficits can widen.","Renewable installations require upfront funding."],"answer":0,"why":"Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0076","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Demand for essentials may be maintained.","Tax revenue may fall.","Output can expand with less pollution per unit.","Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices."],"answer":1,"why":"Tax revenue may fall."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0077","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Capital flows also depend on risk and confidence.","Water shortages could limit future expansion.","Other barriers such as finance may still block entry.","Demand for basic foods may become more affordable."],"answer":3,"why":"Demand for basic foods may become more affordable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0078","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Workers may qualify for better-paid employment.","Growth may become more environmentally sustainable.","Businesses may not pass the full exemption to consumers.","Entrepreneurs may spend less on administration."],"answer":2,"why":"Businesses may not pass the full exemption to consumers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0079","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher output may create jobs.","Inflationary pressure may ease.","Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices.","Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power."],"answer":3,"why":"Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0080","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Real GDP rises from $1100 billion to $1144 billion in one year. What is the real GDP growth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["4%","6%","44%","104%"],"answer":0,"why":"Real GDP growth = ($1144 − $1100) / $1100 × 100 = 4%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0081","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes expansionary fiscal policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Faster output growth can conflict with environmental protection if production increases emissions.","Redistribution policies seek to reduce income inequality through taxes and transfers.","Expansionary fiscal policy uses higher government spending or lower taxes to raise total demand.","Economic growth is an increase in real output, commonly measured using real GDP."],"answer":2,"why":"Expansionary fiscal policy: Expansionary fiscal policy uses higher government spending or lower taxes to raise total demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0082","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Macroeconomic aims","Infrastructure investment","Higher policy interest rate","Expansionary fiscal policy"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates expansionary fiscal policy: Expansionary fiscal policy uses higher government spending or lower taxes to raise total demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0083","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of expansionary fiscal policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A new government sets targets for inflation, joblessness and sustainable growth.","A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession.","A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall.","During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs."],"answer":3,"why":"During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs. This illustrates expansionary fiscal policy uses higher government spending or lower taxes to raise total demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0084","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to expansionary fiscal policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power.","Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards.","Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output.","Higher disposable income and public purchases may support output and employment."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher disposable income and public purchases may support output and employment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0085","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase infrastructure spending during a demand-led recession. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Construction jobs may be created.","Exports may become more expensive abroad.","Renewable installations require upfront funding.","Very tight policy may lower employment."],"answer":0,"why":"Construction jobs may be created."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0086","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase infrastructure spending during a demand-led recession. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Households may plan spending with more certainty.","Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand.","Budget deficits may increase.","Firms may access more productive workers."],"answer":2,"why":"Budget deficits may increase."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0087","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to increase infrastructure spending during a demand-led recession?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Banks may not pass lower rates to borrowers.","Global commodity-price shocks may be difficult to control.","Graduates may leave the local economy.","Improved transport can raise later productive capacity."],"answer":3,"why":"Improved transport can raise later productive capacity."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0088","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The CPI rises from 120 to 123.6. What is the inflation rate for this period?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["3%","6%","103%","123.6%"],"answer":0,"why":"Inflation rate = (123.6 − 120) / 120 × 100 = 3%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0089","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to increase infrastructure spending during a demand-led recession. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices.","More families may afford essential goods.","Construction jobs may be created.","Health damage from emissions may fall."],"answer":2,"why":"Construction jobs may be created."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0090","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased.","More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion.","Higher disposable income and public purchases may support output and employment.","Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher disposable income and public purchases may support output and employment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0091","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes monetary policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Frictional unemployment is short-term joblessness while workers move between jobs.","Demand-pull inflation occurs when total demand grows faster than available output.","Monetary policy uses instruments such as interest rates and money supply to influence the economy.","The unemployment rate is the unemployed divided by the labour force, multiplied by 100."],"answer":2,"why":"Monetary policy: Monetary policy uses instruments such as interest rates and money supply to influence the economy."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0092","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A central bank cuts its policy interest rate after consumer spending weakens. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Expansionary fiscal policy","Monetary policy","Regressive taxation","Policies to promote growth"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates monetary policy: Monetary policy uses instruments such as interest rates and money supply to influence the economy."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0093","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of monetary policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A central bank cuts its policy interest rate after consumer spending weakens.","A government receives $76 billion in revenue but spends $70 billion.","A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall.","A country's inflation-adjusted GDP rises from $200 billion to $210 billion in a year."],"answer":0,"why":"A central bank cuts its policy interest rate after consumer spending weakens. This illustrates monetary policy uses instruments such as interest rates and money supply to influence the economy."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0094","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to monetary policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand.","Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output.","More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion.","Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time."],"answer":0,"why":"Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0095","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to lower interest rates during a demand-led slowdown. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Training takes time to improve employment.","Alternative energy projects require large initial investments.","Support may increase government borrowing.","Borrowing for investment may become more affordable."],"answer":3,"why":"Borrowing for investment may become more affordable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0096","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The labour force of an economy is 70,000 and 2,100 people are unemployed and seeking work. What is the unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["0.03%","97%","3%","5%"],"answer":2,"why":"Unemployment rate = (2,100 / 70,000) × 100 = 3%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0097","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to lower interest rates during a demand-led slowdown?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Graduates may leave the local economy.","Rates alone do not show the quality of employment.","Poorly targeted support can prolong unproductive activity.","Household spending financed by credit may increase."],"answer":3,"why":"Household spending financed by credit may increase."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0098","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to lower interest rates during a demand-led slowdown less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Comparable statistics can guide training and job programmes.","Long-run growth and competitiveness may improve.","Banks may not pass lower rates to borrowers.","Job losses could be smaller during the downturn."],"answer":2,"why":"Banks may not pass lower rates to borrowers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0099","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to lower interest rates during a demand-led slowdown. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Revenue may finance public health and education.","Borrowing for investment may become more affordable.","Construction jobs may be created.","Health damage from emissions may fall."],"answer":1,"why":"Borrowing for investment may become more affordable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0100","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A central bank cuts its policy interest rate after consumer spending weakens. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs.","Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand.","Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output.","Job-search time creates temporary unemployment even in a healthy labour market."],"answer":1,"why":"Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0101","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes higher policy interest rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Money supply refers to the stock of money available in an economy at a given time.","Increasing the policy interest rate generally discourages borrowing and encourages saving.","A progressive tax takes a larger proportion of income as income rises.","Deregulation reduces some government rules affecting business activity."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher policy interest rate: Increasing the policy interest rate generally discourages borrowing and encourages saving."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0102","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A central bank raises rates after the CPI inflation rate accelerates. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Government budget surplus","Higher policy interest rate","Demand-pull inflation","Progressive taxation"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates higher policy interest rate: Increasing the policy interest rate generally discourages borrowing and encourages saving."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0103","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of higher policy interest rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours.","A shop assistant leaves a job and spends six weeks looking for another suitable role.","A central bank raises rates after the CPI inflation rate accelerates.","High-income households pay a higher marginal rate of income tax than low-income households."],"answer":2,"why":"A central bank raises rates after the CPI inflation rate accelerates. This illustrates increasing the policy interest rate generally discourages borrowing and encourages saving."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0104","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to higher policy interest rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output.","A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources.","Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand.","Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0105","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Government revenue is $89 billion and spending is $102 billion. Calculate the budget balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$13 billion deficit","$191 billion deficit","$102 billion deficit","$13 billion surplus"],"answer":0,"why":"Spending exceeds revenue by $102 − $89 = $13 billion, so this is a deficit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0106","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to raise interest rates to contain demand-pull inflation. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Investment and employment may weaken.","Workers may gain access to new jobs.","Workers may find suitable vacancies faster.","Firms may produce more at lower unit cost."],"answer":0,"why":"Investment and employment may weaken."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0107","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to raise interest rates to contain demand-pull inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Water shortages could limit future expansion.","Spending may be inefficient if projects are poorly selected.","Some workers cannot easily relocate to areas with vacancies.","Saving becomes more attractive for some households."],"answer":3,"why":"Saving becomes more attractive for some households."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0108","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to raise interest rates to contain demand-pull inflation less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Productivity gains may support lasting growth.","Cost-push inflation may persist despite weaker demand.","Better roads can support future private investment.","Workers may qualify for better-paid employment."],"answer":1,"why":"Cost-push inflation may persist despite weaker demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0109","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to raise interest rates to contain demand-pull inflation. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices.","More families may afford essential goods.","Inflationary pressure may ease.","Firms may produce more at lower unit cost."],"answer":0,"why":"Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0110","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A central bank raises rates after the CPI inflation rate accelerates. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs.","More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion.","Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand.","Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0111","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes money supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Policies that raise total demand may reduce cyclical unemployment but also increase inflationary pressure.","Demand-pull inflation occurs when total demand grows faster than available output.","Frictional unemployment is short-term joblessness while workers move between jobs.","Money supply refers to the stock of money available in an economy at a given time."],"answer":3,"why":"Money supply: Money supply refers to the stock of money available in an economy at a given time."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0112","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A monetary authority announces action to slow rapid growth in bank-created money. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Supply-side policy","Structural unemployment","Money supply","Redistribution of income"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates money supply: Money supply refers to the stock of money available in an economy at a given time."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0113","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Real GDP rises from $1500 billion to $1620 billion in one year. What is the real GDP growth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["10%","8%","120%","108%"],"answer":1,"why":"Real GDP growth = ($1620 − $1500) / $1500 × 100 = 8%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0114","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to money supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket.","More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages.","Excess demand creates upward pressure on the overall price level.","Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices."],"answer":3,"why":"Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0115","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to monitor excessive money and credit growth. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Training may take years.","Early warnings may help monetary authorities limit inflation risks.","Irrigation projects may be expensive.","Compliance costs may reduce firms' short-run profits."],"answer":1,"why":"Early warnings may help monetary authorities limit inflation risks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0116","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to monitor excessive money and credit growth. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Input costs may become more stable.","More families may afford essential goods.","Construction jobs may be created.","Restricting credit can hurt viable small firms."],"answer":3,"why":"Restricting credit can hurt viable small firms."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0117","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to monitor excessive money and credit growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Graduates may leave the local economy.","External shocks can make targets difficult to achieve.","A stable financial environment can improve confidence.","Some unemployed workers may lack access to courses."],"answer":2,"why":"A stable financial environment can improve confidence."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0118","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to monitor excessive money and credit growth less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Lower exposure to fuel shocks could limit future inflation.","Money demand and velocity can change unpredictably.","Higher spending may increase production and employment.","Better roads can support future private investment."],"answer":1,"why":"Money demand and velocity can change unpredictably."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0119","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to monitor excessive money and credit growth. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources.","Early warnings may help monetary authorities limit inflation risks.","Future interest payments may decline.","Households may plan spending with more certainty."],"answer":1,"why":"Early warnings may help monetary authorities limit inflation risks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0120","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A monetary authority announces action to slow rapid growth in bank-created money. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards.","Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices.","Higher average tax burdens on the wealthy can help redistribute disposable income.","A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources."],"answer":1,"why":"Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0121","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The CPI rises from 130 to 139.1. What is the inflation rate for this period?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["7%","107%","10%","139.1%"],"answer":0,"why":"Inflation rate = (139.1 − 130) / 130 × 100 = 7%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0122","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Higher domestic interest rates attract some foreign investors seeking yield. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Exchange-rate channel of monetary policy","Frictional unemployment","Recession","Supply-side policy"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates exchange-rate channel of monetary policy: Interest-rate changes can affect international capital flows and the exchange rate."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0123","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of exchange-rate channel of monetary policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher domestic interest rates attract some foreign investors seeking yield.","A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million.","A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours.","An economy with high unemployment and weak investment considers tax relief and training."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher domestic interest rates attract some foreign investors seeking yield. This illustrates interest-rate changes can affect international capital flows and the exchange rate."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0124","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to exchange-rate channel of monetary policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power.","Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited.","Stronger demand for domestic currency may cause appreciation and lower import prices.","Job-search time creates temporary unemployment even in a healthy labour market."],"answer":2,"why":"Stronger demand for domestic currency may cause appreciation and lower import prices."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0125","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Cheaper imports may reduce imported inflation.","Governments may focus too narrowly on measured targets.","A typical basket may not reflect every household's purchases.","Maintaining matching services requires funding."],"answer":0,"why":"Cheaper imports may reduce imported inflation."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0126","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources.","Exports may become more expensive abroad.","Health damage from emissions may fall.","Revenue may finance public health and education."],"answer":1,"why":"Exports may become more expensive abroad."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0127","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Firms may not invest if expected demand remains weak.","Benefits depend on whether enough firms use the bridge.","An appreciated currency can lower input costs.","Some unemployed workers may lack access to courses."],"answer":2,"why":"An appreciated currency can lower input costs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0128","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Tax revenue may increase as incomes and profits rise.","Productive capacity could expand over time.","Capital flows also depend on risk and confidence.","Policy-makers can monitor inflation trends."],"answer":2,"why":"Capital flows also depend on risk and confidence."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0129","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Workers may gain access to new jobs.","Health damage from emissions may fall.","Future interest payments may decline.","Cheaper imports may reduce imported inflation."],"answer":3,"why":"Cheaper imports may reduce imported inflation."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0130","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The labour force of an economy is 110,000 and 7,700 people are unemployed and seeking work. What is the unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["9%","93%","7%","0.07%"],"answer":2,"why":"Unemployment rate = (7,700 / 110,000) × 100 = 7%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0131","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes supply-side policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The unemployment rate is the unemployed divided by the labour force, multiplied by 100.","Macroeconomic aims are broad objectives such as growth, employment, price stability and external balance.","Supply-side policy aims to increase productive capacity or efficiency in the economy.","Policies that raise total demand may reduce cyclical unemployment but also increase inflationary pressure."],"answer":2,"why":"Supply-side policy: Supply-side policy aims to increase productive capacity or efficiency in the economy."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0132","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government upgrades freight rail lines and funds technical training. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Growth from better resources","Infrastructure investment","Money supply","Supply-side policy"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates supply-side policy: Supply-side policy aims to increase productive capacity or efficiency in the economy."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0133","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of supply-side policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A statistics office compares the cost of a weighted basket with its cost in a base year.","A flat tax on basic household purchases uses a greater share of a low earner's income.","A government upgrades freight rail lines and funds technical training.","During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs."],"answer":2,"why":"A government upgrades freight rail lines and funds technical training. This illustrates supply-side policy aims to increase productive capacity or efficiency in the economy."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0134","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to supply-side policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time.","Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards.","Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable.","Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0135","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in skills and transport infrastructure. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Firms may produce more at lower unit cost.","Tax revenue may fall.","Training may take years.","Irrigation projects may be expensive."],"answer":0,"why":"Firms may produce more at lower unit cost."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0136","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in skills and transport infrastructure. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Projects need substantial funds up front.","Workers may find suitable vacancies faster.","Borrowing for investment may become more affordable.","Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources."],"answer":0,"why":"Projects need substantial funds up front."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0137","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to invest in skills and transport infrastructure?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Tax avoidance can reduce the expected revenue.","Weak monitoring can undermine the policy.","Long-run growth and competitiveness may improve.","Poorly targeted support can prolong unproductive activity."],"answer":2,"why":"Long-run growth and competitiveness may improve."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0138","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Government revenue is $101 billion and spending is $106 billion. Calculate the budget balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$5 billion surplus","$5 billion deficit","$207 billion deficit","$106 billion deficit"],"answer":1,"why":"Spending exceeds revenue by $106 − $101 = $5 billion, so this is a deficit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0139","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to invest in skills and transport infrastructure. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power.","Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources.","Demand for essentials may be maintained.","Firms may produce more at lower unit cost."],"answer":3,"why":"Firms may produce more at lower unit cost."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0140","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government upgrades freight rail lines and funds technical training. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time.","A $6 billion surplus can reduce borrowing needs or debt.","Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited.","A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources."],"answer":0,"why":"Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0141","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes education and training policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A recession is a period of declining economic activity and real output.","Education and training can improve human capital and worker productivity.","The unemployment rate is the unemployed divided by the labour force, multiplied by 100.","Cyclical unemployment occurs when weak total demand reduces firms' need for workers."],"answer":1,"why":"Education and training policy: Education and training can improve human capital and worker productivity."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0142","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Education and training policy","Progressive taxation","Exchange-rate channel of monetary policy","Money supply"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates education and training policy: Education and training can improve human capital and worker productivity."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0143","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of education and training policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy.","A traditional textile factory closes while new technology firms struggle to recruit technicians.","During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs.","A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers."],"answer":3,"why":"A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers. This illustrates education and training can improve human capital and worker productivity."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0144","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to education and training policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand.","Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited.","Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices.","More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages."],"answer":3,"why":"More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0145","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand targeted vocational education programmes. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Government deficits can widen.","Firms may access more productive workers.","Training takes time to improve employment.","Surveys may miss discouraged workers outside the labour force."],"answer":1,"why":"Firms may access more productive workers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0146","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Real GDP rises from $1900 billion to $1976 billion in one year. What is the real GDP growth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["104%","76%","4%","6%"],"answer":2,"why":"Real GDP growth = ($1976 − $1900) / $1900 × 100 = 4%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0147","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand targeted vocational education programmes?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Intermittent power generation may require storage investment.","Supply shortages may keep some prices elevated.","Businesses may not pass the full exemption to consumers.","Workers may qualify for better-paid employment."],"answer":3,"why":"Workers may qualify for better-paid employment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0148","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand targeted vocational education programmes less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Graduates may leave the local economy.","Job losses could be smaller during the downturn.","An appreciated currency can lower input costs.","Saving becomes more attractive for some households."],"answer":0,"why":"Graduates may leave the local economy."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0149","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand targeted vocational education programmes. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Workers may gain access to new jobs.","Demand for essentials may be maintained.","Firms may access more productive workers.","Inflationary pressure may ease."],"answer":2,"why":"Firms may access more productive workers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0150","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power.","Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards.","Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable.","More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages."],"answer":3,"why":"More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0151","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes deregulation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Redistribution policies seek to reduce income inequality through taxes and transfers.","Deregulation reduces some government rules affecting business activity.","Increasing the policy interest rate generally discourages borrowing and encourages saving.","A progressive tax takes a larger proportion of income as income rises."],"answer":1,"why":"Deregulation: Deregulation reduces some government rules affecting business activity."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0152","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Deregulation","Infrastructure investment","Regressive taxation","Unemployment rate"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates deregulation: Deregulation reduces some government rules affecting business activity."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0153","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of deregulation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["An economy with high unemployment and weak investment considers tax relief and training.","A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall.","A central bank raises rates after the CPI inflation rate accelerates.","An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy."],"answer":1,"why":"A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall. This illustrates deregulation reduces some government rules affecting business activity."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0154","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to deregulation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment.","Job-search time creates temporary unemployment even in a healthy labour market.","The same payment consumes a larger proportion of smaller incomes.","A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket."],"answer":0,"why":"Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0155","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The CPI rises from 140 to 155.4. What is the inflation rate for this period?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["11%","111%","155.4%","14%"],"answer":0,"why":"Inflation rate = (155.4 − 140) / 140 × 100 = 11%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0156","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to simplify licensing rules for safe small businesses. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Weak safeguards could reduce product quality or safety.","Workers may find suitable vacancies faster.","Construction jobs may be created.","Repair spending may restore employment and transport links."],"answer":0,"why":"Weak safeguards could reduce product quality or safety."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0157","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to simplify licensing rules for safe small businesses?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Entrepreneurs may spend less on administration.","Some workers cannot easily relocate to areas with vacancies.","Spending may be inefficient if projects are poorly selected.","Businesses may not pass the full exemption to consumers."],"answer":0,"why":"Entrepreneurs may spend less on administration."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0158","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to simplify licensing rules for safe small businesses less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Long-term unemployment could decline.","Saving becomes more attractive for some households.","Better roads can support future private investment.","Other barriers such as finance may still block entry."],"answer":3,"why":"Other barriers such as finance may still block entry."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0159","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to simplify licensing rules for safe small businesses. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power.","Future interest payments may decline.","Investment demand and employment may rise.","Firms may enter markets more easily."],"answer":3,"why":"Firms may enter markets more easily."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0160","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs.","Excess demand creates upward pressure on the overall price level.","Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable.","Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment."],"answer":3,"why":"Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0161","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes infrastructure investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Supply-side policy aims to increase productive capacity or efficiency in the economy.","Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level of goods and services.","Cyclical unemployment occurs when weak total demand reduces firms' need for workers.","Infrastructure investment improves facilities such as transport, communication and power networks."],"answer":3,"why":"Infrastructure investment: Infrastructure investment improves facilities such as transport, communication and power networks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0162","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Growth versus environmental sustainability","Consequences of growth","Higher policy interest rate","Infrastructure investment"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates infrastructure investment: Infrastructure investment improves facilities such as transport, communication and power networks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0163","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The labour force of an economy is 60,000 and 6,600 people are unemployed and seeking work. What is the unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["11%","0.11%","89%","13%"],"answer":0,"why":"Unemployment rate = (6,600 / 60,000) × 100 = 11%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0164","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to infrastructure investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output.","Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited.","Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards.","Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity."],"answer":3,"why":"Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0165","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Government deficits can widen.","Training takes time to improve employment.","Investment and employment may weaken.","Deliveries may become faster and more reliable."],"answer":3,"why":"Deliveries may become faster and more reliable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0166","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Input costs may become more stable.","Demand for essentials may be maintained.","Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand.","Construction is costly and may disrupt traffic."],"answer":3,"why":"Construction is costly and may disrupt traffic."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0167","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Water shortages could limit future expansion.","Supply shortages may keep some prices elevated.","Firms may increase production because transport costs fall.","Capital flows also depend on risk and confidence."],"answer":2,"why":"Firms may increase production because transport costs fall."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0168","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["After-tax income inequality may fall.","Benefits depend on whether enough firms use the bridge.","Demand for basic foods may become more affordable.","A stable financial environment can improve confidence."],"answer":1,"why":"Benefits depend on whether enough firms use the bridge."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0169","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Deliveries may become faster and more reliable.","Policy-makers can track labour-market changes.","Repair spending may restore employment and transport links.","Input costs may become more stable."],"answer":0,"why":"Deliveries may become faster and more reliable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0170","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power.","More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion.","Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity.","A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket."],"answer":2,"why":"Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0171","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Government revenue is $113 billion and spending is $126 billion. Calculate the budget balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$126 billion deficit","$13 billion surplus","$13 billion deficit","$239 billion deficit"],"answer":2,"why":"Spending exceeds revenue by $126 − $113 = $13 billion, so this is a deficit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0172","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country's inflation-adjusted GDP rises from $200 billion to $210 billion in a year. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Recession","Redistribution of income","Real GDP growth","Government budget surplus"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates real gdp growth: Economic growth is an increase in real output, commonly measured using real GDP."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0173","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of real gdp growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million.","A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods.","A country's inflation-adjusted GDP rises from $200 billion to $210 billion in a year.","A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers."],"answer":2,"why":"A country's inflation-adjusted GDP rises from $200 billion to $210 billion in a year. This illustrates economic growth is an increase in real output, commonly measured using real gdp."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0174","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to real gdp growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices.","More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages.","The 5% real GDP rise signals more goods and services were produced.","Job-search time creates temporary unemployment even in a healthy labour market."],"answer":2,"why":"The 5% real GDP rise signals more goods and services were produced."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0175","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to support sustainable growth through productive investment. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Renewable installations require upfront funding.","Training takes time to improve employment.","Higher output may create jobs.","Training may take years."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher output may create jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0176","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to support sustainable growth through productive investment. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Businesses may make plans with more confidence.","Deliveries may become faster and more reliable.","Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand.","Growth may increase resource depletion and pollution."],"answer":3,"why":"Growth may increase resource depletion and pollution."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0177","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to support sustainable growth through productive investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Money demand and velocity can change unpredictably.","Tax revenue may increase as incomes and profits rise.","Supply shortages may keep some prices elevated.","Global commodity-price shocks may be difficult to control."],"answer":1,"why":"Tax revenue may increase as incomes and profits rise."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0178","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to support sustainable growth through productive investment less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Household spending financed by credit may increase.","Benefits may be unevenly shared across households.","Long-term unemployment could decline.","Fiscal capacity for later emergencies may improve."],"answer":1,"why":"Benefits may be unevenly shared across households."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0179","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to support sustainable growth through productive investment. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Households may plan spending with more certainty.","Higher output may create jobs.","Future interest payments may decline.","Firms may enter markets more easily."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher output may create jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0180","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Real GDP rises from $2300 billion to $2484 billion in one year. What is the real GDP growth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["8%","10%","184%","108%"],"answer":0,"why":"Real GDP growth = ($2484 − $2300) / $2300 × 100 = 8%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0181","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes recession?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A budget deficit occurs when government expenditure exceeds government revenue in a period.","A recession is a period of declining economic activity and real output.","A budget surplus occurs when government revenue exceeds government expenditure in a period.","Cost-push inflation results when rising production costs push up the overall price level."],"answer":1,"why":"Recession: A recession is a period of declining economic activity and real output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0182","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Policies to promote growth","Deregulation","Recession","Inflation"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates recession: A recession is a period of declining economic activity and real output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0183","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of recession?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession.","A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods.","A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours.","A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year."],"answer":2,"why":"A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours. This illustrates a recession is a period of declining economic activity and real output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0184","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to recession?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Falling total demand can cause output, employment and incomes to contract.","Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable.","A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket.","Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited."],"answer":0,"why":"Falling total demand can cause output, employment and incomes to contract."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0185","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use temporary targeted support during a recession. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Investment and employment may weaken.","Training may take years.","Demand for essentials may be maintained.","Tax revenue may fall."],"answer":2,"why":"Demand for essentials may be maintained."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0186","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use temporary targeted support during a recession. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Support may increase government borrowing.","Future interest payments may decline.","Businesses may make plans with more confidence.","Policy-makers can track labour-market changes."],"answer":0,"why":"Support may increase government borrowing."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0187","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to use temporary targeted support during a recession?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Job losses could be smaller during the downturn.","Money demand and velocity can change unpredictably.","Some jobs require qualifications applicants do not possess.","Global commodity-price shocks may be difficult to control."],"answer":0,"why":"Job losses could be smaller during the downturn."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0188","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The CPI rises from 150 to 157.5. What is the inflation rate for this period?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["8%","5%","157.5%","105%"],"answer":1,"why":"Inflation rate = (157.5 − 150) / 150 × 100 = 5%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0189","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to use temporary targeted support during a recession. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices.","Policy-makers can track labour-market changes.","Demand for essentials may be maintained.","Deliveries may become faster and more reliable."],"answer":2,"why":"Demand for essentials may be maintained."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0190","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity.","A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources.","Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment.","Falling total demand can cause output, employment and incomes to contract."],"answer":3,"why":"Falling total demand can cause output, employment and incomes to contract."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0191","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes growth from better resources?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Monetary policy uses instruments such as interest rates and money supply to influence the economy.","Demand-pull inflation occurs when total demand grows faster than available output.","A budget surplus occurs when government revenue exceeds government expenditure in a period.","Improvements in the quality or quantity of production inputs can raise potential output."],"answer":3,"why":"Growth from better resources: Improvements in the quality or quantity of production inputs can raise potential output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0192","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A farming region introduces more productive irrigation methods and trains equipment operators. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Money supply","Real GDP growth","Growth from better resources","Cost-push inflation"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates growth from better resources: Improvements in the quality or quantity of production inputs can raise potential output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0193","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of growth from better resources?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy.","A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers.","A farming region introduces more productive irrigation methods and trains equipment operators.","A government receives $76 billion in revenue but spends $70 billion."],"answer":2,"why":"A farming region introduces more productive irrigation methods and trains equipment operators. This illustrates improvements in the quality or quantity of production inputs can raise potential output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0194","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to growth from better resources?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards.","Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time.","Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable.","Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0195","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to finance efficient irrigation and technical training. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources.","Very high rates may weaken incentives to work or invest.","Training may take years.","Exports may become more expensive abroad."],"answer":0,"why":"Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0196","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The labour force of an economy is 100,000 and 4,000 people are unemployed and seeking work. What is the unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["4%","0.04%","96%","6%"],"answer":0,"why":"Unemployment rate = (4,000 / 100,000) × 100 = 4%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0197","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to finance efficient irrigation and technical training?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["External shocks can make targets difficult to achieve.","Changes in product quality can complicate price comparisons.","Better yields may support farmers' incomes.","Businesses may not pass the full exemption to consumers."],"answer":2,"why":"Better yields may support farmers' incomes."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0198","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to finance efficient irrigation and technical training less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Comparable statistics can guide training and job programmes.","More stable prices can protect real incomes.","Water shortages could limit future expansion.","Entrepreneurs may spend less on administration."],"answer":2,"why":"Water shortages could limit future expansion."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0199","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to finance efficient irrigation and technical training. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Input costs may become more stable.","Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources.","Workers may find suitable vacancies faster.","Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand."],"answer":1,"why":"Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0200","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A farming region introduces more productive irrigation methods and trains equipment operators. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion.","The unemployment rate is 5%, showing the share of the labour force without work and seeking it.","More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages.","Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0201","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes consequences of growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Education and training can improve human capital and worker productivity.","Growth can raise incomes and job opportunities while increasing environmental pressures.","Structural unemployment results from a long-term mismatch between workers' skills or locations and available jobs.","Demand-pull inflation occurs when total demand grows faster than available output."],"answer":1,"why":"Consequences of growth: Growth can raise incomes and job opportunities while increasing environmental pressures."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0202","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A rapidly growing industrial district gains factories and wages but also traffic emissions. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Growth from better resources","Recession","Structural unemployment","Consequences of growth"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates consequences of growth: Growth can raise incomes and job opportunities while increasing environmental pressures."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0203","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of consequences of growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A government upgrades freight rail lines and funds technical training.","A rapidly growing industrial district gains factories and wages but also traffic emissions.","During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs.","A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year."],"answer":1,"why":"A rapidly growing industrial district gains factories and wages but also traffic emissions. This illustrates growth can raise incomes and job opportunities while increasing environmental pressures."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0204","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to consequences of growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output.","Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs.","Aims guide policy priorities but actions towards one aim can affect another.","Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0205","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Government revenue is $125 billion and spending is $130 billion. Calculate the budget balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$5 billion deficit","$130 billion deficit","$5 billion surplus","$255 billion deficit"],"answer":0,"why":"Spending exceeds revenue by $130 − $125 = $5 billion, so this is a deficit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0206","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce pollution controls during industrial growth. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Compliance costs may reduce firms' short-run profits.","Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power.","Firms may enter markets more easily.","Businesses may make plans with more confidence."],"answer":0,"why":"Compliance costs may reduce firms' short-run profits."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0207","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to introduce pollution controls during industrial growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Poor targeting can increase programme costs.","A shortage of skilled builders could raise costs.","Stimulus may be ineffective if households save extra income.","Growth may become more environmentally sustainable."],"answer":3,"why":"Growth may become more environmentally sustainable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0208","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to introduce pollution controls during industrial growth less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Comparable statistics can guide training and job programmes.","Saving becomes more attractive for some households.","Lower exposure to fuel shocks could limit future inflation.","Weak monitoring can undermine the policy."],"answer":3,"why":"Weak monitoring can undermine the policy."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0209","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to introduce pollution controls during industrial growth. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Health damage from emissions may fall.","Output can expand with less pollution per unit.","Borrowing for investment may become more affordable.","Future interest payments may decline."],"answer":0,"why":"Health damage from emissions may fall."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0210","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A rapidly growing industrial district gains factories and wages but also traffic emissions. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output.","More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages.","Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs.","More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0211","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes policies to promote growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Fiscal, monetary and supply-side measures can stimulate growth through different channels.","Infrastructure investment improves facilities such as transport, communication and power networks.","Expansionary fiscal policy uses higher government spending or lower taxes to raise total demand.","Redistribution policies seek to reduce income inequality through taxes and transfers."],"answer":0,"why":"Policies to promote growth: Fiscal, monetary and supply-side measures can stimulate growth through different channels."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0212","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: An economy with high unemployment and weak investment considers tax relief and training. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Unemployment rate","Expansionary fiscal policy","Recession","Policies to promote growth"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates policies to promote growth: Fiscal, monetary and supply-side measures can stimulate growth through different channels."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0213","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Real GDP rises from $2700 billion to $2808 billion in one year. What is the real GDP growth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["108%","4%","104%","6%"],"answer":1,"why":"Real GDP growth = ($2808 − $2700) / $2700 × 100 = 4%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0214","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to policies to promote growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Lower sales lead firms to cut production and employment.","Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output.","A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased.","Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power."],"answer":1,"why":"Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0215","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Inflation may rise if demand grows too quickly.","Training is expensive and time-consuming.","Investment demand and employment may rise.","Alternative energy projects require large initial investments."],"answer":2,"why":"Investment demand and employment may rise."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0216","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand.","Tax relief reduces revenue and training costs money.","Households may plan spending with more certainty.","Workers may gain access to new jobs."],"answer":1,"why":"Tax relief reduces revenue and training costs money."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0217","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Productivity gains may support lasting growth.","Intermittent power generation may require storage investment.","A shortage of skilled builders could raise costs.","Supply shortages may keep some prices elevated."],"answer":0,"why":"Productivity gains may support lasting growth."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0218","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Workers may qualify for better-paid employment.","Productive capacity could expand over time.","Comparable statistics can guide training and job programmes.","Firms may not invest if expected demand remains weak."],"answer":3,"why":"Firms may not invest if expected demand remains weak."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0219","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Investment demand and employment may rise.","Businesses may retain more workers.","Output can expand with less pollution per unit.","Input costs may become more stable."],"answer":0,"why":"Investment demand and employment may rise."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0220","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: An economy with high unemployment and weak investment considers tax relief and training. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand.","Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output.","Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand.","Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time."],"answer":1,"why":"Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0221","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The CPI rises from 160 to 174.4. What is the inflation rate for this period?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["12%","9%","109%","174.4%"],"answer":1,"why":"Inflation rate = (174.4 − 160) / 160 × 100 = 9%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0222","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Structural unemployment","Recession","Unemployment rate","Frictional unemployment"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates unemployment rate: The unemployment rate is the unemployed divided by the labour force, multiplied by 100."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0223","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher domestic interest rates attract some foreign investors seeking yield.","A country's inflation-adjusted GDP rises from $200 billion to $210 billion in a year.","A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million.","A government receives $76 billion in revenue but spends $70 billion."],"answer":2,"why":"A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million. This illustrates the unemployment rate is the unemployed divided by the labour force, multiplied by 100."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0224","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited.","Higher disposable income and public purchases may support output and employment.","The unemployment rate is 5%, showing the share of the labour force without work and seeking it.","Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output."],"answer":2,"why":"The unemployment rate is 5%, showing the share of the labour force without work and seeking it."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0225","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to publish reliable labour-force survey results. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Policy-makers can track labour-market changes.","Restricting credit can hurt viable small firms.","Alternative energy projects require large initial investments.","Support may increase government borrowing."],"answer":0,"why":"Policy-makers can track labour-market changes."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0226","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to publish reliable labour-force survey results. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Surveys may miss discouraged workers outside the labour force.","Early warnings may help monetary authorities limit inflation risks.","Input costs may become more stable.","Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Surveys may miss discouraged workers outside the labour force."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0227","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to publish reliable labour-force survey results?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Supply shortages may keep some prices elevated.","Weak monitoring can undermine the policy.","Comparable statistics can guide training and job programmes.","Some unemployed workers may lack access to courses."],"answer":2,"why":"Comparable statistics can guide training and job programmes."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0228","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to publish reliable labour-force survey results less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Improved transport can raise later productive capacity.","Rates alone do not show the quality of employment.","Better yields may support farmers' incomes.","Growth may become more environmentally sustainable."],"answer":1,"why":"Rates alone do not show the quality of employment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0229","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to publish reliable labour-force survey results. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources.","Policy-makers can track labour-market changes.","Cheaper imports may reduce imported inflation.","Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices."],"answer":1,"why":"Policy-makers can track labour-market changes."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0230","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The labour force of an economy is 50,000 and 4,000 people are unemployed and seeking work. What is the unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["10%","0.08%","92%","8%"],"answer":3,"why":"Unemployment rate = (4,000 / 50,000) × 100 = 8%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0231","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes frictional unemployment?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A recession is a period of declining economic activity and real output.","Frictional unemployment is short-term joblessness while workers move between jobs.","Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level of goods and services.","The unemployment rate is the unemployed divided by the labour force, multiplied by 100."],"answer":1,"why":"Frictional unemployment: Frictional unemployment is short-term joblessness while workers move between jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0232","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A shop assistant leaves a job and spends six weeks looking for another suitable role. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Consequences of growth","Demand-pull inflation","Frictional unemployment","Expansionary fiscal policy"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates frictional unemployment: Frictional unemployment is short-term joblessness while workers move between jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0233","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of frictional unemployment?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A country considers expanding coal-based electricity generation to boost industrial output.","A government proposes a large spending stimulus when factories are near full capacity.","A shop assistant leaves a job and spends six weeks looking for another suitable role.","A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation."],"answer":2,"why":"A shop assistant leaves a job and spends six weeks looking for another suitable role. This illustrates frictional unemployment is short-term joblessness while workers move between jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0234","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to frictional unemployment?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Job-search time creates temporary unemployment even in a healthy labour market.","Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time.","A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased.","Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity."],"answer":0,"why":"Job-search time creates temporary unemployment even in a healthy labour market."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0235","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve job-matching information services. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Very tight policy may lower employment.","Alternative energy projects require large initial investments.","Renewable installations require upfront funding.","Workers may find suitable vacancies faster."],"answer":3,"why":"Workers may find suitable vacancies faster."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0236","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve job-matching information services. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Deliveries may become faster and more reliable.","Construction jobs may be created.","Businesses may make plans with more confidence.","Maintaining matching services requires funding."],"answer":3,"why":"Maintaining matching services requires funding."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0237","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve job-matching information services?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Debt repayment can crowd out urgent public investment.","External shocks can make targets difficult to achieve.","Intermittent power generation may require storage investment.","Firms can fill open posts more quickly."],"answer":3,"why":"Firms can fill open posts more quickly."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0238","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Government revenue is $137 billion and spending is $150 billion. Calculate the budget balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$287 billion deficit","$150 billion deficit","$13 billion surplus","$13 billion deficit"],"answer":3,"why":"Spending exceeds revenue by $150 − $137 = $13 billion, so this is a deficit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0239","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve job-matching information services. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Input costs may become more stable.","Workers may find suitable vacancies faster.","Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices.","Investment demand and employment may rise."],"answer":1,"why":"Workers may find suitable vacancies faster."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0240","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A shop assistant leaves a job and spends six weeks looking for another suitable role. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased.","Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output.","Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand.","Job-search time creates temporary unemployment even in a healthy labour market."],"answer":3,"why":"Job-search time creates temporary unemployment even in a healthy labour market."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0241","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes structural unemployment?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A progressive tax takes a larger proportion of income as income rises.","Structural unemployment results from a long-term mismatch between workers' skills or locations and available jobs.","Redistribution policies seek to reduce income inequality through taxes and transfers.","Education and training can improve human capital and worker productivity."],"answer":1,"why":"Structural unemployment: Structural unemployment results from a long-term mismatch between workers' skills or locations and available jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0242","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A traditional textile factory closes while new technology firms struggle to recruit technicians. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Employment versus inflation","Exchange-rate channel of monetary policy","Structural unemployment","Progressive taxation"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates structural unemployment: Structural unemployment results from a long-term mismatch between workers' skills or locations and available jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0243","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of structural unemployment?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A country's inflation-adjusted GDP rises from $200 billion to $210 billion in a year.","High-income households pay a higher marginal rate of income tax than low-income households.","A traditional textile factory closes while new technology firms struggle to recruit technicians.","A farming region introduces more productive irrigation methods and trains equipment operators."],"answer":2,"why":"A traditional textile factory closes while new technology firms struggle to recruit technicians. This illustrates structural unemployment results from a long-term mismatch between workers' skills or locations and available jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0244","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to structural unemployment?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable.","Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time.","Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output.","A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources."],"answer":0,"why":"Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0245","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to retrain displaced textile workers for growing industries. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Workers may gain access to new jobs.","Reduced spending may slow growth.","Training takes time to improve employment.","Very high rates may weaken incentives to work or invest."],"answer":0,"why":"Workers may gain access to new jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0246","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Real GDP rises from $3100 billion to $3348 billion in one year. What is the real GDP growth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["8%","10%","248%","108%"],"answer":0,"why":"Real GDP growth = ($3348 − $3100) / $3100 × 100 = 8%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0247","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to retrain displaced textile workers for growing industries?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Long-term unemployment could decline.","A shortage of skilled builders could raise costs.","Some unemployed workers may lack access to courses.","Transitioning away from imported fuel takes time."],"answer":0,"why":"Long-term unemployment could decline."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0248","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to retrain displaced textile workers for growing industries less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["After-tax income inequality may fall.","Household spending financed by credit may increase.","Improved transport can raise later productive capacity.","Some workers cannot easily relocate to areas with vacancies."],"answer":3,"why":"Some workers cannot easily relocate to areas with vacancies."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0249","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to retrain displaced textile workers for growing industries. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More families may afford essential goods.","Businesses may make plans with more confidence.","Revenue may finance public health and education.","Workers may gain access to new jobs."],"answer":3,"why":"Workers may gain access to new jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0250","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A traditional textile factory closes while new technology firms struggle to recruit technicians. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages.","Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output.","Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices.","Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable."],"answer":3,"why":"Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0251","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes cyclical unemployment?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Cyclical unemployment occurs when weak total demand reduces firms' need for workers.","Structural unemployment results from a long-term mismatch between workers' skills or locations and available jobs.","Education and training can improve human capital and worker productivity.","Expansionary fiscal policy uses higher government spending or lower taxes to raise total demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Cyclical unemployment: Cyclical unemployment occurs when weak total demand reduces firms' need for workers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0252","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher policy interest rate","Cyclical unemployment","Education and training policy","Growth from better resources"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates cyclical unemployment: Cyclical unemployment occurs when weak total demand reduces firms' need for workers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0253","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of cyclical unemployment?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation.","A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year.","A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession.","A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers."],"answer":2,"why":"A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession. This illustrates cyclical unemployment occurs when weak total demand reduces firms' need for workers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0254","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to cyclical unemployment?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket.","Lower sales lead firms to cut production and employment.","Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards.","Higher disposable income and public purchases may support output and employment."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower sales lead firms to cut production and employment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0255","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The CPI rises from 170 to 175.1. What is the inflation rate for this period?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["3%","175.1%","6%","103%"],"answer":0,"why":"Inflation rate = (175.1 − 170) / 170 × 100 = 3%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0256","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to boost demand temporarily during a recession. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher output may create jobs.","Government deficits can widen.","Revenue may finance public health and education.","More families may afford essential goods."],"answer":1,"why":"Government deficits can widen."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0257","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to boost demand temporarily during a recession?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Graduates may leave the local economy.","Supply shortages may keep some prices elevated.","Businesses may not pass the full exemption to consumers.","Higher spending may increase production and employment."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher spending may increase production and employment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0258","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to boost demand temporarily during a recession less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Saving becomes more attractive for some households.","Workers may qualify for better-paid employment.","Stimulus may be ineffective if households save extra income.","Growth may become more environmentally sustainable."],"answer":2,"why":"Stimulus may be ineffective if households save extra income."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0259","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to boost demand temporarily during a recession. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Firms may enter markets more easily.","Demand for essentials may be maintained.","Businesses may retain more workers.","Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources."],"answer":2,"why":"Businesses may retain more workers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0260","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment.","Lower sales lead firms to cut production and employment.","Stronger demand for domestic currency may cause appreciation and lower import prices.","Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower sales lead firms to cut production and employment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0261","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Supply-side policy aims to increase productive capacity or efficiency in the economy.","Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level of goods and services.","Money supply refers to the stock of money available in an economy at a given time.","Growth can raise incomes and job opportunities while increasing environmental pressures."],"answer":1,"why":"Inflation: Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level of goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0262","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A basket costing $100 last year now costs $106 on average. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Growth versus environmental sustainability","Supply-side policy","Money supply","Inflation"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates inflation: Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level of goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0263","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The labour force of an economy is 90,000 and 10,800 people are unemployed and seeking work. What is the unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["0.12%","12%","14%","88%"],"answer":1,"why":"Unemployment rate = (10,800 / 90,000) × 100 = 12%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0264","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased.","A $6 billion surplus can reduce borrowing needs or debt.","Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time.","Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power."],"answer":0,"why":"A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0265","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to keep inflation low and predictable. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Very high rates may weaken incentives to work or invest.","Investment and employment may weaken.","Training may take years.","Households may plan spending with more certainty."],"answer":3,"why":"Households may plan spending with more certainty."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0266","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to keep inflation low and predictable. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand.","Revenue may finance public health and education.","Very tight policy may lower employment.","Policy-makers can track labour-market changes."],"answer":2,"why":"Very tight policy may lower employment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0267","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to keep inflation low and predictable?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Some jobs require qualifications applicants do not possess.","Firms may make long-term contracts with greater confidence.","Benefits may take years to appear.","Poorly targeted support can prolong unproductive activity."],"answer":1,"why":"Firms may make long-term contracts with greater confidence."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0268","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to keep inflation low and predictable less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Firms may increase production because transport costs fall.","Workers may qualify for better-paid employment.","Demand for basic foods may become more affordable.","Global commodity-price shocks may be difficult to control."],"answer":3,"why":"Global commodity-price shocks may be difficult to control."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0269","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to keep inflation low and predictable. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Policy-makers can track labour-market changes.","Health damage from emissions may fall.","Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power.","Households may plan spending with more certainty."],"answer":3,"why":"Households may plan spending with more certainty."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0270","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A basket costing $100 last year now costs $106 on average. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand.","Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices.","A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased.","The unemployment rate is 5%, showing the share of the labour force without work and seeking it."],"answer":2,"why":"A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0271","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Government revenue is $149 billion and spending is $154 billion. Calculate the budget balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$5 billion surplus","$303 billion deficit","$5 billion deficit","$154 billion deficit"],"answer":2,"why":"Spending exceeds revenue by $154 − $149 = $5 billion, so this is a deficit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0272","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Strong credit growth leads shoppers to increase spending while factories are at full capacity. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Cyclical unemployment","Money supply","Regressive taxation","Demand-pull inflation"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates demand-pull inflation: Demand-pull inflation occurs when total demand grows faster than available output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0273","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of demand-pull inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Strong credit growth leads shoppers to increase spending while factories are at full capacity.","A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours.","A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers.","A government upgrades freight rail lines and funds technical training."],"answer":0,"why":"Strong credit growth leads shoppers to increase spending while factories are at full capacity. This illustrates demand-pull inflation occurs when total demand grows faster than available output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0274","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to demand-pull inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources.","Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs.","Excess demand creates upward pressure on the overall price level.","Stronger demand for domestic currency may cause appreciation and lower import prices."],"answer":2,"why":"Excess demand creates upward pressure on the overall price level."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0275","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Renewable installations require upfront funding.","Training takes time to improve employment.","Inflationary pressure may ease.","Surveys may miss discouraged workers outside the labour force."],"answer":2,"why":"Inflationary pressure may ease."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0276","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Firms may access more productive workers.","Cheaper imports may reduce imported inflation.","Policy-makers can track labour-market changes.","Reduced spending may slow growth."],"answer":3,"why":"Reduced spending may slow growth."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0277","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More stable prices can protect real incomes.","Some workers cannot easily relocate to areas with vacancies.","Debt repayment can crowd out urgent public investment.","A shortage of skilled builders could raise costs."],"answer":0,"why":"More stable prices can protect real incomes."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0278","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Improved transport can raise later productive capacity.","Demand for basic foods may become more affordable.","Greater equality may improve social outcomes.","Supply shortages may keep some prices elevated."],"answer":3,"why":"Supply shortages may keep some prices elevated."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0279","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Inflationary pressure may ease.","Future interest payments may decline.","Early warnings may help monetary authorities limit inflation risks.","Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Inflationary pressure may ease."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0280","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Real GDP rises from $3500 billion to $3640 billion in one year. What is the real GDP growth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["6%","4%","104%","140%"],"answer":1,"why":"Real GDP growth = ($3640 − $3500) / $3500 × 100 = 4%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0281","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes cost-push inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Cost-push inflation results when rising production costs push up the overall price level.","Improvements in the quality or quantity of production inputs can raise potential output.","Frictional unemployment is short-term joblessness while workers move between jobs.","Monetary policy uses instruments such as interest rates and money supply to influence the economy."],"answer":0,"why":"Cost-push inflation: Cost-push inflation results when rising production costs push up the overall price level."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0282","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Policies to promote growth","Recession","Cost-push inflation","Education and training policy"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates cost-push inflation: Cost-push inflation results when rising production costs push up the overall price level."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0283","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of cost-push inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession.","An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy.","A monetary authority announces action to slow rapid growth in bank-created money.","A country's inflation-adjusted GDP rises from $200 billion to $210 billion in a year."],"answer":1,"why":"An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy. This illustrates cost-push inflation results when rising production costs push up the overall price level."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0284","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to cost-push inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output.","The same payment consumes a larger proportion of smaller incomes.","Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power.","A $6 billion surplus can reduce borrowing needs or debt."],"answer":0,"why":"Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0285","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reduce dependence on expensive imported fuel. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Input costs may become more stable.","Governments may focus too narrowly on measured targets.","Higher taxes may affect incentives for some earners.","Support may increase government borrowing."],"answer":0,"why":"Input costs may become more stable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0286","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reduce dependence on expensive imported fuel. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Future interest payments may decline.","Alternative energy projects require large initial investments.","Construction jobs may be created.","Borrowing for investment may become more affordable."],"answer":1,"why":"Alternative energy projects require large initial investments."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0287","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to reduce dependence on expensive imported fuel?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Benefits may be unevenly shared across households.","Banks may not pass lower rates to borrowers.","Lower exposure to fuel shocks could limit future inflation.","Firms may not invest if expected demand remains weak."],"answer":2,"why":"Lower exposure to fuel shocks could limit future inflation."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0288","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The CPI rises from 180 to 192.6. What is the inflation rate for this period?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["7%","107%","10%","192.6%"],"answer":0,"why":"Inflation rate = (192.6 − 180) / 180 × 100 = 7%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0289","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to reduce dependence on expensive imported fuel. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Firms may access more productive workers.","Firms may enter markets more easily.","Input costs may become more stable.","Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand."],"answer":2,"why":"Input costs may become more stable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0290","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output.","Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment.","Falling total demand can cause output, employment and incomes to contract.","Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output."],"answer":3,"why":"Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0291","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes consumer prices index?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Infrastructure investment improves facilities such as transport, communication and power networks.","The Consumer Prices Index tracks the price of a representative basket of consumer goods and services.","Improvements in the quality or quantity of production inputs can raise potential output.","Economic growth is an increase in real output, commonly measured using real GDP."],"answer":1,"why":"Consumer Prices Index: The Consumer Prices Index tracks the price of a representative basket of consumer goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0292","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A statistics office compares the cost of a weighted basket with its cost in a base year. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Consumer Prices Index","Deregulation","Structural unemployment","Frictional unemployment"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates consumer prices index: The Consumer Prices Index tracks the price of a representative basket of consumer goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0293","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of consumer prices index?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A central bank raises rates after the CPI inflation rate accelerates.","A new government sets targets for inflation, joblessness and sustainable growth.","A statistics office compares the cost of a weighted basket with its cost in a base year.","A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods."],"answer":2,"why":"A statistics office compares the cost of a weighted basket with its cost in a base year. This illustrates the consumer prices index tracks the price of a representative basket of consumer goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0294","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to consumer prices index?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices.","A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket.","A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources.","The unemployment rate is 5%, showing the share of the labour force without work and seeking it."],"answer":1,"why":"A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0295","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to publish transparent and regularly updated CPI data. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Budget deficits may increase.","Workers and firms may negotiate with better price information.","Renewable installations require upfront funding.","Government deficits can widen."],"answer":1,"why":"Workers and firms may negotiate with better price information."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0296","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The labour force of an economy is 40,000 and 2,000 people are unemployed and seeking work. What is the unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["7%","5%","0.05%","95%"],"answer":1,"why":"Unemployment rate = (2,000 / 40,000) × 100 = 5%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0297","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to publish transparent and regularly updated CPI data?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Other barriers such as finance may still block entry.","Policy-makers can monitor inflation trends.","Some workers cannot easily relocate to areas with vacancies.","Poorly targeted support can prolong unproductive activity."],"answer":1,"why":"Policy-makers can monitor inflation trends."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0298","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to publish transparent and regularly updated CPI data less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Workers may qualify for better-paid employment.","Productivity gains may support lasting growth.","Changes in product quality can complicate price comparisons.","Entrepreneurs may spend less on administration."],"answer":2,"why":"Changes in product quality can complicate price comparisons."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0299","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to publish transparent and regularly updated CPI data. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Health damage from emissions may fall.","Firms may access more productive workers.","Policy-makers can track labour-market changes.","Workers and firms may negotiate with better price information."],"answer":3,"why":"Workers and firms may negotiate with better price information."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0300","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A statistics office compares the cost of a weighted basket with its cost in a base year. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited.","Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand.","A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket.","Higher average tax burdens on the wealthy can help redistribute disposable income."],"answer":2,"why":"A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0001","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A new government sets targets for inflation, joblessness and sustainable growth. Decision-makers are considering whether to publish clear and measurable economic policy targets. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by macroeconomic aims. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Macroeconomic aims are broad objectives such as growth, employment, price stability and external balance.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A new government sets targets for inflation, joblessness and sustainable growth.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0002","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A country considers expanding coal-based electricity generation to boost industrial output. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by growth versus environmental sustainability. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Faster output growth can conflict with environmental protection if production increases emissions.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A country considers expanding coal-based electricity generation to boost industrial output.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0003","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A government proposes a large spending stimulus when factories are near full capacity. Decision-makers are considering whether to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by employment versus inflation. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Policies that raise total demand may reduce cyclical unemployment but also increase inflationary pressure.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A government proposes a large spending stimulus when factories are near full capacity.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0004","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand targeted support for the lowest-income households. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by redistribution of income. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Redistribution policies seek to reduce income inequality through taxes and transfers.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0005","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year. Decision-makers are considering whether to temporarily run a deficit to repair damaged roads. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by government budget deficit. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A budget deficit occurs when government expenditure exceeds government revenue in a period.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0006","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"High-income households pay a higher marginal rate of income tax than low-income households. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by progressive taxation. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A progressive tax takes a larger proportion of income as income rises.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — High-income households pay a higher marginal rate of income tax than low-income households.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0007","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A flat tax on basic household purchases uses a greater share of a low earner's income. Decision-makers are considering whether to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by regressive taxation. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A regressive tax takes a larger percentage of income from lower-income people than higher-income people.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A flat tax on basic household purchases uses a greater share of a low earner's income.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0008","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase infrastructure spending during a demand-led recession. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by expansionary fiscal policy. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Expansionary fiscal policy uses higher government spending or lower taxes to raise total demand.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0009","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A central bank cuts its policy interest rate after consumer spending weakens. Decision-makers are considering whether to lower interest rates during a demand-led slowdown. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by monetary policy. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Monetary policy uses instruments such as interest rates and money supply to influence the economy.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A central bank cuts its policy interest rate after consumer spending weakens.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0010","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A central bank raises rates after the CPI inflation rate accelerates. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise interest rates to contain demand-pull inflation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by higher policy interest rate. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Increasing the policy interest rate generally discourages borrowing and encourages saving.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A central bank raises rates after the CPI inflation rate accelerates.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0011","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Higher domestic interest rates attract some foreign investors seeking yield. Decision-makers are considering whether to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by exchange-rate channel of monetary policy. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Interest-rate changes can affect international capital flows and the exchange rate.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Higher domestic interest rates attract some foreign investors seeking yield.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0012","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A government upgrades freight rail lines and funds technical training. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in skills and transport infrastructure. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by supply-side policy. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Supply-side policy aims to increase productive capacity or efficiency in the economy.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A government upgrades freight rail lines and funds technical training.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0013","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand targeted vocational education programmes. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by education and training policy. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Education and training can improve human capital and worker productivity.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0014","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall. Decision-makers are considering whether to simplify licensing rules for safe small businesses. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by deregulation. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Deregulation reduces some government rules affecting business activity.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0015","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods. Decision-makers are considering whether to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by infrastructure investment. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Infrastructure investment improves facilities such as transport, communication and power networks.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0016","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours. Decision-makers are considering whether to use temporary targeted support during a recession. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by recession. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A recession is a period of declining economic activity and real output.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0017","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A farming region introduces more productive irrigation methods and trains equipment operators. Decision-makers are considering whether to finance efficient irrigation and technical training. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by growth from better resources. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Improvements in the quality or quantity of production inputs can raise potential output.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A farming region introduces more productive irrigation methods and trains equipment operators.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0018","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A rapidly growing industrial district gains factories and wages but also traffic emissions. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce pollution controls during industrial growth. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by consequences of growth. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Growth can raise incomes and job opportunities while increasing environmental pressures.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A rapidly growing industrial district gains factories and wages but also traffic emissions.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0019","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"An economy with high unemployment and weak investment considers tax relief and training. Decision-makers are considering whether to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by policies to promote growth. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Fiscal, monetary and supply-side measures can stimulate growth through different channels.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — An economy with high unemployment and weak investment considers tax relief and training.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0020","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million. Decision-makers are considering whether to publish reliable labour-force survey results. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by unemployment rate. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — The unemployment rate is the unemployed divided by the labour force, multiplied by 100.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0021","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A traditional textile factory closes while new technology firms struggle to recruit technicians. Decision-makers are considering whether to retrain displaced textile workers for growing industries. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by structural unemployment. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Structural unemployment results from a long-term mismatch between workers' skills or locations and available jobs.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A traditional textile factory closes while new technology firms struggle to recruit technicians.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0022","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession. Decision-makers are considering whether to boost demand temporarily during a recession. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by cyclical unemployment. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Cyclical unemployment occurs when weak total demand reduces firms' need for workers.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0023","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A basket costing $100 last year now costs $106 on average. Decision-makers are considering whether to keep inflation low and predictable. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by inflation. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level of goods and services.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A basket costing $100 last year now costs $106 on average.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0024","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Strong credit growth leads shoppers to increase spending while factories are at full capacity. Decision-makers are considering whether to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by demand-pull inflation. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Demand-pull inflation occurs when total demand grows faster than available output.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Strong credit growth leads shoppers to increase spending while factories are at full capacity.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0025","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce dependence on expensive imported fuel. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by cost-push inflation. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Cost-push inflation results when rising production costs push up the overall price level.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0001","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A new government sets targets for inflation, joblessness and sustainable growth. Decision-makers are considering whether to publish clear and measurable economic policy targets. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to publish clear and measurable economic policy targets. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Businesses may make plans with more confidence. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Voters can assess whether economic outcomes match promises. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0002","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country considers expanding coal-based electricity generation to boost industrial output. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Output can expand with less pollution per unit. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Cleaner technology may create new employment opportunities. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0003","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A government proposes a large spending stimulus when factories are near full capacity. Decision-makers are considering whether to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Productive capacity could expand over time. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0004","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand targeted support for the lowest-income households. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers expand targeted support for the lowest-income households. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: More families may afford essential goods. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Greater equality may improve social outcomes. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0005","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year. Decision-makers are considering whether to temporarily run a deficit to repair damaged roads. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to temporarily run a deficit to repair damaged roads could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Repair spending may restore employment and transport links. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Better roads can support future private investment. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0006","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A government receives $76 billion in revenue but spends $70 billion. Decision-makers are considering whether to use a temporary surplus to reduce public debt. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to use a temporary surplus to reduce public debt. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Future interest payments may decline. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Fiscal capacity for later emergencies may improve. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0007","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"High-income households pay a higher marginal rate of income tax than low-income households. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Revenue may finance public health and education. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: After-tax income inequality may fall. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0008","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A flat tax on basic household purchases uses a greater share of a low earner's income. Decision-makers are considering whether to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Demand for basic foods may become more affordable. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0009","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase infrastructure spending during a demand-led recession. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers increase infrastructure spending during a demand-led recession. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Construction jobs may be created. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Improved transport can raise later productive capacity. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0010","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A central bank cuts its policy interest rate after consumer spending weakens. Decision-makers are considering whether to lower interest rates during a demand-led slowdown. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to lower interest rates during a demand-led slowdown could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Borrowing for investment may become more affordable. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Household spending financed by credit may increase. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0011","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A central bank raises rates after the CPI inflation rate accelerates. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise interest rates to contain demand-pull inflation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to raise interest rates to contain demand-pull inflation. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Saving becomes more attractive for some households. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0012","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A monetary authority announces action to slow rapid growth in bank-created money. Decision-makers are considering whether to monitor excessive money and credit growth. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to monitor excessive money and credit growth might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Early warnings may help monetary authorities limit inflation risks. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: A stable financial environment can improve confidence. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0013","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Higher domestic interest rates attract some foreign investors seeking yield. Decision-makers are considering whether to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Cheaper imports may reduce imported inflation. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: An appreciated currency can lower input costs. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0014","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A government upgrades freight rail lines and funds technical training. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in skills and transport infrastructure. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers invest in skills and transport infrastructure. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Firms may produce more at lower unit cost. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Long-run growth and competitiveness may improve. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0015","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand targeted vocational education programmes. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to expand targeted vocational education programmes could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Firms may access more productive workers. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Workers may qualify for better-paid employment. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0016","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall. Decision-makers are considering whether to simplify licensing rules for safe small businesses. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to simplify licensing rules for safe small businesses. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Firms may enter markets more easily. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Entrepreneurs may spend less on administration. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0017","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods. Decision-makers are considering whether to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Deliveries may become faster and more reliable. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Firms may increase production because transport costs fall. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0018","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country's inflation-adjusted GDP rises from $200 billion to $210 billion in a year. Decision-makers are considering whether to support sustainable growth through productive investment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to support sustainable growth through productive investment. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Higher output may create jobs. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Tax revenue may increase as incomes and profits rise. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0019","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours. Decision-makers are considering whether to use temporary targeted support during a recession. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers use temporary targeted support during a recession. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Demand for essentials may be maintained. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Job losses could be smaller during the downturn. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0020","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A farming region introduces more productive irrigation methods and trains equipment operators. Decision-makers are considering whether to finance efficient irrigation and technical training. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to finance efficient irrigation and technical training could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Better yields may support farmers' incomes. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0021","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A rapidly growing industrial district gains factories and wages but also traffic emissions. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce pollution controls during industrial growth. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to introduce pollution controls during industrial growth. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Health damage from emissions may fall. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Growth may become more environmentally sustainable. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0022","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"An economy with high unemployment and weak investment considers tax relief and training. Decision-makers are considering whether to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Investment demand and employment may rise. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Productivity gains may support lasting growth. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0023","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million. Decision-makers are considering whether to publish reliable labour-force survey results. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to publish reliable labour-force survey results. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Policy-makers can track labour-market changes. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Comparable statistics can guide training and job programmes. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0024","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A shop assistant leaves a job and spends six weeks looking for another suitable role. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve job-matching information services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers improve job-matching information services. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Workers may find suitable vacancies faster. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Firms can fill open posts more quickly. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0025","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A traditional textile factory closes while new technology firms struggle to recruit technicians. Decision-makers are considering whether to retrain displaced textile workers for growing industries. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to retrain displaced textile workers for growing industries could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Workers may gain access to new jobs. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Long-term unemployment could decline. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0026","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession. Decision-makers are considering whether to boost demand temporarily during a recession. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to boost demand temporarily during a recession. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Businesses may retain more workers. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Higher spending may increase production and employment. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0027","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A basket costing $100 last year now costs $106 on average. Decision-makers are considering whether to keep inflation low and predictable. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to keep inflation low and predictable might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Households may plan spending with more certainty. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Firms may make long-term contracts with greater confidence. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0028","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Strong credit growth leads shoppers to increase spending while factories are at full capacity. Decision-makers are considering whether to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Inflationary pressure may ease. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: More stable prices can protect real incomes. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0029","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce dependence on expensive imported fuel. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers reduce dependence on expensive imported fuel. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Input costs may become more stable. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Lower exposure to fuel shocks could limit future inflation. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0030","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A statistics office compares the cost of a weighted basket with its cost in a base year. Decision-makers are considering whether to publish transparent and regularly updated CPI data. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to publish transparent and regularly updated CPI data could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Workers and firms may negotiate with better price information. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Policy-makers can monitor inflation trends. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0001","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A new government sets targets for inflation, joblessness and sustainable growth. Decision-makers are considering whether to publish clear and measurable economic policy targets. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to publish clear and measurable economic policy targets. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Businesses may make plans with more confidence. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Voters can assess whether economic outcomes match promises. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Aims guide policy priorities but actions towards one aim can affect another. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0002","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country considers expanding coal-based electricity generation to boost industrial output. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Output can expand with less pollution per unit. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Cleaner technology may create new employment opportunities. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0003","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A government proposes a large spending stimulus when factories are near full capacity. Decision-makers are considering whether to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Productive capacity could expand over time. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0004","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand targeted support for the lowest-income households. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to expand targeted support for the lowest-income households in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: More families may afford essential goods. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Greater equality may improve social outcomes. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0005","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year. Decision-makers are considering whether to temporarily run a deficit to repair damaged roads. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to temporarily run a deficit to repair damaged roads could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Repair spending may restore employment and transport links. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Better roads can support future private investment. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0006","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A government receives $76 billion in revenue but spends $70 billion. Decision-makers are considering whether to use a temporary surplus to reduce public debt. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to use a temporary surplus to reduce public debt. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Future interest payments may decline. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Fiscal capacity for later emergencies may improve. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: A $6 billion surplus can reduce borrowing needs or debt. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0007","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"High-income households pay a higher marginal rate of income tax than low-income households. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Revenue may finance public health and education. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: After-tax income inequality may fall. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Higher average tax burdens on the wealthy can help redistribute disposable income. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0008","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A flat tax on basic household purchases uses a greater share of a low earner's income. Decision-makers are considering whether to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Demand for basic foods may become more affordable. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The same payment consumes a larger proportion of smaller incomes. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0009","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase infrastructure spending during a demand-led recession. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to increase infrastructure spending during a demand-led recession in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Construction jobs may be created. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Improved transport can raise later productive capacity. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Higher disposable income and public purchases may support output and employment. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0010","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A central bank cuts its policy interest rate after consumer spending weakens. Decision-makers are considering whether to lower interest rates during a demand-led slowdown. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to lower interest rates during a demand-led slowdown could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Borrowing for investment may become more affordable. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Household spending financed by credit may increase. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0011","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A central bank raises rates after the CPI inflation rate accelerates. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise interest rates to contain demand-pull inflation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to raise interest rates to contain demand-pull inflation. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Saving becomes more attractive for some households. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0012","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A monetary authority announces action to slow rapid growth in bank-created money. Decision-makers are considering whether to monitor excessive money and credit growth. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to monitor excessive money and credit growth may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Early warnings may help monetary authorities limit inflation risks. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: A stable financial environment can improve confidence. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0013","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Higher domestic interest rates attract some foreign investors seeking yield. Decision-makers are considering whether to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Cheaper imports may reduce imported inflation. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: An appreciated currency can lower input costs. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Stronger demand for domestic currency may cause appreciation and lower import prices. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0014","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A government upgrades freight rail lines and funds technical training. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in skills and transport infrastructure. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to invest in skills and transport infrastructure in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Firms may produce more at lower unit cost. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Long-run growth and competitiveness may improve. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0015","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand targeted vocational education programmes. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to expand targeted vocational education programmes could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Firms may access more productive workers. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Workers may qualify for better-paid employment. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0016","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall. Decision-makers are considering whether to simplify licensing rules for safe small businesses. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to simplify licensing rules for safe small businesses. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Firms may enter markets more easily. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Entrepreneurs may spend less on administration. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0017","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods. Decision-makers are considering whether to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Deliveries may become faster and more reliable. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Firms may increase production because transport costs fall. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0018","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country's inflation-adjusted GDP rises from $200 billion to $210 billion in a year. Decision-makers are considering whether to support sustainable growth through productive investment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to support sustainable growth through productive investment could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Higher output may create jobs. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Tax revenue may increase as incomes and profits rise. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The 5% real GDP rise signals more goods and services were produced. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0019","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours. Decision-makers are considering whether to use temporary targeted support during a recession. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to use temporary targeted support during a recession in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Demand for essentials may be maintained. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Job losses could be smaller during the downturn. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Falling total demand can cause output, employment and incomes to contract. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0020","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A farming region introduces more productive irrigation methods and trains equipment operators. Decision-makers are considering whether to finance efficient irrigation and technical training. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to finance efficient irrigation and technical training could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Better yields may support farmers' incomes. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0021","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A rapidly growing industrial district gains factories and wages but also traffic emissions. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce pollution controls during industrial growth. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to introduce pollution controls during industrial growth. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Health damage from emissions may fall. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Growth may become more environmentally sustainable. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0022","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"An economy with high unemployment and weak investment considers tax relief and training. Decision-makers are considering whether to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Investment demand and employment may rise. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Productivity gains may support lasting growth. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0023","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million. Decision-makers are considering whether to publish reliable labour-force survey results. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to publish reliable labour-force survey results could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Policy-makers can track labour-market changes. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Comparable statistics can guide training and job programmes. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The unemployment rate is 5%, showing the share of the labour force without work and seeking it. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0024","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A shop assistant leaves a job and spends six weeks looking for another suitable role. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve job-matching information services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to improve job-matching information services in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Workers may find suitable vacancies faster. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Firms can fill open posts more quickly. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Job-search time creates temporary unemployment even in a healthy labour market. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0025","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A traditional textile factory closes while new technology firms struggle to recruit technicians. Decision-makers are considering whether to retrain displaced textile workers for growing industries. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to retrain displaced textile workers for growing industries could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Workers may gain access to new jobs. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Long-term unemployment could decline. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0026","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession. Decision-makers are considering whether to boost demand temporarily during a recession. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to boost demand temporarily during a recession. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Businesses may retain more workers. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Higher spending may increase production and employment. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Lower sales lead firms to cut production and employment. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0027","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A basket costing $100 last year now costs $106 on average. Decision-makers are considering whether to keep inflation low and predictable. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to keep inflation low and predictable may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Households may plan spending with more certainty. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Firms may make long-term contracts with greater confidence. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0028","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Strong credit growth leads shoppers to increase spending while factories are at full capacity. Decision-makers are considering whether to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Inflationary pressure may ease. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: More stable prices can protect real incomes. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Excess demand creates upward pressure on the overall price level. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0029","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce dependence on expensive imported fuel. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to reduce dependence on expensive imported fuel in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Input costs may become more stable. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Lower exposure to fuel shocks could limit future inflation. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0030","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A statistics office compares the cost of a weighted basket with its cost in a base year. Decision-makers are considering whether to publish transparent and regularly updated CPI data. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to publish transparent and regularly updated CPI data could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Workers and firms may negotiate with better price information. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Policy-makers can monitor inflation trends. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0001","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A new government sets targets for inflation, joblessness and sustainable growth. Decision-makers are considering whether to publish clear and measurable economic policy targets. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to publish clear and measurable economic policy targets would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Businesses may make plans with more confidence.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Voters can assess whether economic outcomes match promises.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Governments may focus too narrowly on measured targets.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): External shocks can make targets difficult to achieve.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0002","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country considers expanding coal-based electricity generation to boost industrial output. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Output can expand with less pollution per unit.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Cleaner technology may create new employment opportunities.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Renewable installations require upfront funding.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Intermittent power generation may require storage investment.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0003","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A government proposes a large spending stimulus when factories are near full capacity. Decision-makers are considering whether to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Productive capacity could expand over time.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Training takes time to improve employment.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Some unemployed workers may lack access to courses.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0004","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand targeted support for the lowest-income households. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to expand targeted support for the lowest-income households is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): More families may afford essential goods.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Greater equality may improve social outcomes.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Higher taxes may affect incentives for some earners.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Poor targeting can increase programme costs.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0005","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year. Decision-makers are considering whether to temporarily run a deficit to repair damaged roads. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to temporarily run a deficit to repair damaged roads would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Repair spending may restore employment and transport links.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Better roads can support future private investment.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Public debt and interest obligations may increase.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Spending may be inefficient if projects are poorly selected.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0006","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A government receives $76 billion in revenue but spends $70 billion. Decision-makers are considering whether to use a temporary surplus to reduce public debt. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to use a temporary surplus to reduce public debt would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Future interest payments may decline.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Fiscal capacity for later emergencies may improve.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Lower government spending may weaken current demand.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Debt repayment can crowd out urgent public investment.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0007","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"High-income households pay a higher marginal rate of income tax than low-income households. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Revenue may finance public health and education.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): After-tax income inequality may fall.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Very high rates may weaken incentives to work or invest.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Tax avoidance can reduce the expected revenue.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0008","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A flat tax on basic household purchases uses a greater share of a low earner's income. Decision-makers are considering whether to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Demand for basic foods may become more affordable.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Tax revenue may fall.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Businesses may not pass the full exemption to consumers.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0009","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs. Decision-makers are considering whether to increase infrastructure spending during a demand-led recession. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to increase infrastructure spending during a demand-led recession. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Construction jobs may be created.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Improved transport can raise later productive capacity.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Budget deficits may increase.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): A shortage of skilled builders could raise costs.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0010","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A central bank cuts its policy interest rate after consumer spending weakens. Decision-makers are considering whether to lower interest rates during a demand-led slowdown. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to lower interest rates during a demand-led slowdown is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Borrowing for investment may become more affordable.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Household spending financed by credit may increase.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Inflation may rise if demand grows too quickly.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Banks may not pass lower rates to borrowers.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0011","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A central bank raises rates after the CPI inflation rate accelerates. Decision-makers are considering whether to raise interest rates to contain demand-pull inflation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to raise interest rates to contain demand-pull inflation would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Saving becomes more attractive for some households.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Investment and employment may weaken.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Cost-push inflation may persist despite weaker demand.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0012","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A monetary authority announces action to slow rapid growth in bank-created money. Decision-makers are considering whether to monitor excessive money and credit growth. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to monitor excessive money and credit growth would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Early warnings may help monetary authorities limit inflation risks.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): A stable financial environment can improve confidence.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Restricting credit can hurt viable small firms.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Money demand and velocity can change unpredictably.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0013","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Higher domestic interest rates attract some foreign investors seeking yield. Decision-makers are considering whether to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Cheaper imports may reduce imported inflation.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): An appreciated currency can lower input costs.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Exports may become more expensive abroad.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Capital flows also depend on risk and confidence.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0014","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A government upgrades freight rail lines and funds technical training. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in skills and transport infrastructure. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to invest in skills and transport infrastructure in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Firms may produce more at lower unit cost.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Long-run growth and competitiveness may improve.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Projects need substantial funds up front.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Benefits may take years to appear.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0015","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand targeted vocational education programmes. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to expand targeted vocational education programmes. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Firms may access more productive workers.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Workers may qualify for better-paid employment.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Training is expensive and time-consuming.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Graduates may leave the local economy.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0016","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall. Decision-makers are considering whether to simplify licensing rules for safe small businesses. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to simplify licensing rules for safe small businesses is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Firms may enter markets more easily.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Entrepreneurs may spend less on administration.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Weak safeguards could reduce product quality or safety.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Other barriers such as finance may still block entry.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0017","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods. Decision-makers are considering whether to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Deliveries may become faster and more reliable.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Firms may increase production because transport costs fall.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Construction is costly and may disrupt traffic.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Benefits depend on whether enough firms use the bridge.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0018","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country's inflation-adjusted GDP rises from $200 billion to $210 billion in a year. Decision-makers are considering whether to support sustainable growth through productive investment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to support sustainable growth through productive investment would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Higher output may create jobs.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Tax revenue may increase as incomes and profits rise.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Growth may increase resource depletion and pollution.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Benefits may be unevenly shared across households.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0019","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours. Decision-makers are considering whether to use temporary targeted support during a recession. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to use temporary targeted support during a recession would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Demand for essentials may be maintained.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Job losses could be smaller during the downturn.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Support may increase government borrowing.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Poorly targeted support can prolong unproductive activity.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0020","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A farming region introduces more productive irrigation methods and trains equipment operators. Decision-makers are considering whether to finance efficient irrigation and technical training. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to finance efficient irrigation and technical training in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Better yields may support farmers' incomes.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Irrigation projects may be expensive.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Water shortages could limit future expansion.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0021","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A rapidly growing industrial district gains factories and wages but also traffic emissions. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce pollution controls during industrial growth. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to introduce pollution controls during industrial growth. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Health damage from emissions may fall.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Growth may become more environmentally sustainable.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Compliance costs may reduce firms' short-run profits.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Weak monitoring can undermine the policy.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0022","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"An economy with high unemployment and weak investment considers tax relief and training. Decision-makers are considering whether to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Investment demand and employment may rise.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Productivity gains may support lasting growth.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Tax relief reduces revenue and training costs money.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Firms may not invest if expected demand remains weak.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0023","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million. Decision-makers are considering whether to publish reliable labour-force survey results. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to publish reliable labour-force survey results would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Policy-makers can track labour-market changes.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Comparable statistics can guide training and job programmes.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Surveys may miss discouraged workers outside the labour force.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Rates alone do not show the quality of employment.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0024","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A shop assistant leaves a job and spends six weeks looking for another suitable role. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve job-matching information services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to improve job-matching information services would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Workers may find suitable vacancies faster.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Firms can fill open posts more quickly.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Maintaining matching services requires funding.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Some jobs require qualifications applicants do not possess.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0025","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A traditional textile factory closes while new technology firms struggle to recruit technicians. Decision-makers are considering whether to retrain displaced textile workers for growing industries. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to retrain displaced textile workers for growing industries would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Workers may gain access to new jobs.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Long-term unemployment could decline.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Training may take years.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Some workers cannot easily relocate to areas with vacancies.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0026","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession. Decision-makers are considering whether to boost demand temporarily during a recession. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to boost demand temporarily during a recession in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Businesses may retain more workers.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Higher spending may increase production and employment.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Government deficits can widen.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Stimulus may be ineffective if households save extra income.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0027","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A basket costing $100 last year now costs $106 on average. Decision-makers are considering whether to keep inflation low and predictable. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to keep inflation low and predictable. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Households may plan spending with more certainty.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Firms may make long-term contracts with greater confidence.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Very tight policy may lower employment.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Global commodity-price shocks may be difficult to control.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0028","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Strong credit growth leads shoppers to increase spending while factories are at full capacity. Decision-makers are considering whether to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Inflationary pressure may ease.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): More stable prices can protect real incomes.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Reduced spending may slow growth.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Supply shortages may keep some prices elevated.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0029","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce dependence on expensive imported fuel. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to reduce dependence on expensive imported fuel would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Input costs may become more stable.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Lower exposure to fuel shocks could limit future inflation.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Alternative energy projects require large initial investments.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Transitioning away from imported fuel takes time.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0030","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A statistics office compares the cost of a weighted basket with its cost in a base year. Decision-makers are considering whether to publish transparent and regularly updated CPI data. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to publish transparent and regularly updated CPI data would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Workers and firms may negotiate with better price information.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Policy-makers can monitor inflation trends.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): A typical basket may not reflect every household's purchases.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Changes in product quality can complicate price comparisons.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0001","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes real gdp per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Rapid population growth can affect income per head and demands on public services.","Net migration is immigration minus emigration over a period.","The balance between primary, secondary and tertiary production varies with development.","Real GDP per head is inflation-adjusted total domestic output divided by population."],"answer":3,"why":"Real GDP per head: Real GDP per head is inflation-adjusted total domestic output divided by population."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0002","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Country A produces $60 billion of real output for 6 million residents. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Natural resource endowments","Real GDP per head","Death rate","Progressive tax and poverty"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates real gdp per head: Real GDP per head is inflation-adjusted total domestic output divided by population."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0003","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of real gdp per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Country A produces $60 billion of real output for 6 million residents.","Full-time farm workers struggle to pay for rent and childcare.","A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold.","A country receives 100,000 arrivals while 70,000 residents leave."],"answer":0,"why":"Country A produces $60 billion of real output for 6 million residents. This illustrates real gdp per head is inflation-adjusted total domestic output divided by population."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0004","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to real gdp per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The $10,000 figure estimates average real output per resident, not each person's income.","Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty.","Lost income and treatment costs can deepen poverty."],"answer":0,"why":"The $10,000 figure estimates average real output per resident, not each person's income."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0005","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 18 births and 6 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["18","24","12","6"],"answer":2,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 18 − 6 = 12 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0006","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use real GDP per head alongside other living-standard indicators. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Transport and electricity networks may improve.","The figure hides inequalities in income distribution.","Families may maintain essential spending while searching.","More children may access education."],"answer":1,"why":"The figure hides inequalities in income distribution."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0007","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to use real GDP per head alongside other living-standard indicators?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Changes over time can help monitor average material prosperity.","A growing child population also raises schooling costs.","Workers outside formal employment may not benefit.","New jobs may be scarce in a local downturn."],"answer":0,"why":"Changes over time can help monitor average material prosperity."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0008","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to use real GDP per head alongside other living-standard indicators less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Social and workplace participation may improve.","New industries may become possible outside mining.","It excludes much unpaid work and environmental damage.","New sectors may create skilled employment."],"answer":2,"why":"It excludes much unpaid work and environmental damage."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0009","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to use real GDP per head alongside other living-standard indicators. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Transport and electricity networks may improve.","Firms may keep experienced employees.","More children may access education.","Analysts can compare average economic output across populations."],"answer":3,"why":"Analysts can compare average economic output across populations."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0010","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Country A produces $60 billion of real output for 6 million residents. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal.","Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head.","Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity.","The $10,000 figure estimates average real output per resident, not each person's income."],"answer":3,"why":"The $10,000 figure estimates average real output per resident, not each person's income."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0011","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes human development index?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Saving provides funds that may finance investment in productive capital.","The Human Development Index combines indicators of health, education and income into a composite measure.","Limited education can reduce access to well-paid employment and perpetuate poverty.","Purchasing power is the quantity of goods and services a given income can buy."],"answer":1,"why":"Human Development Index: The Human Development Index combines indicators of health, education and income into a composite measure."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0012","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Country B reports life expectancy, years of schooling and national income per head. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Education and poverty","Human Development Index","Real GDP per head","Population age structure"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates human development index: The Human Development Index combines indicators of health, education and income into a composite measure."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0013","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 65 thousand people immigrate and 18 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["65 thousand","83 thousand","47 thousand","18 thousand"],"answer":2,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 65 − 18 = 47 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0014","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to human development index?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty.","The HDI gives a broader view of development than output alone.","Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families."],"answer":2,"why":"The HDI gives a broader view of development than output alone."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0015","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","High tax rates may reduce incentives for some earners.","Some employers may reduce hours or hiring.","Spending requirements can exceed fiscal capacity."],"answer":0,"why":"Health and education outcomes receive greater attention."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0016","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More learners may acquire employable skills.","A national average conceals regional inequalities.","Work absences caused by illness may fall.","Workers may use their skills more effectively."],"answer":1,"why":"A national average conceals regional inequalities."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0017","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Remote households may still face travel barriers.","Weak financial institutions may misallocate investment funds.","Governments may identify non-income development gaps.","Some workers may still prefer opportunities abroad."],"answer":2,"why":"Governments may identify non-income development gaps."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0018","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A suitable workforce can help resources be used productively.","Reduced financial exclusion can support opportunity.","Child poverty may fall in the short term.","The index omits some environmental and freedom-related outcomes."],"answer":3,"why":"The index omits some environmental and freedom-related outcomes."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0019","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","Work absences caused by illness may fall.","Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand.","Infant and maternal health may improve."],"answer":0,"why":"Health and education outcomes receive greater attention."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0020","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Country B reports life expectancy, years of schooling and national income per head. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity.","The HDI gives a broader view of development than output alone.","Insufficient basic consumption directly damages health and welfare.","Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output."],"answer":1,"why":"The HDI gives a broader view of development than output alone."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0021","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $30 billion and a population of 5 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$6,000","$1,200","$30,000","$30"],"answer":0,"why":"GDP per head = $30 billion / 5 million people = $6,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0022","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country's life expectancy rises after drinking-water systems and clinics improve. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Life expectancy","Unemployment and poverty","Healthcare differences","Relative poverty"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates life expectancy: Life expectancy indicates the average years a newborn would live if current mortality patterns continued."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0023","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of life expectancy?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A country records eight deaths per 1,000 inhabitants in a year.","A country's life expectancy rises after drinking-water systems and clinics improve.","One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates.","An illness stops a self-employed driver working for three months."],"answer":1,"why":"A country's life expectancy rises after drinking-water systems and clinics improve. This illustrates life expectancy indicates the average years a newborn would live if current mortality patterns continued."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0024","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to life expectancy?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Insufficient basic consumption directly damages health and welfare.","Better health conditions can improve longevity and support quality of life.","Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential."],"answer":1,"why":"Better health conditions can improve longevity and support quality of life."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0025","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Public spending commitments may rise.","Fewer preventable deaths may improve welfare.","Teacher recruitment and school buildings are costly.","Higher saving can reduce current consumption."],"answer":1,"why":"Fewer preventable deaths may improve welfare."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0026","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A larger skilled workforce may raise output.","Output per worker may increase.","Hospitals and programmes require reliable funding.","Infant and maternal health may improve."],"answer":2,"why":"Hospitals and programmes require reliable funding."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0027","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Some workers may still prefer opportunities abroad.","Infrastructure expansion has a high upfront cost.","Poor governance may cause the revenues to be wasted.","Healthier workers may miss fewer working days."],"answer":3,"why":"Healthier workers may miss fewer working days."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0028","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["New sectors may create skilled employment.","Life expectancy and well-being may increase.","Benefits may be limited by poor sanitation outside healthcare.","Children may become healthier future workers."],"answer":2,"why":"Benefits may be limited by poor sanitation outside healthcare."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0029","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Fewer preventable deaths may improve welfare.","Pay may rise for some low-wage employees.","Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies.","Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Fewer preventable deaths may improve welfare."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0030","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 21 births and 9 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["30","9","21","12"],"answer":3,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 21 − 9 = 12 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0031","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes education indicator?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Rapid population growth can affect income per head and demands on public services.","Unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work.","Saving provides funds that may finance investment in productive capital.","Years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development."],"answer":3,"why":"Education indicator: Years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0032","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Non-material living standards","Low wages and poverty","Education indicator","Purchasing power"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates education indicator: Years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0033","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of education indicator?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Some families cannot afford enough nutritious meals or safe drinking water.","A country has a growing share of people aged over 65.","A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold.","A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers."],"answer":3,"why":"A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers. This illustrates years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0034","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to education indicator?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents.","Changes in mortality affect total population and can reflect health conditions or age structure.","Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential.","Resources may support export revenue if infrastructure and institutions enable efficient use."],"answer":2,"why":"Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0035","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to provide more affordable secondary school places. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["New industries may need expensive infrastructure.","Healthcare projects need recurrent spending.","Forecasts about future population can be wrong.","More students may complete formal education."],"answer":3,"why":"More students may complete formal education."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0036","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to provide more affordable secondary school places. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand.","Teacher shortages may reduce quality.","Preventable deaths may decline.","Families may maintain essential spending while searching."],"answer":1,"why":"Teacher shortages may reduce quality."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0037","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to provide more affordable secondary school places?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Future workers may become more productive.","Workers outside formal employment may not benefit.","Poor governance may cause the revenues to be wasted.","Weak financial institutions may misallocate investment funds."],"answer":0,"why":"Future workers may become more productive."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0038","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 95 thousand people immigrate and 27 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["95 thousand","68 thousand","27 thousand","122 thousand"],"answer":1,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 95 − 27 = 68 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0039","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to provide more affordable secondary school places. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies.","The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price.","More students may complete formal education.","Families may maintain essential spending while searching."],"answer":2,"why":"More students may complete formal education."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0040","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Transfers can immediately increase disposable income and reduce hardship.","More productive inputs can support higher wages and output per head.","Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential.","Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold."],"answer":2,"why":"Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0041","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes income inequality?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Rapid population growth can affect income per head and demands on public services.","Unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work.","An optimum population is a population size consistent with the highest potential output or income per person under given conditions.","Income inequality describes how unevenly income is distributed between people or households."],"answer":3,"why":"Income inequality: Income inequality describes how unevenly income is distributed between people or households."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0042","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Average incomes rise in a city, but most extra income goes to its highest earners. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Human Development Index","Income inequality","Education and poverty","Net migration"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates income inequality: Income inequality describes how unevenly income is distributed between people or households."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0043","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of income inequality?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Average incomes rise in a city, but most extra income goes to its highest earners.","A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff.","An illness stops a self-employed driver working for three months.","A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure."],"answer":0,"why":"Average incomes rise in a city, but most extra income goes to its highest earners. This illustrates income inequality describes how unevenly income is distributed between people or households."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0044","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to income inequality?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty.","Growth in averages need not improve the living standard of every group.","Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head."],"answer":2,"why":"Growth in averages need not improve the living standard of every group."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0045","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce targeted transfers to reduce severe income inequality. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Education benefits appear after a long delay.","Higher saving can reduce current consumption.","Programmes require funding and trained staff.","Lower-income families may afford more essential goods."],"answer":3,"why":"Lower-income families may afford more essential goods."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0046","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $72 billion and a population of 8 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$72,000","$1,125","$9,000","$72"],"answer":2,"why":"GDP per head = $72 billion / 8 million people = $9,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0047","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to introduce targeted transfers to reduce severe income inequality?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Workers outside formal employment may not benefit.","Some workers may still prefer opportunities abroad.","Transfers can reduce hardship and support participation in education.","The relative poverty threshold can rise as median incomes rise."],"answer":2,"why":"Transfers can reduce hardship and support participation in education."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0048","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to introduce targeted transfers to reduce severe income inequality less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Young people may gain better-paid employment opportunities.","Weak targeting may fail to reach the poorest households.","Tax revenues may increase as migrants work.","Children may become healthier future workers."],"answer":1,"why":"Weak targeting may fail to reach the poorest households."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0049","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to introduce targeted transfers to reduce severe income inequality. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Preventable deaths may decline.","Older people may receive more suitable healthcare.","Nutrition and health may improve.","Lower-income families may afford more essential goods."],"answer":3,"why":"Lower-income families may afford more essential goods."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0050","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Average incomes rise in a city, but most extra income goes to its highest earners. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Growth in averages need not improve the living standard of every group.","Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity.","Transfers can immediately increase disposable income and reduce hardship.","A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal."],"answer":0,"why":"Growth in averages need not improve the living standard of every group."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0051","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes purchasing power?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["State benefits transfer resources to eligible people, often to support those on low incomes.","Income inequality describes how unevenly income is distributed between people or households.","Life expectancy indicates the average years a newborn would live if current mortality patterns continued.","Purchasing power is the quantity of goods and services a given income can buy."],"answer":3,"why":"Purchasing power: Purchasing power is the quantity of goods and services a given income can buy."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0052","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A worker's wage rises by 2% while consumer prices rise by 5%. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Birth rate","Purchasing power","Productivity differences","Natural resource endowments"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates purchasing power: Purchasing power is the quantity of goods and services a given income can buy."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0053","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of purchasing power?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income.","A worker's wage rises by 2% while consumer prices rise by 5%.","Workers in one country miss many days because preventable diseases are common.","Full-time farm workers struggle to pay for rent and childcare."],"answer":1,"why":"A worker's wage rises by 2% while consumer prices rise by 5%. This illustrates purchasing power is the quantity of goods and services a given income can buy."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0054","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to purchasing power?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families.","Tax-and-transfer arrangements can narrow differences in disposable income.","Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income.","Relative deprivation can restrict participation in ordinary social and economic activities."],"answer":2,"why":"Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0055","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 24 births and 5 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["24","19","29","5"],"answer":1,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 24 − 5 = 19 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0056","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to protect low-income households during rapid inflation. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More funds may become available for business finance.","Support payments increase fiscal costs.","More households may participate in education and community life.","Preventable deaths may decline."],"answer":1,"why":"Support payments increase fiscal costs."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0057","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to protect low-income households during rapid inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Some workers may still prefer opportunities abroad.","Automation may displace some existing jobs.","A smaller working-age share can constrain tax revenue.","Lower real-income losses may reduce poverty risks."],"answer":3,"why":"Lower real-income losses may reduce poverty risks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0058","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to protect low-income households during rapid inflation less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Payments may not keep pace with very rapid inflation.","Income inequality may decrease.","Workers may retain more productive working time.","Social and workplace participation may improve."],"answer":0,"why":"Payments may not keep pace with very rapid inflation."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0059","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to protect low-income households during rapid inflation. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Families may maintain spending on essentials.","More learners may acquire employable skills.","More households may participate in education and community life.","Infant and maternal health may improve."],"answer":0,"why":"Families may maintain spending on essentials."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0060","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A worker's wage rises by 2% while consumer prices rise by 5%. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income.","Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity.","Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head.","Ageing can raise demand for healthcare and pensions relative to some tax revenues."],"answer":0,"why":"Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0061","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes non-material living standards?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Living standards also involve health, safety, leisure and environmental quality, not just output.","Unequal healthcare access can contribute to differences in development and productivity.","An optimum population is a population size consistent with the highest potential output or income per person under given conditions.","Emigration occurs when residents leave their country to live abroad."],"answer":0,"why":"Non-material living standards: Living standards also involve health, safety, leisure and environmental quality, not just output."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0062","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Residents earn more after a factory opens but face noisier streets and worse air quality. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Population age structure","Population growth differences","Unemployment and poverty","Non-material living standards"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates non-material living standards: Living standards also involve health, safety, leisure and environmental quality, not just output."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0063","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 125 thousand people immigrate and 36 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["89 thousand","36 thousand","125 thousand","161 thousand"],"answer":0,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 125 − 36 = 89 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0064","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to non-material living standards?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher money incomes can coexist with reduced well-being.","New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods.","Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher money incomes can coexist with reduced well-being."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0065","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to include environmental quality when assessing development. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Healthcare projects need recurrent spending.","Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life.","Healthcare programmes need investment.","Investment needs finance and technical skills."],"answer":1,"why":"Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0066","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to include environmental quality when assessing development. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Measuring non-material benefits is difficult.","Families may maintain essential spending while searching.","Transport and electricity networks may improve.","Pay may rise for some low-wage employees."],"answer":0,"why":"Measuring non-material benefits is difficult."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0067","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to include environmental quality when assessing development?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Overseas recruitment may harm source-country labour supply.","Automation may displace some existing jobs.","New jobs may be scarce in a local downturn.","Communities could avoid some hidden costs of growth."],"answer":3,"why":"Communities could avoid some hidden costs of growth."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0068","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to include environmental quality when assessing development less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Social and workplace participation may improve.","Environmental protection may raise short-run production costs.","Future workforce productivity may improve.","Children may become healthier future workers."],"answer":1,"why":"Environmental protection may raise short-run production costs."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0069","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to include environmental quality when assessing development. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Older people may receive more suitable healthcare.","Illness may be treated earlier and at lower cost.","Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life.","Preventable deaths may decline."],"answer":2,"why":"Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0070","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Residents earn more after a factory opens but face noisier streets and worse air quality. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Ageing can raise demand for healthcare and pensions relative to some tax revenues.","More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents.","New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods.","Higher money incomes can coexist with reduced well-being."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher money incomes can coexist with reduced well-being."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0071","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $10 billion and a population of 2 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$2,500","$10","$5,000","$10,000"],"answer":2,"why":"GDP per head = $10 billion / 2 million people = $5,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0072","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Two countries have similar GDP per head but different school attendance and healthcare access. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Income inequality","Saving and investment","Progressive tax and poverty","International comparison limits"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates international comparison limits: Living-standard comparisons can be misleading because national averages and measurement methods differ."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0073","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of international comparison limits?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Two countries have similar GDP per head but different school attendance and healthcare access.","One economy relies heavily on commodity farming while another has a diverse services sector.","Workers in one country miss many days because preventable diseases are common.","A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income."],"answer":0,"why":"Two countries have similar GDP per head but different school attendance and healthcare access. This illustrates living-standard comparisons can be misleading because national averages and measurement methods differ."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0074","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to international comparison limits?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Economic indicators require interpretation alongside social conditions.","A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime.","Lost income and treatment costs can deepen poverty.","More productive inputs can support higher wages and output per head."],"answer":0,"why":"Economic indicators require interpretation alongside social conditions."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0075","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to compare several living-standard indicators across countries. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income.","Teacher recruitment and school buildings are costly.","Healthcare programmes need investment.","Pension and care expenditure may rise."],"answer":0,"why":"Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0076","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to compare several living-standard indicators across countries. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price.","Data quality may differ between countries.","More learners may acquire employable skills.","Extra resources may reach households with urgent needs."],"answer":1,"why":"Data quality may differ between countries."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0077","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to compare several living-standard indicators across countries?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["An ageing population can keep the crude death rate high.","New jobs may be scarce in a local downturn.","Policy may target the most serious social gaps.","Infrastructure expansion has a high upfront cost."],"answer":2,"why":"Policy may target the most serious social gaps."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0078","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to compare several living-standard indicators across countries less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Income inequality may decrease.","A large collection of indicators can be difficult to summarise.","A suitable workforce can help resources be used productively.","Workers may retain more productive working time."],"answer":1,"why":"A large collection of indicators can be difficult to summarise."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0079","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to compare several living-standard indicators across countries. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income.","Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies.","Workers may use their skills more effectively.","Output per worker may increase."],"answer":0,"why":"Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0080","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 17 births and 8 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["25","8","17","9"],"answer":3,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 17 − 8 = 9 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0081","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes absolute poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Absolute poverty means lacking the resources to meet basic needs such as food, shelter and clean water.","Years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development.","Immigration occurs when people enter a country to live there.","An optimum population is a population size consistent with the highest potential output or income per person under given conditions."],"answer":0,"why":"Absolute poverty: Absolute poverty means lacking the resources to meet basic needs such as food, shelter and clean water."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0082","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Some families cannot afford enough nutritious meals or safe drinking water. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Absolute poverty","Natural resource endowments","Death rate","Net migration"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates absolute poverty: Absolute poverty means lacking the resources to meet basic needs such as food, shelter and clean water."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0083","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of absolute poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A country receives 100,000 arrivals while 70,000 residents leave.","Residents earn more after a factory opens but face noisier streets and worse air quality.","One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates.","Some families cannot afford enough nutritious meals or safe drinking water."],"answer":3,"why":"Some families cannot afford enough nutritious meals or safe drinking water. This illustrates absolute poverty means lacking the resources to meet basic needs such as food, shelter and clean water."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0084","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to absolute poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential.","Insufficient basic consumption directly damages health and welfare.","Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs.","More productive inputs can support higher wages and output per head."],"answer":1,"why":"Insufficient basic consumption directly damages health and welfare."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0085","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand access to essential food and water for very poor households. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Nutrition and health may improve.","Redistribution needs tax revenue.","Higher saving can reduce current consumption.","Spending requirements can exceed fiscal capacity."],"answer":0,"why":"Nutrition and health may improve."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0086","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand access to essential food and water for very poor households. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income.","Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","Providing services needs sustained funding.","Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled."],"answer":2,"why":"Providing services needs sustained funding."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0087","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand access to essential food and water for very poor households?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["An ageing population can keep the crude death rate high.","Children may be better able to attend school.","Benefits may be limited by poor sanitation outside healthcare.","A growing child population also raises schooling costs."],"answer":1,"why":"Children may be better able to attend school."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0088","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 155 thousand people immigrate and 45 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["110 thousand","45 thousand","155 thousand","200 thousand"],"answer":0,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 155 − 45 = 110 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0089","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand access to essential food and water for very poor households. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Workers may use their skills more effectively.","Nutrition and health may improve.","Firms may keep experienced employees.","The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price."],"answer":1,"why":"Nutrition and health may improve."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0090","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Some families cannot afford enough nutritious meals or safe drinking water. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Insufficient basic consumption directly damages health and welfare.","Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output.","The $10,000 figure estimates average real output per resident, not each person's income.","Better health conditions can improve longevity and support quality of life."],"answer":0,"why":"Insufficient basic consumption directly damages health and welfare."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0091","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes relative poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Population age structure is the distribution of people across different age groups.","Relative poverty refers to having much lower resources than the typical standard in a society.","Years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development.","Emigration occurs when residents leave their country to live abroad."],"answer":1,"why":"Relative poverty: Relative poverty refers to having much lower resources than the typical standard in a society."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0092","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A household can buy basic food but cannot afford services considered normal in its community. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Sector composition","Non-material living standards","Natural resource endowments","Relative poverty"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates relative poverty: Relative poverty refers to having much lower resources than the typical standard in a society."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0093","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of relative poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Two countries have similar GDP per head but different school attendance and healthcare access.","Country B reports life expectancy, years of schooling and national income per head.","A household can buy basic food but cannot afford services considered normal in its community.","A city attracts healthcare workers from abroad."],"answer":2,"why":"A household can buy basic food but cannot afford services considered normal in its community. This illustrates relative poverty refers to having much lower resources than the typical standard in a society."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0094","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to relative poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Relative deprivation can restrict participation in ordinary social and economic activities.","A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal.","Changes in mortality affect total population and can reflect health conditions or age structure.","The HDI gives a broader view of development than output alone."],"answer":0,"why":"Relative deprivation can restrict participation in ordinary social and economic activities."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0095","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to provide targeted assistance to low-income households. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Teacher recruitment and school buildings are costly.","More households may participate in education and community life.","The figure hides inequalities in income distribution.","Housing and services may face extra demand."],"answer":1,"why":"More households may participate in education and community life."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0096","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $40 billion and a population of 5 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$8,000","$1,600","$40,000","$40"],"answer":0,"why":"GDP per head = $40 billion / 5 million people = $8,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0097","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to provide targeted assistance to low-income households?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Reduced financial exclusion can support opportunity.","Payments may not keep pace with very rapid inflation.","Some families may still require children to work.","Poor governance may cause the revenues to be wasted."],"answer":0,"why":"Reduced financial exclusion can support opportunity."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0098","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to provide targeted assistance to low-income households less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Children may become healthier future workers.","The relative poverty threshold can rise as median incomes rise.","Changes over time can help monitor average material prosperity.","Policy may target the most serious social gaps."],"answer":1,"why":"The relative poverty threshold can rise as median incomes rise."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0099","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to provide targeted assistance to low-income households. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life.","Output per worker may increase.","More households may participate in education and community life.","Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income."],"answer":2,"why":"More households may participate in education and community life."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0100","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A household can buy basic food but cannot afford services considered normal in its community. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Economic indicators require interpretation alongside social conditions.","The HDI gives a broader view of development than output alone.","Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity.","Relative deprivation can restrict participation in ordinary social and economic activities."],"answer":3,"why":"Relative deprivation can restrict participation in ordinary social and economic activities."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0101","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes unemployment and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work.","Saving provides funds that may finance investment in productive capital.","Living standards also involve health, safety, leisure and environmental quality, not just output.","Living-standard comparisons can be misleading because national averages and measurement methods differ."],"answer":0,"why":"Unemployment and poverty: Unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0102","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Non-material living standards","Life expectancy","Unemployment and poverty","Relative poverty"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates unemployment and poverty: Unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0103","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of unemployment and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Country A produces $60 billion of real output for 6 million residents.","A worker's wage rises by 2% while consumer prices rise by 5%.","A country's output grows 3% while population grows 4% in the same year.","A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income."],"answer":3,"why":"A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income. This illustrates unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0104","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to unemployment and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs.","Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families.","Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","Changes in mortality affect total population and can reflect health conditions or age structure."],"answer":2,"why":"Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0105","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 20 births and 11 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["11","31","20","9"],"answer":3,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 20 − 11 = 9 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0106","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to provide job-search support and temporary income assistance. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life.","More funds may become available for business finance.","Government benefit expenditure may increase.","Preventable deaths may decline."],"answer":2,"why":"Government benefit expenditure may increase."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0107","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to provide job-search support and temporary income assistance?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Poor sanitation can undermine treatment benefits.","Better job matching may speed the return to work.","Benefits may be limited by poor sanitation outside healthcare.","A large collection of indicators can be difficult to summarise."],"answer":1,"why":"Better job matching may speed the return to work."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0108","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to provide job-search support and temporary income assistance less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More people may remain able to work.","New sectors may create skilled employment.","New jobs may be scarce in a local downturn.","Communities could avoid some hidden costs of growth."],"answer":2,"why":"New jobs may be scarce in a local downturn."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0109","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to provide job-search support and temporary income assistance. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled.","A larger skilled workforce may raise output.","Older people may receive more suitable healthcare.","Families may maintain essential spending while searching."],"answer":3,"why":"Families may maintain essential spending while searching."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0110","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential.","Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income.","The $10,000 figure estimates average real output per resident, not each person's income."],"answer":1,"why":"Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0111","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes low wages and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Low wages may leave employed people unable to afford basic needs.","Immigration occurs when people enter a country to live there.","Relative poverty refers to having much lower resources than the typical standard in a society.","The Human Development Index combines indicators of health, education and income into a composite measure."],"answer":0,"why":"Low wages and poverty: Low wages may leave employed people unable to afford basic needs."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0112","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Full-time farm workers struggle to pay for rent and childcare. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Low wages and poverty","State benefits","Productivity differences","Real GDP per head"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates low wages and poverty: Low wages may leave employed people unable to afford basic needs."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0113","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 185 thousand people immigrate and 54 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["239 thousand","131 thousand","185 thousand","54 thousand"],"answer":1,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 185 − 54 = 131 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0114","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to low wages and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs.","Growth in averages need not improve the living standard of every group.","Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty."],"answer":3,"why":"A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0115","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce a carefully set minimum wage. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Pay may rise for some low-wage employees.","Programmes require funding and trained staff.","A national average conceals regional inequalities.","Hospitals and programmes require reliable funding."],"answer":0,"why":"Pay may rise for some low-wage employees."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0116","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce a carefully set minimum wage. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Some employers may reduce hours or hiring.","Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled.","Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Some employers may reduce hours or hiring."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0117","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to introduce a carefully set minimum wage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Poor governance may cause the revenues to be wasted.","Benefits depend on graduates finding appropriate jobs.","Higher earnings could improve household nutrition and housing.","Recognition of qualifications may remain difficult."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher earnings could improve household nutrition and housing."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0118","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to introduce a carefully set minimum wage less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Workers outside formal employment may not benefit.","Tax revenues may increase as migrants work.","New industries may become possible outside mining.","Lower real-income losses may reduce poverty risks."],"answer":0,"why":"Workers outside formal employment may not benefit."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0119","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to introduce a carefully set minimum wage. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled.","Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life.","Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income.","Pay may rise for some low-wage employees."],"answer":3,"why":"Pay may rise for some low-wage employees."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0120","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Full-time farm workers struggle to pay for rent and childcare. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Better health conditions can improve longevity and support quality of life.","Higher money incomes can coexist with reduced well-being.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty.","Economic indicators require interpretation alongside social conditions."],"answer":2,"why":"A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0121","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $32 billion and a population of 8 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$32,000","$500","$4,000","$32"],"answer":2,"why":"GDP per head = $32 billion / 8 million people = $4,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0122","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: An illness stops a self-employed driver working for three months. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Progressive tax and poverty","Healthcare and poverty","Relative poverty","Purchasing power"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates healthcare and poverty: Poor health can lower earning ability and impose medical expenses on families."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0123","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of healthcare and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["An illness stops a self-employed driver working for three months.","Two countries have similar GDP per head but different school attendance and healthcare access.","Average incomes rise in a city, but most extra income goes to its highest earners.","One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates."],"answer":0,"why":"An illness stops a self-employed driver working for three months. This illustrates poor health can lower earning ability and impose medical expenses on families."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0124","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to healthcare and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents.","Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families.","Lost income and treatment costs can deepen poverty.","Better health conditions can improve longevity and support quality of life."],"answer":2,"why":"Lost income and treatment costs can deepen poverty."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0125","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to make essential primary healthcare more accessible. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Data quality may differ between countries.","Illness may be treated earlier and at lower cost.","New industries may need expensive infrastructure.","Healthcare programmes need investment."],"answer":1,"why":"Illness may be treated earlier and at lower cost."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0126","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to make essential primary healthcare more accessible. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Public healthcare requires doctors and funding.","Output per worker may increase.","A larger skilled workforce may raise output.","Lower-income families may afford more essential goods."],"answer":0,"why":"Public healthcare requires doctors and funding."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0127","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to make essential primary healthcare more accessible?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Workers may retain more productive working time.","Poor sanitation can undermine treatment benefits.","Environmental protection may raise short-run production costs.","A growing child population also raises schooling costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Workers may retain more productive working time."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0128","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to make essential primary healthcare more accessible less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Policy may target the most serious social gaps.","Healthier workers may miss fewer working days.","New sectors may create skilled employment.","Remote households may still face travel barriers."],"answer":3,"why":"Remote households may still face travel barriers."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0129","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to make essential primary healthcare more accessible. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled.","More children may access education.","Illness may be treated earlier and at lower cost.","Transport and electricity networks may improve."],"answer":2,"why":"Illness may be treated earlier and at lower cost."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0130","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 23 births and 7 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["30","23","7","16"],"answer":3,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 23 − 7 = 16 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0131","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes education and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development.","Low wages may leave employed people unable to afford basic needs.","Living-standard comparisons can be misleading because national averages and measurement methods differ.","Limited education can reduce access to well-paid employment and perpetuate poverty."],"answer":3,"why":"Education and poverty: Limited education can reduce access to well-paid employment and perpetuate poverty."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0132","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Birth rate","Purchasing power","Education and poverty","Net migration"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates education and poverty: Limited education can reduce access to well-paid employment and perpetuate poverty."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0133","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of education and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations.","A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure.","Country B reports life expectancy, years of schooling and national income per head.","Country C's factories use modern machines while Country D's rely on outdated equipment."],"answer":0,"why":"Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations. This illustrates limited education can reduce access to well-paid employment and perpetuate poverty."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0134","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to education and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime.","The HDI gives a broader view of development than output alone.","Higher money incomes can coexist with reduced well-being.","Ageing can raise demand for healthcare and pensions relative to some tax revenues."],"answer":0,"why":"A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0135","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to subsidise schooling and vocational courses in low-income areas. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher saving can reduce current consumption.","Teacher recruitment and school buildings are costly.","More learners may acquire employable skills.","Healthcare projects need recurrent spending."],"answer":2,"why":"More learners may acquire employable skills."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0136","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to subsidise schooling and vocational courses in low-income areas. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Education benefits appear after a long delay.","Preventable deaths may decline.","Lower-income families may afford more essential goods.","Work absences caused by illness may fall."],"answer":0,"why":"Education benefits appear after a long delay."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0137","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to subsidise schooling and vocational courses in low-income areas?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Recognition of qualifications may remain difficult.","Future wages and productivity may increase.","Payments may not keep pace with very rapid inflation.","Weak financial institutions may misallocate investment funds."],"answer":1,"why":"Future wages and productivity may increase."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0138","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 215 thousand people immigrate and 63 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["63 thousand","152 thousand","215 thousand","278 thousand"],"answer":1,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 215 − 63 = 152 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0139","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to subsidise schooling and vocational courses in low-income areas. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More learners may acquire employable skills.","Fewer preventable deaths may improve welfare.","Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","More funds may become available for business finance."],"answer":0,"why":"More learners may acquire employable skills."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0140","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs.","More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents.","Higher money incomes can coexist with reduced well-being.","A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime."],"answer":3,"why":"A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0141","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes state benefits?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Relative poverty refers to having much lower resources than the typical standard in a society.","The birth rate measures live births per thousand people in the population in a year.","Unequal healthcare access can contribute to differences in development and productivity.","State benefits transfer resources to eligible people, often to support those on low incomes."],"answer":3,"why":"State benefits: State benefits transfer resources to eligible people, often to support those on low incomes."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0142","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Education and poverty","State benefits","Low wages and poverty","Purchasing power"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates state benefits: State benefits transfer resources to eligible people, often to support those on low incomes."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0143","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of state benefits?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A country records eight deaths per 1,000 inhabitants in a year.","A country records 20 births per 1,000 inhabitants in a year.","Country A produces $60 billion of real output for 6 million residents.","A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold."],"answer":3,"why":"A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold. This illustrates state benefits transfer resources to eligible people, often to support those on low incomes."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0144","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to state benefits?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Transfers can immediately increase disposable income and reduce hardship.","Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs.","Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income.","Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head."],"answer":0,"why":"Transfers can immediately increase disposable income and reduce hardship."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0145","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand well-targeted child-support benefits. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Investment needs finance and technical skills.","Pension and care expenditure may rise.","Teacher recruitment and school buildings are costly.","Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies."],"answer":3,"why":"Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0146","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $14 billion and a population of 2 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$14","$14,000","$7,000","$3,500"],"answer":2,"why":"GDP per head = $14 billion / 2 million people = $7,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0147","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand well-targeted child-support benefits?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Weak targeting may fail to reach the poorest households.","Benefits may be limited by poor sanitation outside healthcare.","The index omits some environmental and freedom-related outcomes.","Child poverty may fall in the short term."],"answer":3,"why":"Child poverty may fall in the short term."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0148","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand well-targeted child-support benefits less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Future workforce productivity may improve.","Young people may gain better-paid employment opportunities.","Incorrect eligibility decisions may exclude needy households.","Communities may use existing skills and experience of older residents."],"answer":2,"why":"Incorrect eligibility decisions may exclude needy households."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0149","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand well-targeted child-support benefits. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life.","Workers may use their skills more effectively.","More students may complete formal education.","Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies."],"answer":3,"why":"Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0150","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity.","Ageing can raise demand for healthcare and pensions relative to some tax revenues.","Transfers can immediately increase disposable income and reduce hardship.","Resources may support export revenue if infrastructure and institutions enable efficient use."],"answer":2,"why":"Transfers can immediately increase disposable income and reduce hardship."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0151","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes progressive tax and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Immigration occurs when people enter a country to live there.","Population age structure is the distribution of people across different age groups.","Progressive taxation can finance redistribution by taking a larger income share from higher earners.","Relative poverty refers to having much lower resources than the typical standard in a society."],"answer":2,"why":"Progressive tax and poverty: Progressive taxation can finance redistribution by taking a larger income share from higher earners."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0152","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A finance ministry uses income-tax receipts to support low-income families. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Progressive tax and poverty","Life expectancy","Emigration","Healthcare and poverty"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates progressive tax and poverty: Progressive taxation can finance redistribution by taking a larger income share from higher earners."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0153","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of progressive tax and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure.","A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers.","A finance ministry uses income-tax receipts to support low-income families.","One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates."],"answer":2,"why":"A finance ministry uses income-tax receipts to support low-income families. This illustrates progressive taxation can finance redistribution by taking a larger income share from higher earners."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0154","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to progressive tax and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Tax-and-transfer arrangements can narrow differences in disposable income.","Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential.","Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs.","New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods."],"answer":0,"why":"Tax-and-transfer arrangements can narrow differences in disposable income."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0155","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 26 births and 10 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["10","36","16","26"],"answer":2,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 26 − 10 = 16 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0156","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use progressive taxes to fund anti-poverty programmes. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["High tax rates may reduce incentives for some earners.","Lower-income families may afford more essential goods.","Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life.","Analysts can compare average economic output across populations."],"answer":0,"why":"High tax rates may reduce incentives for some earners."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0157","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to use progressive taxes to fund anti-poverty programmes?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Some families may still require children to work.","Income inequality may decrease.","Recognition of qualifications may remain difficult.","A smaller working-age share can constrain tax revenue."],"answer":1,"why":"Income inequality may decrease."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0158","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to use progressive taxes to fund anti-poverty programmes less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Future workforce productivity may improve.","Avoidance and administration costs can reduce effectiveness.","Social and workplace participation may improve.","Children may become healthier future workers."],"answer":1,"why":"Avoidance and administration costs can reduce effectiveness."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0159","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to use progressive taxes to fund anti-poverty programmes. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand.","Families may maintain spending on essentials.","More children may access education.","Extra resources may reach households with urgent needs."],"answer":3,"why":"Extra resources may reach households with urgent needs."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0160","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A finance ministry uses income-tax receipts to support low-income families. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Tax-and-transfer arrangements can narrow differences in disposable income.","Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output.","Growth in averages need not improve the living standard of every group.","Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head."],"answer":0,"why":"Tax-and-transfer arrangements can narrow differences in disposable income."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0161","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes birth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Unequal access to education can cause persistent gaps in workforce skills between countries.","Years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development.","The birth rate measures live births per thousand people in the population in a year.","Life expectancy indicates the average years a newborn would live if current mortality patterns continued."],"answer":2,"why":"Birth rate: The birth rate measures live births per thousand people in the population in a year."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0162","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country records 20 births per 1,000 inhabitants in a year. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Absolute poverty","Natural resource endowments","Birth rate","Low wages and poverty"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates birth rate: The birth rate measures live births per thousand people in the population in a year."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0163","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 245 thousand people immigrate and 72 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["173 thousand","317 thousand","72 thousand","245 thousand"],"answer":0,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 245 − 72 = 173 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0164","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to birth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Relative deprivation can restrict participation in ordinary social and economic activities.","More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents.","The $10,000 figure estimates average real output per resident, not each person's income.","Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential."],"answer":1,"why":"More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0165","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Support payments increase fiscal costs.","Education benefits appear after a long delay.","Infant and maternal health may improve.","Public healthcare requires doctors and funding."],"answer":2,"why":"Infant and maternal health may improve."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0166","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life.","The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price.","Healthcare programmes need investment.","A larger skilled workforce may raise output."],"answer":2,"why":"Healthcare programmes need investment."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0167","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Benefits depend on the quality of public services.","Children may become healthier future workers.","Workers outside formal employment may not benefit.","A large collection of indicators can be difficult to summarise."],"answer":1,"why":"Children may become healthier future workers."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0168","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Lower real-income losses may reduce poverty risks.","Income inequality may decrease.","A growing child population also raises schooling costs.","Communities could avoid some hidden costs of growth."],"answer":2,"why":"A growing child population also raises schooling costs."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0169","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income.","Pay may rise for some low-wage employees.","Infant and maternal health may improve."],"answer":3,"why":"Infant and maternal health may improve."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0170","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country records 20 births per 1,000 inhabitants in a year. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents.","Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs.","Insufficient basic consumption directly damages health and welfare.","More productive inputs can support higher wages and output per head."],"answer":0,"why":"More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0171","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $15 billion and a population of 5 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$3,000","$15,000","$15","$600"],"answer":0,"why":"GDP per head = $15 billion / 5 million people = $3,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0172","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country records eight deaths per 1,000 inhabitants in a year. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Education differences","Purchasing power","Income inequality","Death rate"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates death rate: The death rate measures deaths per thousand people in the population in a year."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0173","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of death rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates.","A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers.","A country records eight deaths per 1,000 inhabitants in a year.","A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff."],"answer":2,"why":"A country records eight deaths per 1,000 inhabitants in a year. This illustrates the death rate measures deaths per thousand people in the population in a year."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0174","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to death rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Changes in mortality affect total population and can reflect health conditions or age structure.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty.","Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output.","A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime."],"answer":0,"why":"Changes in mortality affect total population and can reflect health conditions or age structure."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0175","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Investment needs finance and technical skills.","Measuring non-material benefits is difficult.","Data quality may differ between countries.","Preventable deaths may decline."],"answer":3,"why":"Preventable deaths may decline."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0176","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Programmes require funding and trained staff.","A larger skilled workforce may raise output.","Output per worker may increase.","Lower-income families may afford more essential goods."],"answer":0,"why":"Programmes require funding and trained staff."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0177","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Weak targeting may fail to reach the poorest households.","Environmental protection may raise short-run production costs.","More people may remain able to work.","Weak financial institutions may misallocate investment funds."],"answer":2,"why":"More people may remain able to work."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0178","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Reduced financial exclusion can support opportunity.","Lower real-income losses may reduce poverty risks.","An ageing population can keep the crude death rate high.","Future workers may become more productive."],"answer":2,"why":"An ageing population can keep the crude death rate high."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0179","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More students may complete formal education.","Lower-income families may afford more essential goods.","Work absences caused by illness may fall.","Preventable deaths may decline."],"answer":3,"why":"Preventable deaths may decline."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0180","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 19 births and 6 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["25","6","13","19"],"answer":2,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 19 − 6 = 13 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0181","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Net migration is immigration minus emigration over a period.","Poor health can lower earning ability and impose medical expenses on families.","Unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work.","The death rate measures deaths per thousand people in the population in a year."],"answer":0,"why":"Net migration: Net migration is immigration minus emigration over a period."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0182","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country receives 100,000 arrivals while 70,000 residents leave. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Birth rate","Relative poverty","Real GDP per head","Net migration"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates net migration: Net migration is immigration minus emigration over a period."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0183","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Workers in one country miss many days because preventable diseases are common.","A country receives 100,000 arrivals while 70,000 residents leave.","A country's output grows 3% while population grows 4% in the same year.","A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income."],"answer":1,"why":"A country receives 100,000 arrivals while 70,000 residents leave. This illustrates net migration is immigration minus emigration over a period."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0184","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential.","Economic indicators require interpretation alongside social conditions.","Resources may support export revenue if infrastructure and institutions enable efficient use.","A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal."],"answer":3,"why":"A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0185","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to make it easier for needed skilled workers to migrate legally. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Public spending commitments may rise.","The figure hides inequalities in income distribution.","Hospitals and programmes require reliable funding.","Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled."],"answer":3,"why":"Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0186","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to make it easier for needed skilled workers to migrate legally. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Extra resources may reach households with urgent needs.","Transport and electricity networks may improve.","Housing and services may face extra demand.","More funds may become available for business finance."],"answer":2,"why":"Housing and services may face extra demand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0187","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to make it easier for needed skilled workers to migrate legally?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Weak financial institutions may misallocate investment funds.","Environmental protection may raise short-run production costs.","Tax revenues may increase as migrants work.","Remote households may still face travel barriers."],"answer":2,"why":"Tax revenues may increase as migrants work."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0188","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 275 thousand people immigrate and 81 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["81 thousand","356 thousand","194 thousand","275 thousand"],"answer":2,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 275 − 81 = 194 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0189","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to make it easier for needed skilled workers to migrate legally. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled.","Lower-income families may afford more essential goods.","The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price.","Work absences caused by illness may fall."],"answer":0,"why":"Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0190","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country receives 100,000 arrivals while 70,000 residents leave. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal.","Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty.","Relative deprivation can restrict participation in ordinary social and economic activities."],"answer":0,"why":"A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0191","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes immigration?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Rapid population growth can affect income per head and demands on public services.","The balance between primary, secondary and tertiary production varies with development.","Progressive taxation can finance redistribution by taking a larger income share from higher earners.","Immigration occurs when people enter a country to live there."],"answer":3,"why":"Immigration: Immigration occurs when people enter a country to live there."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0192","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A city attracts healthcare workers from abroad. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Optimum population","Immigration","Education and poverty","Purchasing power"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates immigration: Immigration occurs when people enter a country to live there."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0193","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of immigration?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A city attracts healthcare workers from abroad.","A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff.","Average incomes rise in a city, but most extra income goes to its highest earners.","A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure."],"answer":0,"why":"A city attracts healthcare workers from abroad. This illustrates immigration occurs when people enter a country to live there."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0194","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to immigration?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Lost income and treatment costs can deepen poverty.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty.","Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity.","New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods."],"answer":3,"why":"New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0195","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to provide integration and language support for new migrants. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Education benefits appear after a long delay.","Higher saving can reduce current consumption.","Workers may use their skills more effectively.","Providing services needs sustained funding."],"answer":2,"why":"Workers may use their skills more effectively."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0196","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $48 billion and a population of 8 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$750","$6,000","$48","$48,000"],"answer":1,"why":"GDP per head = $48 billion / 8 million people = $6,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0197","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to provide integration and language support for new migrants?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Poor sanitation can undermine treatment benefits.","Weak targeting may fail to reach the poorest households.","Payments may not keep pace with very rapid inflation.","Social and workplace participation may improve."],"answer":3,"why":"Social and workplace participation may improve."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0198","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to provide integration and language support for new migrants less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Firms may become more competitive abroad.","Recognition of qualifications may remain difficult.","Healthier workers may miss fewer working days.","Governments may identify non-income development gaps."],"answer":1,"why":"Recognition of qualifications may remain difficult."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0199","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to provide integration and language support for new migrants. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Lower-income families may afford more essential goods.","Families may maintain essential spending while searching.","More children may access education.","Workers may use their skills more effectively."],"answer":3,"why":"Workers may use their skills more effectively."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0200","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A city attracts healthcare workers from abroad. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs.","Tax-and-transfer arrangements can narrow differences in disposable income.","New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods.","Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity."],"answer":2,"why":"New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0201","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes emigration?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The balance between primary, secondary and tertiary production varies with development.","Emigration occurs when residents leave their country to live abroad.","An optimum population is a population size consistent with the highest potential output or income per person under given conditions.","Absolute poverty means lacking the resources to meet basic needs such as food, shelter and clean water."],"answer":1,"why":"Emigration: Emigration occurs when residents leave their country to live abroad."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0202","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Young engineers move overseas after receiving attractive job offers. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Saving and investment","Absolute poverty","Emigration","Death rate"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates emigration: Emigration occurs when residents leave their country to live abroad."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0203","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of emigration?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure.","Young engineers move overseas after receiving attractive job offers.","A household can buy basic food but cannot afford services considered normal in its community.","A worker's wage rises by 2% while consumer prices rise by 5%."],"answer":1,"why":"Young engineers move overseas after receiving attractive job offers. This illustrates emigration occurs when residents leave their country to live abroad."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0204","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to emigration?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families.","Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity.","Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output."],"answer":1,"why":"Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0205","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 22 births and 9 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["13","9","31","22"],"answer":0,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 22 − 9 = 13 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0206","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve domestic career opportunities to retain skilled workers. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More students may complete formal education.","Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","Higher public and private wage costs may follow.","Nutrition and health may improve."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher public and private wage costs may follow."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0207","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve domestic career opportunities to retain skilled workers?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["It excludes much unpaid work and environmental damage.","Benefits may be limited by poor sanitation outside healthcare.","Automation may displace some existing jobs.","The tax base may be more stable."],"answer":3,"why":"The tax base may be more stable."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0208","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to improve domestic career opportunities to retain skilled workers less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Life expectancy and well-being may increase.","Some workers may still prefer opportunities abroad.","Workers may retain more productive working time.","Healthier workers may miss fewer working days."],"answer":1,"why":"Some workers may still prefer opportunities abroad."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0209","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve domestic career opportunities to retain skilled workers. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Nutrition and health may improve.","Firms may keep experienced employees.","Transport and electricity networks may improve.","More learners may acquire employable skills."],"answer":1,"why":"Firms may keep experienced employees."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0210","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Young engineers move overseas after receiving attractive job offers. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity.","Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families.","Lost income and treatment costs can deepen poverty.","Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity."],"answer":1,"why":"Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0211","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes population age structure?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Unequal access to education can cause persistent gaps in workforce skills between countries.","An optimum population is a population size consistent with the highest potential output or income per person under given conditions.","Population age structure is the distribution of people across different age groups.","Differences in output per worker help explain why countries have different income levels."],"answer":2,"why":"Population age structure: Population age structure is the distribution of people across different age groups."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0212","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country has a growing share of people aged over 65. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Death rate","Progressive tax and poverty","Non-material living standards","Population age structure"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates population age structure: Population age structure is the distribution of people across different age groups."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0213","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 305 thousand people immigrate and 90 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["305 thousand","395 thousand","90 thousand","215 thousand"],"answer":3,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 305 − 90 = 215 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0214","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to population age structure?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income.","Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential.","Economic indicators require interpretation alongside social conditions.","Ageing can raise demand for healthcare and pensions relative to some tax revenues."],"answer":3,"why":"Ageing can raise demand for healthcare and pensions relative to some tax revenues."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0215","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to adapt public services for an ageing population. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Older people may receive more suitable healthcare.","Government benefit expenditure may increase.","Some employers may reduce hours or hiring.","Healthcare projects need recurrent spending."],"answer":0,"why":"Older people may receive more suitable healthcare."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0216","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to adapt public services for an ageing population. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Families may maintain spending on essentials.","Pension and care expenditure may rise.","Lower-income families may afford more essential goods.","Pay may rise for some low-wage employees."],"answer":1,"why":"Pension and care expenditure may rise."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0217","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to adapt public services for an ageing population?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Poor governance may cause the revenues to be wasted.","Poor sanitation can undermine treatment benefits.","Environmental protection may raise short-run production costs.","Communities may use existing skills and experience of older residents."],"answer":3,"why":"Communities may use existing skills and experience of older residents."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0218","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to adapt public services for an ageing population less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher earnings could improve household nutrition and housing.","Changes over time can help monitor average material prosperity.","A smaller working-age share can constrain tax revenue.","Healthier workers may miss fewer working days."],"answer":2,"why":"A smaller working-age share can constrain tax revenue."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0219","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to adapt public services for an ageing population. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Extra resources may reach households with urgent needs.","More students may complete formal education.","The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price.","Older people may receive more suitable healthcare."],"answer":3,"why":"Older people may receive more suitable healthcare."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0220","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country has a growing share of people aged over 65. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Relative deprivation can restrict participation in ordinary social and economic activities.","Ageing can raise demand for healthcare and pensions relative to some tax revenues.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty.","Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity."],"answer":1,"why":"Ageing can raise demand for healthcare and pensions relative to some tax revenues."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0221","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $18 billion and a population of 2 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$18","$4,500","$9,000","$18,000"],"answer":2,"why":"GDP per head = $18 billion / 2 million people = $9,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0222","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Productivity differences","International comparison limits","Optimum population","Healthcare differences"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates optimum population: An optimum population is a population size consistent with the highest potential output or income per person under given conditions."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0223","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of optimum population?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure.","Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations.","A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure.","One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates."],"answer":2,"why":"A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure. This illustrates an optimum population is a population size consistent with the highest potential output or income per person under given conditions."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0224","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to optimum population?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher money incomes can coexist with reduced well-being.","Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head.","A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime."],"answer":2,"why":"Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0225","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to plan population and infrastructure together. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Education benefits appear after a long delay.","Teacher shortages may reduce quality.","Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand.","Data quality may differ between countries."],"answer":2,"why":"Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0226","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to plan population and infrastructure together. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More students may complete formal education.","Forecasts about future population can be wrong.","Illness may be treated earlier and at lower cost.","Work absences caused by illness may fall."],"answer":1,"why":"Forecasts about future population can be wrong."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0227","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to plan population and infrastructure together?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Benefits depend on the quality of public services.","Domestic firms may struggle against established foreign competitors.","Automation may displace some existing jobs.","A suitable workforce can help resources be used productively."],"answer":3,"why":"A suitable workforce can help resources be used productively."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0228","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to plan population and infrastructure together less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Infrastructure expansion has a high upfront cost.","Communities could avoid some hidden costs of growth.","Future workforce productivity may improve.","Children may be better able to attend school."],"answer":0,"why":"Infrastructure expansion has a high upfront cost."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0229","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to plan population and infrastructure together. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","Transport and electricity networks may improve.","Work absences caused by illness may fall.","Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand."],"answer":3,"why":"Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0230","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 25 births and 5 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["20","5","30","25"],"answer":0,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 25 − 5 = 20 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0231","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes productivity differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Differences in output per worker help explain why countries have different income levels.","Real GDP per head is inflation-adjusted total domestic output divided by population.","Unequal access to education can cause persistent gaps in workforce skills between countries.","Unequal healthcare access can contribute to differences in development and productivity."],"answer":0,"why":"Productivity differences: Differences in output per worker help explain why countries have different income levels."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0232","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Country C's factories use modern machines while Country D's rely on outdated equipment. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Immigration","Population growth differences","Productivity differences","Birth rate"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates productivity differences: Differences in output per worker help explain why countries have different income levels."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0233","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of productivity differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Country C's factories use modern machines while Country D's rely on outdated equipment.","Country B reports life expectancy, years of schooling and national income per head.","A household can buy basic food but cannot afford services considered normal in its community.","A finance ministry uses income-tax receipts to support low-income families."],"answer":0,"why":"Country C's factories use modern machines while Country D's rely on outdated equipment. This illustrates differences in output per worker help explain why countries have different income levels."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0234","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to productivity differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents.","Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head.","More productive inputs can support higher wages and output per head.","A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal."],"answer":2,"why":"More productive inputs can support higher wages and output per head."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0235","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in productivity-enhancing machinery in lower-income regions. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Government benefit expenditure may increase.","A national average conceals regional inequalities.","Teacher shortages may reduce quality.","Output per worker may increase."],"answer":3,"why":"Output per worker may increase."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0236","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in productivity-enhancing machinery in lower-income regions. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Preventable deaths may decline.","Investment needs finance and technical skills.","Older people may receive more suitable healthcare.","Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand."],"answer":1,"why":"Investment needs finance and technical skills."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0237","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to invest in productivity-enhancing machinery in lower-income regions?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Firms may become more competitive abroad.","It excludes much unpaid work and environmental damage.","Some workers may still prefer opportunities abroad.","A smaller working-age share can constrain tax revenue."],"answer":0,"why":"Firms may become more competitive abroad."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0238","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 335 thousand people immigrate and 99 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["99 thousand","434 thousand","236 thousand","335 thousand"],"answer":2,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 335 − 99 = 236 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0239","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to invest in productivity-enhancing machinery in lower-income regions. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Analysts can compare average economic output across populations.","Output per worker may increase.","More learners may acquire employable skills.","Preventable deaths may decline."],"answer":1,"why":"Output per worker may increase."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0240","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Country C's factories use modern machines while Country D's rely on outdated equipment. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods.","A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime.","More productive inputs can support higher wages and output per head.","Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head."],"answer":2,"why":"More productive inputs can support higher wages and output per head."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0241","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes saving and investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Poor health can lower earning ability and impose medical expenses on families.","Saving provides funds that may finance investment in productive capital.","Years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development.","The quantity and quality of natural resources can influence productive capacity and development."],"answer":1,"why":"Saving and investment: Saving provides funds that may finance investment in productive capital."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0242","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Saving and investment","Real GDP per head","Education differences","Non-material living standards"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates saving and investment: Saving provides funds that may finance investment in productive capital."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0243","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of saving and investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure.","Young engineers move overseas after receiving attractive job offers.","A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure.","A country records 20 births per 1,000 inhabitants in a year."],"answer":0,"why":"A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure. This illustrates saving provides funds that may finance investment in productive capital."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0244","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to saving and investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Changes in mortality affect total population and can reflect health conditions or age structure.","Economic indicators require interpretation alongside social conditions.","Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential.","Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity."],"answer":3,"why":"Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0245","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage accessible safe saving and productive investment. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More funds may become available for business finance.","Measuring non-material benefits is difficult.","Housing and services may face extra demand.","The figure hides inequalities in income distribution."],"answer":0,"why":"More funds may become available for business finance."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0246","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $25 billion and a population of 5 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$25,000","$5,000","$25","$1,000"],"answer":1,"why":"GDP per head = $25 billion / 5 million people = $5,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0247","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to encourage accessible safe saving and productive investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Benefits may be limited by poor sanitation outside healthcare.","A growing child population also raises schooling costs.","Capital formation may raise future output.","Recognition of qualifications may remain difficult."],"answer":2,"why":"Capital formation may raise future output."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0248","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to encourage accessible safe saving and productive investment less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Communities may use existing skills and experience of older residents.","Weak financial institutions may misallocate investment funds.","Social and workplace participation may improve.","Tax revenues may increase as migrants work."],"answer":1,"why":"Weak financial institutions may misallocate investment funds."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0249","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to encourage accessible safe saving and productive investment. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Nutrition and health may improve.","More learners may acquire employable skills.","More households may participate in education and community life.","More funds may become available for business finance."],"answer":3,"why":"More funds may become available for business finance."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0250","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head.","A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal.","Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity."],"answer":3,"why":"Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0251","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes education differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Differences in output per worker help explain why countries have different income levels.","Unequal access to education can cause persistent gaps in workforce skills between countries.","Unequal healthcare access can contribute to differences in development and productivity.","Rapid population growth can affect income per head and demands on public services."],"answer":1,"why":"Education differences: Unequal access to education can cause persistent gaps in workforce skills between countries."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0252","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Education differences","Absolute poverty","Education indicator","Birth rate"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates education differences: Unequal access to education can cause persistent gaps in workforce skills between countries."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0253","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of education differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates.","Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations.","Residents earn more after a factory opens but face noisier streets and worse air quality.","A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold."],"answer":0,"why":"One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates. This illustrates unequal access to education can cause persistent gaps in workforce skills between countries."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0254","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to education differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents.","Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential.","Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty."],"answer":2,"why":"Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0255","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 18 births and 8 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["10","18","26","8"],"answer":0,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 18 − 8 = 10 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0256","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand quality education in lower-income communities. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Workers may use their skills more effectively.","More households may participate in education and community life.","More learners may acquire employable skills.","Teacher recruitment and school buildings are costly."],"answer":3,"why":"Teacher recruitment and school buildings are costly."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0257","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand quality education in lower-income communities?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Some workers may still prefer opportunities abroad.","Avoidance and administration costs can reduce effectiveness.","Young people may gain better-paid employment opportunities.","Some families may still require children to work."],"answer":2,"why":"Young people may gain better-paid employment opportunities."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0258","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand quality education in lower-income communities less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A suitable workforce can help resources be used productively.","The tax base may be more stable.","Future workers may become more productive.","Benefits depend on graduates finding appropriate jobs."],"answer":3,"why":"Benefits depend on graduates finding appropriate jobs."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0259","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand quality education in lower-income communities. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Workers may use their skills more effectively.","Nutrition and health may improve.","Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","A larger skilled workforce may raise output."],"answer":3,"why":"A larger skilled workforce may raise output."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0260","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential.","Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential.","Changes in mortality affect total population and can reflect health conditions or age structure.","Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families."],"answer":0,"why":"Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0261","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes healthcare differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work.","Relative poverty refers to having much lower resources than the typical standard in a society.","State benefits transfer resources to eligible people, often to support those on low incomes.","Unequal healthcare access can contribute to differences in development and productivity."],"answer":3,"why":"Healthcare differences: Unequal healthcare access can contribute to differences in development and productivity."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0262","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Workers in one country miss many days because preventable diseases are common. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Healthcare differences","Healthcare and poverty","Death rate","Immigration"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates healthcare differences: Unequal healthcare access can contribute to differences in development and productivity."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0263","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 365 thousand people immigrate and 108 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["365 thousand","473 thousand","108 thousand","257 thousand"],"answer":3,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 365 − 108 = 257 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0264","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to healthcare differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents.","Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income.","Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential.","Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity."],"answer":3,"why":"Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0265","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Providing services needs sustained funding.","A national average conceals regional inequalities.","Work absences caused by illness may fall.","Healthcare programmes need investment."],"answer":2,"why":"Work absences caused by illness may fall."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0266","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Workers may use their skills more effectively.","Older people may receive more suitable healthcare.","Healthcare projects need recurrent spending.","Analysts can compare average economic output across populations."],"answer":2,"why":"Healthcare projects need recurrent spending."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0267","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Life expectancy and well-being may increase.","Workers outside formal employment may not benefit.","Some families may still require children to work.","Environmental protection may raise short-run production costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Life expectancy and well-being may increase."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0268","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Poor sanitation can undermine treatment benefits.","Tax revenues may increase as migrants work.","Transfers can reduce hardship and support participation in education.","Policy may target the most serious social gaps."],"answer":0,"why":"Poor sanitation can undermine treatment benefits."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0269","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Pay may rise for some low-wage employees.","Work absences caused by illness may fall.","More households may participate in education and community life.","Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies."],"answer":1,"why":"Work absences caused by illness may fall."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0270","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Workers in one country miss many days because preventable diseases are common. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods.","Lost income and treatment costs can deepen poverty.","Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity."],"answer":3,"why":"Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0271","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $64 billion and a population of 8 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$64,000","$64","$8,000","$1,000"],"answer":2,"why":"GDP per head = $64 billion / 8 million people = $8,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0272","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: One economy relies heavily on commodity farming while another has a diverse services sector. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Saving and investment","Sector composition","Emigration","Productivity differences"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates sector composition: The balance between primary, secondary and tertiary production varies with development."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0273","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of sector composition?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations.","Country B reports life expectancy, years of schooling and national income per head.","A finance ministry uses income-tax receipts to support low-income families.","One economy relies heavily on commodity farming while another has a diverse services sector."],"answer":3,"why":"One economy relies heavily on commodity farming while another has a diverse services sector. This illustrates the balance between primary, secondary and tertiary production varies with development."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0274","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to sector composition?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs.","Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income.","New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods.","The HDI gives a broader view of development than output alone."],"answer":0,"why":"Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0275","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Healthcare programmes need investment.","The figure hides inequalities in income distribution.","The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price.","Hospitals and programmes require reliable funding."],"answer":2,"why":"The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0276","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["New industries may need expensive infrastructure.","Workers may use their skills more effectively.","Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand.","Firms may keep experienced employees."],"answer":0,"why":"New industries may need expensive infrastructure."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0277","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["It excludes much unpaid work and environmental damage.","Infrastructure expansion has a high upfront cost.","Overseas recruitment may harm source-country labour supply.","New sectors may create skilled employment."],"answer":3,"why":"New sectors may create skilled employment."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0278","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Reduced financial exclusion can support opportunity.","Social and workplace participation may improve.","Domestic firms may struggle against established foreign competitors.","Governments may identify non-income development gaps."],"answer":2,"why":"Domestic firms may struggle against established foreign competitors."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0279","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Illness may be treated earlier and at lower cost.","Families may maintain essential spending while searching.","Workers may use their skills more effectively.","The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price."],"answer":3,"why":"The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0280","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 21 births and 11 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["11","21","10","32"],"answer":2,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 21 − 11 = 10 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0281","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes natural resource endowments?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The quantity and quality of natural resources can influence productive capacity and development.","Unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work.","Unequal access to education can cause persistent gaps in workforce skills between countries.","Population age structure is the distribution of people across different age groups."],"answer":0,"why":"Natural resource endowments: The quantity and quality of natural resources can influence productive capacity and development."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0282","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Natural resource endowments","Healthcare differences","Unemployment and poverty","Population growth differences"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates natural resource endowments: The quantity and quality of natural resources can influence productive capacity and development."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0283","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of natural resource endowments?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A country has a growing share of people aged over 65.","A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff.","A worker's wage rises by 2% while consumer prices rise by 5%.","A country records eight deaths per 1,000 inhabitants in a year."],"answer":1,"why":"A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff. This illustrates the quantity and quality of natural resources can influence productive capacity and development."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0284","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to natural resource endowments?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods.","Resources may support export revenue if infrastructure and institutions enable efficient use.","A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime.","Economic indicators require interpretation alongside social conditions."],"answer":1,"why":"Resources may support export revenue if infrastructure and institutions enable efficient use."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0285","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest resource-export earnings in public infrastructure. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Transfer payments may require higher taxation.","Education benefits appear after a long delay.","Transport and electricity networks may improve.","Public healthcare requires doctors and funding."],"answer":2,"why":"Transport and electricity networks may improve."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0286","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest resource-export earnings in public infrastructure. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Nutrition and health may improve.","World commodity prices are volatile.","Pay may rise for some low-wage employees.","Preventable deaths may decline."],"answer":1,"why":"World commodity prices are volatile."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0287","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to invest resource-export earnings in public infrastructure?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Overseas recruitment may harm source-country labour supply.","Weak distribution systems may prevent aid reaching people.","New jobs may be scarce in a local downturn.","New industries may become possible outside mining."],"answer":3,"why":"New industries may become possible outside mining."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0288","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 395 thousand people immigrate and 117 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["512 thousand","278 thousand","395 thousand","117 thousand"],"answer":1,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 395 − 117 = 278 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0289","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to invest resource-export earnings in public infrastructure. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Transport and electricity networks may improve.","Infant and maternal health may improve.","Extra resources may reach households with urgent needs.","Fewer preventable deaths may improve welfare."],"answer":0,"why":"Transport and electricity networks may improve."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0290","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty.","Lost income and treatment costs can deepen poverty.","Resources may support export revenue if infrastructure and institutions enable efficient use."],"answer":3,"why":"Resources may support export revenue if infrastructure and institutions enable efficient use."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0291","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes population growth differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Differences in output per worker help explain why countries have different income levels.","Life expectancy indicates the average years a newborn would live if current mortality patterns continued.","Rapid population growth can affect income per head and demands on public services.","The balance between primary, secondary and tertiary production varies with development."],"answer":2,"why":"Population growth differences: Rapid population growth can affect income per head and demands on public services."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0292","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country's output grows 3% while population grows 4% in the same year. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Relative poverty","Population growth differences","Purchasing power","Life expectancy"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates population growth differences: Rapid population growth can affect income per head and demands on public services."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0293","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of population growth differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A country's output grows 3% while population grows 4% in the same year.","A country's life expectancy rises after drinking-water systems and clinics improve.","Some families cannot afford enough nutritious meals or safe drinking water.","A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers."],"answer":0,"why":"A country's output grows 3% while population grows 4% in the same year. This illustrates rapid population growth can affect income per head and demands on public services."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0294","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to population growth differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime.","Better health conditions can improve longevity and support quality of life.","Transfers can immediately increase disposable income and reduce hardship.","Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output."],"answer":3,"why":"Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0295","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand schools and infrastructure as population grows. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More children may access education.","Teacher shortages may reduce quality.","Some employers may reduce hours or hiring.","Government benefit expenditure may increase."],"answer":0,"why":"More children may access education."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0296","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $8 billion and a population of 2 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$8,000","$4,000","$2,000","$8"],"answer":1,"why":"GDP per head = $8 billion / 2 million people = $4,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0297","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand schools and infrastructure as population grows?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Overseas recruitment may harm source-country labour supply.","Future workforce productivity may improve.","Payments may not keep pace with very rapid inflation.","Remote households may still face travel barriers."],"answer":1,"why":"Future workforce productivity may improve."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0298","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand schools and infrastructure as population grows less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Communities could avoid some hidden costs of growth.","Benefits depend on the quality of public services.","The tax base may be more stable.","Changes over time can help monitor average material prosperity."],"answer":1,"why":"Benefits depend on the quality of public services."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0299","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand schools and infrastructure as population grows. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","Older people may receive more suitable healthcare.","More children may access education.","Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled."],"answer":2,"why":"More children may access education."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0300","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country's output grows 3% while population grows 4% in the same year. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal.","Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output.","New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods."],"answer":2,"why":"Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0001","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Country A produces $60 billion of real output for 6 million residents. Decision-makers are considering whether to use real GDP per head alongside other living-standard indicators. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by real gdp per head. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Real GDP per head is inflation-adjusted total domestic output divided by population.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Country A produces $60 billion of real output for 6 million residents.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0002","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Country B reports life expectancy, years of schooling and national income per head. Decision-makers are considering whether to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by human development index. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — The Human Development Index combines indicators of health, education and income into a composite measure.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Country B reports life expectancy, years of schooling and national income per head.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0003","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A country's life expectancy rises after drinking-water systems and clinics improve. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by life expectancy. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Life expectancy indicates the average years a newborn would live if current mortality patterns continued.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A country's life expectancy rises after drinking-water systems and clinics improve.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0004","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide more affordable secondary school places. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by education indicator. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0005","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Average incomes rise in a city, but most extra income goes to its highest earners. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce targeted transfers to reduce severe income inequality. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by income inequality. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Income inequality describes how unevenly income is distributed between people or households.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Average incomes rise in a city, but most extra income goes to its highest earners.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0006","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Residents earn more after a factory opens but face noisier streets and worse air quality. Decision-makers are considering whether to include environmental quality when assessing development. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by non-material living standards. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Living standards also involve health, safety, leisure and environmental quality, not just output.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Residents earn more after a factory opens but face noisier streets and worse air quality.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0007","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Two countries have similar GDP per head but different school attendance and healthcare access. Decision-makers are considering whether to compare several living-standard indicators across countries. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by international comparison limits. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Living-standard comparisons can be misleading because national averages and measurement methods differ.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Two countries have similar GDP per head but different school attendance and healthcare access.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0008","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Some families cannot afford enough nutritious meals or safe drinking water. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand access to essential food and water for very poor households. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by absolute poverty. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Absolute poverty means lacking the resources to meet basic needs such as food, shelter and clean water.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Some families cannot afford enough nutritious meals or safe drinking water.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0009","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A household can buy basic food but cannot afford services considered normal in its community. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide targeted assistance to low-income households. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by relative poverty. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Relative poverty refers to having much lower resources than the typical standard in a society.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A household can buy basic food but cannot afford services considered normal in its community.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0010","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide job-search support and temporary income assistance. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by unemployment and poverty. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0011","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"An illness stops a self-employed driver working for three months. Decision-makers are considering whether to make essential primary healthcare more accessible. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by healthcare and poverty. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Poor health can lower earning ability and impose medical expenses on families.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — An illness stops a self-employed driver working for three months.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0012","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations. Decision-makers are considering whether to subsidise schooling and vocational courses in low-income areas. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by education and poverty. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Limited education can reduce access to well-paid employment and perpetuate poverty.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0013","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand well-targeted child-support benefits. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by state benefits. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — State benefits transfer resources to eligible people, often to support those on low incomes.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0014","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A finance ministry uses income-tax receipts to support low-income families. Decision-makers are considering whether to use progressive taxes to fund anti-poverty programmes. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by progressive tax and poverty. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Progressive taxation can finance redistribution by taking a larger income share from higher earners.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A finance ministry uses income-tax receipts to support low-income families.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0015","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A country records 20 births per 1,000 inhabitants in a year. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by birth rate. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — The birth rate measures live births per thousand people in the population in a year.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A country records 20 births per 1,000 inhabitants in a year.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0016","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A country receives 100,000 arrivals while 70,000 residents leave. Decision-makers are considering whether to make it easier for needed skilled workers to migrate legally. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by net migration. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Net migration is immigration minus emigration over a period.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A country receives 100,000 arrivals while 70,000 residents leave.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0017","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A city attracts healthcare workers from abroad. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide integration and language support for new migrants. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by immigration. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Immigration occurs when people enter a country to live there.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A city attracts healthcare workers from abroad.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0018","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Young engineers move overseas after receiving attractive job offers. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve domestic career opportunities to retain skilled workers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by emigration. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Emigration occurs when residents leave their country to live abroad.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Young engineers move overseas after receiving attractive job offers.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0019","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A country has a growing share of people aged over 65. Decision-makers are considering whether to adapt public services for an ageing population. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by population age structure. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Population age structure is the distribution of people across different age groups.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A country has a growing share of people aged over 65.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0020","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure. Decision-makers are considering whether to plan population and infrastructure together. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by optimum population. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — An optimum population is a population size consistent with the highest potential output or income per person under given conditions.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0021","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage accessible safe saving and productive investment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by saving and investment. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Saving provides funds that may finance investment in productive capital.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0022","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand quality education in lower-income communities. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by education differences. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Unequal access to education can cause persistent gaps in workforce skills between countries.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0023","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Workers in one country miss many days because preventable diseases are common. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by healthcare differences. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Unequal healthcare access can contribute to differences in development and productivity.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Workers in one country miss many days because preventable diseases are common.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0024","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"One economy relies heavily on commodity farming while another has a diverse services sector. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by sector composition. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — The balance between primary, secondary and tertiary production varies with development.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — One economy relies heavily on commodity farming while another has a diverse services sector.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0025","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest resource-export earnings in public infrastructure. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by natural resource endowments. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — The quantity and quality of natural resources can influence productive capacity and development.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0001","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Country A produces $60 billion of real output for 6 million residents. Decision-makers are considering whether to use real GDP per head alongside other living-standard indicators. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to use real GDP per head alongside other living-standard indicators. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Analysts can compare average economic output across populations. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Changes over time can help monitor average material prosperity. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0002","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Country B reports life expectancy, years of schooling and national income per head. Decision-makers are considering whether to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Health and education outcomes receive greater attention. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Governments may identify non-income development gaps. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0003","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country's life expectancy rises after drinking-water systems and clinics improve. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Fewer preventable deaths may improve welfare. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Healthier workers may miss fewer working days. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0004","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide more affordable secondary school places. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers provide more affordable secondary school places. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: More students may complete formal education. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Future workers may become more productive. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0005","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Average incomes rise in a city, but most extra income goes to its highest earners. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce targeted transfers to reduce severe income inequality. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to introduce targeted transfers to reduce severe income inequality could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Lower-income families may afford more essential goods. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Transfers can reduce hardship and support participation in education. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0006","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A worker's wage rises by 2% while consumer prices rise by 5%. Decision-makers are considering whether to protect low-income households during rapid inflation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to protect low-income households during rapid inflation. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Families may maintain spending on essentials. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Lower real-income losses may reduce poverty risks. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0007","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Residents earn more after a factory opens but face noisier streets and worse air quality. Decision-makers are considering whether to include environmental quality when assessing development. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to include environmental quality when assessing development might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Communities could avoid some hidden costs of growth. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0008","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Two countries have similar GDP per head but different school attendance and healthcare access. Decision-makers are considering whether to compare several living-standard indicators across countries. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to compare several living-standard indicators across countries. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Policy may target the most serious social gaps. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0009","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Some families cannot afford enough nutritious meals or safe drinking water. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand access to essential food and water for very poor households. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers expand access to essential food and water for very poor households. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Nutrition and health may improve. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Children may be better able to attend school. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0010","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A household can buy basic food but cannot afford services considered normal in its community. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide targeted assistance to low-income households. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to provide targeted assistance to low-income households could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: More households may participate in education and community life. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Reduced financial exclusion can support opportunity. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0011","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide job-search support and temporary income assistance. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to provide job-search support and temporary income assistance. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Families may maintain essential spending while searching. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Better job matching may speed the return to work. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0012","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Full-time farm workers struggle to pay for rent and childcare. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce a carefully set minimum wage. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to introduce a carefully set minimum wage might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Pay may rise for some low-wage employees. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Higher earnings could improve household nutrition and housing. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0013","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"An illness stops a self-employed driver working for three months. Decision-makers are considering whether to make essential primary healthcare more accessible. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to make essential primary healthcare more accessible. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Illness may be treated earlier and at lower cost. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Workers may retain more productive working time. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0014","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations. Decision-makers are considering whether to subsidise schooling and vocational courses in low-income areas. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers subsidise schooling and vocational courses in low-income areas. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: More learners may acquire employable skills. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Future wages and productivity may increase. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0015","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand well-targeted child-support benefits. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to expand well-targeted child-support benefits could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Child poverty may fall in the short term. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0016","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A finance ministry uses income-tax receipts to support low-income families. Decision-makers are considering whether to use progressive taxes to fund anti-poverty programmes. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to use progressive taxes to fund anti-poverty programmes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Extra resources may reach households with urgent needs. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Income inequality may decrease. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0017","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country records 20 births per 1,000 inhabitants in a year. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Infant and maternal health may improve. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Children may become healthier future workers. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0018","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country records eight deaths per 1,000 inhabitants in a year. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Preventable deaths may decline. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: More people may remain able to work. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0019","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country receives 100,000 arrivals while 70,000 residents leave. Decision-makers are considering whether to make it easier for needed skilled workers to migrate legally. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers make it easier for needed skilled workers to migrate legally. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Tax revenues may increase as migrants work. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0020","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A city attracts healthcare workers from abroad. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide integration and language support for new migrants. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to provide integration and language support for new migrants could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Workers may use their skills more effectively. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Social and workplace participation may improve. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0021","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Young engineers move overseas after receiving attractive job offers. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve domestic career opportunities to retain skilled workers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to improve domestic career opportunities to retain skilled workers. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Firms may keep experienced employees. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: The tax base may be more stable. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0022","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country has a growing share of people aged over 65. Decision-makers are considering whether to adapt public services for an ageing population. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to adapt public services for an ageing population might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Older people may receive more suitable healthcare. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Communities may use existing skills and experience of older residents. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0023","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure. Decision-makers are considering whether to plan population and infrastructure together. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to plan population and infrastructure together. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: A suitable workforce can help resources be used productively. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0024","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Country C's factories use modern machines while Country D's rely on outdated equipment. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in productivity-enhancing machinery in lower-income regions. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers invest in productivity-enhancing machinery in lower-income regions. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Output per worker may increase. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Firms may become more competitive abroad. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0025","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage accessible safe saving and productive investment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to encourage accessible safe saving and productive investment could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: More funds may become available for business finance. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Capital formation may raise future output. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0026","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand quality education in lower-income communities. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to expand quality education in lower-income communities. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: A larger skilled workforce may raise output. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Young people may gain better-paid employment opportunities. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0027","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Workers in one country miss many days because preventable diseases are common. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Work absences caused by illness may fall. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Life expectancy and well-being may increase. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0028","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"One economy relies heavily on commodity farming while another has a diverse services sector. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: New sectors may create skilled employment. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0029","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest resource-export earnings in public infrastructure. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers invest resource-export earnings in public infrastructure. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Transport and electricity networks may improve. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: New industries may become possible outside mining. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0030","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country's output grows 3% while population grows 4% in the same year. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand schools and infrastructure as population grows. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to expand schools and infrastructure as population grows could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: More children may access education. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Future workforce productivity may improve. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0001","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Country A produces $60 billion of real output for 6 million residents. Decision-makers are considering whether to use real GDP per head alongside other living-standard indicators. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to use real GDP per head alongside other living-standard indicators. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Analysts can compare average economic output across populations. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Changes over time can help monitor average material prosperity. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The $10,000 figure estimates average real output per resident, not each person's income. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0002","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Country B reports life expectancy, years of schooling and national income per head. Decision-makers are considering whether to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Health and education outcomes receive greater attention. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Governments may identify non-income development gaps. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The HDI gives a broader view of development than output alone. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0003","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country's life expectancy rises after drinking-water systems and clinics improve. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Fewer preventable deaths may improve welfare. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Healthier workers may miss fewer working days. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Better health conditions can improve longevity and support quality of life. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0004","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide more affordable secondary school places. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to provide more affordable secondary school places in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: More students may complete formal education. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Future workers may become more productive. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0005","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Average incomes rise in a city, but most extra income goes to its highest earners. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce targeted transfers to reduce severe income inequality. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to introduce targeted transfers to reduce severe income inequality could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Lower-income families may afford more essential goods. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Transfers can reduce hardship and support participation in education. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Growth in averages need not improve the living standard of every group. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0006","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A worker's wage rises by 2% while consumer prices rise by 5%. Decision-makers are considering whether to protect low-income households during rapid inflation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to protect low-income households during rapid inflation. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Families may maintain spending on essentials. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Lower real-income losses may reduce poverty risks. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0007","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Residents earn more after a factory opens but face noisier streets and worse air quality. Decision-makers are considering whether to include environmental quality when assessing development. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to include environmental quality when assessing development may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Communities could avoid some hidden costs of growth. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Higher money incomes can coexist with reduced well-being. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0008","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Two countries have similar GDP per head but different school attendance and healthcare access. Decision-makers are considering whether to compare several living-standard indicators across countries. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to compare several living-standard indicators across countries could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Policy may target the most serious social gaps. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Economic indicators require interpretation alongside social conditions. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0009","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Some families cannot afford enough nutritious meals or safe drinking water. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand access to essential food and water for very poor households. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to expand access to essential food and water for very poor households in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Nutrition and health may improve. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Children may be better able to attend school. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Insufficient basic consumption directly damages health and welfare. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0010","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A household can buy basic food but cannot afford services considered normal in its community. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide targeted assistance to low-income households. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to provide targeted assistance to low-income households could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: More households may participate in education and community life. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Reduced financial exclusion can support opportunity. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Relative deprivation can restrict participation in ordinary social and economic activities. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0011","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide job-search support and temporary income assistance. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to provide job-search support and temporary income assistance. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Families may maintain essential spending while searching. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Better job matching may speed the return to work. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0012","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Full-time farm workers struggle to pay for rent and childcare. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce a carefully set minimum wage. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to introduce a carefully set minimum wage may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Pay may rise for some low-wage employees. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Higher earnings could improve household nutrition and housing. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0013","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"An illness stops a self-employed driver working for three months. Decision-makers are considering whether to make essential primary healthcare more accessible. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to make essential primary healthcare more accessible could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Illness may be treated earlier and at lower cost. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Workers may retain more productive working time. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Lost income and treatment costs can deepen poverty. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0014","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations. Decision-makers are considering whether to subsidise schooling and vocational courses in low-income areas. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to subsidise schooling and vocational courses in low-income areas in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: More learners may acquire employable skills. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Future wages and productivity may increase. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0015","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand well-targeted child-support benefits. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to expand well-targeted child-support benefits could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Child poverty may fall in the short term. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Transfers can immediately increase disposable income and reduce hardship. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0016","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A finance ministry uses income-tax receipts to support low-income families. Decision-makers are considering whether to use progressive taxes to fund anti-poverty programmes. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to use progressive taxes to fund anti-poverty programmes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Extra resources may reach households with urgent needs. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Income inequality may decrease. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Tax-and-transfer arrangements can narrow differences in disposable income. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0017","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country records 20 births per 1,000 inhabitants in a year. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Infant and maternal health may improve. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Children may become healthier future workers. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0018","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country records eight deaths per 1,000 inhabitants in a year. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Preventable deaths may decline. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: More people may remain able to work. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Changes in mortality affect total population and can reflect health conditions or age structure. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0019","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country receives 100,000 arrivals while 70,000 residents leave. Decision-makers are considering whether to make it easier for needed skilled workers to migrate legally. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to make it easier for needed skilled workers to migrate legally in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Tax revenues may increase as migrants work. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0020","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A city attracts healthcare workers from abroad. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide integration and language support for new migrants. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to provide integration and language support for new migrants could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Workers may use their skills more effectively. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Social and workplace participation may improve. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0021","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Young engineers move overseas after receiving attractive job offers. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve domestic career opportunities to retain skilled workers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to improve domestic career opportunities to retain skilled workers. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Firms may keep experienced employees. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: The tax base may be more stable. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0022","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country has a growing share of people aged over 65. Decision-makers are considering whether to adapt public services for an ageing population. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to adapt public services for an ageing population may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Older people may receive more suitable healthcare. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Communities may use existing skills and experience of older residents. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Ageing can raise demand for healthcare and pensions relative to some tax revenues. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0023","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure. Decision-makers are considering whether to plan population and infrastructure together. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to plan population and infrastructure together could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: A suitable workforce can help resources be used productively. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0024","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Country C's factories use modern machines while Country D's rely on outdated equipment. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in productivity-enhancing machinery in lower-income regions. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to invest in productivity-enhancing machinery in lower-income regions in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Output per worker may increase. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Firms may become more competitive abroad. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: More productive inputs can support higher wages and output per head. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0025","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage accessible safe saving and productive investment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to encourage accessible safe saving and productive investment could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: More funds may become available for business finance. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Capital formation may raise future output. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0026","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand quality education in lower-income communities. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to expand quality education in lower-income communities. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: A larger skilled workforce may raise output. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Young people may gain better-paid employment opportunities. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0027","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Workers in one country miss many days because preventable diseases are common. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Work absences caused by illness may fall. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Life expectancy and well-being may increase. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0028","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"One economy relies heavily on commodity farming while another has a diverse services sector. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: New sectors may create skilled employment. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0029","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest resource-export earnings in public infrastructure. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to invest resource-export earnings in public infrastructure in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Transport and electricity networks may improve. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: New industries may become possible outside mining. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Resources may support export revenue if infrastructure and institutions enable efficient use. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0030","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country's output grows 3% while population grows 4% in the same year. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand schools and infrastructure as population grows. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to expand schools and infrastructure as population grows could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: More children may access education. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Future workforce productivity may improve. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0001","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Country A produces $60 billion of real output for 6 million residents. Decision-makers are considering whether to use real GDP per head alongside other living-standard indicators. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to use real GDP per head alongside other living-standard indicators would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Analysts can compare average economic output across populations.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Changes over time can help monitor average material prosperity.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): The figure hides inequalities in income distribution.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): It excludes much unpaid work and environmental damage.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0002","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Country B reports life expectancy, years of schooling and national income per head. Decision-makers are considering whether to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Governments may identify non-income development gaps.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): A national average conceals regional inequalities.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): The index omits some environmental and freedom-related outcomes.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0003","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country's life expectancy rises after drinking-water systems and clinics improve. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Fewer preventable deaths may improve welfare.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Healthier workers may miss fewer working days.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Hospitals and programmes require reliable funding.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Benefits may be limited by poor sanitation outside healthcare.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0004","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide more affordable secondary school places. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to provide more affordable secondary school places is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): More students may complete formal education.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Future workers may become more productive.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Teacher shortages may reduce quality.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Some families may still require children to work.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0005","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Average incomes rise in a city, but most extra income goes to its highest earners. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce targeted transfers to reduce severe income inequality. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to introduce targeted transfers to reduce severe income inequality would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Lower-income families may afford more essential goods.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Transfers can reduce hardship and support participation in education.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Redistribution needs tax revenue.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Weak targeting may fail to reach the poorest households.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0006","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A worker's wage rises by 2% while consumer prices rise by 5%. Decision-makers are considering whether to protect low-income households during rapid inflation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to protect low-income households during rapid inflation would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Families may maintain spending on essentials.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Lower real-income losses may reduce poverty risks.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Support payments increase fiscal costs.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Payments may not keep pace with very rapid inflation.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0007","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Residents earn more after a factory opens but face noisier streets and worse air quality. Decision-makers are considering whether to include environmental quality when assessing development. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to include environmental quality when assessing development would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Communities could avoid some hidden costs of growth.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Measuring non-material benefits is difficult.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Environmental protection may raise short-run production costs.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0008","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Two countries have similar GDP per head but different school attendance and healthcare access. Decision-makers are considering whether to compare several living-standard indicators across countries. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to compare several living-standard indicators across countries in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Policy may target the most serious social gaps.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Data quality may differ between countries.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): A large collection of indicators can be difficult to summarise.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0009","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Some families cannot afford enough nutritious meals or safe drinking water. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand access to essential food and water for very poor households. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to expand access to essential food and water for very poor households. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Nutrition and health may improve.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Children may be better able to attend school.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Providing services needs sustained funding.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Weak distribution systems may prevent aid reaching people.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0010","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A household can buy basic food but cannot afford services considered normal in its community. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide targeted assistance to low-income households. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to provide targeted assistance to low-income households is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): More households may participate in education and community life.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Reduced financial exclusion can support opportunity.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Transfer payments may require higher taxation.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): The relative poverty threshold can rise as median incomes rise.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0011","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide job-search support and temporary income assistance. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to provide job-search support and temporary income assistance would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Families may maintain essential spending while searching.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Better job matching may speed the return to work.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Government benefit expenditure may increase.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): New jobs may be scarce in a local downturn.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0012","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Full-time farm workers struggle to pay for rent and childcare. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce a carefully set minimum wage. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to introduce a carefully set minimum wage would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Pay may rise for some low-wage employees.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Higher earnings could improve household nutrition and housing.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Some employers may reduce hours or hiring.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Workers outside formal employment may not benefit.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0013","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"An illness stops a self-employed driver working for three months. Decision-makers are considering whether to make essential primary healthcare more accessible. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to make essential primary healthcare more accessible would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Illness may be treated earlier and at lower cost.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Workers may retain more productive working time.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Public healthcare requires doctors and funding.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Remote households may still face travel barriers.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0014","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations. Decision-makers are considering whether to subsidise schooling and vocational courses in low-income areas. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to subsidise schooling and vocational courses in low-income areas in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): More learners may acquire employable skills.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Future wages and productivity may increase.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Education benefits appear after a long delay.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Courses may not match labour-market needs.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0015","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand well-targeted child-support benefits. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to expand well-targeted child-support benefits. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Child poverty may fall in the short term.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Public spending commitments may rise.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Incorrect eligibility decisions may exclude needy households.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0016","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A finance ministry uses income-tax receipts to support low-income families. Decision-makers are considering whether to use progressive taxes to fund anti-poverty programmes. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to use progressive taxes to fund anti-poverty programmes is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Extra resources may reach households with urgent needs.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Income inequality may decrease.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): High tax rates may reduce incentives for some earners.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Avoidance and administration costs can reduce effectiveness.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0017","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country records 20 births per 1,000 inhabitants in a year. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Infant and maternal health may improve.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Children may become healthier future workers.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Healthcare programmes need investment.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): A growing child population also raises schooling costs.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0018","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country records eight deaths per 1,000 inhabitants in a year. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Preventable deaths may decline.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): More people may remain able to work.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Programmes require funding and trained staff.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): An ageing population can keep the crude death rate high.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0019","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country receives 100,000 arrivals while 70,000 residents leave. Decision-makers are considering whether to make it easier for needed skilled workers to migrate legally. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to make it easier for needed skilled workers to migrate legally would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Tax revenues may increase as migrants work.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Housing and services may face extra demand.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Overseas recruitment may harm source-country labour supply.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0020","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A city attracts healthcare workers from abroad. Decision-makers are considering whether to provide integration and language support for new migrants. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to provide integration and language support for new migrants in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Workers may use their skills more effectively.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Social and workplace participation may improve.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Public services may face short-run pressure.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Recognition of qualifications may remain difficult.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0021","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Young engineers move overseas after receiving attractive job offers. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve domestic career opportunities to retain skilled workers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to improve domestic career opportunities to retain skilled workers. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Firms may keep experienced employees.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): The tax base may be more stable.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Higher public and private wage costs may follow.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Some workers may still prefer opportunities abroad.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0022","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country has a growing share of people aged over 65. Decision-makers are considering whether to adapt public services for an ageing population. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to adapt public services for an ageing population is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Older people may receive more suitable healthcare.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Communities may use existing skills and experience of older residents.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Pension and care expenditure may rise.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): A smaller working-age share can constrain tax revenue.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0023","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure. Decision-makers are considering whether to plan population and infrastructure together. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to plan population and infrastructure together would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): A suitable workforce can help resources be used productively.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Forecasts about future population can be wrong.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Infrastructure expansion has a high upfront cost.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0024","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Country C's factories use modern machines while Country D's rely on outdated equipment. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest in productivity-enhancing machinery in lower-income regions. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to invest in productivity-enhancing machinery in lower-income regions would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Output per worker may increase.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Firms may become more competitive abroad.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Investment needs finance and technical skills.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Automation may displace some existing jobs.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0025","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage accessible safe saving and productive investment. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to encourage accessible safe saving and productive investment would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): More funds may become available for business finance.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Capital formation may raise future output.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Higher saving can reduce current consumption.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Weak financial institutions may misallocate investment funds.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0026","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand quality education in lower-income communities. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to expand quality education in lower-income communities in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): A larger skilled workforce may raise output.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Young people may gain better-paid employment opportunities.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Teacher recruitment and school buildings are costly.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Benefits depend on graduates finding appropriate jobs.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0027","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Workers in one country miss many days because preventable diseases are common. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Work absences caused by illness may fall.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Life expectancy and well-being may increase.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Healthcare projects need recurrent spending.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Poor sanitation can undermine treatment benefits.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0028","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"One economy relies heavily on commodity farming while another has a diverse services sector. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): New sectors may create skilled employment.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): New industries may need expensive infrastructure.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Domestic firms may struggle against established foreign competitors.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0029","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest resource-export earnings in public infrastructure. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to invest resource-export earnings in public infrastructure would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Transport and electricity networks may improve.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): New industries may become possible outside mining.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): World commodity prices are volatile.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Poor governance may cause the revenues to be wasted.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0030","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country's output grows 3% while population grows 4% in the same year. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand schools and infrastructure as population grows. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to expand schools and infrastructure as population grows would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): More children may access education.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Future workforce productivity may improve.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Spending requirements can exceed fiscal capacity.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Benefits depend on the quality of public services.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0001","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes international specialisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["An import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported.","Open international markets can increase competitive pressure on domestic firms.","International specialisation occurs when a country concentrates resources on goods it can produce relatively efficiently.","Appreciation is a rise in the value of a currency under a floating exchange-rate system."],"answer":2,"why":"International specialisation: International specialisation occurs when a country concentrates resources on goods it can produce relatively efficiently."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0002","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A coastal country exports fish products while importing machines it cannot make cheaply. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["International specialisation","Communication technology","Tariff","Consumer gains from trade"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates international specialisation: International specialisation occurs when a country concentrates resources on goods it can produce relatively efficiently."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0003","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of international specialisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A coastal country exports fish products while importing machines it cannot make cheaply.","Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts.","A weaker currency raises local-currency costs of imported wheat and diesel.","A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services."],"answer":0,"why":"A coastal country exports fish products while importing machines it cannot make cheaply. This illustrates international specialisation occurs when a country concentrates resources on goods it can produce relatively efficiently."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0004","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to international specialisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Combining these flows produces the current-account balance.","Banning trade can prevent specified imports but may disrupt supply.","Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade.","Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates."],"answer":2,"why":"Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0005","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country exports $55 billion of goods and imports $68 billion. What is its trade-in-goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$13 billion surplus","$68 billion deficit","$123 billion surplus","$13 billion deficit"],"answer":3,"why":"Goods balance = exports − imports = 55 − 68 = -13 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0006","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to specialise in sustainable seafood processing for export. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The country may become dependent on global seafood demand.","Exports may become more price competitive abroad.","Future import-cost shocks may be smaller.","Export earnings may rise over time."],"answer":0,"why":"The country may become dependent on global seafood demand."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0007","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to specialise in sustainable seafood processing for export?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Some energy resources cannot be produced domestically.","Export demand may respond slowly if contracts are fixed.","Export earnings could finance needed imports.","Poorly selected projects can waste the earnings."],"answer":2,"why":"Export earnings could finance needed imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0008","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to specialise in sustainable seafood processing for export less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Government may collect customs revenue.","Imported inputs may become more affordable.","Firms may understand export and import trends.","Overfishing could damage future productive resources."],"answer":3,"why":"Overfishing could damage future productive resources."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0009","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to specialise in sustainable seafood processing for export. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Export demand may increase.","Local workers may gain employment and training.","Firms may gain experience and lower average costs.","Exports may become more price competitive abroad."],"answer":2,"why":"Firms may gain experience and lower average costs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0010","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A coastal country exports fish products while importing machines it cannot make cheaply. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions.","Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts.","Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade.","Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions."],"answer":2,"why":"Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0011","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes benefits of specialisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Secondary income records one-way current transfers, such as remittances and some international aid.","Foreign demand for a country's currency can arise from its exports and investment opportunities.","Specialisation can enable economies of scale and higher labour productivity.","Depreciation can raise domestic prices when imported inputs or consumer goods cost more."],"answer":2,"why":"Benefits of specialisation: Specialisation can enable economies of scale and higher labour productivity."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0012","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country develops clusters of skilled bicycle-component manufacturers. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Import quota","Exchange rate","Demand for currency","Benefits of specialisation"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates benefits of specialisation: Specialisation can enable economies of scale and higher labour productivity."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0013","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country's balances are: goods +$14bn, services +$5bn, primary income −$4bn and secondary income +$3bn. Calculate the current-account balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$18 billion surplus","$26 billion surplus","$18 billion deficit","$19 billion surplus"],"answer":0,"why":"Current account = 14 + 5 − 4 + 3 = +$18 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0014","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to benefits of specialisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts.","Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions.","Repeated production can improve skills and reduce unit costs.","The services balance is an $8 billion surplus."],"answer":2,"why":"Repeated production can improve skills and reduce unit costs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0015","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Imported fuel and machinery may become more expensive.","Large outflows could weaken the domestic currency.","Specialist workers may become more productive.","Training costs money and takes time."],"answer":2,"why":"Specialist workers may become more productive."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0016","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["New firms may develop production experience.","Firms may sell services into new markets.","More investment income may remain with local residents.","Demand for bicycle parts could decline."],"answer":3,"why":"Demand for bicycle parts could decline."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0017","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Trading partners may retaliate with tariffs of their own.","Suppliers may benefit from shared infrastructure.","Long-distance transport may increase emissions.","An overseas slowdown may still reduce exports."],"answer":1,"why":"Suppliers may benefit from shared infrastructure."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0018","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Regions may become vulnerable to one industry's downturn.","Employment in protected firms may be preserved.","Imported inputs may become more affordable.","Greater capital mobility may increase diversification opportunities."],"answer":0,"why":"Regions may become vulnerable to one industry's downturn."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0019","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Future output and living standards may increase.","Investment equipment may cost less.","Specialist workers may become more productive.","Export demand may increase."],"answer":2,"why":"Specialist workers may become more productive."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0020","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country develops clusters of skilled bicycle-component manufacturers. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions.","Repeated production can improve skills and reduce unit costs.","Combining these flows produces the current-account balance.","More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate."],"answer":1,"why":"Repeated production can improve skills and reduce unit costs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0021","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"One home dollar previously bought 83 foreign cents and now buys 108 foreign cents. What has happened to the home currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Its value has remained unchanged.","It has appreciated.","It has depreciated.","Its purchasing power in foreign currency has fallen."],"answer":1,"why":"It now buys more foreign currency (108 rather than 83 foreign cents), so it has appreciated."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0022","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country relies on one mineral for most of its export receipts. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Current-account surplus","Currency appreciation","Supply of currency","Risks of specialisation"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates risks of specialisation: Heavy concentration on one industry can expose an economy to price and demand shocks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0023","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of risks of specialisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Citizens working abroad regularly send money home to their families.","Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services.","A country relies on one mineral for most of its export receipts.","A new container terminal reduces the cost of exporting refrigerated fruit."],"answer":2,"why":"A country relies on one mineral for most of its export receipts. This illustrates heavy concentration on one industry can expose an economy to price and demand shocks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0024","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to risks of specialisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Combining these flows produces the current-account balance.","A fall in that mineral's world price can sharply reduce foreign earnings.","Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts.","Banning trade can prevent specified imports but may disrupt supply."],"answer":1,"why":"A fall in that mineral's world price can sharply reduce foreign earnings."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0025","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to diversify export production away from one mineral. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Exporters may lose price competitiveness abroad.","A stronger currency may later reduce export competitiveness.","The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock.","Training and investment take time."],"answer":2,"why":"The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0026","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to diversify export production away from one mineral. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Domestic environmental risks may decrease.","Future output and living standards may increase.","Exporters may reach more customers.","Developing new industries costs money."],"answer":3,"why":"Developing new industries costs money."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0027","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to diversify export production away from one mineral?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Some communities may remain digitally excluded.","Funds invested abroad may reduce investment at home.","New activities may create additional jobs.","Foreign competition may limit prices charged."],"answer":2,"why":"New activities may create additional jobs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0028","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to diversify export production away from one mineral less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Supplier firms may win new contracts.","More funds may enter regulated financial channels.","Domestic capital formation may rise.","Diversification may reduce some existing scale advantages."],"answer":3,"why":"Diversification may reduce some existing scale advantages."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0029","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to diversify export production away from one mineral. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock.","Service export receipts may increase.","Investors may make better informed decisions.","Exports may become more price competitive abroad."],"answer":0,"why":"The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0030","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The exchange rate is 1 home dollar = 6 foreign units. A machine costs 420 foreign units. How many home dollars are needed, ignoring fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$420","$130","$70","$2520"],"answer":2,"why":"Home-dollar cost = 420 foreign units / 6 = $70."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0031","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes free trade?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Free trade means international exchange with few government-imposed trade barriers.","People supply domestic currency on foreign-exchange markets when buying imports or overseas assets.","A tariff is a tax on imported goods that raises their cost to importers.","The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports."],"answer":0,"why":"Free trade: Free trade means international exchange with few government-imposed trade barriers."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0032","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Two neighbouring countries remove import tariffs on most manufactured goods. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Lower transport costs","Free trade","Supply of currency","Consumer gains from trade"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates free trade: Free trade means international exchange with few government-imposed trade barriers."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0033","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of free trade?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Two neighbouring countries remove import tariffs on most manufactured goods.","Traders buy a currency after predicting that it will strengthen next month.","An exchange booth lists one domestic dollar as worth 0.80 foreign units.","A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients."],"answer":0,"why":"Two neighbouring countries remove import tariffs on most manufactured goods. This illustrates free trade means international exchange with few government-imposed trade barriers."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0034","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to free trade?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal.","Temporary protection may provide time to develop skills and scale.","Combining these flows produces the current-account balance.","Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports."],"answer":3,"why":"Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0035","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to remove tariffs on common household appliances. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Port upgrades require significant public or private funds.","Consumers could face higher prices for substitutes.","Quality upgrades can be expensive.","Consumers may pay lower prices."],"answer":3,"why":"Consumers may pay lower prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0036","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to remove tariffs on common household appliances. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","Some local appliance makers may lose market share.","New firms may develop production experience.","Exporters may reach more customers."],"answer":1,"why":"Some local appliance makers may lose market share."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0037","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to remove tariffs on common household appliances?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The plant may relocate if costs rise elsewhere.","Domestic firms may obtain cheaper imported components.","Expanded shipping may increase local pollution.","Global demand may remain weak."],"answer":1,"why":"Domestic firms may obtain cheaper imported components."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0038","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country exports $75 billion of goods and imports $100 billion. What is its trade-in-goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$175 billion surplus","$25 billion surplus","$100 billion deficit","$25 billion deficit"],"answer":3,"why":"Goods balance = exports − imports = 75 − 100 = -25 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0039","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to remove tariffs on common household appliances. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Consumers may pay lower prices.","Firms may sell services into new markets.","Exports may become more price competitive abroad.","Service export receipts may increase."],"answer":0,"why":"Consumers may pay lower prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0040","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Two neighbouring countries remove import tariffs on most manufactured goods. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The goods balance is a $15 billion deficit.","Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports.","A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness.","Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0041","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes consumer gains from trade?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["An import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported.","International trade can give households access to goods they could not buy domestically.","Open international markets can increase competitive pressure on domestic firms.","The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports."],"answer":1,"why":"Consumer gains from trade: International trade can give households access to goods they could not buy domestically."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0042","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Residents of a cold country buy affordable imported tropical fruit. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Exchange-rate speculation","Multinational company","Consumer gains from trade","Producer gains from trade"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates consumer gains from trade: International trade can give households access to goods they could not buy domestically."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0043","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of consumer gains from trade?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients.","Foreign-owned firms send dividends overseas while residents receive interest from foreign investments.","Residents of a cold country buy affordable imported tropical fruit.","Traders buy a currency after predicting that it will strengthen next month."],"answer":2,"why":"Residents of a cold country buy affordable imported tropical fruit. This illustrates international trade can give households access to goods they could not buy domestically."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0044","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to consumer gains from trade?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Banning trade can prevent specified imports but may disrupt supply.","Imports enlarge product choice and may increase consumer welfare.","The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions.","A surplus can reflect strong exports or restrained domestic spending and investment."],"answer":1,"why":"Imports enlarge product choice and may increase consumer welfare."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0045","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to allow more competition from imported food products. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Protection can keep prices artificially high.","Consumers may gain greater variety.","Quality upgrades can be expensive.","Poor cybersecurity could disrupt business."],"answer":1,"why":"Consumers may gain greater variety."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0046","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country's balances are: goods +$22bn, services +$3bn, primary income −$3bn and secondary income +$3bn. Calculate the current-account balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$31 billion surplus","$28 billion surplus","$25 billion deficit","$25 billion surplus"],"answer":3,"why":"Current account = 22 + 3 − 3 + 3 = +$25 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0047","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to allow more competition from imported food products?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Lower prices may improve real purchasing power.","An overseas slowdown may still reduce exports.","Remittance income may fall if overseas employment weakens.","Global demand may remain weak."],"answer":0,"why":"Lower prices may improve real purchasing power."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0048","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to allow more competition from imported food products less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Domestic capital formation may rise.","International coordination may become more efficient.","Transport-related emissions may increase.","Skilled employment could expand."],"answer":2,"why":"Transport-related emissions may increase."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0049","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to allow more competition from imported food products. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","Consumers may gain greater variety.","Exporters may reach more customers.","Future output and living standards may increase."],"answer":1,"why":"Consumers may gain greater variety."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0050","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Residents of a cold country buy affordable imported tropical fruit. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness.","Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports.","The stronger currency makes imports cheaper in domestic money but exports dearer abroad.","Imports enlarge product choice and may increase consumer welfare."],"answer":3,"why":"Imports enlarge product choice and may increase consumer welfare."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0051","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes producer gains from trade?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Export markets allow firms to sell beyond the size of domestic demand.","A current-account surplus occurs when current-account receipts exceed payments.","The current account records trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income transactions.","Falling shipping costs can make trading goods across borders more affordable."],"answer":0,"why":"Producer gains from trade: Export markets allow firms to sell beyond the size of domestic demand."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0052","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Import dependence","Supply of currency","Globalisation","Producer gains from trade"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates producer gains from trade: Export markets allow firms to sell beyond the size of domestic demand."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0053","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of producer gains from trade?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries.","A country earns more from exports and income flows than it spends on imports and transfers.","A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients.","Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services."],"answer":0,"why":"A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries. This illustrates export markets allow firms to sell beyond the size of domestic demand."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0054","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to producer gains from trade?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The services balance is an $8 billion surplus.","A surplus can reflect strong exports or restrained domestic spending and investment.","Higher import costs can protect local suppliers while raising consumer prices.","Larger potential markets can support higher output and scale economies."],"answer":3,"why":"Larger potential markets can support higher output and scale economies."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0055","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"One home dollar previously bought 87 foreign cents and now buys 107 foreign cents. What has happened to the home currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Its purchasing power in foreign currency has fallen.","Its value has remained unchanged.","It has depreciated.","It has appreciated."],"answer":3,"why":"It now buys more foreign currency (107 rather than 87 foreign cents), so it has appreciated."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0056","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to help small manufacturers access export markets. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["New firms may develop production experience.","Firms may access specialised inputs.","Firms may sell services into new markets.","Exchange-rate changes can reduce export earnings."],"answer":3,"why":"Exchange-rate changes can reduce export earnings."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0057","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to help small manufacturers access export markets?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Poorly selected projects can waste the earnings.","Trading partners may retaliate with tariffs of their own.","Higher volumes may allow more efficient production.","Export demand may respond slowly if contracts are fixed."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher volumes may allow more efficient production."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0058","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to help small manufacturers access export markets less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Meeting foreign standards may be costly.","Domestic capital formation may rise.","Skilled employment could expand.","Importing productive machinery may improve efficiency."],"answer":0,"why":"Meeting foreign standards may be costly."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0059","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to help small manufacturers access export markets. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Sales and revenue may increase.","Future output and living standards may increase.","Export earnings may rise over time.","Domestic tile makers may gain market share."],"answer":0,"why":"Sales and revenue may increase."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0060","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports.","Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods.","Higher input costs may be passed to households as inflation.","Larger potential markets can support higher output and scale economies."],"answer":3,"why":"Larger potential markets can support higher output and scale economies."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0061","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes import dependence?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Relying heavily on imported essentials can make supply vulnerable to overseas disruptions.","The services balance is the value of services exports minus services imports.","An exchange rate is the price of one currency expressed in another currency.","A current-account surplus occurs when current-account receipts exceed payments."],"answer":0,"why":"Import dependence: Relying heavily on imported essentials can make supply vulnerable to overseas disruptions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0062","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country imports almost all its fertiliser from a single foreign supplier. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Infant industry protection","Import dependence","Free trade","Producer gains from trade"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates import dependence: Relying heavily on imported essentials can make supply vulnerable to overseas disruptions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0063","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The exchange rate is 1 home dollar = 4 foreign units. A machine costs 440 foreign units. How many home dollars are needed, ignoring fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$440","$110","$1760","$150"],"answer":1,"why":"Home-dollar cost = 440 foreign units / 4 = $110."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0064","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to import dependence?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports.","Foreign investment can transfer capital, jobs and know-how across borders.","Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders.","Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices."],"answer":3,"why":"Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0065","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Consumers could face higher prices for substitutes.","Training and investment take time.","Shortages may become less likely.","Port upgrades require significant public or private funds."],"answer":2,"why":"Shortages may become less likely."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0066","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Future output and living standards may increase.","Export demand may increase.","Multiple supplier contracts may be more expensive.","Exporters may reach more customers."],"answer":2,"why":"Multiple supplier contracts may be more expensive."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0067","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["An overseas slowdown may still reduce exports.","Competition between suppliers may reduce reliance on one firm.","Export demand may respond slowly if contracts are fixed.","The plant may relocate if costs rise elsewhere."],"answer":1,"why":"Competition between suppliers may reduce reliance on one firm."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0068","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Global shortages can affect all source countries.","Reliable information may reduce some rumours.","Export-oriented firms may increase output and jobs.","Exporters may plan receipts and payments more accurately."],"answer":0,"why":"Global shortages can affect all source countries."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0069","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Service export receipts may increase.","Shortages may become less likely.","Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","Firms may access specialised inputs."],"answer":1,"why":"Shortages may become less likely."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0070","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country imports almost all its fertiliser from a single foreign supplier. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders.","More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate.","Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices.","Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates."],"answer":2,"why":"Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0071","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country exports $95 billion of goods and imports $76 billion. What is its trade-in-goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$171 billion surplus","$19 billion deficit","$19 billion surplus","$76 billion deficit"],"answer":2,"why":"Goods balance = exports − imports = 95 − 76 = 19 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0072","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Imported sports shoes enter a market previously served by only two local brands. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Trade and competition","Import dependence","Multinational company","Trade in goods balance"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates trade and competition: Open international markets can increase competitive pressure on domestic firms."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0073","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of trade and competition?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services.","Imported sports shoes enter a market previously served by only two local brands.","Domestic investors exchange local money for foreign currency to purchase overseas shares.","A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers."],"answer":1,"why":"Imported sports shoes enter a market previously served by only two local brands. This illustrates open international markets can increase competitive pressure on domestic firms."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0074","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to trade and competition?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates.","Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports.","More rival suppliers can push domestic firms to improve price and quality.","A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness."],"answer":2,"why":"More rival suppliers can push domestic firms to improve price and quality."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0075","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Buyers may gain more choice and lower prices.","Developing local energy supplies is expensive.","A stronger currency may later reduce export competitiveness.","Training costs money and takes time."],"answer":0,"why":"Buyers may gain more choice and lower prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0076","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Domestic environmental risks may decrease.","Some domestic workers may lose jobs.","Local workers may gain employment and training.","Exports may become more price competitive abroad."],"answer":1,"why":"Some domestic workers may lose jobs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0077","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["An overseas slowdown may still reduce exports.","Global demand may remain weak.","Future depreciation could reverse the cost saving.","Local firms may become more efficient to compete."],"answer":3,"why":"Local firms may become more efficient to compete."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0078","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Reliable information may reduce some rumours.","Foreign firms may dominate a very small local market.","Government may collect customs revenue.","International coordination may become more efficient."],"answer":1,"why":"Foreign firms may dominate a very small local market."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0079","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Businesses may understand currency risks better.","Firms may sell services into new markets.","Buyers may gain more choice and lower prices.","Local workers may gain employment and training."],"answer":2,"why":"Buyers may gain more choice and lower prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0080","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country's balances are: goods +$14bn, services +$7bn, primary income −$2bn and secondary income +$3bn. Calculate the current-account balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$21 billion surplus","$26 billion surplus","$22 billion surplus","$22 billion deficit"],"answer":2,"why":"Current account = 14 + 7 − 2 + 3 = +$22 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0081","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes globalisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Globalisation is the growing economic interconnection and interdependence of countries.","Falling shipping costs can make trading goods across borders more affordable.","A current-account deficit occurs when total current-account payments exceed total receipts.","An exchange rate is the price of one currency expressed in another currency."],"answer":0,"why":"Globalisation: Globalisation is the growing economic interconnection and interdependence of countries."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0082","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A phone designed in one country is assembled using components made in several others. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Tariff","Currency depreciation","Risks of specialisation","Globalisation"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates globalisation: Globalisation is the growing economic interconnection and interdependence of countries."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0083","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of globalisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts.","A phone designed in one country is assembled using components made in several others.","Foreign-owned firms send dividends overseas while residents receive interest from foreign investments.","An exchange booth lists one domestic dollar as worth 0.80 foreign units."],"answer":1,"why":"A phone designed in one country is assembled using components made in several others. This illustrates globalisation is the growing economic interconnection and interdependence of countries."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0084","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to globalisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices.","Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts.","Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders.","More rival suppliers can push domestic firms to improve price and quality."],"answer":2,"why":"Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0085","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to participate in international production networks. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Extra domestic demand can reduce the surplus.","Demand for bicycle parts could decline.","Training and investment take time.","Firms may access specialised inputs."],"answer":3,"why":"Firms may access specialised inputs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0086","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to participate in international production networks. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Global disruptions can interrupt supply chains.","More investment income may remain with local residents.","Buyers may gain more choice and lower prices.","Higher export sales may earn foreign currency."],"answer":0,"why":"Global disruptions can interrupt supply chains."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0087","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to participate in international production networks?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Export demand may respond slowly if contracts are fixed.","Global shortages can affect all source countries.","Workers may gain employment in export-oriented industries.","Overfishing could damage future productive resources."],"answer":2,"why":"Workers may gain employment in export-oriented industries."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0088","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"One home dollar previously bought 91 foreign cents and now buys 106 foreign cents. What has happened to the home currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["It has appreciated.","Its purchasing power in foreign currency has fallen.","Its value has remained unchanged.","It has depreciated."],"answer":0,"why":"It now buys more foreign currency (106 rather than 91 foreign cents), so it has appreciated."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0089","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to participate in international production networks. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Export demand may increase.","Higher export sales may earn foreign currency.","Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance.","Firms may access specialised inputs."],"answer":3,"why":"Firms may access specialised inputs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0090","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A phone designed in one country is assembled using components made in several others. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal.","Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates.","Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders.","Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods."],"answer":2,"why":"Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0091","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes lower transport costs?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["International trade can give households access to goods they could not buy domestically.","Falling shipping costs can make trading goods across borders more affordable.","International specialisation occurs when a country concentrates resources on goods it can produce relatively efficiently.","Free trade means international exchange with few government-imposed trade barriers."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower transport costs: Falling shipping costs can make trading goods across borders more affordable."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0092","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A new container terminal reduces the cost of exporting refrigerated fruit. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Consumer gains from trade","Imported inflation","Lower transport costs","Supply of currency"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates lower transport costs: Falling shipping costs can make trading goods across borders more affordable."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0093","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of lower transport costs?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A new container terminal reduces the cost of exporting refrigerated fruit.","A country imports almost all its fertiliser from a single foreign supplier.","One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.80 to buying 0.90 foreign units.","A country relies on one mineral for most of its export receipts."],"answer":0,"why":"A new container terminal reduces the cost of exporting refrigerated fruit. This illustrates falling shipping costs can make trading goods across borders more affordable."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0094","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to lower transport costs?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices.","Lower transport costs improve the competitiveness of distant suppliers.","Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates.","The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower transport costs improve the competitiveness of distant suppliers."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0095","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to modernise ports to reduce international shipping delays. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Some domestic workers may lose jobs.","Data can arrive with reporting delays.","Exporters may reach more customers.","Exchange rates can fluctuate between quotation and payment."],"answer":2,"why":"Exporters may reach more customers."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0096","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The exchange rate is 1 home dollar = 2 foreign units. A machine costs 120 foreign units. How many home dollars are needed, ignoring fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$80","$120","$60","$240"],"answer":2,"why":"Home-dollar cost = 120 foreign units / 2 = $60."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0097","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to modernise ports to reduce international shipping delays?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Overfishing could damage future productive resources.","Imported production inputs may become cheaper.","Global shortages can affect all source countries.","Some energy resources cannot be produced domestically."],"answer":1,"why":"Imported production inputs may become cheaper."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0098","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to modernise ports to reduce international shipping delays less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Importing productive machinery may improve efficiency.","Exporting firms may support additional jobs.","Expanded shipping may increase local pollution.","Domestic capital formation may rise."],"answer":2,"why":"Expanded shipping may increase local pollution."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0099","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to modernise ports to reduce international shipping delays. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Sales and revenue may increase.","Future output and living standards may increase.","Exporters may reach more customers.","Firms may gain experience and lower average costs."],"answer":2,"why":"Exporters may reach more customers."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0100","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A new container terminal reduces the cost of exporting refrigerated fruit. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Lower transport costs improve the competitiveness of distant suppliers.","The stronger currency makes imports cheaper in domestic money but exports dearer abroad.","Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts.","The goods balance is a $15 billion deficit."],"answer":0,"why":"Lower transport costs improve the competitiveness of distant suppliers."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0101","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes communication technology?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["An import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported.","Falling shipping costs can make trading goods across borders more affordable.","Cheaper and faster communication can support international coordination of trade and investment.","Depreciation is a fall in a currency's value under a floating exchange-rate system."],"answer":2,"why":"Communication technology: Cheaper and faster communication can support international coordination of trade and investment."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0102","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Producer gains from trade","Communication technology","Trade and competition","Exchange rate"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates communication technology: Cheaper and faster communication can support international coordination of trade and investment."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0103","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of communication technology?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients.","A country develops clusters of skilled bicycle-component manufacturers.","One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.80 to buying 0.90 foreign units.","One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.90 to buying 0.75 foreign units."],"answer":0,"why":"A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients. This illustrates cheaper and faster communication can support international coordination of trade and investment."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0104","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to communication technology?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions.","Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal.","Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports.","Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports."],"answer":0,"why":"Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0105","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country exports $65 billion of goods and imports $108 billion. What is its trade-in-goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$43 billion deficit","$108 billion deficit","$173 billion surplus","$43 billion surplus"],"answer":0,"why":"Goods balance = exports − imports = 65 − 108 = -43 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0106","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand reliable broadband for exporting service firms. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance.","Investors may make better informed decisions.","The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock.","Poor cybersecurity could disrupt business."],"answer":3,"why":"Poor cybersecurity could disrupt business."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0107","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand reliable broadband for exporting service firms?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Export demand may respond slowly if contracts are fixed.","Not all future political or economic events are predictable.","Global shortages can affect all source countries.","International coordination may become more efficient."],"answer":3,"why":"International coordination may become more efficient."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0108","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand reliable broadband for exporting service firms less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Suppliers may benefit from shared infrastructure.","Domestic firms may obtain cheaper imported components.","Some communities may remain digitally excluded.","Lower prices may improve real purchasing power."],"answer":2,"why":"Some communities may remain digitally excluded."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0109","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand reliable broadband for exporting service firms. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher export sales may earn foreign currency.","Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","Firms may sell services into new markets.","Export earnings may rise over time."],"answer":2,"why":"Firms may sell services into new markets."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0110","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The services balance is an $8 billion surplus.","A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness.","The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions.","Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions."],"answer":3,"why":"Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0111","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes multinational company?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Falling shipping costs can make trading goods across borders more affordable.","The services balance is the value of services exports minus services imports.","A multinational company owns or controls productive operations in more than one country.","The current account records trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income transactions."],"answer":2,"why":"Multinational company: A multinational company owns or controls productive operations in more than one country."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0112","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: An international electronics firm opens an assembly plant in another country. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Embargo","Trade in goods balance","Tariff","Multinational company"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates multinational company: A multinational company owns or controls productive operations in more than one country."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0113","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country's balances are: goods +$22bn, services +$5bn, primary income −$6bn and secondary income +$3bn. Calculate the current-account balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$36 billion surplus","$27 billion surplus","$24 billion surplus","$24 billion deficit"],"answer":2,"why":"Current account = 22 + 5 − 6 + 3 = +$24 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0114","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to multinational company?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Repeated production can improve skills and reduce unit costs.","Larger potential markets can support higher output and scale economies.","Foreign investment can transfer capital, jobs and know-how across borders.","Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade."],"answer":2,"why":"Foreign investment can transfer capital, jobs and know-how across borders."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0115","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Exchange rates can fluctuate between quotation and payment.","Demand for bicycle parts could decline.","Payment services require compliance and monitoring.","Local workers may gain employment and training."],"answer":3,"why":"Local workers may gain employment and training."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0116","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance.","The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock.","Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","Profits may be remitted to the firm's home country."],"answer":3,"why":"Profits may be remitted to the firm's home country."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0117","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Future depreciation could reverse the cost saving.","Overfishing could damage future productive resources.","Supplier firms may win new contracts.","Some energy resources cannot be produced domestically."],"answer":2,"why":"Supplier firms may win new contracts."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0118","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Export-oriented firms may increase output and jobs.","Higher volumes may allow more efficient production.","The plant may relocate if costs rise elsewhere.","New activities may create additional jobs."],"answer":2,"why":"The plant may relocate if costs rise elsewhere."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0119","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance.","The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock.","Local workers may gain employment and training."],"answer":3,"why":"Local workers may gain employment and training."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0120","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: An international electronics firm opens an assembly plant in another country. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods.","Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts.","Larger potential markets can support higher output and scale economies.","Foreign investment can transfer capital, jobs and know-how across borders."],"answer":3,"why":"Foreign investment can transfer capital, jobs and know-how across borders."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0121","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"One home dollar previously bought 95 foreign cents and now buys 105 foreign cents. What has happened to the home currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["It has appreciated.","It has depreciated.","Its value has remained unchanged.","Its purchasing power in foreign currency has fallen."],"answer":0,"why":"It now buys more foreign currency (105 rather than 95 foreign cents), so it has appreciated."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0122","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country imposes a 15% tariff on imported ceramic tiles. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Current account","Exchange-rate speculation","Import dependence","Tariff"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates tariff: A tariff is a tax on imported goods that raises their cost to importers."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0123","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of tariff?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A country imposes a 15% tariff on imported ceramic tiles.","Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services.","A country imports almost all its fertiliser from a single foreign supplier.","An exchange booth lists one domestic dollar as worth 0.80 foreign units."],"answer":0,"why":"A country imposes a 15% tariff on imported ceramic tiles. This illustrates a tariff is a tax on imported goods that raises their cost to importers."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0124","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to tariff?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A surplus can reflect strong exports or restrained domestic spending and investment.","Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal.","Higher import costs can protect local suppliers while raising consumer prices.","The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher import costs can protect local suppliers while raising consumer prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0125","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Domestic tile makers may gain market share.","Developing new industries costs money.","Exchange rates can fluctuate between quotation and payment.","Multiple supplier contracts may be more expensive."],"answer":0,"why":"Domestic tile makers may gain market share."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0126","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Households and builders may face higher prices.","Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","More investment income may remain with local residents.","Future output and living standards may increase."],"answer":0,"why":"Households and builders may face higher prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0127","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Global shortages can affect all source countries.","Government may collect customs revenue.","Overfishing could damage future productive resources.","Funds invested abroad may reduce investment at home."],"answer":1,"why":"Government may collect customs revenue."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0128","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Trading partners may retaliate with tariffs of their own.","Competitive firms may develop sustainable overseas markets.","Domestic manufacturing employment may expand.","Reliable information may reduce some rumours."],"answer":0,"why":"Trading partners may retaliate with tariffs of their own."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0129","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Domestic tile makers may gain market share.","Service export receipts may increase.","Sales and revenue may increase.","Export demand may increase."],"answer":0,"why":"Domestic tile makers may gain market share."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0130","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The exchange rate is 1 home dollar = 6 foreign units. A machine costs 600 foreign units. How many home dollars are needed, ignoring fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$100","$3600","$160","$600"],"answer":0,"why":"Home-dollar cost = 600 foreign units / 6 = $100."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0131","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes import quota?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["An import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported.","The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports.","Foreign demand for a country's currency can arise from its exports and investment opportunities.","People supply domestic currency on foreign-exchange markets when buying imports or overseas assets."],"answer":0,"why":"Import quota: An import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0132","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government permits no more than 40,000 imported shoes each year. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Trade in services balance","Import quota","Imported inflation","Multinational company"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates import quota: An import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0133","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of import quota?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Traders buy a currency after predicting that it will strengthen next month.","A government permits no more than 40,000 imported shoes each year.","A country earns export receipts but also pays for imports and income transfers abroad.","One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.80 to buying 0.90 foreign units."],"answer":1,"why":"A government permits no more than 40,000 imported shoes each year. This illustrates an import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0134","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to import quota?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A fall in that mineral's world price can sharply reduce foreign earnings.","Combining these flows produces the current-account balance.","Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices.","Restricting imported supply can support local firms but reduce competition."],"answer":3,"why":"Restricting imported supply can support local firms but reduce competition."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0135","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to cap annual imports of low-cost footwear. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A stronger currency may later reduce export competitiveness.","Some domestic workers may lose jobs.","Some domestic shoe producers may sell more.","Speculative flows can still cause sharp fluctuations."],"answer":2,"why":"Some domestic shoe producers may sell more."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0136","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to cap annual imports of low-cost footwear. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Service export receipts may increase.","Consumers may face less choice and higher prices.","Sales and revenue may increase.","Firms may gain experience and lower average costs."],"answer":1,"why":"Consumers may face less choice and higher prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0137","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to cap annual imports of low-cost footwear?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Some energy resources cannot be produced domestically.","Employment in protected firms may be preserved.","Export demand may respond slowly if contracts are fixed.","Regions may become vulnerable to one industry's downturn."],"answer":1,"why":"Employment in protected firms may be preserved."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0138","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country exports $85 billion of goods and imports $84 billion. What is its trade-in-goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$1 billion deficit","$1 billion surplus","$84 billion deficit","$169 billion surplus"],"answer":1,"why":"Goods balance = exports − imports = 85 − 84 = 1 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0139","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to cap annual imports of low-cost footwear. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","Sales and revenue may increase.","Some domestic shoe producers may sell more.","Specialist workers may become more productive."],"answer":2,"why":"Some domestic shoe producers may sell more."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0140","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government permits no more than 40,000 imported shoes each year. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Restricting imported supply can support local firms but reduce competition.","The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions.","Combining these flows produces the current-account balance.","Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates."],"answer":0,"why":"Restricting imported supply can support local firms but reduce competition."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0141","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes embargo?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Appreciation is a rise in the value of a currency under a floating exchange-rate system.","International trade can give households access to goods they could not buy domestically.","Open international markets can increase competitive pressure on domestic firms.","An embargo is an official prohibition on trade with a particular country or in a particular product."],"answer":3,"why":"Embargo: An embargo is an official prohibition on trade with a particular country or in a particular product."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0142","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Current-account deficit","Benefits of specialisation","Tariff","Embargo"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates embargo: An embargo is an official prohibition on trade with a particular country or in a particular product."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0143","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of embargo?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product.","A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries.","A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services.","Foreign-owned firms send dividends overseas while residents receive interest from foreign investments."],"answer":0,"why":"A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product. This illustrates an embargo is an official prohibition on trade with a particular country or in a particular product."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0144","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to embargo?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods.","The services balance is an $8 billion surplus.","Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts.","Banning trade can prevent specified imports but may disrupt supply."],"answer":3,"why":"Banning trade can prevent specified imports but may disrupt supply."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0145","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to ban imports of a product associated with severe environmental harm. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Domestic environmental risks may decrease.","Imported fuel and machinery may become more expensive.","Domestic savings may be insufficient for large projects.","Large outflows could weaken the domestic currency."],"answer":0,"why":"Domestic environmental risks may decrease."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0146","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country's balances are: goods +$14bn, services +$3bn, primary income −$5bn and secondary income +$3bn. Calculate the current-account balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$15 billion deficit","$15 billion surplus","$17 billion surplus","$25 billion surplus"],"answer":1,"why":"Current account = 14 + 3 − 5 + 3 = +$15 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0147","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to ban imports of a product associated with severe environmental harm?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Transport-related emissions may increase.","Firms may develop safer alternative products.","Competitors abroad may lower their prices.","Transaction fees may reduce the amount received."],"answer":1,"why":"Firms may develop safer alternative products."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0148","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to ban imports of a product associated with severe environmental harm less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Competition between suppliers may reduce reliance on one firm.","Enforcement and illegal trade may be difficult to control.","Greater capital mobility may increase diversification opportunities.","Skilled employment could expand."],"answer":1,"why":"Enforcement and illegal trade may be difficult to control."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0149","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to ban imports of a product associated with severe environmental harm. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance.","Exports may become more price competitive abroad.","Domestic environmental risks may decrease.","Investment equipment may cost less."],"answer":2,"why":"Domestic environmental risks may decrease."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0150","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports.","Banning trade can prevent specified imports but may disrupt supply.","Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates.","A fall in that mineral's world price can sharply reduce foreign earnings."],"answer":1,"why":"Banning trade can prevent specified imports but may disrupt supply."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0151","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes infant industry protection?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Infant industry protection temporarily shields new domestic firms from established foreign competitors.","A tariff is a tax on imported goods that raises their cost to importers.","A multinational company owns or controls productive operations in more than one country.","An embargo is an official prohibition on trade with a particular country or in a particular product."],"answer":0,"why":"Infant industry protection: Infant industry protection temporarily shields new domestic firms from established foreign competitors."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0152","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Infant industry protection","Consumer gains from trade","Exchange-rate speculation","Free trade"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates infant industry protection: Infant industry protection temporarily shields new domestic firms from established foreign competitors."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0153","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of infant industry protection?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Imported sports shoes enter a market previously served by only two local brands.","A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers.","A country earns more from exports and income flows than it spends on imports and transfers.","Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts."],"answer":1,"why":"A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers. This illustrates infant industry protection temporarily shields new domestic firms from established foreign competitors."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0154","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to infant industry protection?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports.","Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade.","Temporary protection may provide time to develop skills and scale.","More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate."],"answer":2,"why":"Temporary protection may provide time to develop skills and scale."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0155","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"One home dollar previously bought 99 foreign cents and now buys 129 foreign cents. What has happened to the home currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Its purchasing power in foreign currency has fallen.","It has appreciated.","Its value has remained unchanged.","It has depreciated."],"answer":1,"why":"It now buys more foreign currency (129 rather than 99 foreign cents), so it has appreciated."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0156","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to protect a new solar-equipment industry for a limited period. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Businesses may understand currency risks better.","Protection can keep prices artificially high.","Investors may make better informed decisions.","Firms may gain experience and lower average costs."],"answer":1,"why":"Protection can keep prices artificially high."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0157","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to protect a new solar-equipment industry for a limited period?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Global shortages can affect all source countries.","Export demand may respond slowly if contracts are fixed.","Domestic manufacturing jobs may expand.","Foreign direct investment can bring expertise that local finance lacks."],"answer":2,"why":"Domestic manufacturing jobs may expand."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0158","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to protect a new solar-equipment industry for a limited period less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Exporters may plan receipts and payments more accurately.","More funds may enter regulated financial channels.","Firms may become dependent on continuing protection.","Higher volumes may allow more efficient production."],"answer":2,"why":"Firms may become dependent on continuing protection."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0159","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to protect a new solar-equipment industry for a limited period. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher export sales may earn foreign currency.","New firms may develop production experience.","Export earnings may rise over time.","Consumers may pay lower prices."],"answer":1,"why":"New firms may develop production experience."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0160","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal.","The services balance is an $8 billion surplus.","Temporary protection may provide time to develop skills and scale.","A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness."],"answer":2,"why":"Temporary protection may provide time to develop skills and scale."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0161","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes exchange rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["An exchange rate is the price of one currency expressed in another currency.","The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports.","Infant industry protection temporarily shields new domestic firms from established foreign competitors.","People supply domestic currency on foreign-exchange markets when buying imports or overseas assets."],"answer":0,"why":"Exchange rate: An exchange rate is the price of one currency expressed in another currency."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0162","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: An exchange booth lists one domestic dollar as worth 0.80 foreign units. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Current-account deficit","Exchange rate","Import quota","Current account"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates exchange rate: An exchange rate is the price of one currency expressed in another currency."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0163","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The exchange rate is 1 home dollar = 4 foreign units. A machine costs 200 foreign units. How many home dollars are needed, ignoring fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$800","$200","$90","$50"],"answer":3,"why":"Home-dollar cost = 200 foreign units / 4 = $50."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0164","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to exchange rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders.","The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions.","Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods.","A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness."],"answer":1,"why":"The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0165","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Training and investment take time.","Some local appliance makers may lose market share.","Exchange-rate changes can reduce export earnings.","Travellers can compare currency conversion costs."],"answer":3,"why":"Travellers can compare currency conversion costs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0166","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Firms may sell services into new markets.","Local workers may gain employment and training.","Exchange rates can fluctuate between quotation and payment.","Consumers may gain greater variety."],"answer":2,"why":"Exchange rates can fluctuate between quotation and payment."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0167","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Regions may become vulnerable to one industry's downturn.","Trading partners may retaliate with tariffs of their own.","Exporters may plan receipts and payments more accurately.","Remittance income may fall if overseas employment weakens."],"answer":2,"why":"Exporters may plan receipts and payments more accurately."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0168","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["International coordination may become more efficient.","Transaction fees may reduce the amount received.","Higher volumes may allow more efficient production.","Employment in protected firms may be preserved."],"answer":1,"why":"Transaction fees may reduce the amount received."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0169","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Travellers can compare currency conversion costs.","Domestic environmental risks may decrease.","Firms may sell services into new markets.","Consumers may pay lower prices."],"answer":0,"why":"Travellers can compare currency conversion costs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0170","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: An exchange booth lists one domestic dollar as worth 0.80 foreign units. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions.","Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions.","A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness.","Combining these flows produces the current-account balance."],"answer":0,"why":"The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0171","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country exports $55 billion of goods and imports $60 billion. What is its trade-in-goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$60 billion deficit","$5 billion surplus","$5 billion deficit","$115 billion surplus"],"answer":2,"why":"Goods balance = exports − imports = 55 − 60 = -5 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0172","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.80 to buying 0.90 foreign units. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Risks of specialisation","International specialisation","Current account","Currency appreciation"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates currency appreciation: Appreciation is a rise in the value of a currency under a floating exchange-rate system."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0173","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of currency appreciation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A country earns more from exports and income flows than it spends on imports and transfers.","Two neighbouring countries remove import tariffs on most manufactured goods.","One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.80 to buying 0.90 foreign units.","A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries."],"answer":2,"why":"One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.80 to buying 0.90 foreign units. This illustrates appreciation is a rise in the value of a currency under a floating exchange-rate system."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0174","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to currency appreciation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The goods balance is a $15 billion deficit.","Restricting imported supply can support local firms but reduce competition.","Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade.","The stronger currency makes imports cheaper in domestic money but exports dearer abroad."],"answer":3,"why":"The stronger currency makes imports cheaper in domestic money but exports dearer abroad."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0175","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Some domestic workers may lose jobs.","Investment equipment may cost less.","Consumers may face less choice and higher prices.","Poor cybersecurity could disrupt business."],"answer":1,"why":"Investment equipment may cost less."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0176","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance.","New firms may develop production experience.","Exporters may lose price competitiveness abroad.","Local workers may gain employment and training."],"answer":2,"why":"Exporters may lose price competitiveness abroad."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0177","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Foreign suppliers may respond with trade restrictions.","Imported inputs may become more affordable.","A headline balance does not capture all economic conditions.","Poorly selected projects can waste the earnings."],"answer":1,"why":"Imported inputs may become more affordable."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0178","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Future depreciation could reverse the cost saving.","Government may collect customs revenue.","Importing productive machinery may improve efficiency.","International coordination may become more efficient."],"answer":0,"why":"Future depreciation could reverse the cost saving."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0179","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Export earnings may rise over time.","Export demand may increase.","Some domestic shoe producers may sell more.","Investment equipment may cost less."],"answer":3,"why":"Investment equipment may cost less."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0180","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country's balances are: goods +$22bn, services +$7bn, primary income −$4bn and secondary income +$3bn. Calculate the current-account balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$28 billion deficit","$29 billion surplus","$28 billion surplus","$36 billion surplus"],"answer":2,"why":"Current account = 22 + 7 − 4 + 3 = +$28 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0181","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes currency depreciation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Depreciation is a fall in a currency's value under a floating exchange-rate system.","Relying heavily on imported essentials can make supply vulnerable to overseas disruptions.","An import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported.","International specialisation occurs when a country concentrates resources on goods it can produce relatively efficiently."],"answer":0,"why":"Currency depreciation: Depreciation is a fall in a currency's value under a floating exchange-rate system."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0182","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.90 to buying 0.75 foreign units. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Currency depreciation","Infant industry protection","Free trade","Tariff"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates currency depreciation: Depreciation is a fall in a currency's value under a floating exchange-rate system."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0183","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of currency depreciation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A country imposes a 15% tariff on imported ceramic tiles.","One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.90 to buying 0.75 foreign units.","Citizens working abroad regularly send money home to their families.","Two neighbouring countries remove import tariffs on most manufactured goods."],"answer":1,"why":"One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.90 to buying 0.75 foreign units. This illustrates depreciation is a fall in a currency's value under a floating exchange-rate system."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0184","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to currency depreciation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Repeated production can improve skills and reduce unit costs.","A fall in that mineral's world price can sharply reduce foreign earnings.","More rival suppliers can push domestic firms to improve price and quality.","Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports."],"answer":3,"why":"Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0185","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to promote exports after a currency depreciation. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Demand for bicycle parts could decline.","Some domestic workers may lose jobs.","Consumers could face higher prices for substitutes.","Exports may become more price competitive abroad."],"answer":3,"why":"Exports may become more price competitive abroad."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0186","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to promote exports after a currency depreciation. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More investment income may remain with local residents.","Imported fuel and machinery may become more expensive.","Consumers may gain greater variety.","Domestic environmental risks may decrease."],"answer":1,"why":"Imported fuel and machinery may become more expensive."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0187","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to promote exports after a currency depreciation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Export-oriented firms may increase output and jobs.","The plant may relocate if costs rise elsewhere.","An overseas slowdown may still reduce exports.","Overfishing could damage future productive resources."],"answer":0,"why":"Export-oriented firms may increase output and jobs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0188","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"One home dollar previously bought 103 foreign cents and now buys 128 foreign cents. What has happened to the home currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Its value has remained unchanged.","Its purchasing power in foreign currency has fallen.","It has appreciated.","It has depreciated."],"answer":2,"why":"It now buys more foreign currency (128 rather than 103 foreign cents), so it has appreciated."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0189","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to promote exports after a currency depreciation. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Shortages may become less likely.","Firms may sell services into new markets.","More investment income may remain with local residents.","Exports may become more price competitive abroad."],"answer":3,"why":"Exports may become more price competitive abroad."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0190","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.90 to buying 0.75 foreign units. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness.","Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade.","More rival suppliers can push domestic firms to improve price and quality.","Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports."],"answer":3,"why":"Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0191","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes demand for currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Foreign demand for a country's currency can arise from its exports and investment opportunities.","Currency speculation is trading currencies based on expectations of future exchange-rate movements.","The current account records trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income transactions.","The services balance is the value of services exports minus services imports."],"answer":0,"why":"Demand for currency: Foreign demand for a country's currency can arise from its exports and investment opportunities."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0192","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Primary income balance","Demand for currency","Import quota","Consumer gains from trade"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates demand for currency: Foreign demand for a country's currency can arise from its exports and investment opportunities."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0193","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of demand for currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients.","A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product.","Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services.","A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers."],"answer":2,"why":"Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services. This illustrates foreign demand for a country's currency can arise from its exports and investment opportunities."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0194","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to demand for currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders.","Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions.","Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal.","Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0195","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve export quality to attract overseas buyers. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Consumers may face less choice and higher prices.","The country may become dependent on global seafood demand.","Extra domestic demand can reduce the surplus.","Higher export sales may earn foreign currency."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher export sales may earn foreign currency."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0196","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The exchange rate is 1 home dollar = 2 foreign units. A machine costs 180 foreign units. How many home dollars are needed, ignoring fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$360","$180","$90","$110"],"answer":2,"why":"Home-dollar cost = 180 foreign units / 2 = $90."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0197","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve export quality to attract overseas buyers?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A headline balance does not capture all economic conditions.","Regions may become vulnerable to one industry's downturn.","Exporting firms may support additional jobs.","Import dependence may increase in strategic product categories."],"answer":2,"why":"Exporting firms may support additional jobs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0198","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to improve export quality to attract overseas buyers less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Competition between suppliers may reduce reliance on one firm.","Employment in protected firms may be preserved.","Imported production inputs may become cheaper.","Competitors abroad may lower their prices."],"answer":3,"why":"Competitors abroad may lower their prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0199","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve export quality to attract overseas buyers. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Consumers may gain greater variety.","Export earnings may rise over time.","Higher export sales may earn foreign currency.","Firms may access specialised inputs."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher export sales may earn foreign currency."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0200","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher import costs can protect local suppliers while raising consumer prices.","Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions.","Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal.","Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0201","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes supply of currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["People supply domestic currency on foreign-exchange markets when buying imports or overseas assets.","Open international markets can increase competitive pressure on domestic firms.","Primary income includes cross-border earnings from work and investments, such as interest, profits and dividends.","An import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported."],"answer":0,"why":"Supply of currency: People supply domestic currency on foreign-exchange markets when buying imports or overseas assets."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0202","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Domestic investors exchange local money for foreign currency to purchase overseas shares. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Imported inflation","Trade and competition","Supply of currency","Risks of specialisation"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates supply of currency: People supply domestic currency on foreign-exchange markets when buying imports or overseas assets."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0203","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of supply of currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers.","Domestic investors exchange local money for foreign currency to purchase overseas shares.","A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries.","Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts."],"answer":1,"why":"Domestic investors exchange local money for foreign currency to purchase overseas shares. This illustrates people supply domestic currency on foreign-exchange markets when buying imports or overseas assets."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0204","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to supply of currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions.","Foreign investment can transfer capital, jobs and know-how across borders.","More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate.","The services balance is an $8 billion surplus."],"answer":2,"why":"More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0205","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country exports $75 billion of goods and imports $92 billion. What is its trade-in-goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$92 billion deficit","$17 billion deficit","$17 billion surplus","$167 billion surplus"],"answer":1,"why":"Goods balance = exports − imports = 75 − 92 = -17 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0206","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to make overseas investment rules more transparent. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Firms may sell services into new markets.","Large outflows could weaken the domestic currency.","New firms may develop production experience.","Consumers may gain greater variety."],"answer":1,"why":"Large outflows could weaken the domestic currency."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0207","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to make overseas investment rules more transparent?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Meeting foreign standards may be costly.","Overfishing could damage future productive resources.","Diversification may reduce some existing scale advantages.","Greater capital mobility may increase diversification opportunities."],"answer":3,"why":"Greater capital mobility may increase diversification opportunities."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0208","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to make overseas investment rules more transparent less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Domestic firms may obtain cheaper imported components.","Supplier firms may win new contracts.","Suppliers may benefit from shared infrastructure.","Funds invested abroad may reduce investment at home."],"answer":3,"why":"Funds invested abroad may reduce investment at home."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0209","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to make overseas investment rules more transparent. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More investment income may remain with local residents.","Consumers may pay lower prices.","Firms may sell services into new markets.","Investors may make better informed decisions."],"answer":3,"why":"Investors may make better informed decisions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0210","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Domestic investors exchange local money for foreign currency to purchase overseas shares. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports.","More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate.","Higher import costs can protect local suppliers while raising consumer prices.","The services balance is an $8 billion surplus."],"answer":1,"why":"More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0211","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes exchange-rate speculation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Currency speculation is trading currencies based on expectations of future exchange-rate movements.","The current account records trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income transactions.","International specialisation occurs when a country concentrates resources on goods it can produce relatively efficiently.","The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports."],"answer":0,"why":"Exchange-rate speculation: Currency speculation is trading currencies based on expectations of future exchange-rate movements."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0212","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Traders buy a currency after predicting that it will strengthen next month. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Producer gains from trade","Exchange-rate speculation","Current account","Tariff"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates exchange-rate speculation: Currency speculation is trading currencies based on expectations of future exchange-rate movements."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0213","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country's balances are: goods +$14bn, services +$5bn, primary income −$3bn and secondary income +$3bn. Calculate the current-account balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$22 billion surplus","$19 billion deficit","$19 billion surplus","$25 billion surplus"],"answer":2,"why":"Current account = 14 + 5 − 3 + 3 = +$19 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0214","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to exchange-rate speculation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices.","Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions.","Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates.","Combining these flows produces the current-account balance."],"answer":2,"why":"Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0215","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Data can arrive with reporting delays.","Profits may be remitted to the firm's home country.","Businesses may understand currency risks better.","Extra domestic demand can reduce the surplus."],"answer":2,"why":"Businesses may understand currency risks better."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0216","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Speculative flows can still cause sharp fluctuations.","Exporters may reach more customers.","Buyers may gain more choice and lower prices.","Local workers may gain employment and training."],"answer":0,"why":"Speculative flows can still cause sharp fluctuations."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0217","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Import dependence may increase in strategic product categories.","Foreign suppliers may respond with trade restrictions.","Reliable information may reduce some rumours.","A headline balance does not capture all economic conditions."],"answer":2,"why":"Reliable information may reduce some rumours."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0218","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Local firms may become more efficient to compete.","Suppliers may benefit from shared infrastructure.","Not all future political or economic events are predictable.","Domestic capital formation may rise."],"answer":2,"why":"Not all future political or economic events are predictable."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0219","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Some domestic shoe producers may sell more.","Service export receipts may increase.","Businesses may understand currency risks better.","Recipient households may receive more of each transfer."],"answer":2,"why":"Businesses may understand currency risks better."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0220","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Traders buy a currency after predicting that it will strengthen next month. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates.","Repeated production can improve skills and reduce unit costs.","Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports.","Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade."],"answer":0,"why":"Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0221","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"One home dollar previously bought 107 foreign cents and now buys 127 foreign cents. What has happened to the home currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Its purchasing power in foreign currency has fallen.","Its value has remained unchanged.","It has depreciated.","It has appreciated."],"answer":3,"why":"It now buys more foreign currency (127 rather than 107 foreign cents), so it has appreciated."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0222","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A weaker currency raises local-currency costs of imported wheat and diesel. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Currency depreciation","Lower transport costs","Imported inflation","Current account"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates imported inflation: Depreciation can raise domestic prices when imported inputs or consumer goods cost more."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0223","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of imported inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Imported sports shoes enter a market previously served by only two local brands.","Residents of a cold country buy affordable imported tropical fruit.","A weaker currency raises local-currency costs of imported wheat and diesel.","A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product."],"answer":2,"why":"A weaker currency raises local-currency costs of imported wheat and diesel. This illustrates depreciation can raise domestic prices when imported inputs or consumer goods cost more."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0224","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to imported inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The goods balance is a $15 billion deficit.","Higher input costs may be passed to households as inflation.","A fall in that mineral's world price can sharply reduce foreign earnings.","Higher import costs can protect local suppliers while raising consumer prices."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher input costs may be passed to households as inflation."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0225","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Global disruptions can interrupt supply chains.","Future import-cost shocks may be smaller.","Developing new industries costs money.","Profits may be remitted to the firm's home country."],"answer":1,"why":"Future import-cost shocks may be smaller."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0226","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Exporters may reach more customers.","Developing local energy supplies is expensive.","More investment income may remain with local residents.","Sales and revenue may increase."],"answer":1,"why":"Developing local energy supplies is expensive."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0227","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Global demand may remain weak.","Domestic energy industries may gain investment.","Diversification may reduce some existing scale advantages.","Trading partners may retaliate with tariffs of their own."],"answer":1,"why":"Domestic energy industries may gain investment."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0228","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Some energy resources cannot be produced domestically.","Employment in protected firms may be preserved.","Importing productive machinery may improve efficiency.","Firms may understand export and import trends."],"answer":0,"why":"Some energy resources cannot be produced domestically."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0229","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Some domestic shoe producers may sell more.","Future import-cost shocks may be smaller.","Firms may access specialised inputs.","Export demand may increase."],"answer":1,"why":"Future import-cost shocks may be smaller."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0230","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The exchange rate is 1 home dollar = 6 foreign units. A machine costs 240 foreign units. How many home dollars are needed, ignoring fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$40","$100","$1440","$240"],"answer":0,"why":"Home-dollar cost = 240 foreign units / 6 = $40."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0231","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes current account?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Appreciation is a rise in the value of a currency under a floating exchange-rate system.","The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports.","Depreciation is a fall in a currency's value under a floating exchange-rate system.","The current account records trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income transactions."],"answer":3,"why":"Current account: The current account records trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income transactions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0232","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country earns export receipts but also pays for imports and income transfers abroad. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Import dependence","Tariff","Current account","Producer gains from trade"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates current account: The current account records trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income transactions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0233","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of current account?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A country earns export receipts but also pays for imports and income transfers abroad.","A country develops clusters of skilled bicycle-component manufacturers.","A country imposes a 15% tariff on imported ceramic tiles.","A weaker currency raises local-currency costs of imported wheat and diesel."],"answer":0,"why":"A country earns export receipts but also pays for imports and income transfers abroad. This illustrates the current account records trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income transactions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0234","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to current account?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports.","Foreign investment can transfer capital, jobs and know-how across borders.","Combining these flows produces the current-account balance.","Higher import costs can protect local suppliers while raising consumer prices."],"answer":2,"why":"Combining these flows produces the current-account balance."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0235","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to track the components of the current account separately. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Exporters may lose price competitiveness abroad.","Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance.","Consumers may face less choice and higher prices.","Port upgrades require significant public or private funds."],"answer":1,"why":"Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0236","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to track the components of the current account separately. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Exporters may reach more customers.","Consumers may gain greater variety.","Future import-cost shocks may be smaller.","Data can arrive with reporting delays."],"answer":3,"why":"Data can arrive with reporting delays."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0237","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to track the components of the current account separately?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Firms may understand export and import trends.","Long-distance transport may increase emissions.","Some communities may remain digitally excluded.","Transaction fees may reduce the amount received."],"answer":0,"why":"Firms may understand export and import trends."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0238","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country exports $95 billion of goods and imports $68 billion. What is its trade-in-goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$163 billion surplus","$68 billion deficit","$27 billion deficit","$27 billion surplus"],"answer":3,"why":"Goods balance = exports − imports = 95 − 68 = 27 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0239","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to track the components of the current account separately. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance.","Consumers may pay lower prices.","Exporters may reach more customers.","Domestic tile makers may gain market share."],"answer":0,"why":"Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0240","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country earns export receipts but also pays for imports and income transfers abroad. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate.","Combining these flows produces the current-account balance.","Lower transport costs improve the competitiveness of distant suppliers.","Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices."],"answer":1,"why":"Combining these flows produces the current-account balance."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0241","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes trade in goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Primary income includes cross-border earnings from work and investments, such as interest, profits and dividends.","Cheaper and faster communication can support international coordination of trade and investment.","The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports.","Heavy concentration on one industry can expose an economy to price and demand shocks."],"answer":2,"why":"Trade in goods balance: The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0242","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: An economy exports $50 billion of merchandise and imports $65 billion. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Embargo","Globalisation","Current-account surplus","Trade in goods balance"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates trade in goods balance: The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0243","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of trade in goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Domestic investors exchange local money for foreign currency to purchase overseas shares.","One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.90 to buying 0.75 foreign units.","An economy exports $50 billion of merchandise and imports $65 billion.","A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product."],"answer":2,"why":"An economy exports $50 billion of merchandise and imports $65 billion. This illustrates the goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0244","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to trade in goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A fall in that mineral's world price can sharply reduce foreign earnings.","The goods balance is a $15 billion deficit.","More rival suppliers can push domestic firms to improve price and quality.","More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate."],"answer":1,"why":"The goods balance is a $15 billion deficit."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0245","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve the quality of domestically produced export goods. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Domestic farmers may face more competition.","Export demand may increase.","Exporters may lose price competitiveness abroad.","Port upgrades require significant public or private funds."],"answer":1,"why":"Export demand may increase."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0246","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country's balances are: goods +$22bn, services +$3bn, primary income −$2bn and secondary income +$3bn. Calculate the current-account balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$26 billion surplus","$30 billion surplus","$25 billion surplus","$26 billion deficit"],"answer":0,"why":"Current account = 22 + 3 − 2 + 3 = +$26 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0247","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve the quality of domestically produced export goods?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Transaction fees may reduce the amount received.","Export demand may respond slowly if contracts are fixed.","Domestic manufacturing employment may expand.","Funds invested abroad may reduce investment at home."],"answer":2,"why":"Domestic manufacturing employment may expand."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0248","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to improve the quality of domestically produced export goods less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Export-oriented firms may increase output and jobs.","Exporters may plan receipts and payments more accurately.","Domestic manufacturing jobs may expand.","Global demand may remain weak."],"answer":3,"why":"Global demand may remain weak."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0249","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve the quality of domestically produced export goods. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Businesses may understand currency risks better.","Export demand may increase.","Buyers may gain more choice and lower prices.","Shortages may become less likely."],"answer":1,"why":"Export demand may increase."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0250","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: An economy exports $50 billion of merchandise and imports $65 billion. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices.","The goods balance is a $15 billion deficit.","A fall in that mineral's world price can sharply reduce foreign earnings.","The stronger currency makes imports cheaper in domestic money but exports dearer abroad."],"answer":1,"why":"The goods balance is a $15 billion deficit."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0251","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes trade in services balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Depreciation can raise domestic prices when imported inputs or consumer goods cost more.","The services balance is the value of services exports minus services imports.","Specialisation can enable economies of scale and higher labour productivity.","Free trade means international exchange with few government-imposed trade barriers."],"answer":1,"why":"Trade in services balance: The services balance is the value of services exports minus services imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0252","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Trade in services balance","Producer gains from trade","Demand for currency","Globalisation"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates trade in services balance: The services balance is the value of services exports minus services imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0253","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of trade in services balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services.","A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries.","A country develops clusters of skilled bicycle-component manufacturers.","A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product."],"answer":0,"why":"A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services. This illustrates the services balance is the value of services exports minus services imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0254","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to trade in services balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher import costs can protect local suppliers while raising consumer prices.","The services balance is an $8 billion surplus.","Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal.","Larger potential markets can support higher output and scale economies."],"answer":1,"why":"The services balance is an $8 billion surplus."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0255","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"One home dollar previously bought 111 foreign cents and now buys 126 foreign cents. What has happened to the home currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["It has depreciated.","It has appreciated.","Its purchasing power in foreign currency has fallen.","Its value has remained unchanged."],"answer":1,"why":"It now buys more foreign currency (126 rather than 111 foreign cents), so it has appreciated."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0256","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to develop skills needed to export digital services. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Training costs money and takes time.","New firms may develop production experience.","Consumers may pay lower prices.","Sales and revenue may increase."],"answer":0,"why":"Training costs money and takes time."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0257","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to develop skills needed to export digital services?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Global shortages can affect all source countries.","Skilled employment could expand.","Not all future political or economic events are predictable.","Long-distance transport may increase emissions."],"answer":1,"why":"Skilled employment could expand."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0258","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to develop skills needed to export digital services less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Employment in protected firms may be preserved.","Government may collect customs revenue.","Foreign competition may limit prices charged.","Workers may gain employment in export-oriented industries."],"answer":2,"why":"Foreign competition may limit prices charged."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0259","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to develop skills needed to export digital services. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Investment equipment may cost less.","Exports may become more price competitive abroad.","Consumers may pay lower prices.","Service export receipts may increase."],"answer":3,"why":"Service export receipts may increase."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0260","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports.","The services balance is an $8 billion surplus.","Higher input costs may be passed to households as inflation.","Temporary protection may provide time to develop skills and scale."],"answer":1,"why":"The services balance is an $8 billion surplus."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0261","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes primary income balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Primary income includes cross-border earnings from work and investments, such as interest, profits and dividends.","Cheaper and faster communication can support international coordination of trade and investment.","Foreign demand for a country's currency can arise from its exports and investment opportunities.","A tariff is a tax on imported goods that raises their cost to importers."],"answer":0,"why":"Primary income balance: Primary income includes cross-border earnings from work and investments, such as interest, profits and dividends."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0262","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Foreign-owned firms send dividends overseas while residents receive interest from foreign investments. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Currency appreciation","Lower transport costs","Demand for currency","Primary income balance"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates primary income balance: Primary income includes cross-border earnings from work and investments, such as interest, profits and dividends."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0263","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The exchange rate is 1 home dollar = 4 foreign units. A machine costs 320 foreign units. How many home dollars are needed, ignoring fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$1280","$320","$80","$120"],"answer":2,"why":"Home-dollar cost = 320 foreign units / 4 = $80."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0264","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to primary income balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Larger potential markets can support higher output and scale economies.","Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods.","Temporary protection may provide time to develop skills and scale.","Restricting imported supply can support local firms but reduce competition."],"answer":1,"why":"Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0265","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More investment income may remain with local residents.","Speculative flows can still cause sharp fluctuations.","Imported fuel and machinery may become more expensive.","Multiple supplier contracts may be more expensive."],"answer":0,"why":"More investment income may remain with local residents."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0266","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Exporters may reach more customers.","Firms may gain experience and lower average costs.","Domestic savings may be insufficient for large projects.","Future import-cost shocks may be smaller."],"answer":2,"why":"Domestic savings may be insufficient for large projects."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0267","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Transaction fees may reduce the amount received.","Foreign suppliers may respond with trade restrictions.","Domestic capital formation may rise.","Regions may become vulnerable to one industry's downturn."],"answer":2,"why":"Domestic capital formation may rise."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0268","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Local firms may become more efficient to compete.","Lower prices may improve real purchasing power.","Higher volumes may allow more efficient production.","Foreign direct investment can bring expertise that local finance lacks."],"answer":3,"why":"Foreign direct investment can bring expertise that local finance lacks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0269","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Firms may access specialised inputs.","More investment income may remain with local residents.","Consumers may gain greater variety.","Domestic environmental risks may decrease."],"answer":1,"why":"More investment income may remain with local residents."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0270","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Foreign-owned firms send dividends overseas while residents receive interest from foreign investments. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Lower transport costs improve the competitiveness of distant suppliers.","Higher input costs may be passed to households as inflation.","Repeated production can improve skills and reduce unit costs.","Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods."],"answer":3,"why":"Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0271","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country exports $65 billion of goods and imports $100 billion. What is its trade-in-goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$100 billion deficit","$165 billion surplus","$35 billion deficit","$35 billion surplus"],"answer":2,"why":"Goods balance = exports − imports = 65 − 100 = -35 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0272","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Citizens working abroad regularly send money home to their families. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Import quota","Import dependence","Secondary income balance","Lower transport costs"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates secondary income balance: Secondary income records one-way current transfers, such as remittances and some international aid."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0273","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of secondary income balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Traders buy a currency after predicting that it will strengthen next month.","Citizens working abroad regularly send money home to their families.","One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.80 to buying 0.90 foreign units.","A phone designed in one country is assembled using components made in several others."],"answer":1,"why":"Citizens working abroad regularly send money home to their families. This illustrates secondary income records one-way current transfers, such as remittances and some international aid."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0274","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to secondary income balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Restricting imported supply can support local firms but reduce competition.","Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts.","Higher input costs may be passed to households as inflation.","Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates."],"answer":1,"why":"Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0275","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Imported fuel and machinery may become more expensive.","Some domestic workers may lose jobs.","Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","Exporters may lose price competitiveness abroad."],"answer":2,"why":"Recipient households may receive more of each transfer."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0276","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Travellers can compare currency conversion costs.","Firms may gain experience and lower average costs.","Consumers may pay lower prices.","Payment services require compliance and monitoring."],"answer":3,"why":"Payment services require compliance and monitoring."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0277","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More funds may enter regulated financial channels.","Expanded shipping may increase local pollution.","The plant may relocate if costs rise elsewhere.","Transport-related emissions may increase."],"answer":0,"why":"More funds may enter regulated financial channels."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0278","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Remittance income may fall if overseas employment weakens.","Higher volumes may allow more efficient production.","Reliable information may reduce some rumours.","New activities may create additional jobs."],"answer":0,"why":"Remittance income may fall if overseas employment weakens."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0279","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Some domestic shoe producers may sell more.","Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","Businesses may understand currency risks better.","Investors may make better informed decisions."],"answer":1,"why":"Recipient households may receive more of each transfer."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0280","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country's balances are: goods +$14bn, services +$7bn, primary income −$6bn and secondary income +$3bn. Calculate the current-account balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$18 billion surplus","$21 billion surplus","$18 billion deficit","$30 billion surplus"],"answer":0,"why":"Current account = 14 + 7 − 6 + 3 = +$18 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0281","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes current-account deficit?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Depreciation can raise domestic prices when imported inputs or consumer goods cost more.","Heavy concentration on one industry can expose an economy to price and demand shocks.","Foreign demand for a country's currency can arise from its exports and investment opportunities.","A current-account deficit occurs when total current-account payments exceed total receipts."],"answer":3,"why":"Current-account deficit: A current-account deficit occurs when total current-account payments exceed total receipts."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0282","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Current-account deficit","Currency depreciation","Primary income balance","Exchange-rate speculation"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates current-account deficit: A current-account deficit occurs when total current-account payments exceed total receipts."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0283","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of current-account deficit?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A phone designed in one country is assembled using components made in several others.","A coastal country exports fish products while importing machines it cannot make cheaply.","Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts.","A country imports almost all its fertiliser from a single foreign supplier."],"answer":2,"why":"Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts. This illustrates a current-account deficit occurs when total current-account payments exceed total receipts."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0284","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to current-account deficit?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports.","Banning trade can prevent specified imports but may disrupt supply.","A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness.","Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices."],"answer":2,"why":"A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0285","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve export competitiveness rather than imposing broad import bans. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Poor cybersecurity could disrupt business.","Export earnings may rise over time.","Developing local energy supplies is expensive.","Households and builders may face higher prices."],"answer":1,"why":"Export earnings may rise over time."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0286","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve export competitiveness rather than imposing broad import bans. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Investment equipment may cost less.","Investors may make better informed decisions.","Sales and revenue may increase.","Training and investment take time."],"answer":3,"why":"Training and investment take time."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0287","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve export competitiveness rather than imposing broad import bans?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Competitive firms may develop sustainable overseas markets.","Trading partners may retaliate with tariffs of their own.","Overfishing could damage future productive resources.","Regions may become vulnerable to one industry's downturn."],"answer":0,"why":"Competitive firms may develop sustainable overseas markets."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0288","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"One home dollar previously bought 115 foreign cents and now buys 125 foreign cents. What has happened to the home currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Its purchasing power in foreign currency has fallen.","Its value has remained unchanged.","It has depreciated.","It has appreciated."],"answer":3,"why":"It now buys more foreign currency (125 rather than 115 foreign cents), so it has appreciated."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0289","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve export competitiveness rather than imposing broad import bans. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock.","Firms may sell services into new markets.","Consumers may pay lower prices.","Export earnings may rise over time."],"answer":3,"why":"Export earnings may rise over time."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0290","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates.","The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions.","Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports.","A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness."],"answer":3,"why":"A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0291","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes current-account surplus?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A multinational company owns or controls productive operations in more than one country.","Depreciation can raise domestic prices when imported inputs or consumer goods cost more.","A current-account surplus occurs when current-account receipts exceed payments.","International trade can give households access to goods they could not buy domestically."],"answer":2,"why":"Current-account surplus: A current-account surplus occurs when current-account receipts exceed payments."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0292","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country earns more from exports and income flows than it spends on imports and transfers. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Current-account deficit","Trade in services balance","Trade in goods balance","Current-account surplus"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates current-account surplus: A current-account surplus occurs when current-account receipts exceed payments."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0293","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of current-account surplus?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A country earns more from exports and income flows than it spends on imports and transfers.","A new container terminal reduces the cost of exporting refrigerated fruit.","A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers.","A weaker currency raises local-currency costs of imported wheat and diesel."],"answer":0,"why":"A country earns more from exports and income flows than it spends on imports and transfers. This illustrates a current-account surplus occurs when current-account receipts exceed payments."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0294","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to current-account surplus?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal.","Restricting imported supply can support local firms but reduce competition.","A surplus can reflect strong exports or restrained domestic spending and investment.","The stronger currency makes imports cheaper in domestic money but exports dearer abroad."],"answer":2,"why":"A surplus can reflect strong exports or restrained domestic spending and investment."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0295","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest some surplus earnings in productive domestic capacity. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Future output and living standards may increase.","Exchange-rate changes can reduce export earnings.","Poor cybersecurity could disrupt business.","Domestic farmers may face more competition."],"answer":0,"why":"Future output and living standards may increase."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0296","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The exchange rate is 1 home dollar = 2 foreign units. A machine costs 60 foreign units. How many home dollars are needed, ignoring fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$60","$30","$120","$50"],"answer":1,"why":"Home-dollar cost = 60 foreign units / 2 = $30."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0297","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to invest some surplus earnings in productive domestic capacity?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Foreign suppliers may respond with trade restrictions.","Future depreciation could reverse the cost saving.","Importing productive machinery may improve efficiency.","Regions may become vulnerable to one industry's downturn."],"answer":2,"why":"Importing productive machinery may improve efficiency."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0298","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to invest some surplus earnings in productive domestic capacity less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Exporting firms may support additional jobs.","Supplier firms may win new contracts.","Exporters may plan receipts and payments more accurately.","Poorly selected projects can waste the earnings."],"answer":3,"why":"Poorly selected projects can waste the earnings."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0299","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to invest some surplus earnings in productive domestic capacity. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Future output and living standards may increase.","Travellers can compare currency conversion costs.","Businesses may understand currency risks better.","Firms may sell services into new markets."],"answer":0,"why":"Future output and living standards may increase."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0300","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country earns more from exports and income flows than it spends on imports and transfers. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions.","Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates.","Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders.","A surplus can reflect strong exports or restrained domestic spending and investment."],"answer":3,"why":"A surplus can reflect strong exports or restrained domestic spending and investment."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0001","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A coastal country exports fish products while importing machines it cannot make cheaply. Decision-makers are considering whether to specialise in sustainable seafood processing for export. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by international specialisation. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — International specialisation occurs when a country concentrates resources on goods it can produce relatively efficiently.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A coastal country exports fish products while importing machines it cannot make cheaply.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0002","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A country develops clusters of skilled bicycle-component manufacturers. Decision-makers are considering whether to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by benefits of specialisation. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Specialisation can enable economies of scale and higher labour productivity.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A country develops clusters of skilled bicycle-component manufacturers.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0003","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A country relies on one mineral for most of its export receipts. Decision-makers are considering whether to diversify export production away from one mineral. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by risks of specialisation. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Heavy concentration on one industry can expose an economy to price and demand shocks.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A country relies on one mineral for most of its export receipts.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0004","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Two neighbouring countries remove import tariffs on most manufactured goods. Decision-makers are considering whether to remove tariffs on common household appliances. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by free trade. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Free trade means international exchange with few government-imposed trade barriers.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Two neighbouring countries remove import tariffs on most manufactured goods.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0005","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Residents of a cold country buy affordable imported tropical fruit. Decision-makers are considering whether to allow more competition from imported food products. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by consumer gains from trade. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — International trade can give households access to goods they could not buy domestically.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Residents of a cold country buy affordable imported tropical fruit.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0006","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A country imports almost all its fertiliser from a single foreign supplier. Decision-makers are considering whether to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by import dependence. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Relying heavily on imported essentials can make supply vulnerable to overseas disruptions.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A country imports almost all its fertiliser from a single foreign supplier.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0007","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Imported sports shoes enter a market previously served by only two local brands. Decision-makers are considering whether to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by trade and competition. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Open international markets can increase competitive pressure on domestic firms.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Imported sports shoes enter a market previously served by only two local brands.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0008","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A phone designed in one country is assembled using components made in several others. Decision-makers are considering whether to participate in international production networks. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by globalisation. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Globalisation is the growing economic interconnection and interdependence of countries.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A phone designed in one country is assembled using components made in several others.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0009","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A new container terminal reduces the cost of exporting refrigerated fruit. Decision-makers are considering whether to modernise ports to reduce international shipping delays. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by lower transport costs. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Falling shipping costs can make trading goods across borders more affordable.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A new container terminal reduces the cost of exporting refrigerated fruit.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0010","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand reliable broadband for exporting service firms. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by communication technology. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Cheaper and faster communication can support international coordination of trade and investment.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0011","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A country imposes a 15% tariff on imported ceramic tiles. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by tariff. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A tariff is a tax on imported goods that raises their cost to importers.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A country imposes a 15% tariff on imported ceramic tiles.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0012","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A government permits no more than 40,000 imported shoes each year. Decision-makers are considering whether to cap annual imports of low-cost footwear. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by import quota. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — An import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A government permits no more than 40,000 imported shoes each year.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0013","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product. Decision-makers are considering whether to ban imports of a product associated with severe environmental harm. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by embargo. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — An embargo is an official prohibition on trade with a particular country or in a particular product.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0014","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers. Decision-makers are considering whether to protect a new solar-equipment industry for a limited period. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by infant industry protection. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Infant industry protection temporarily shields new domestic firms from established foreign competitors.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0015","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"An exchange booth lists one domestic dollar as worth 0.80 foreign units. Decision-makers are considering whether to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by exchange rate. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — An exchange rate is the price of one currency expressed in another currency.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — An exchange booth lists one domestic dollar as worth 0.80 foreign units.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0016","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.90 to buying 0.75 foreign units. Decision-makers are considering whether to promote exports after a currency depreciation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by currency depreciation. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Depreciation is a fall in a currency's value under a floating exchange-rate system.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.90 to buying 0.75 foreign units.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0017","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve export quality to attract overseas buyers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by demand for currency. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Foreign demand for a country's currency can arise from its exports and investment opportunities.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0018","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Domestic investors exchange local money for foreign currency to purchase overseas shares. Decision-makers are considering whether to make overseas investment rules more transparent. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by supply of currency. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — People supply domestic currency on foreign-exchange markets when buying imports or overseas assets.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Domestic investors exchange local money for foreign currency to purchase overseas shares.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0019","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Traders buy a currency after predicting that it will strengthen next month. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by exchange-rate speculation. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Currency speculation is trading currencies based on expectations of future exchange-rate movements.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Traders buy a currency after predicting that it will strengthen next month.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0020","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A weaker currency raises local-currency costs of imported wheat and diesel. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by imported inflation. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Depreciation can raise domestic prices when imported inputs or consumer goods cost more.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A weaker currency raises local-currency costs of imported wheat and diesel.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0021","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"An economy exports $50 billion of merchandise and imports $65 billion. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve the quality of domestically produced export goods. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by trade in goods balance. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — An economy exports $50 billion of merchandise and imports $65 billion.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0022","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services. Decision-makers are considering whether to develop skills needed to export digital services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by trade in services balance. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — The services balance is the value of services exports minus services imports.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0023","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Foreign-owned firms send dividends overseas while residents receive interest from foreign investments. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by primary income balance. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Primary income includes cross-border earnings from work and investments, such as interest, profits and dividends.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Foreign-owned firms send dividends overseas while residents receive interest from foreign investments.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0024","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Citizens working abroad regularly send money home to their families. Decision-makers are considering whether to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by secondary income balance. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — Secondary income records one-way current transfers, such as remittances and some international aid.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Citizens working abroad regularly send money home to their families.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0025","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve export competitiveness rather than imposing broad import bans. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Using the information above, explain what is meant by current-account deficit. [2]","scheme":"1. 1 mark: accurate meaning — A current-account deficit occurs when total current-account payments exceed total receipts.\n  2. 1 mark: appropriate use of the case — Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts.","note":"Do not award a second mark for merely repeating the definition; require a meaningful case link."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0001","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A coastal country exports fish products while importing machines it cannot make cheaply. Decision-makers are considering whether to specialise in sustainable seafood processing for export. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to specialise in sustainable seafood processing for export. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Firms may gain experience and lower average costs. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Export earnings could finance needed imports. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0002","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country develops clusters of skilled bicycle-component manufacturers. Decision-makers are considering whether to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Specialist workers may become more productive. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Suppliers may benefit from shared infrastructure. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0003","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country relies on one mineral for most of its export receipts. Decision-makers are considering whether to diversify export production away from one mineral. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to diversify export production away from one mineral. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: New activities may create additional jobs. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0004","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Two neighbouring countries remove import tariffs on most manufactured goods. Decision-makers are considering whether to remove tariffs on common household appliances. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers remove tariffs on common household appliances. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Consumers may pay lower prices. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Domestic firms may obtain cheaper imported components. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0005","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Residents of a cold country buy affordable imported tropical fruit. Decision-makers are considering whether to allow more competition from imported food products. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to allow more competition from imported food products could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Consumers may gain greater variety. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Lower prices may improve real purchasing power. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0006","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries. Decision-makers are considering whether to help small manufacturers access export markets. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to help small manufacturers access export markets. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Sales and revenue may increase. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Higher volumes may allow more efficient production. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0007","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country imports almost all its fertiliser from a single foreign supplier. Decision-makers are considering whether to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Shortages may become less likely. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Competition between suppliers may reduce reliance on one firm. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0008","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Imported sports shoes enter a market previously served by only two local brands. Decision-makers are considering whether to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Buyers may gain more choice and lower prices. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Local firms may become more efficient to compete. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0009","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A phone designed in one country is assembled using components made in several others. Decision-makers are considering whether to participate in international production networks. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers participate in international production networks. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Firms may access specialised inputs. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Workers may gain employment in export-oriented industries. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0010","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A new container terminal reduces the cost of exporting refrigerated fruit. Decision-makers are considering whether to modernise ports to reduce international shipping delays. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to modernise ports to reduce international shipping delays could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Exporters may reach more customers. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Imported production inputs may become cheaper. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0011","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand reliable broadband for exporting service firms. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to expand reliable broadband for exporting service firms. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Firms may sell services into new markets. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: International coordination may become more efficient. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0012","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"An international electronics firm opens an assembly plant in another country. Decision-makers are considering whether to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Local workers may gain employment and training. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Supplier firms may win new contracts. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0013","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country imposes a 15% tariff on imported ceramic tiles. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Domestic tile makers may gain market share. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Government may collect customs revenue. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0014","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A government permits no more than 40,000 imported shoes each year. Decision-makers are considering whether to cap annual imports of low-cost footwear. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers cap annual imports of low-cost footwear. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Some domestic shoe producers may sell more. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Employment in protected firms may be preserved. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0015","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product. Decision-makers are considering whether to ban imports of a product associated with severe environmental harm. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to ban imports of a product associated with severe environmental harm could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Domestic environmental risks may decrease. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Firms may develop safer alternative products. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0016","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers. Decision-makers are considering whether to protect a new solar-equipment industry for a limited period. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to protect a new solar-equipment industry for a limited period. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: New firms may develop production experience. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Domestic manufacturing jobs may expand. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0017","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"An exchange booth lists one domestic dollar as worth 0.80 foreign units. Decision-makers are considering whether to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Travellers can compare currency conversion costs. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Exporters may plan receipts and payments more accurately. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0018","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.80 to buying 0.90 foreign units. Decision-makers are considering whether to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Investment equipment may cost less. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Imported inputs may become more affordable. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0019","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.90 to buying 0.75 foreign units. Decision-makers are considering whether to promote exports after a currency depreciation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers promote exports after a currency depreciation. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Exports may become more price competitive abroad. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Export-oriented firms may increase output and jobs. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0020","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve export quality to attract overseas buyers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to improve export quality to attract overseas buyers could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Higher export sales may earn foreign currency. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Exporting firms may support additional jobs. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0021","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Domestic investors exchange local money for foreign currency to purchase overseas shares. Decision-makers are considering whether to make overseas investment rules more transparent. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to make overseas investment rules more transparent. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Investors may make better informed decisions. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Greater capital mobility may increase diversification opportunities. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0022","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Traders buy a currency after predicting that it will strengthen next month. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Businesses may understand currency risks better. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Reliable information may reduce some rumours. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0023","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A weaker currency raises local-currency costs of imported wheat and diesel. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Future import-cost shocks may be smaller. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Domestic energy industries may gain investment. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0024","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country earns export receipts but also pays for imports and income transfers abroad. Decision-makers are considering whether to track the components of the current account separately. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers track the components of the current account separately. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Firms may understand export and import trends. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0025","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"An economy exports $50 billion of merchandise and imports $65 billion. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve the quality of domestically produced export goods. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to improve the quality of domestically produced export goods could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Export demand may increase. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Domestic manufacturing employment may expand. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0026","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services. Decision-makers are considering whether to develop skills needed to export digital services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to develop skills needed to export digital services. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Service export receipts may increase. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Skilled employment could expand. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0027","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Foreign-owned firms send dividends overseas while residents receive interest from foreign investments. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: More investment income may remain with local residents. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Domestic capital formation may rise. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0028","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Citizens working abroad regularly send money home to their families. Decision-makers are considering whether to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Recipient households may receive more of each transfer. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: More funds may enter regulated financial channels. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0029","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve export competitiveness rather than imposing broad import bans. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers improve export competitiveness rather than imposing broad import bans. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Export earnings may rise over time. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Competitive firms may develop sustainable overseas markets. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0030","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"A country earns more from exports and income flows than it spends on imports and transfers. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest some surplus earnings in productive domestic capacity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to invest some surplus earnings in productive domestic capacity could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"1. Point A, up to 2 marks: Future output and living standards may increase. Award 1 for identifying the benefit and 1 for showing an appropriate economic link or consequence.\n  2. Point B, up to 2 marks: Importing productive machinery may improve efficiency. Award 1 for identifying the distinct benefit and 1 for explaining the causal link.","note":"Maximum 2 marks per distinct explained benefit; do not double-credit the same chain."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0001","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A coastal country exports fish products while importing machines it cannot make cheaply. Decision-makers are considering whether to specialise in sustainable seafood processing for export. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to specialise in sustainable seafood processing for export. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Firms may gain experience and lower average costs. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Export earnings could finance needed imports. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0002","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country develops clusters of skilled bicycle-component manufacturers. Decision-makers are considering whether to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Specialist workers may become more productive. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Suppliers may benefit from shared infrastructure. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Repeated production can improve skills and reduce unit costs. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0003","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country relies on one mineral for most of its export receipts. Decision-makers are considering whether to diversify export production away from one mineral. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to diversify export production away from one mineral could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: New activities may create additional jobs. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: A fall in that mineral's world price can sharply reduce foreign earnings. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0004","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Two neighbouring countries remove import tariffs on most manufactured goods. Decision-makers are considering whether to remove tariffs on common household appliances. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to remove tariffs on common household appliances in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Consumers may pay lower prices. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Domestic firms may obtain cheaper imported components. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0005","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Residents of a cold country buy affordable imported tropical fruit. Decision-makers are considering whether to allow more competition from imported food products. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to allow more competition from imported food products could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Consumers may gain greater variety. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Lower prices may improve real purchasing power. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Imports enlarge product choice and may increase consumer welfare. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0006","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries. Decision-makers are considering whether to help small manufacturers access export markets. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to help small manufacturers access export markets. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Sales and revenue may increase. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Higher volumes may allow more efficient production. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Larger potential markets can support higher output and scale economies. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0007","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country imports almost all its fertiliser from a single foreign supplier. Decision-makers are considering whether to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Shortages may become less likely. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Competition between suppliers may reduce reliance on one firm. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0008","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Imported sports shoes enter a market previously served by only two local brands. Decision-makers are considering whether to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Buyers may gain more choice and lower prices. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Local firms may become more efficient to compete. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: More rival suppliers can push domestic firms to improve price and quality. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0009","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A phone designed in one country is assembled using components made in several others. Decision-makers are considering whether to participate in international production networks. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to participate in international production networks in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Firms may access specialised inputs. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Workers may gain employment in export-oriented industries. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0010","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A new container terminal reduces the cost of exporting refrigerated fruit. Decision-makers are considering whether to modernise ports to reduce international shipping delays. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to modernise ports to reduce international shipping delays could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Exporters may reach more customers. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Imported production inputs may become cheaper. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Lower transport costs improve the competitiveness of distant suppliers. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0011","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand reliable broadband for exporting service firms. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to expand reliable broadband for exporting service firms. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Firms may sell services into new markets. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: International coordination may become more efficient. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0012","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"An international electronics firm opens an assembly plant in another country. Decision-makers are considering whether to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Local workers may gain employment and training. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Supplier firms may win new contracts. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Foreign investment can transfer capital, jobs and know-how across borders. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0013","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country imposes a 15% tariff on imported ceramic tiles. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Domestic tile makers may gain market share. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Government may collect customs revenue. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Higher import costs can protect local suppliers while raising consumer prices. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0014","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A government permits no more than 40,000 imported shoes each year. Decision-makers are considering whether to cap annual imports of low-cost footwear. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to cap annual imports of low-cost footwear in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Some domestic shoe producers may sell more. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Employment in protected firms may be preserved. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Restricting imported supply can support local firms but reduce competition. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0015","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product. Decision-makers are considering whether to ban imports of a product associated with severe environmental harm. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to ban imports of a product associated with severe environmental harm could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Domestic environmental risks may decrease. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Firms may develop safer alternative products. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Banning trade can prevent specified imports but may disrupt supply. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0016","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers. Decision-makers are considering whether to protect a new solar-equipment industry for a limited period. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to protect a new solar-equipment industry for a limited period. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: New firms may develop production experience. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Domestic manufacturing jobs may expand. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Temporary protection may provide time to develop skills and scale. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0017","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"An exchange booth lists one domestic dollar as worth 0.80 foreign units. Decision-makers are considering whether to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Travellers can compare currency conversion costs. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Exporters may plan receipts and payments more accurately. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0018","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.80 to buying 0.90 foreign units. Decision-makers are considering whether to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Investment equipment may cost less. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Imported inputs may become more affordable. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The stronger currency makes imports cheaper in domestic money but exports dearer abroad. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0019","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.90 to buying 0.75 foreign units. Decision-makers are considering whether to promote exports after a currency depreciation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to promote exports after a currency depreciation in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Exports may become more price competitive abroad. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Export-oriented firms may increase output and jobs. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0020","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve export quality to attract overseas buyers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to improve export quality to attract overseas buyers could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Higher export sales may earn foreign currency. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Exporting firms may support additional jobs. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0021","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Domestic investors exchange local money for foreign currency to purchase overseas shares. Decision-makers are considering whether to make overseas investment rules more transparent. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to make overseas investment rules more transparent. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Investors may make better informed decisions. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Greater capital mobility may increase diversification opportunities. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0022","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Traders buy a currency after predicting that it will strengthen next month. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Businesses may understand currency risks better. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Reliable information may reduce some rumours. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0023","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A weaker currency raises local-currency costs of imported wheat and diesel. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Future import-cost shocks may be smaller. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Domestic energy industries may gain investment. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Higher input costs may be passed to households as inflation. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0024","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country earns export receipts but also pays for imports and income transfers abroad. Decision-makers are considering whether to track the components of the current account separately. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to track the components of the current account separately in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Firms may understand export and import trends. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Combining these flows produces the current-account balance. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0025","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"An economy exports $50 billion of merchandise and imports $65 billion. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve the quality of domestically produced export goods. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to improve the quality of domestically produced export goods could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Export demand may increase. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Domestic manufacturing employment may expand. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The goods balance is a $15 billion deficit. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0026","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services. Decision-makers are considering whether to develop skills needed to export digital services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to develop skills needed to export digital services. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Service export receipts may increase. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Skilled employment could expand. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: The services balance is an $8 billion surplus. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0027","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Foreign-owned firms send dividends overseas while residents receive interest from foreign investments. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a decision to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: More investment income may remain with local residents. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Domestic capital formation may rise. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0028","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Citizens working abroad regularly send money home to their families. Decision-makers are considering whether to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Recipient households may receive more of each transfer. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: More funds may enter regulated financial channels. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0029","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve export competitiveness rather than imposing broad import bans. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to improve export competitiveness rather than imposing broad import bans in the circumstances described. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Export earnings may rise over time. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Competitive firms may develop sustainable overseas markets. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0030","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"A country earns more from exports and income flows than it spends on imports and transfers. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest some surplus earnings in productive domestic capacity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Analyse how a policy to invest some surplus earnings in productive domestic capacity could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"1. Chain A, up to 3 marks: Future output and living standards may increase. Identify the relevant mechanism; explain how the change occurs; connect it to the situation.\n  2. Chain B, up to 3 marks: Importing productive machinery may improve efficiency. Identify a different mechanism; develop its economic consequence; apply it to the case.","note":"Use the economic mechanism as support where relevant: A surplus can reflect strong exports or restrained domestic spending and investment. Award 0–3 for each independently developed causal chain (1 valid point, 1 explanation, 1 further development/application)."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0001","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A coastal country exports fish products while importing machines it cannot make cheaply. Decision-makers are considering whether to specialise in sustainable seafood processing for export. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to specialise in sustainable seafood processing for export would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Firms may gain experience and lower average costs.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Export earnings could finance needed imports.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): The country may become dependent on global seafood demand.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Overfishing could damage future productive resources.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0002","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country develops clusters of skilled bicycle-component manufacturers. Decision-makers are considering whether to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Specialist workers may become more productive.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Suppliers may benefit from shared infrastructure.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Demand for bicycle parts could decline.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Regions may become vulnerable to one industry's downturn.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0003","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country relies on one mineral for most of its export receipts. Decision-makers are considering whether to diversify export production away from one mineral. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to diversify export production away from one mineral. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): New activities may create additional jobs.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Developing new industries costs money.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Diversification may reduce some existing scale advantages.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0004","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Two neighbouring countries remove import tariffs on most manufactured goods. Decision-makers are considering whether to remove tariffs on common household appliances. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to remove tariffs on common household appliances is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Consumers may pay lower prices.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Domestic firms may obtain cheaper imported components.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Some local appliance makers may lose market share.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Import dependence may increase in strategic product categories.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0005","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Residents of a cold country buy affordable imported tropical fruit. Decision-makers are considering whether to allow more competition from imported food products. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to allow more competition from imported food products would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Consumers may gain greater variety.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Lower prices may improve real purchasing power.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Domestic farmers may face more competition.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Transport-related emissions may increase.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0006","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries. Decision-makers are considering whether to help small manufacturers access export markets. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to help small manufacturers access export markets would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Sales and revenue may increase.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Higher volumes may allow more efficient production.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Exchange-rate changes can reduce export earnings.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Meeting foreign standards may be costly.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0007","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country imports almost all its fertiliser from a single foreign supplier. Decision-makers are considering whether to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Shortages may become less likely.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Competition between suppliers may reduce reliance on one firm.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Multiple supplier contracts may be more expensive.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Global shortages can affect all source countries.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0008","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Imported sports shoes enter a market previously served by only two local brands. Decision-makers are considering whether to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Buyers may gain more choice and lower prices.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Local firms may become more efficient to compete.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Some domestic workers may lose jobs.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Foreign firms may dominate a very small local market.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0009","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A phone designed in one country is assembled using components made in several others. Decision-makers are considering whether to participate in international production networks. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to participate in international production networks. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Firms may access specialised inputs.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Workers may gain employment in export-oriented industries.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Global disruptions can interrupt supply chains.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Long-distance transport may increase emissions.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0010","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A new container terminal reduces the cost of exporting refrigerated fruit. Decision-makers are considering whether to modernise ports to reduce international shipping delays. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to modernise ports to reduce international shipping delays is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Exporters may reach more customers.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Imported production inputs may become cheaper.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Port upgrades require significant public or private funds.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Expanded shipping may increase local pollution.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0011","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients. Decision-makers are considering whether to expand reliable broadband for exporting service firms. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to expand reliable broadband for exporting service firms would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Firms may sell services into new markets.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): International coordination may become more efficient.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Poor cybersecurity could disrupt business.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Some communities may remain digitally excluded.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0012","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"An international electronics firm opens an assembly plant in another country. Decision-makers are considering whether to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Local workers may gain employment and training.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Supplier firms may win new contracts.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Profits may be remitted to the firm's home country.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): The plant may relocate if costs rise elsewhere.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0013","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country imposes a 15% tariff on imported ceramic tiles. Decision-makers are considering whether to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Domestic tile makers may gain market share.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Government may collect customs revenue.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Households and builders may face higher prices.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Trading partners may retaliate with tariffs of their own.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0014","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A government permits no more than 40,000 imported shoes each year. Decision-makers are considering whether to cap annual imports of low-cost footwear. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to cap annual imports of low-cost footwear in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Some domestic shoe producers may sell more.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Employment in protected firms may be preserved.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Consumers may face less choice and higher prices.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Foreign suppliers may respond with trade restrictions.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0015","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product. Decision-makers are considering whether to ban imports of a product associated with severe environmental harm. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to ban imports of a product associated with severe environmental harm. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Domestic environmental risks may decrease.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Firms may develop safer alternative products.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Consumers could face higher prices for substitutes.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Enforcement and illegal trade may be difficult to control.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0016","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers. Decision-makers are considering whether to protect a new solar-equipment industry for a limited period. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to protect a new solar-equipment industry for a limited period is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): New firms may develop production experience.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Domestic manufacturing jobs may expand.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Protection can keep prices artificially high.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Firms may become dependent on continuing protection.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0017","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"An exchange booth lists one domestic dollar as worth 0.80 foreign units. Decision-makers are considering whether to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Travellers can compare currency conversion costs.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Exporters may plan receipts and payments more accurately.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Exchange rates can fluctuate between quotation and payment.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Transaction fees may reduce the amount received.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0018","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.80 to buying 0.90 foreign units. Decision-makers are considering whether to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Investment equipment may cost less.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Imported inputs may become more affordable.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Exporters may lose price competitiveness abroad.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Future depreciation could reverse the cost saving.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0019","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.90 to buying 0.75 foreign units. Decision-makers are considering whether to promote exports after a currency depreciation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to promote exports after a currency depreciation would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Exports may become more price competitive abroad.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Export-oriented firms may increase output and jobs.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Imported fuel and machinery may become more expensive.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Export demand may respond slowly if contracts are fixed.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0020","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve export quality to attract overseas buyers. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to improve export quality to attract overseas buyers in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Higher export sales may earn foreign currency.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Exporting firms may support additional jobs.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): A stronger currency may later reduce export competitiveness.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Competitors abroad may lower their prices.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0021","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Domestic investors exchange local money for foreign currency to purchase overseas shares. Decision-makers are considering whether to make overseas investment rules more transparent. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to make overseas investment rules more transparent. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Investors may make better informed decisions.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Greater capital mobility may increase diversification opportunities.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Large outflows could weaken the domestic currency.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Funds invested abroad may reduce investment at home.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0022","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Traders buy a currency after predicting that it will strengthen next month. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Businesses may understand currency risks better.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Reliable information may reduce some rumours.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Speculative flows can still cause sharp fluctuations.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Not all future political or economic events are predictable.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0023","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A weaker currency raises local-currency costs of imported wheat and diesel. Decision-makers are considering whether to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Future import-cost shocks may be smaller.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Domestic energy industries may gain investment.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Developing local energy supplies is expensive.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Some energy resources cannot be produced domestically.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0024","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country earns export receipts but also pays for imports and income transfers abroad. Decision-makers are considering whether to track the components of the current account separately. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to track the components of the current account separately would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Firms may understand export and import trends.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Data can arrive with reporting delays.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): A headline balance does not capture all economic conditions.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0025","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"An economy exports $50 billion of merchandise and imports $65 billion. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve the quality of domestically produced export goods. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to improve the quality of domestically produced export goods would outweigh the disadvantages in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Export demand may increase.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Domestic manufacturing employment may expand.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Quality upgrades can be expensive.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Global demand may remain weak.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0026","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services. Decision-makers are considering whether to develop skills needed to export digital services. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to develop skills needed to export digital services in this situation. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Service export receipts may increase.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Skilled employment could expand.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Training costs money and takes time.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Foreign competition may limit prices charged.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0027","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Foreign-owned firms send dividends overseas while residents receive interest from foreign investments. Decision-makers are considering whether to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): More investment income may remain with local residents.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Domestic capital formation may rise.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Domestic savings may be insufficient for large projects.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Foreign direct investment can bring expertise that local finance lacks.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0028","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Citizens working abroad regularly send money home to their families. Decision-makers are considering whether to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a plan to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances is likely to improve economic outcomes in this case. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): More funds may enter regulated financial channels.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Payment services require compliance and monitoring.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Remittance income may fall if overseas employment weakens.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0029","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts. Decision-makers are considering whether to improve export competitiveness rather than imposing broad import bans. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss how far a decision to improve export competitiveness rather than imposing broad import bans would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Export earnings may rise over time.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Competitive firms may develop sustainable overseas markets.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Training and investment take time.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): An overseas slowdown may still reduce exports.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0030","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"A country earns more from exports and income flows than it spends on imports and transfers. Decision-makers are considering whether to invest some surplus earnings in productive domestic capacity. They must weigh the potential effects on resources, costs, incomes and well-being.","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to invest some surplus earnings in productive domestic capacity would be justified. [8]","scheme":"1. Argument supporting the action (up to 2 marks): Future output and living standards may increase.\n  2. Further developed supporting argument (up to 2 marks): Importing productive machinery may improve efficiency.\n  3. Counterargument (up to 1 mark): Extra domestic demand can reduce the surplus.\n  4. Second distinct counterargument (up to 1 mark): Poorly selected projects can waste the earnings.\n  5. Reasoned conclusion / evaluation (up to 2 marks): weigh both sides, connect judgement to the case and identify a condition, timescale or stakeholder that affects success.","note":"Credit equivalent accurate arguments; max 4 for developed support, 2 for genuine limitations, 2 for supported judgement. A conclusion without justified reasoning earns at most 1 of the evaluation marks."}];
